// LC Chemistry Walkthroughs Data (auto-generated)
var WALKTHROUGHS_CHEMISTRY = {
  "2025": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Volumetric Analysis – Iron(II) in Iron Tablets (KMnO4 Titration)",
     q:"A student determined the iron(II) content of iron supplement tablets.\\nSix tablets (total 2.496 g) dissolved in dilute H2SO4, made up to 250 cm3.\\n25.0 cm3 portions titrated against 0.010 M KMnO4.\\nReaction: MnO4- + 5Fe2+ + 8H+ -> Mn2+ + 5Fe3+ + 4H2O\\n\\n(a) Identify a primary standard to standardise the KMnO4 solution.\\n(b)(i) Describe how to bring the solution to the 250 cm3 mark precisely.\\n(ii) Explain why using more than one tablet increases accuracy.\\n(c)(i) Name the apparatus to measure 25.0 cm3 accurately.\\n(ii) Describe how it was rinsed before use.\\n(d)(i) Why was no indicator needed?\\n(ii) What was observed at the end point?\\n(iii) What would happen if insufficient acid was present? Explain.\\n(e) On average, 22.5 cm3 of 0.010 M KMnO4 was needed. Calculate:\\n(i) moles of MnO4- in 22.5 cm3\\n(ii) moles of Fe2+ in the 250 cm3 flask\\n(iii) average mass of Fe(II) in one tablet\\n(iv) percentage by mass of Fe(II) in one tablet.",
     a:"(a) Ammonium iron(II) sulfate / (NH4)2SO4.FeSO4.6H2O. A primary standard is a substance of known high purity used to make a solution of precise concentration.\\n\\n(b)(i) Add deionised water drop by drop / slowly until the bottom of the meniscus is on the 250 cm3 mark.\\n(ii) Using more tablets gives a smaller percentage error / creates a higher concentration of iron solution requiring a larger volume of KMnO4, which reduces the percentage error from a single tablet.\\n\\n(c)(i) Pipette (or burette).\\n(ii) Rinse first with deionised water, then with the iron(II) solution it will contain. This prevents contamination and dilution.\\n\\n(d)(i) Potassium manganate(VII) acts as its own indicator -- it is deep purple and becomes colourless (Mn2+) when it reacts with Fe2+.\\n(ii) Solution turns (pale) pink and the colour persists (first drop of excess KMnO4 not decolourised).\\n(iii) A brown precipitate would form. This is MnO2 / Mn4+. Without sufficient acid (H+), the MnO4- is only partially reduced to MnO2 instead of Mn2+.\\n\\n(e)(i) n = c x V/1000 = 0.010 x 22.5/1000 = 2.25 x 10^-4 mol.\\n(ii) From equation: 1 MnO4- : 5 Fe2+.\\nMoles Fe2+ in 25 cm3 = 2.25 x 10^-4 x 5 = 1.125 x 10^-3 mol.\\nMoles Fe2+ in 250 cm3 = 1.125 x 10^-3 x 10 = 1.125 x 10^-2 mol.\\n(iii) Moles per tablet = 0.01125 / 6 = 1.875 x 10^-3 mol.\\nMass per tablet = 1.875 x 10^-3 x 56 = 0.105 g.\\n(iv) Mass of one tablet = 2.496 / 6 = 0.416 g.\\n% = (0.105 / 0.416) x 100 = 25.24%."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Steam Distillation & Extraction of Clove Oil (Eugenol)",
     q:"Clove oil extracted from dried clove buds by steam distillation.\\n\\n(a)(i) Identify substance A in the distillate.\\n(ii) State and explain the appearance of the distillate.\\n(iii) State one advantage of using steam distillation.\\n\\n(b) Eugenol extracted by liquid-liquid extraction using cyclohexane.\\n(i) Describe, with diagram, the extraction procedure.\\n(ii) State two properties of cyclohexane suitable for this extraction.\\n(iii) State one precaution to maximise yield.\\n(iv) How to isolate eugenol from the dried eugenol-cyclohexane mixture.\\n\\n(c)(i) Suggest one use of eugenol.\\n(ii) Draw the structure of eugenol.\\n(iii) Name a spectroscopic technique to confirm identity.\\n(iv) Calculate volume of eugenol (density 1.06 g/cm3) from 18.0 g cloves at 7% yield.",
     a:"(a)(i) Water / H2O.\\n(ii) Milky appearance -- it is an emulsion / oil droplets dispersed in water. The two immiscible liquids are mixed together.\\n(iii) Minimises decomposition / maximises yield / removes the oil at a lower temperature than its actual boiling point (steam carries it over below 100 degrees C, protecting delicate molecules).\\n\\n(b)(i) Pour the distillate into a separating funnel. Add cyclohexane. Shake (vent regularly to release pressure). Allow layers to separate. The organic layer (cyclohexane + eugenol) forms on top (lower density). Run off the aqueous layer from below. Collect the organic layer. (Labelled diagram showing separating funnel, stopcock, layers.)\\n(ii) Any two: non-polar (dissolves non-polar eugenol) / low solubility in water (immiscible) / low boiling point (easy to evaporate off) / lower density than water (separates on top) / low reactivity.\\n(iii) Repeat the extraction / wash the aqueous layer with fresh cyclohexane / avoid spillage / vent the funnel.\\n(iv) Evaporate the solvent. Cyclohexane has a low boiling point, so gentle heating removes it, leaving eugenol behind.\\n\\n(c)(i) Flavouring / antiseptic / fragrance / dental applications (any one).\\n(ii) Eugenol has a benzene ring with -OH, -OCH3, and -CH2CH=CH2 groups. Show all atoms and bonds.\\n(iii) Infra-red (IR) / mass spectroscopy / ultra-violet (UV).\\n(iv) Mass of eugenol = 18.0 x 0.07 = 1.26 g.\\nVolume = mass / density = 1.26 / 1.06 = 1.2 cm3."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Rate of Reaction – H2O2 Decomposition with MnO2 Catalyst",
     q:"A student monitored O2 production from H2O2 using MnO2 catalyst.\\n5.0 cm3 H2O2 diluted to 20 cm3, 0.5 g finely-powdered MnO2 added.\\nReaction: 2H2O2 -> O2 + 2H2O\\n\\nData: Time (min): 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\nVolume O2 (cm3): 0, 60, 82, 92, 98, 100, 100\\n\\n(a)(i) Plot a graph of volume vs time.\\n(ii) Find the instantaneous rate at 2.5 minutes.\\n\\n(b)(i) Find the average rate over the first 2.5 minutes.\\n(ii) Would granular MnO2 increase, decrease or not change the rate? Explain.\\n(iii) Would more concentrated H2O2 increase, decrease or not change the rate? Explain.\\n\\n(c)(i) Average rate during the first minute.\\n(ii) Average rate during the second minute (t=1 to t=2).\\n(iii) Account for the difference.\\n\\n(d)(i) How does a catalyst alter the rate?\\n(ii) What type of catalysis is involved? Justify.",
     a:"(a)(i) Graph: Axes labelled (time/min on x-axis, volume O2/cm3 on y-axis). Plot all 7 points. Draw a smooth curve with good fit -- rises steeply at first then levels off (plateau at 100 cm3).\\n(ii) Draw a tangent to the curve at t = 2.5 min. Slope of tangent gives instantaneous rate. Rate = approximately 9.8 cm3 min^-1.\\n\\n(b)(i) Average rate = total volume / total time. Volume at 2.5 min (from graph) = approx. 87 cm3. Rate = 87 / 2.5 = 34.8 cm3 min^-1.\\n(ii) Rate would decrease. Granular particles have smaller surface area than fine powder, so fewer collisions between H2O2 and the catalyst surface per unit time.\\n(iii) Rate would increase. More particles (H2O2 molecules) per unit volume means more collisions per unit time, so more successful reactions.\\n\\n(c)(i) Rate = (60 - 0) / (1 - 0) = 60 cm3 min^-1.\\n(ii) Rate = (82 - 60) / (2 - 1) = 22 cm3 min^-1.\\n(iii) As the reaction proceeds, the concentration of H2O2 decreases. Fewer reactant molecules means fewer collisions per unit time, so the rate drops.\\n\\n(d)(i) A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy. More particles have sufficient energy to react, so the rate increases. The catalyst is not consumed.\\n(ii) Surface adsorption / heterogeneous catalysis. Justification: the reactant (H2O2, liquid/aqueous) and catalyst (MnO2, solid) are in different phases. The reaction occurs on the surface of the solid catalyst."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions – Mixed Topics",
     q:"Answer eight of the following:\\n(a) Describe the arrangement of elements in Mendeleev's 1869 periodic table.\\n(b) How many (i) protons, (ii) electrons in a Cr3+ (Z=24, A=52) ion?\\n(c) Write the chemical formulae for (i) calcium hydroxide, (ii) aluminium sulfate.\\n(d) Write the full s, p, d electron configuration for vanadium (V, Z=23).\\n(e) A compound contains 57.84% C, 3.61% H, 38.55% O. Find the empirical formula.\\n(f) State Avogadro's law.\\n(g) Draw the molecular structure of two isomers of C4H10.\\n(h) A dog (8.3 kg) needs 140 mg/kg of a 5.0% (w/v) medicine. Calculate the volume needed.\\n(i) Explain the term BOD (biochemical oxygen demand).\\n(j) How does the melting point of impure benzoic acid differ from pure?\\n(k) Assign oxidation numbers: 3Cu + 2NO3- + 8H+ -> 3Cu2+ + 2NO + 4H2O. Which element is reduced?\\n(l) A: Explain how O2 is separated industrially from air. OR B: Identify the addition polymers from monomers X and Y.",
     a:"(a) Elements arranged by atomic mass and grouped by similar (chemical) properties. Mendeleev left gaps for undiscovered elements.\\n\\n(b)(i) Protons = 24 (atomic number). (ii) Electrons = 21 (24 - 3 = 21, because the 3+ charge means 3 electrons lost).\\n\\n(c)(i) Ca(OH)2. (ii) Al2(SO4)3.\\n\\n(d) 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d3. (23 electrons total: 2+2+6+2+6+2+3 = 23.)\\n\\n(e) C: 57.84/12 = 4.82; H: 3.61/1 = 3.61; O: 38.55/16 = 2.41.\\nDivide by smallest (2.41): C = 2.0, H = 1.5, O = 1.0.\\nMultiply by 2: C4H3O2.\\n\\n(f) At the same temperature and pressure, equal volumes of gases have equal numbers of particles (molecules).\\n\\n(g) Butane: CH3-CH2-CH2-CH3 (straight chain). Methylpropane: CH3-CH(CH3)-CH3 (branched). Show all atoms and bonds.\\n\\n(h) Dose = 140 x 8.3 = 1162 mg. 5.0% (w/v) = 5 g per 100 cm3 = 50 mg per cm3. Volume = 1162 / 50 = 23.24 cm3.\\n\\n(i) BOD = quantity of dissolved oxygen consumed by biological action (microorganisms) over 5 days in the dark at 20 degrees C.\\n\\n(j) Impure benzoic acid has (1) a lower melting point and (2) a broader/less sharp melting range than pure benzoic acid.\\n\\n(k) N: +5 in NO3- -> +2 in NO (reduced, gained electrons). Cu: 0 -> +2 (oxidised). Element reduced: Nitrogen (N).\\n\\n(l) A: By fractional distillation of liquefied air. Air is cooled and compressed until it liquefies, then slowly warmed. N2 boils off first (-196 degrees C), leaving O2 (-183 degrees C).\\nB: X = polyphenylethene / polystyrene. Y = polypropylene / polypropene."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Atomic Structure – Bromine Isotopes, Orbitals & Atomic Radius",
     q:"(a) A sample of bromine contains 50.69% Br-79 and 49.31% Br-81.\\n(i) Name the instrument used to determine isotope abundance.\\n(ii) Compare the nuclei of Br-79 and Br-81.\\n(iii) Explain what is meant by relative atomic mass.\\n(iv) Calculate the relative atomic mass of bromine (2 decimal places).\\n\\n(b) Atomic orbital diagram for bromine in its ground state shown.\\n(i) What is an atomic orbital?\\n(ii) Draw the shape of the lowest energy orbital.\\n(iii) Maximum electrons in a single p-orbital?\\n(iv) How are orbitals of equal energy filled with electrons?\\n(v) Under what circumstance might an electron occupy a higher energy orbital?\\n\\n(c)(i) Define atomic (covalent) radius.\\n(ii) Explain why bromine has a greater radius than chlorine.\\n(iii) Explain why bromine has a smaller radius than selenium.",
     a:"(a)(i) Mass spectrometer.\\n(ii) Both have 35 protons (same element). Br-79 has 44 neutrons (79-35), Br-81 has 46 neutrons (81-35). Same protons, different neutrons = isotopes.\\n(iii) The average mass of an atom of an element, compared to one-twelfth the mass of a carbon-12 isotope. It is a weighted average based on the abundance of each isotope.\\n(iv) (50.69 x 79) + (49.31 x 81) = 4004.51 + 3994.11 = 7998.62.\\n7998.62 / 100 = 79.99.\\n\\n(b)(i) A region in space around the nucleus where there is a high probability of finding an electron.\\n(ii) Sphere / circle (the 1s orbital is spherical).\\n(iii) 2 (each single orbital holds max 2 electrons with opposite spins). Note: a p-subshell has 3 orbitals holding 6 electrons total, but one p-orbital holds only 2.\\n(iv) Electrons occupy orbitals singly first, all with the same spin, then pair up (Hund's Rule). Like passengers on a bus -- everyone takes an empty seat before sitting beside someone.\\n(v) When the atom absorbs a fixed amount of energy (a photon of light or heat), an electron can be promoted (excited) to a higher energy orbital temporarily.\\n\\n(c)(i) Half the distance between the nuclei of two atoms of the same element joined by a single covalent bond.\\n(ii) Bromine has more energy levels (electron shells) than chlorine (Br has 4 shells, Cl has 3). More shells = bigger atom.\\n(iii) Bromine has a greater nuclear charge / greater atomic number than selenium (same number of shells/period 4, but Br has more protons pulling electrons in more tightly, so the atom is smaller)."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Fuels, Fractional Distillation & Thermochemistry (Hydrazine)",
     q:"(a) Crude oil is a mixture of mostly saturated hydrocarbons, separated by fractional distillation.\\n(i) What is meant by saturated, as applied to hydrocarbons?\\n(ii) Describe, with diagram, how refinery gas is obtained by fractional distillation.\\n(iii) Identify the two major hydrocarbon components of LPG.\\n(iv) Name the process that converts octane to 2,2,4-trimethylpentane.\\n(v) Identify the inorganic co-product when hexane is converted to cyclohexane.\\n\\n(b)(i) Define heat of combustion.\\n(ii) Name an instrument to measure heat of combustion accurately.\\n(iii) State two industrial methods for producing hydrogen gas.\\n\\n(c) 2NH3 + H2O2 -> N2H4 + 2H2O\\nCalculate the heat change given: Hf(NH3) = -45.9, Hf(H2O2) = -187.8, Hf(N2H4) = +50.6, Hf(H2O) = -285.8 kJ/mol.",
     a:"(a)(i) A saturated hydrocarbon contains only single bonds between carbon atoms (no C=C or C-C triple bonds).\\n(ii) Crude oil is heated and vapours rise through a fractionating column with trays/outlet pipes at various levels. Temperature decreases from bottom to top. Heated crude oil enters near the base. Lighter fractions (low boiling point, like refinery gas) rise to the top and are collected. Heavier fractions stay lower. Refinery gas exits at the top of the column.\\n(iii) Propane (C3H8) and butane (C4H10).\\n(iv) Isomerisation (same molecular formula C8H18, different structure -- branched instead of straight chain, giving higher octane number).\\n(v) Hydrogen gas (H2). This is a dehydrogenation/reforming reaction -- the straight chain forms a ring, releasing H2.\\n\\n(b)(i) The heat released when one mole of a substance reacts completely with excess oxygen (under standard conditions).\\n(ii) Bomb calorimeter.\\n(iii) (1) Steam reforming of natural gas (CH4 + H2O -> CO + 3H2). (2) Electrolysis of water (2H2O -> 2H2 + O2).\\n\\n(c) Using Hess's Law: delta H = sum Hf(products) - sum Hf(reactants).\\nProducts: Hf(N2H4) + 2 x Hf(H2O) = (+50.6) + 2(-285.8) = 50.6 - 571.6 = -521.0 kJ.\\nReactants: 2 x Hf(NH3) + Hf(H2O2) = 2(-45.9) + (-187.8) = -91.8 - 187.8 = -279.6 kJ.\\ndelta H = -521.0 - (-279.6) = -521.0 + 279.6 = -241.4 kJ.\\nThe reaction is exothermic (negative delta H)."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Acids, Bases, pH Calculations & Indicators",
     q:"(a)(i) Explain how an acid differs from a base (Bronsted-Lowry theory).\\n(ii) How does a strong acid differ from a weak acid?\\n(iii) What is a conjugate acid-base pair?\\n(iv) Identify the conjugate acid-base pairs: HCO3-(aq) + H2O(l) <=> H2CO3(aq) + OH-(aq)\\n\\n(b)(i) Define pH.\\n(ii) Calculate [H3O+] for a strong acid with pH = 1.4.\\n(iii) Calculate the pH of a 0.015 M weak monobasic acid with Ka = 3.2 x 10^-4.\\n\\n(c)(i) Write an expression for Kw (ionic product of water).\\nKw at 60 degrees C = 9.61 x 10^-14 mol2/L2.\\n(ii) Calculate the pH of pure water at 60 degrees C.\\n\\n(d) Indicator HA (yellow) dissociates: HA + H2O <=> H3O+ + A- (blue). pH range 4.2-5.4.\\n(i) What colour in a strong base? Justify.\\n(ii) Is HA suitable for a strong acid / strong base titration? Justify using the pH curve.",
     a:"(a)(i) An acid is a proton (H+) donor. A base is a proton (H+) acceptor.\\n(ii) A strong acid is a good proton donor (fully dissociates in water). A weak acid is a poor proton donor (only partially dissociates -- equilibrium lies to the left).\\n(iii) A conjugate acid-base pair consists of two species that differ by one proton (H+). When an acid donates H+, it becomes its conjugate base, and vice versa.\\n(iv) Pair 1: HCO3- (acid/base) and H2CO3 (conjugate acid) -- differ by H+.\\nPair 2: H2O (acid/base) and OH- (conjugate base) -- differ by H+.\\n\\n(b)(i) pH = -log10[H+] (the negative logarithm of the hydrogen ion concentration in mol/L).\\n(ii) [H+] = 10^(-pH) = 10^(-1.4) = 0.0398 mol/L.\\n(iii) For a weak acid: [H+] = sqrt(Ka x c) = sqrt(3.2 x 10^-4 x 0.015) = sqrt(4.8 x 10^-6) = 2.19 x 10^-3 mol/L.\\npH = -log(2.19 x 10^-3) = 2.66 (accept 2.7).\\n\\n(c)(i) Kw = [H+][OH-].\\n(ii) In pure water: [H+] = [OH-] = sqrt(Kw) = sqrt(9.61 x 10^-14) = 3.1 x 10^-7 mol/L.\\npH = -log(3.1 x 10^-7) = 6.5.\\nImportant: Pure water at 60 degrees C has pH 6.5, NOT 7. But it is still neutral because [H+] = [OH-]. At higher temperatures Kw increases, so [H+] increases and pH drops below 7.\\n\\n(d)(i) Blue. In a strong base (high pH), the equilibrium HA <=> H3O+ + A- shifts right (Le Chatelier -- base removes H3O+). More A- (blue form) is present.\\n(ii) Yes, HA is suitable. The indicator changes colour in the pH range 4.2-5.4, and this range falls within the steep vertical portion of the pH curve at the equivalence point of a strong acid/strong base titration. Any indicator that changes colour within the steep region gives an accurate end point."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Reaction Scheme – Ethanal, Ethanol & Esterification",
     q:"Reaction scheme: ethanal converted to ethanol (reaction A), to ethanoic acid (reaction B), and ethanol + ethanoic acid -> ethyl ethanoate (reaction C).\\n\\n(a) Identify a reaction (A to C) that is: (i) a substitution reaction, (ii) an oxidation reaction, (iii) a reduction reaction.\\n\\n(b)(i) Name the homologous series to which ethanal belongs.\\n(ii) State the colour change when ethanal is warmed with Fehling's reagent.\\n(iii) Identify the reagent and catalyst to convert ethanal to ethanol.\\n\\n(c) Ethanol with O-18 (O*) reacts with ethanoic acid:\\nC2H5O*H + CH3COOH <=> CH3COO*C2H5 + H2O\\n(i) Draw the structure of ethyl ethanoate showing all atoms and bonds.\\n(ii) Indicate the position of O* in ethyl ethanoate.\\n(iii) Indicate the bond formed during esterification.\\n(iv-vi) Count sigma bond electrons, pi bond electrons, and lone pairs in ethyl ethanoate.\\n\\n(d)(i) Write a balanced equation for the reaction of ethanol with sodium.\\n(ii) Explain why the ethanoate ion (CH3COO-) is a weaker base than the ethoxide ion (C2H5O-).",
     a:"(a)(i) Substitution reaction: C (esterification -- OH group from acid replaced by OR from alcohol).\\n(ii) Oxidation reaction: B (ethanal -> ethanoic acid, adding oxygen / gaining O).\\n(iii) Reduction reaction: A (ethanal -> ethanol, adding hydrogen / gaining H).\\nMemory aid: Oxidation = adding O or removing H. Reduction = adding H or removing O.\\n\\n(b)(i) Aldehyde (functional group -CHO).\\n(ii) Blue to red (brick-red precipitate of Cu2O forms). This test distinguishes aldehydes from ketones -- only aldehydes give a positive result.\\n(iii) Reagent: Hydrogen gas (H2). Catalyst: Nickel (Ni). This is catalytic hydrogenation / reduction.\\n\\n(c)(i) Ethyl ethanoate: H3C-C(=O)-O-CH2-CH3 showing all atoms and bonds (ester linkage: C-O-C).\\n(ii) The O* (oxygen-18) is the single-bonded oxygen in the ester linkage (C-O*-C). It came from the ethanol.\\n(iii) The new bond formed is the C-O* bond between the carbonyl carbon of the acid and the O* from ethanol. The water molecule forms from the -OH of the acid and the H from the alcohol's OH.\\n(iv) Sigma bond electrons: 26 (count every single bond x 2 electrons, plus each bond in the C=O counts 1 sigma).\\n(v) Pi bond electrons: 2 (one C=O double bond has one pi bond = 2 electrons).\\n(vi) Lone pairs: 4 (two on each oxygen atom that are not involved in bonding).\\n\\n(d)(i) 2C2H5OH + 2Na -> 2C2H5ONa + H2 (sodium ethoxide and hydrogen gas produced).\\n(ii) The ethanoate ion (CH3COO-) is more stable because the negative charge is delocalised (spread) over two oxygen atoms. This makes it less likely to accept a proton, so it is a weaker base. In ethoxide (C2H5O-), the charge is localised on one oxygen, making it a stronger base and more reactive toward protons."},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Chemical Equilibrium – NOBr Decomposition",
     q:"Reaction: 2NOBr(g) <=> 2NO(g) + Br2(g)\\n55 g of NOBr placed in a 2 L container at temperature T. At equilibrium 78% has decomposed.\\n\\n(a)(i) What is meant by a reversible reaction?\\n(ii) Explain why concentrations remain constant at equilibrium.\\n\\n(b)(i) Calculate the initial moles of NOBr.\\n(ii) Calculate moles of NOBr remaining at equilibrium.\\n(iii) Write the expression for Kc.\\n(iv) Calculate Kc.\\n\\n(c)(i) State Le Chatelier's Principle.\\n(ii) What is the effect of increasing pressure on the amount of Br2? Explain.\\n(iii) Given that Kc increases with temperature, is the forward reaction endothermic or exothermic? Explain.",
     a:"(a)(i) A reversible reaction is one where reactants can form products AND products can re-form reactants. It can proceed in both directions (represented by the double arrow <=>).\\n(ii) At equilibrium, the rates of the forward and reverse reactions are equal. Reactants are consumed by the forward reaction at the same rate they are reformed by the reverse reaction, so all concentrations remain constant over time.\\n\\n(b)(i) Mr of NOBr = 14 + 16 + 80 = 110.\\nMoles = 55 / 110 = 0.5 mol.\\n(ii) 78% decomposed = 0.5 x 0.78 = 0.390 mol decomposed.\\nMoles remaining = 0.5 - 0.390 = 0.110 mol NOBr.\\n(iii) Kc = [NO]^2 [Br2] / [NOBr]^2.\\n(iv) From stoichiometry (2NOBr -> 2NO + Br2):\\nIf 0.390 mol NOBr decompose: 0.390 mol NO formed and 0.195 mol Br2 formed.\\nConcentrations (divide by 2 L):\\n[NOBr] = 0.110/2 = 0.055 M.\\n[NO] = 0.390/2 = 0.195 M.\\n[Br2] = 0.195/2 = 0.0975 M.\\nKc = (0.195)^2 x (0.0975) / (0.055)^2\\n= (0.038025 x 0.0975) / 0.003025\\n= 0.003707 / 0.003025\\n= 1.2 mol/L (accept 1.2-1.3).\\n\\n(c)(i) Le Chatelier's Principle: When a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change (in concentration, pressure, or temperature), the equilibrium shifts in the direction that tends to counteract/oppose that change.\\n(ii) Moles of Br2 decrease. Increased pressure causes equilibrium to shift to the side with fewer moles of gas. Left side has 2 mol gas, right side has 3 mol gas (2NO + Br2). So equilibrium shifts LEFT, reducing the amount of Br2.\\n(iii) The forward reaction is endothermic. If Kc increases with temperature, more products are formed at higher temperature. By Le Chatelier's, the system shifts to absorb the added heat -- it shifts in the endothermic (forward) direction. So the forward reaction absorbs heat (endothermic)."},

    {num:10, label:"Q10: Bonding, Molecular Shape & Electrophilic Addition",
     q:"(a) Nitrogen trifluoride (NF3):\\n(i) Using electronegativity values, predict the type of bond in NF3.\\n(ii) Draw a dot and cross diagram for NF3.\\n(iii) What is the shape of NF3? Explain using VSEPR theory.\\n(iv) Draw a diagram showing intermolecular forces between two NF3 molecules.\\n(v) Name the intermolecular force between NF3 molecules.\\n(vi) Explain why BF3 is non-polar while NF3 is polar, despite both containing polar bonds.\\n\\n(b) Electrophilic addition of Cl2 to ethene:\\n(i) Draw the structural formula of ethene.\\n(ii) What bond is broken in ethene during addition?\\n(iii) Name the two species formed when Cl2 undergoes heterolytic fission near ethene.\\n(iv) Describe the intermediate ionic species formed.\\n(v) Explain how the product 1,2-dichloroethane forms.\\n(vi) What molecule is eliminated when 1,2-dichloroethane is converted to the PVC monomer?",
     a:"(a)(i) Electronegativity difference = 3.98(F) - 3.04(N) = 0.94. Bond type: polar covalent (between 0.4 and 1.7 on the electronegativity difference scale).\\n(ii) N in centre with 3 F atoms around it. Each N-F bond is a shared pair. N has one lone pair. Each F has 3 lone pairs.\\n(iii) Shape: pyramidal. Reason: one lone pair and three bonding pairs around the central N atom. VSEPR theory: the lone pair repels more strongly than bonding pairs, pushing the bonding pairs closer together and creating a pyramid shape (bond angle approximately 107 degrees, less than 109.5 degrees).\\n(iv) Show two NF3 molecules with delta+ on N and delta- on F atoms. Dotted lines between opposite partial charges on adjacent molecules represent the intermolecular forces.\\n(v) Dipole-dipole forces (also Van der Waals forces are present, but dipole-dipole is the dominant type since NF3 is a polar molecule).\\n(vi) BF3 is trigonal planar (symmetrical) -- the three B-F bond dipoles cancel out exactly, so the molecule has no net dipole. NF3 is pyramidal (asymmetrical) -- the lone pair on nitrogen makes the molecule asymmetric, so the N-F bond dipoles do NOT cancel out. The result is a net dipole moment, making NF3 polar.\\n\\n(b)(i) Ethene: H2C=CH2, showing the double bond and all H atoms.\\n(ii) The pi bond in the C=C double bond is broken (the sigma bond remains).\\n(iii) Cl+ (electrophile) and Cl- (nucleophile). As Cl2 approaches the electron-rich C=C bond, it polarises: one Cl gets both bonding electrons (Cl-) and the other gets none (Cl+).\\n(iv) A carbocation / bridged chloronium ion intermediate. The Cl+ adds to one carbon of the double bond. The other carbon carries a positive charge (or both carbons share a bridged positive chlorine).\\n(v) The Cl- ion (nucleophile) attacks the positively charged carbon of the intermediate, adding to the other carbon. This completes the addition, forming 1,2-dichloroethane (ClCH2CH2Cl).\\n(vi) HCl (hydrogen chloride). 1,2-dichloroethane loses HCl in an elimination reaction to form chloroethene (vinyl chloride, CH2=CHCl), which then polymerises to PVC."},

    {num:11, label:"Q11: Environmental & Applied Chemistry – Water, Heat of Reaction & Radioactivity",
     q:"(a) Water quality:\\n(i) What is meant by total hardness of water?\\n(ii) Give the full name of EDTA.\\n(iii) Give one method of removing total hardness.\\n(iv) Describe a test for nitrate ions in water.\\n(v) Name a technique to measure lead ion concentration in water.\\n(vi) Give one method of removing lead ions from water.\\n(vii) 5.0 L of 1% (w/v) NaOCl diluted to 25 L. Calculate concentration in ppm.\\n\\n(b) Heat of reaction experiment: HCl + NaOH -> NaCl + H2O (80 cm3 of 1.0 M each, delta T = 6.8 K):\\n(i) Why did the temperature rise?\\n(ii) Define heat of reaction.\\n(iii) Calculate moles of HCl.\\n(iv) Calculate heat produced (q = mcT, density 1.0 g/cm3, c = 4.2 kJ/kg/K).\\n(v) Calculate delta H in kJ/mol.\\n\\n(c) Radioactivity:\\n(i) Who discovered radium?\\n(ii) What is an alpha particle?\\n(iii) Identify the product of alpha decay of Ra-226.\\n(iv) Why is radium paint on a watch face not dangerous to the wearer?\\n(v) Why is a watch painted 100 years ago still hazardous?\\n(vi-viii) Beta decay of Ra-225: find mass number, atomic number, and element name of the product.\\n\\n(d) Option A: Nitrogen cycle or Option B: Electrolysis of PbBr2.",
     a:"(a)(i) Total hardness = temporary hardness + permanent hardness -- all dissolved Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions in the water.\\n(ii) Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid.\\n(iii) Ion exchange / use of a deioniser. Hard water passes through a resin that swaps Ca2+/Mg2+ for Na+ ions.\\n(iv) Brown ring test: add iron(II) sulfate (FeSO4), then carefully add concentrated sulfuric acid (H2SO4). A brown ring forms at the interface if nitrate ions are present.\\n(v) Atomic absorption spectrometry (AAS).\\n(vi) Precipitation / reverse osmosis / ion exchange.\\n(vii) 1% (w/v) = 1 g per 100 cm3 = 10 g per L = 10,000 mg/L.\\n5.0 L contains 5 x 10,000 = 50,000 mg.\\nDiluted to 25 L: 50,000/25 = 2000 ppm (mg/L).\\n\\n(b)(i) Heat released / the reaction is exothermic.\\n(ii) Heat of reaction = the heat change when the amounts shown in a balanced equation react completely.\\n(iii) n = c x V/1000 = 1.0 x 80/1000 = 0.08 mol.\\n(iv) Total volume = 80 + 80 = 160 cm3. Mass = 160 x 1.0 = 160 g = 0.160 kg.\\nq = mcT = 0.160 x 4.2 x 6.8 = 4.5696 kJ.\\n(v) delta H = -q/n = -(4.5696/0.08) = -57.12 kJ/mol (negative because exothermic).\\n\\n(c)(i) Marie Curie (and Pierre Curie).\\n(ii) An alpha particle = 2 protons + 2 neutrons = a helium-4 nucleus.\\n(iii) Ra-226 -> Rn-222 + He-4. (226-4 = 222 mass number, 88-2 = 86 atomic number = radon.)\\n(iv) Alpha radiation has very low penetrating power. It cannot pass through the watch glass or even a sheet of paper, so it does not reach the wearer's skin.\\n(v) Ra-226 has a half-life of 1600 years. In 100 years, very little of the sample has decayed, so it is still highly radioactive and hazardous if the watch is broken open (ingestion/inhalation risk).\\n(vi) Mass number X = 225 (beta decay does not change mass number).\\n(vii) Atomic number Y = 89 (88+1 = 89, because a neutron converts to a proton).\\n(viii) Element Z = Actinium (Ac, Z=89).\\n\\n(d) Option A (Nitrogen Cycle):\\n(i) N2 is chemically unreactive due to its strong triple bond (very high bond energy).\\n(ii) N2 + O2 -> 2NO (in lightning/engines), then 2NO + O2 -> 2NO2.\\n(iii) Acids formed: HNO2 (nitrous acid) and HNO3 (nitric acid).\\n(iv) Plants need nitrogen to make protein / DNA / amino acids.\\n(v) Nitrates are recycled by denitrification (denitrifying bacteria convert NO3- back to N2).\\n(vi) Animals return nitrogen to the soil through decay / decomposition / excretion.\\n\\nOption B (Electrolysis of PbBr2):\\n(i) Electrolysis = breaking down a compound using electricity.\\n(ii) Inert electrodes: graphite (C) or platinum (Pt).\\n(iii) Solid PbBr2 does not conduct; molten PbBr2 conducts because ions are free to move.\\n(iv) Oxidation = loss of electrons.\\n(v) Element at anode: bromine (Br2).\\n(vi) Half-equation at anode: 2Br- -> Br2 + 2e-."}
  ],

  "2024": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Volumetric Analysis – Ethanoic Acid in Vinegar",
     q:"A student determined the concentration of ethanoic acid in vinegar.\\n10.0 cm3 of vinegar was diluted to exactly 50.0 cm3. The diluted vinegar was titrated against 0.09 M NaOH.\\nOn average, 12.4 cm3 of diluted vinegar neutralised 25.0 cm3 of NaOH.\\nReaction: CH3COOH + NaOH -> CH3COONa + H2O\\n\\n(a)(i) Name the apparatus used to measure 10.0 cm3 of vinegar accurately.\\n(ii) Describe how it was rinsed before use.\\n(iii) Describe how to dilute the 10.0 cm3 to exactly 50.0 cm3.\\n\\n(b)(i) Calculate the volume of undiluted vinegar needed to neutralise 25.0 cm3 of 0.09 M NaOH.\\n(ii) Outline an advantage of diluting the vinegar before titration.\\n\\n(c)(i) Name a suitable indicator.\\n(ii) State the colour change at the end point.\\n\\n(d) Calculate:\\n(i) moles of NaOH in 25.0 cm3\\n(ii) moles of ethanoic acid per cm3 of diluted vinegar\\n(iii) concentration of ethanoic acid in original vinegar (mol/L)\\n(iv) concentration of ethanoic acid in original vinegar in % (w/v).",
     a:"(a)(i) Pipette (or burette; accept graduated cylinder/syringe).\\n(ii) Rinse first with deionised water, then with the vinegar itself.\\n(iii) Add the 10.0 cm3 of vinegar to a 50 cm3 volumetric flask. Add deionised water carefully until the bottom of the meniscus is on the 50.0 cm3 mark.\\n\\n(b)(i) Dilution factor = 50/10 = 5.\\nVolume of undiluted vinegar = 12.4 / 5 = 2.48 cm3 (accept 2.5 cm3).\\n(ii) Diluting gives a larger, more measurable titre, reducing percentage error / more accurate volume reading.\\n\\n(c)(i) Phenolphthalein.\\n(ii) Pink to colourless (in the conical flask containing NaOH, the pink colour disappears as acid is added from the burette).\\n\\n(d)(i) Moles NaOH = (0.09 x 25.0) / 1000 = 0.00225 mol.\\n(ii) From equation, 1:1 ratio. Moles ethanoic acid = 0.00225 mol in 12.4 cm3.\\nPer cm3 = 0.00225 / 12.4 = 0.000181 mol/cm3.\\n(iii) Per litre of diluted vinegar = 0.000181 x 1000 = 0.181 M.\\nOriginal vinegar was 5x more concentrated: 0.181 x 5 = 0.91 M.\\n(iv) Mr of CH3COOH = 60.\\n% (w/v) = (0.91 x 60) / 10 = 5.46%.\\n\\nKey point: The dilution factor of 5 must be applied to convert from diluted to original concentration. % (w/v) = (mass in g per 100 cm3), so (mol/L x Mr) / 10."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Recrystallisation, Ethanal Oxidation & Soap Preparation",
     q:"(a) Recrystallisation of benzoic acid using water:\\n(i) Why is water a suitable solvent?\\n(ii) What is the purpose of the first (hot) filtration?\\n(iii) Why is fluted filter paper in a warm funnel suitable for hot filtration?\\n(iv) Why is the second filtration only done after sufficient cooling?\\n(v) What observation shows the recrystallised benzoic acid is pure?\\n\\n(b) Testing that ethanal is easily oxidised:\\n(i) Identify the reagent(s) used.\\n(ii) State the colour change observed.\\n(iii) Identify the organic product.\\n(iv) Write a half equation for the reduction of the inorganic reagent.\\n\\n(c) Preparation of soap:\\n(i) Identify the reactants.\\n(ii) Identify the solvent used.\\n(iii) Name the co-product formed and draw its molecular structure.",
     a:"(a)(i) Benzoic acid has low solubility in cold water but high solubility in hot water -- ideal for recrystallisation.\\n(ii) To remove insoluble impurities.\\n(iii) The fluted paper increases surface area for faster filtration; the warm funnel keeps the solution hot and prevents premature crystallisation (avoids benzoic acid coming out of solution in the funnel).\\n(iv) To allow maximum crystallisation of pure benzoic acid, giving the best yield.\\n(v) The melting point is sharp (narrow range) and close to the expected value (122 degrees C).\\n\\n(b)(i) Acidified KMnO4 solution, OR Fehling's solution (A and B), OR Tollens' reagent.\\n(ii) KMnO4: purple/pink to colourless. Fehling's: blue to red precipitate. Tollens': colourless to silver mirror.\\n(iii) Ethanoic acid (CH3COOH).\\n(iv) For KMnO4: MnO4- + 8H+ + 5e- -> Mn2+ + 4H2O.\\nFor Fehling's: Cu2+ + e- -> Cu+.\\nFor Tollens': Ag+ + e- -> Ag.\\n\\n(c)(i) A fat/oil/triglyceride AND sodium hydroxide (NaOH) or potassium hydroxide (KOH).\\n(ii) Ethanol.\\n(iii) Propane-1,2,3-triol (glycerol). Structure: HOCH2-CH(OH)-CH2OH, with all atoms and bonds shown."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Rates of Reaction – Na2S2O3 & HCl (Concentration Effect)",
     q:"Na2S2O3 + 2HCl -> 2NaCl + SO2 + S + H2O\\nDifferent concentrations of Na2S2O3 tested. 100 cm3 Na2S2O3 mixed with 10 cm3 of 3 M HCl each time.\\nTime measured for same mass of sulfur to precipitate. Rate = 1/time.\\n\\nData:\\nConcentration (M): 0.10, 0.09, 0.08, 0.07, 0.06, 0.05, 0.04, 0.03\\nTime (s): 50, 56, 63, 72, 84, 100, 125, 168\\n\\n(a)(i) Describe a method to know when the same mass of sulfur has precipitated.\\n(ii) Describe how to prepare 100 cm3 of 0.09 M from 0.10 M Na2S2O3.\\n\\n(b)(i) Complete the rate row (1/time, 3 decimal places).\\n(ii) Draw a graph of rate vs. concentration.\\n(iii) What can be concluded?\\n\\n(c)(i) Estimate the rate for 0.015 M from your graph.\\n(ii) Calculate the time for sulfur to precipitate in that case.\\n\\n(d) Effect of temperature (fixed concentrations, times: T1=40s, T2=10s, T3=25s):\\n(i) Describe a method for heating and measuring temperature.\\n(ii) Which temperature was highest? Justify.",
     a:"(a)(i) Place an X (cross mark) on paper under the flask. Start the stopwatch when the reactants are mixed. Time is measured until the X is no longer visible through the solution due to the sulfur precipitate.\\n(ii) Take 90 cm3 of the 0.10 M Na2S2O3 solution and make it up to 100 cm3 by adding 10 cm3 of deionised water (using a volumetric flask or graduated cylinder).\\n\\n(b)(i) Rate values (1/time):\\n0.08 M: 1/63 = 0.016; 0.07 M: 1/72 = 0.014; 0.06 M: 1/84 = 0.012;\\n0.05 M: 1/100 = 0.010; 0.04 M: 1/125 = 0.008; 0.03 M: 1/168 = 0.006.\\n(ii) Plot rate (y-axis) vs concentration (x-axis). Label axes. Plot all 8 points. Draw a line of best fit.\\n(iii) The rate of reaction is directly proportional to the concentration of sodium thiosulfate (straight line through origin).\\n\\n(c)(i) From the graph, at 0.015 M the rate is approximately 0.003 s^-1.\\n(ii) Time = 1/rate = 1/0.003 = 333 seconds.\\n\\n(d)(i) Heat the reactants in a water bath (beaker of water on a hotplate or over a Bunsen burner). Use a thermometer to measure the temperature of the solution before mixing.\\n(ii) T2 was the highest temperature. It had the shortest time (10 s), meaning the fastest rate. Higher temperature increases kinetic energy of particles, leading to more frequent and energetic collisions."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions – Mixed Topics",
     q:"Answer eight of the following:\\n(a) How many (i) protons, (ii) neutrons in helium-3?\\n(b) Identify two reagents to test for phosphate ions in aqueous solution.\\n(c) State two reasons why real gases deviate from ideal behaviour.\\n(d) Two identical flasks at same T and P: one has O2 (2.4 g), one has He. Calculate mass of He.\\n(e) Standard units of (i) first ionisation energy, (ii) BOD.\\n(f) Name three homologous series containing a carbonyl group.\\n(g)(i) Why are oxygenates added to petrol? (ii) Identify a suitable oxygenate.\\n(h) Purpose of aluminium sulfate (i) in water treatment, (ii) in tertiary sewage treatment.\\n(i) Write a balanced half equation for the cathode reaction in electrolysis of aqueous Na2SO4.\\n(j) Explain the fundamental principle of chromatography.\\n(k) Calculate % yield: 2412.3 g of benzocaine obtained when theoretical yield was 21.5 moles (Mr = 165).\\n(l) A: Identify three species when CO2 dissolves in pure water. OR B: Outline contributions of Lawrence Bragg and Dorothy Hodgkin to crystallography.",
     a:"(a)(i) 2 protons. (ii) 1 neutron (mass number 3 minus 2 protons).\\n\\n(b) Ammonium molybdate and nitric acid. (A yellow precipitate indicates phosphate.)\\n\\n(c) (1) Gas particles have a finite volume (not zero as assumed by ideal gas model). (2) Intermolecular forces exist between gas particles (attractions/repulsions are not zero). (Also: collisions are not perfectly elastic.)\\n\\n(d) By Avogadro's law, equal volumes at same T and P contain equal moles.\\nMoles O2 = 2.4 / 32 = 0.075 mol. So moles He = 0.075 mol.\\nMass He = 0.075 x 4 = 0.3 g.\\n\\n(e)(i) kJ mol^-1. (ii) ppm (mg/L).\\n\\n(f) Aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids (also esters -- any three).\\n\\n(g)(i) To increase the octane number of the fuel / reduce knocking / promote smoother combustion.\\n(ii) Ethanol, or MTBE (methyl tert-butyl ether).\\n\\n(h)(i) To cause flocculation (suspended solids clump together and settle).\\n(ii) To remove phosphates (phosphate ions precipitate as insoluble aluminium phosphate).\\n\\n(i) 2H2O + 2e- -> H2 + 2OH- (hydrogen gas produced at cathode).\\n\\n(j) Chromatography involves a mobile phase and a stationary phase. Different substances have different affinities for each phase (selective adsorption), so they separate as the mobile phase moves.\\n\\n(k) Mr of benzocaine = 165.\\nTheoretical yield in grams = 21.5 x 165 = 3547.5 g.\\n% yield = (2412.3 / 3547.5) x 100 = 68%.\\n\\n(l) A: H2CO3 (carbonic acid), HCO3- (hydrogencarbonate ion), H+ (hydrogen ion). Also CO3(2-) or CO2(aq).\\nB: (i) Lawrence Bragg developed the X-ray crystallography technique for determining crystal structures.\\n(ii) Dorothy Hodgkin used X-ray crystallography to determine the structures of important biomolecules (e.g. insulin, penicillin, vitamin B12)."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Atomic Structure – Bohr, Energy Levels & Orbitals",
     q:"Context: In 1885 Balmer described visible line spectrum of hydrogen. In 1913 Bohr's model was shown consistent with the Balmer series.\\n\\n(a)(i) Explain the term energy level.\\n(ii) Distinguish between the ground state and excited states of the electron in hydrogen.\\n(iii) What happens when an electron moves from an excited state to the ground state?\\n(iv) Name the instrument used to examine the line spectrum of an element.\\n\\n(b)(i) Explain how the Heisenberg uncertainty principle applies to an electron in an atom.\\n(ii) Write the full s, p electron configuration for neon (Ne) in its ground state, including the number of electrons in each orbital.\\n(iii) Distinguish between an energy sublevel and an atomic orbital, using neon as an example.\\n(iv) Explain why neon is chemically unreactive, by reference to its electron configuration.\\n(v) Explain how successive ionisation energies of neon provide evidence for energy levels.\\n(vi) Draw the shape of a p orbital.",
     a:"(a)(i) An energy level is a fixed (quantised) amount of energy that an electron in an atom may have. Electrons can only occupy specific energy levels, not energies in between.\\n(ii) Ground state: the electron is in the lowest available energy level (n=1 for hydrogen). Excited state: the electron has absorbed energy and moved to a higher energy level (n=2, 3, ...).\\n(iii) The electron loses energy. This energy is emitted as a photon of electromagnetic radiation (light). The frequency/wavelength of the photon corresponds to the energy difference between the levels (E = hf).\\n(iv) A spectrometer (or spectroscope / prism / diffraction grating apparatus).\\n\\n(b)(i) It is impossible to simultaneously know both the exact position and the exact momentum (velocity) of an electron. This means we can only describe the probability of finding an electron in a given region, not its exact path or orbit.\\n(ii) 1s2 2s2 2p6. In orbital detail: 1s(2) 2s(2) 2px(2) 2py(2) 2pz(2).\\n(iii) A sublevel is a group of orbitals of the same energy within a main energy level (e.g. the 2p sublevel in neon contains three orbitals: 2px, 2py, 2pz). An atomic orbital is a single region of space within a sublevel that can hold a maximum of 2 electrons.\\n(iv) Neon has a full outer shell (2s2 2p6 = 8 electrons in the second energy level). It has a stable, complete electron configuration and no tendency to gain, lose, or share electrons.\\n(v) The first 8 ionisation energies of neon increase gradually (removing electrons from the same shell, n=2). There is then a very large jump to the 9th and 10th ionisation energies (removing electrons from the inner 1s shell, much closer to the nucleus). This pattern of gradual increase followed by a large jump is evidence for distinct energy levels.\\n(vi) A p orbital is dumbbell-shaped (two lobes on either side of the nucleus, with a node at the centre)."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Hydrocarbons, Isomerism & Thermochemistry (Octane)",
     q:"Hydrocarbon A (in naphtha fraction) can be converted to hydrocarbon B.\\n\\n(a)(i) Describe one property A has in common with other hydrocarbons in the naphtha fraction.\\n(ii) State the IUPAC names of compounds A and B.\\n(iii) Explain why A and B are referred to as saturated alkanes.\\n\\n(b)(i) Name the process that converts A to B.\\n(ii) Why is this conversion carried out?\\n\\n(c) Catalytic cracking of compound A produces methylpropane and compound C.\\n(i) Draw the molecular structure of methylpropane.\\n(ii) Give the IUPAC names and structures of three possible isomers of compound C.\\n\\n(d) CH3(CH2)6CH3(l) + 12.5 O2(g) -> 8CO2(g) + 9H2O(l)\\n(i) Calculate the heat of combustion of compound A given: Hf(A) = -250.3, Hf(CO2) = -393.5, Hf(H2O) = -285.8 kJ/mol.\\n(ii) Hf(B) = -260.5 kJ/mol. Calculate the heat of reaction for converting A to B.",
     a:"(a)(i) Similar boiling point range / similar number of carbon atoms / liquid at room temperature / similar molecular mass.\\n(ii) A = octane. B = 2,2,4-trimethylpentane (isooctane).\\n(iii) They are saturated because all carbon-carbon bonds are single bonds (no double or triple bonds). They are alkanes because they are hydrocarbons with the general formula CnH(2n+2).\\n\\n(b)(i) Isomerisation (or reforming).\\n(ii) To increase the octane number of the fuel. 2,2,4-trimethylpentane has octane number 100, much higher than straight-chain octane.\\n\\n(c)(i) Methylpropane (2-methylpropane): CH3-CH(CH3)-CH3. Draw showing all C-H and C-C bonds.\\n(ii) Compound A (C8H18) -> methylpropane (C4H10) + compound C.\\nC = C8H18 - C4H10 = C4H8. Three isomers of C4H8:\\n- But-1-ene: CH2=CH-CH2-CH3\\n- But-2-ene: CH3-CH=CH-CH3\\n- 2-methylpropene: CH2=C(CH3)-CH3\\n(Also cyclobutane is a valid structural isomer of C4H8.)\\n\\n(d)(i) delta H(combustion) = [8(-393.5) + 9(-285.8)] - [(-250.3)]\\n= [-3148.0 + (-2572.2)] - [-250.3]\\n= -5720.2 + 250.3\\n= -5469.9 kJ/mol.\\n\\n(ii) delta H(reaction) = Hf(B) - Hf(A) = (-260.5) - (-250.3) = -10.2 kJ/mol.\\n\\nKey point: The heat of reaction for an isomerisation depends only on the difference in their heats of formation (Hess's Law). The negative value shows the branched isomer is slightly more stable."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Chemical Equilibrium – Water-Gas Shift Reaction",
     q:"4.0 moles of steam and 1.0 mole of CO mixed at temperature T in a container of fixed volume V.\\nH2O(g) + CO(g) <=> H2(g) + CO2(g), delta H = -38.8 kJ/mol\\nAt equilibrium, 0.8 moles of H2 are present.\\n\\n(a)(i) Explain what is meant by chemical equilibrium.\\n(ii) Write an expression for Kc.\\n(iii) Calculate Kc.\\n\\n(b)(i) If 2.5 moles of steam and 2.5 moles of CO were used at the same temperature T, explain why Kc would not change.\\n(ii) Calculate the mass of CO2 at equilibrium in this case.\\n\\n(c)(i) State the effect of decreasing pressure on the % conversion of steam to hydrogen. Justify.\\n(ii) How could the value of Kc be increased? Justify.\\n(iii) State the effect of using a catalyst on Kc at temperature T. Justify.",
     a:"(a)(i) Chemical equilibrium is a dynamic state where the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction, and the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant (though not necessarily equal).\\n(ii) Kc = [H2][CO2] / ([H2O][CO])\\n(iii) At equilibrium: H2 = 0.8 mol, CO2 = 0.8 mol (formed in 1:1 ratio from equation).\\nH2O = 4.0 - 0.8 = 3.2 mol. CO = 1.0 - 0.8 = 0.2 mol.\\nAll in volume V, so concentrations are proportional to moles (V cancels):\\nKc = (0.8)(0.8) / (3.2)(0.2) = 0.64 / 0.64 = 1.0.\\n\\n(b)(i) Kc depends only on temperature. Since the temperature is unchanged (still T), Kc remains the same regardless of the initial amounts of reactants.\\n(ii) Let x moles of CO2 form at equilibrium.\\nKc = 1.0 = x^2 / (2.5-x)^2.\\nTaking square root: 1.0 = x / (2.5-x), so x = 2.5-x, giving 2x = 2.5, x = 1.25 mol CO2.\\nMass of CO2 = 1.25 x 44 = 55 g.\\n\\n(c)(i) No effect. There are equal numbers of moles of gas on both sides of the equation (2 moles each side), so changing pressure does not shift the equilibrium position.\\n(ii) Decrease the temperature. The forward reaction is exothermic (delta H = -38.8 kJ/mol). Decreasing temperature favours the exothermic (forward) direction, increasing Kc.\\n(iii) A catalyst has no effect on Kc. A catalyst speeds up both forward and reverse reactions equally. Equilibrium is reached faster but the position (and Kc) is unchanged."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Reaction Scheme – Free Radical Substitution & PVC Synthesis",
     q:"Reaction scheme: ethene -> ethane (reaction A), ethane + Cl2/UV -> chloroethane (reaction B), ethene -> poly(ethene) (reaction C), ethene -> compound X -> compound Y -> PVC.\\n\\n(a) How does the geometry around carbon atoms change during reaction A (ethene to ethane)?\\n\\n(b) Reaction B: ethane + Cl2 (UV light).\\n(i) Is reaction B addition, substitution, or elimination?\\n(ii) Describe the mechanism for reaction B.\\n(iii) Explain how trace C4H10 is evidence for this mechanism.\\n(iv) State one other piece of evidence for this mechanism.\\n\\n(c) PVC synthesis from ethene in three steps: addition -> compound X, elimination -> compound Y, polymerisation -> PVC.\\n(i) State the IUPAC name for compound X.\\n(ii) Name the inorganic compound eliminated to form compound Y.\\n(iii) Draw two repeating units of PVC.\\n(iv) Name the type of polymerisation.",
     a:"(a) The geometry changes from trigonal planar (120 degree bond angles around C=C in ethene) to tetrahedral (109.5 degree bond angles around C-C in ethane). This is because the pi bond breaks and a new sigma bond forms, changing from 3 bonding regions to 4 around each carbon.\\n\\n(b)(i) Substitution reaction (free radical substitution). A hydrogen atom is replaced by a chlorine atom.\\n(ii) Mechanism (three stages):\\nInitiation: Cl2 -> 2Cl* (UV light breaks the Cl-Cl bond homolytically to form two chlorine free radicals).\\nPropagation: Cl* + CH3CH3 -> CH3CH2* + HCl (chlorine radical abstracts a hydrogen atom).\\nThen: CH3CH2* + Cl2 -> CH3CH2Cl + Cl* (ethyl radical reacts with Cl2, regenerating a Cl radical).\\nTermination: Two radicals combine, e.g. Cl* + Cl* -> Cl2, or CH3CH2* + Cl* -> CH3CH2Cl, or CH3CH2* + CH3CH2* -> C4H10.\\n(iii) C4H10 (butane) forms when two ethyl radicals combine: CH3CH2* + CH3CH2* -> C4H10. This can only happen if free radicals are present as intermediates, providing direct evidence for the free radical mechanism.\\n(iv) The reaction requires UV light to initiate it (does not proceed in the dark). This is evidence that homolytic bond breaking (requiring energy from UV photons) is the first step.\\n\\n(c)(i) Compound X: 1,2-dichloroethane (ClCH2CH2Cl) -- formed by addition of Cl2 across the C=C of ethene.\\n(ii) HCl (hydrogen chloride) is eliminated to form compound Y (chloroethene / vinyl chloride, CH2=CHCl).\\n(iii) PVC repeating unit: -[-CH2-CHCl-]-[-CH2-CHCl-]- (show the Cl on alternating carbons).\\n(iv) Addition polymerisation."}
  ],

  "2023": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Volumetric Analysis — Standardising KMnO4 with Fe2+",
     q:"(NH4)2SO4.FeSO4.6H2O (hydrated ammonium iron(II) sulfate) was used to prepare a standard solution.\n\n(a) State two properties of this substance that make it suitable as a primary standard.\n\n(b)(i) Describe how you would prepare exactly 250 cm3 of a standard solution containing 9.31 g of this substance, supplied on a clock glass.\n(ii) Why was dilute sulfuric acid added during the preparation?\n\nThe standard solution was titrated with KMnO4 from a burette.\nMnO4- + 5Fe2+ + 8H+ -> Mn2+ + 5Fe3+ + 4H2O\nOn average, 22.6 cm3 of KMnO4 required per 25.0 cm3 of Fe2+ solution.\n\n(c)(i) How was the end point detected?\n(ii) What would be observed if insufficient acid was present? Explain.\n\n(d) Calculate:\n(i) concentration of (NH4)2SO4.FeSO4.6H2O in mol/L\n(ii) moles of Fe2+ in 25.0 cm3\n(iii) moles of KMnO4 reduced\n(iv) concentration of KMnO4 in mol/L.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Any two of: It is a pure solid, soluble in water, stable on storage (not hygroscopic, not easily oxidised in air), has a high molecular mass (reducing percentage weighing error), readily available, not efflorescent.\n\n(b)(i) Dissolve the 9.31 g in a beaker containing some deionised water and dilute sulfuric acid. Add the rinsings of the clock glass to the beaker. Transfer the solution to a 250 cm3 volumetric flask (using a funnel). Add the rinsings of the beaker, funnel, and glass rod to the flask. Add deionised water slowly until the bottom of the meniscus is on the 250 cm3 mark. Stopper and invert the flask several times to mix thoroughly.\n(ii) To prevent air oxidation of Fe2+ to Fe3+. Without acid, Fe2+ would be oxidised by dissolved oxygen, giving an inaccurate result. It also prevents hydrolysis of Fe2+ ions.\n\n(c)(i) The end point is self-indicating. A faint permanent pink colour remains in the conical flask after the last addition of KMnO4 (the purple KMnO4 is normally decolourised by Fe2+, but when all Fe2+ is consumed, the next drop of KMnO4 gives a lasting pink).\n(ii) If insufficient acid was present, a brown precipitate (MnO2) would form instead of the colourless Mn2+ product. MnO4- is reduced to MnO2 (Mn4+) in neutral/alkaline conditions rather than to Mn2+.\n\n(d)(i) Mr of (NH4)2SO4.FeSO4.6H2O = 2(14+4) + 32 + 64 + 56 + 32 + 64 + 108 = 392.\nMass per litre = 9.31 x (1000/250) = 37.24 g/L.\nConcentration = 37.24 / 392 = 0.095 mol/L.\n(ii) Moles Fe2+ in 25.0 cm3 = 0.095 x 25.0/1000 = 0.002375 mol (since each formula unit contains one Fe2+).\n(iii) From the equation, Fe2+ : MnO4- = 5 : 1.\nMoles KMnO4 = 0.002375 / 5 = 0.000475 mol.\n(iv) Concentration of KMnO4 = 0.000475 / (22.6/1000) = 0.000475 / 0.0226 = 0.021 mol/L (accept 0.02-0.022 M).\n\nWhy: This is a redox titration. The 5:1 mole ratio of Fe2+ to MnO4- is essential. The self-indicating end point (permanent pink) avoids the need for a separate indicator."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Preparation of Ethyne, Unsaturation Tests & Combustion",
     q:"(a)(i) Draw a labelled diagram for the preparation of ethyne from calcium carbide and water, collecting over water in test tubes.\n(ii) Why would the first test tube of gas be less pure than the others?\n\n(b)(i) What was observed when dilute bromine solution was added to a test tube of ethyne?\n(ii) What was observed with acidified, dilute KMnO4?\n(iii) What information do these test results give about ethyne?\n\n(c)(i) Describe how to confirm ethyne is flammable.\n(ii) Describe the flame observed when ethyne is burned in air.\n(iii) Write a balanced equation for the combustion of ethyne in excess oxygen.\n(iv) How does the oxyacetylene welding flame differ from the flame in air?\n\n(d) CaC2 + 2H2O -> C2H2 + Ca(OH)2\nFind the maximum volume of ethyne gas at RTP from 2.0 g of calcium carbide that is 80% pure.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Diagram: A flask or test tube containing calcium carbide (CaC2). Water is added via a dropping funnel or thistle funnel. A delivery tube leads from the flask to a trough of water, where ethyne gas is collected in inverted test tubes over water.\n(ii) The first test tube contains air that was originally in the apparatus and the delivery tube. It must be displaced before pure ethyne can be collected.\n\n(b)(i) Bromine solution: The brown/orange colour decolourises (becomes colourless) as bromine reacts with ethyne.\n(ii) Acidified KMnO4: The purple/pink colour decolourises (becomes colourless) as KMnO4 oxidises ethyne.\n(iii) These results show that ethyne is unsaturated -- it contains a multiple bond (triple bond) and undergoes addition reactions (and is easily oxidised).\n\n(c)(i) Hold a burning splint/taper at the mouth of (or insert it into) an inverted test tube of ethyne. The gas ignites.\n(ii) The flame is bright (luminous), yellow, and sooty/smoky (due to incomplete combustion and the high carbon-to-hydrogen ratio in ethyne).\n(iii) 2C2H2 + 5O2 -> 4CO2 + 2H2O (or C2H2 + 2.5O2 -> 2CO2 + H2O).\n(iv) The oxyacetylene flame (burning in pure oxygen) is hotter, cleaner (less sooty, no smoke), less bright, and blue compared to the flame in air.\n\n(d) Mr of CaC2 = 40 + 2(12) = 64.\n80% pure: usable mass = 0.80 x 2.0 = 1.6 g of pure CaC2.\nMoles CaC2 = 1.6 / 64 = 0.025 mol.\nFrom the equation: 1 mol CaC2 produces 1 mol C2H2.\nMoles C2H2 = 0.025 mol.\nVolume at RTP = 0.025 x 24.0 = 0.6 litres (600 cm3).\n\nWhy: The 80% purity must be applied before calculating moles. At RTP, 1 mole of gas = 24.0 L (given in the exam)."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Heat of Reaction — Neutralisation (HCl + NaOH)",
     q:"50 cm3 of 1.0 M HCl and 50 cm3 of 1.0 M NaOH mixed in an expanded polystyrene cup. Temperature rose by 6.8 K.\nHCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) -> NaCl(aq) + H2O(l)\n\n(a) Define heat of reaction.\n\n(b) State (i) one advantage, (ii) one disadvantage of using a 50 cm3 burette to measure the base and add it directly.\n\n(c)(i) Why did the temperature rise when the solutions were mixed?\n(ii) Suggest why the temperature then began to fall gradually.\n(iii) Explain the advantage of polystyrene over a glass beaker.\n\n(d) Calculate:\n(i) moles of HCl neutralised\n(ii) heat produced (kJ), assuming density = 1.0 g/cm3, specific heat capacity = 4.2 kJ/kg/K\n(iii) delta H in kJ/mol for the neutralisation reaction.\n\n(e)(i) Explain why 1.0 M solutions give a more accurate result than 0.1 M.\n(ii) Why would the same temperature rise of 6.8 K be expected if 150 cm3 of each 1.0 M solution were used?",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Heat of reaction is the heat change (energy released or absorbed) when a reaction takes place according to a balanced equation, with the number of moles of reactants shown in the equation reacting completely.\n\n(b)(i) Advantage: Accurate measurement of volume.\n(ii) Disadvantage: Slow -- allows heat loss to the surroundings before mixing is complete.\n\n(c)(i) The neutralisation reaction is exothermic (delta H is negative). Heat is released when HCl reacts with NaOH, causing the temperature to rise.\n(ii) The polystyrene cup is not a perfect insulator. The reaction mixture is hotter than the surroundings, so heat gradually escapes to the environment.\n(iii) Polystyrene is a better insulator than glass -- it has a much lower thermal conductivity and does not absorb as much heat. A glass beaker would conduct heat away from the solution, and its own heat capacity would need to be accounted for in calculations.\n\n(d)(i) Moles HCl = (50 x 1.0) / 1000 = 0.05 mol.\n(ii) Total volume = 50 + 50 = 100 cm3. Mass = 100 x 1.0 = 100 g = 0.1 kg.\nHeat = mc(delta T) = 0.1 x 4.2 x 6.8 = 2.856 kJ (accept 2.856 to 2.9 kJ).\n(iii) delta H = -2.856 / 0.05 = -57.12 kJ/mol (accept -57.1 to -58 kJ/mol).\nThe negative sign indicates the reaction is exothermic.\n\n(e)(i) With 1.0 M solutions, a larger temperature rise is obtained (6.8 K vs about 0.68 K for 0.1 M). The larger rise is easier to measure accurately and gives a smaller percentage error.\n(ii) With 150 cm3 of each 1.0 M solution: three times more moles react (0.15 mol), producing three times more heat. But there is also three times more solution (300 cm3 = 0.3 kg) absorbing that heat. Since heat = mc(delta T), the three times more heat and three times more mass cancel out, giving the same temperature rise of 6.8 K.\n\nWhy: This is a classic calorimetry experiment. The key formula is H = mc(delta T). Using concentrated solutions gives a bigger temperature change and therefore more accurate results."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions (Atomic Number, Shapes, Mass Spec, Oxidation Numbers)",
     q:"Answer eight of the following:\n(a) Define (i) atomic number, (ii) mass number.\n(b) Taking the valency of gallium (Ga) as 3, write the formula for (i) gallium nitride, (ii) gallium oxide.\n(c) Predict the two possible shapes of a molecule ABn where n = 2.\n(d) Identify the energy change: X+(g) -> X2+(g) + e-\n(e) What is diffusion?\n(f) Mass spectrometer diagram -- identify the processes at stages 2 and 4.\n(g) A 0.12 M solution (25.0 cm3) was diluted to 30.0 cm3 with deionised water. Find the new concentration.\n(h) What is an enzyme?\n(i) Draw the structure of propane-1,2,3-triol (glycerol) showing all atoms and bonds.\n(j) Give the oxidation number of sulfur in (i) SO4(2-), (ii) Na2S4O6.\n(k) Calculate the minimum number of molecules of 2-methoxybenzoic acid (Mr = 152) in a 250 g jar of manuka honey certified to contain at least 1 x 10^-3 g per kg.\n(l) A: Write balanced equation(s) showing how limestone removes SO2 from waste gases. OR B: Explain why metallic crystals conduct electricity.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Atomic number: the number of protons in the nucleus of an atom.\n(ii) Mass number: the total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.\n\n(b)(i) GaN (gallium nitride).\n(ii) Ga2O3 (gallium oxide).\n\n(c) Two possible shapes: V-shaped (bent/angular) if there are lone pairs on the central atom, or linear if there are no lone pairs.\n\n(d) This is the second ionisation energy of element X (energy required to remove a second electron from the gaseous 1+ ion).\n\n(e) Diffusion is the spreading or movement of a substance (particles, molecules) from a region of high concentration to a region of low concentration.\n\n(f) Stage 2: Ionisation (atoms/molecules are bombarded with electrons to form positive ions).\nStage 4: Separation (ions are separated by the magnetic field according to their mass-to-charge ratio).\n\n(g) Using C1V1 = C2V2: 0.12 x 25.0 = M x 30.0.\nM = (0.12 x 25.0) / 30.0 = 3.0 / 30.0 = 0.10 M.\n\n(h) An enzyme is a biological catalyst -- a protein produced by living organisms that speeds up the rate of a specific biochemical reaction without being consumed.\n\n(i) Glycerol structure:\nHO-CH2-CH(OH)-CH2-OH\nDrawn showing all C-H, O-H, and C-O bonds explicitly, with three carbon atoms each bonded to an OH group.\n\n(j)(i) In SO4(2-): Let oxidation number of S = x. x + 4(-2) = -2, so x = +6.\n(ii) In Na2S4O6: 2(+1) + 4x + 6(-2) = 0, so 2 + 4x - 12 = 0, 4x = 10, x = +2.5 (average). In reality, two S atoms have oxidation number +5 and two have 0, but the average is +2.5.\n\n(k) Mr of 2-methoxybenzoic acid = 152.\nMass per kg honey = 1 x 10^-3 g. The jar is 250 g = 0.25 kg.\nMass in jar = 1 x 10^-3 x 0.25 = 2.5 x 10^-4 g.\nMoles = 2.5 x 10^-4 / 152 = 1.645 x 10^-6 mol.\nMolecules = 1.645 x 10^-6 x 6.0 x 10^23 = 9.87 x 10^17.\nTo 1 significant figure: 1 x 10^18 molecules.\n\n(l) A: CaCO3 + SO2 -> CaSO3 + CO2 (limestone directly reacts with sulfur dioxide).\nAlternatively: CaCO3 -> CaO + CO2 (limestone decomposes to quicklime), then CaO + SO2 -> CaSO3.\nB: Metallic crystals contain valence electrons that are delocalised (free to move) through the lattice of positive metal ions. When a potential difference is applied, these electrons can flow, carrying electric current."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Atomic Theory, Electron Configuration & Electronegativity",
     q:"(a)(i) How did Thomson account for atoms being electrically neutral?\n(ii) State one piece of evidence for the existence of energy levels in atoms.\n(iii) State two limitations of Bohr's atomic theory that led to its modification.\n(iv) Define an atomic orbital.\n(v) Write the s, p, d electron configuration for bromine in its ground state.\n\n(b) Graph shows electronegativity trends across period 2 and down Group 17.\n(i) Define electronegativity.\n(ii) Account for the increase in electronegativity across the second period.\n(iii) Explain why electronegativity values decrease down Group 17.\n(iv) Predict the type of bonding in bromine monochloride (BrCl).\n(v) Which atom in an O-F bond in OF2 has a partial negative charge (delta-)? Justify.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Thomson proposed the plum-pudding model: electrons (negative charges) are embedded in a sphere of uniformly distributed positive charge. The total positive charge equals the total negative charge, making the atom electrically neutral overall.\n(ii) Evidence: Line emission spectra (e.g. the Balmer series of hydrogen) show that only photons of specific energies/frequencies are emitted, corresponding to transitions between fixed energy levels. Also: ionisation energies of elements, flame tests.\n(iii) Two limitations:\n1. Bohr's theory only works for hydrogen (and He+ ion); it cannot accurately predict the spectra of multi-electron atoms.\n2. It does not account for the wave nature of the electron (wave-particle duality) or the Heisenberg uncertainty principle (we cannot simultaneously know the exact position and momentum of an electron).\nAlso: It did not explain the splitting of spectral lines (fine structure/Zeeman effect) or the existence of sublevels and orbitals.\n(iv) An atomic orbital is a region of space around the nucleus of an atom where there is a high probability of finding an electron.\n(v) Bromine (Z = 35): 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p5.\n\n(b)(i) Electronegativity is a measure of the relative attraction of an atom (nucleus) for a shared pair of electrons in a covalent bond.\n(ii) Across the second period (Li to F), the nuclear charge increases (more protons) while electrons are added to the same shell (same shielding). The increased effective nuclear charge means the nucleus attracts bonding electrons more strongly, so electronegativity increases.\n(iii) Down Group 17 (F to At), each successive element has electrons in higher energy levels (more shells), so the bonding electrons are further from the nucleus and are more shielded by inner electrons. The effective nuclear charge felt by bonding electrons decreases, so electronegativity decreases.\n(iv) Covalent bonding (polar covalent). Both Br and Cl are non-metals with relatively high electronegativity values. The difference in electronegativity is small, so the bond is covalent but polar (Cl is more electronegative, so it has a partial negative charge).\n(v) Fluorine has the partial negative charge (delta-). Fluorine is the most electronegative element (electronegativity approximately 4.0, compared to oxygen at approximately 3.5). In the O-F bond, fluorine attracts the shared electrons more strongly, giving it a partial negative charge.\n\nWhy: Electronegativity trends are explained by effective nuclear charge (across periods) and atomic radius/shielding (down groups). OF2 is unusual because fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Alkanes, Cracking & Heats of Combustion",
     q:"(a)(i) What is a hydrocarbon?\n(ii) Why are alkanes described as saturated hydrocarbons?\n\n(b) Pentane and 2-methylbutane are structural isomers of C5H12. Give the IUPAC name and draw the structure of the other structural isomer of C5H12.\n\n(c) The alkane C15H32 is catalytically cracked to produce 2-methylbutane, but-1-ene, and one other molecule. What is the molecular formula of the third molecule?\n\n(d) C15H32(l) + 23O2(g) -> 15CO2(g) + 16H2O(l)\nCalculate the heat of combustion of C15H32 using:\ndelta Hf: C15H32 = -428.8, CO2 = -393.5, H2O = -285.8 kJ/mol.\n\n(e) Graph shows variation in heat of combustion for first eight alkanes.\n(i) What difference in molecular formulae of successive alkanes accounts for the near-linear trend?\n(ii) Estimate from the graph the difference between delta H of combustion of two successive alkanes.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) A hydrocarbon is a compound that contains only carbon and hydrogen atoms.\n(ii) Alkanes are described as saturated because all the carbon-carbon bonds in their molecules are single bonds. There are no double or triple bonds, so no more atoms can be added -- they are 'saturated' with hydrogen.\n\n(b) The third isomer of C5H12 is 2,2-dimethylpropane (neopentane).\nStructure: A central carbon bonded to four CH3 groups:\nC(CH3)4, i.e. (CH3)3C-CH3, drawn showing all bonds.\n\n(c) C15H32 -> C5H12 (2-methylbutane) + C4H8 (but-1-ene) + ?\nCarbon: 15 = 5 + 4 + x, so x = 6 carbons.\nHydrogen: 32 = 12 + 8 + y, so y = 12 hydrogens.\nThe third molecule has formula C6H12.\n(Check: C5H12 + C4H8 + C6H12 = C15H32. Correct.)\n\n(d) delta H(combustion) = [sum delta Hf(products)] - [sum delta Hf(reactants)]\n= [15(-393.5) + 16(-285.8)] - [(-428.8) + 23(0)]\n= [-5902.5 + (-4572.8)] - [-428.8]\n= -10475.3 - (-428.8)\n= -10475.3 + 428.8\n= -10046.5 kJ/mol.\n\n(e)(i) Each successive alkane differs by CH2 (one extra -CH2- group). Since each additional CH2 contributes roughly the same amount of energy when burned, the heat of combustion increases approximately linearly.\n(ii) From the graph, the difference between successive alkanes is approximately 650-660 kJ/mol (the increase per CH2 unit).\n\nWhy: The near-linear relationship in heats of combustion is because each added CH2 group contributes approximately the same bond energies when burned. This is a defining characteristic of a homologous series."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Acids, Bases, pH & Water Treatment",
     q:"Sulfuric acid is a strong dibasic acid; chloric(I) acid (HOCl) is a weak monobasic acid.\n\n(a)(i) Define an acid according to Bronsted-Lowry theory.\n(ii) What is a strong acid?\n(iii) Identify the conjugate base of HOCl.\n(iv) Identify the conjugate acid of HSO4-.\n\n(b) Calculate the pH (correct to one decimal place) of:\n(i) a 0.04 M solution of sulfuric acid\n(ii) a 0.04 M solution of chloric(I) acid (Ka = 3.0 x 10^-8)\n\n(c)(i) Identify two processes that help remove suspended solids in water treatment.\n(ii) 1.24 g was collected on filter paper when 800 cm3 of water was filtered. Calculate the concentration of suspended solids in p.p.m.\n\n(d)(i) Write a balanced equation for the reaction of aluminium sulfate with sodium phosphate (Na3PO4) to form insoluble AlPO4 and soluble Na2SO4.\n(ii) Describe how to test for the nitrate ion in aqueous solution.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Bronsted-Lowry definition: An acid is a proton (H+) donor -- a substance that donates a proton to another substance.\n(ii) A strong acid is one that fully dissociates (ionises completely) in water, releasing all of its available H+ ions.\n(iii) Conjugate base of HOCl: OCl- (hypochlorite ion). When HOCl donates a proton, it becomes OCl-.\n(iv) Conjugate acid of HSO4-: H2SO4. When HSO4- accepts a proton, it becomes H2SO4.\n\n(b)(i) H2SO4 is dibasic and strong -- it fully dissociates:\nH2SO4 -> 2H+ + SO4(2-).\n[H+] = 2 x 0.04 = 0.08 M.\npH = -log10(0.08) = -log10(8 x 10^-2) = -(log 8 + log 10^-2) = -(0.903 - 2) = 1.1.\n\n(ii) HOCl is a weak acid. Ka = [H+][OCl-] / [HOCl] = 3.0 x 10^-8.\nLet [H+] = x. Then Ka = x^2 / 0.04 (approximation: 0.04 - x ≈ 0.04).\nx^2 = 3.0 x 10^-8 x 0.04 = 1.2 x 10^-9.\nx = sqrt(1.2 x 10^-9) = 3.46 x 10^-5 M.\npH = -log10(3.46 x 10^-5) = 4.5.\n\n(c)(i) Two processes: (1) Flocculation -- adding a flocculating agent (e.g. aluminium sulfate/alum) causes suspended particles to clump together. (2) Sedimentation/settling -- the flocs settle to the bottom of a settling tank. (Also accept filtration through sand beds.)\n(ii) Concentration = mass/volume = 1.24 g / 800 cm3.\n1 p.p.m. = 1 mg per litre = 1 g per 1,000,000 cm3.\n= (1.24 / 800) x 1,000,000 = 1550 p.p.m.\nOr: 1.24 g in 800 cm3 = (1.24/800) x 10^6 mg/L = 1550 p.p.m.\n\n(d)(i) Al2(SO4)3 + 2Na3PO4 -> 2AlPO4 + 3Na2SO4.\n(ii) Test for nitrate ion: Add sodium hydroxide solution to the test sample. Add a small piece of aluminium foil (or Devarda's alloy). Warm gently. Hold a piece of damp red litmus paper at the mouth of the test tube. If nitrate is present, the litmus turns blue due to ammonia gas (NH3) being released. The hydroxide and aluminium reduce the nitrate ion to ammonia.\n\nWhy: The pH calculation for the strong dibasic acid requires doubling the concentration (2 H+ per molecule). For the weak acid, the Ka expression is used with the standard approximation."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Reaction Scheme — Ethanol, Ethene, Polymerisation",
     q:"A reaction scheme with ethanol, ethene, ethane, and related products.\nConversions: A (ethanol -> ethene, elimination), B (ethene -> ethane, addition of H2), C (ethene -> poly(ethene), polymerisation), D (ethane -> chloroethane), E (ethene -> ethanol, addition of H2O).\n\n(a)(i) What is an elimination reaction?\n(ii) Identify the elimination reaction in the scheme.\n\n(b) The same reactant and metal catalyst can be used in conversions B and E.\nIdentify (i) the reactant, (ii) a suitable metal catalyst.\n\n(c)(i) Draw two repeating units of poly(ethene).\n(ii) How does the geometry around the carbon atoms change during conversion C?\n\n(d)(i) Identify the inorganic product of conversion A.\n(ii) Traces of butane are formed during conversion D (ethane + Cl2 in UV). What does this tell us about the mechanism?\n(iii) Describe the mechanism of conversion D.\n\n(e)(i) Name the homologous series to which ethanol belongs.\n(ii) Give the IUPAC name and structure of the next member of this series.\n(iii) Describe a chemical test to distinguish ethanol from ethanoic acid.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) An elimination reaction is a reaction in which a small molecule (e.g. H2O, HCl) is removed from a larger molecule, usually forming a double bond.\n(ii) Conversion A: ethanol -> ethene (dehydration). Water is eliminated and a C=C double bond forms.\n\n(b)(i) Hydrogen gas (H2) is the common reactant. In B, H2 adds across the double bond of ethene to form ethane. In E, steam (H2O, in the presence of H2 and catalyst) adds to ethene to form ethanol -- though note that for industrial hydration, H3PO4 catalyst is used. If B and E share the same catalyst, the reactant is H2 (for B: hydrogenation) and H2O (for E: hydration). The question states the same reactant -- this is hydrogen (H2), and the metal catalyst is nickel (Ni) or platinum (Pt).\n(ii) Nickel (Ni) or platinum (Pt) -- used as a metal catalyst for hydrogenation.\n\n(c)(i) Two repeating units of poly(ethene): -[-CH2-CH2-]-[-CH2-CH2-]- (showing the backbone of four CH2 groups with continuation bonds at each end).\n(ii) During conversion C (polymerisation of ethene to poly(ethene)), the geometry around each carbon atom changes from trigonal planar (120 degrees, sp2 hybridisation in ethene's C=C) to tetrahedral (109.5 degrees, sp3 hybridisation in the polymer's C-C single bonds).\n\n(d)(i) The inorganic product of conversion A (dehydration of ethanol) is water (H2O).\n(ii) The formation of trace butane (C4H10) during conversion D shows that free radicals are intermediates in the reaction. Two ethyl radicals (C2H5*) combine to form butane: C2H5* + C2H5* -> C4H10. This is strong evidence for a free radical mechanism.\n(iii) Mechanism of conversion D (free radical substitution of ethane with Cl2 in UV light):\nInitiation: Cl2 -> 2Cl* (UV light causes homolytic fission of the Cl-Cl bond).\nPropagation step 1: Cl* + C2H6 -> C2H5* + HCl.\nPropagation step 2: C2H5* + Cl2 -> C2H5Cl + Cl*.\nTermination: Two radicals combine, e.g. C2H5* + C2H5* -> C4H10, or Cl* + Cl* -> Cl2, or C2H5* + Cl* -> C2H5Cl.\n\n(e)(i) Ethanol belongs to the alcohol (alkanol) homologous series.\n(ii) The next member is propan-1-ol (C3H7OH). Structure: CH3-CH2-CH2-OH.\n(iii) Test: Add sodium carbonate (Na2CO3) or sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO3) to each substance. Ethanoic acid will produce effervescence (bubbles of CO2 gas), while ethanol will not react. Alternatively, test with litmus paper -- ethanoic acid turns blue litmus red; ethanol does not.\n\nWhy: This question covers the core organic chemistry transformations: elimination, addition, polymerisation, and free radical substitution. The free radical mechanism is a key exam topic, and the trace butane evidence is commonly tested."},
  ],

  "2022": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Volumetric Analysis — Water Hardness (EDTA Titration)",
     q:"A laboratory deioniser was tested by comparing total hardness of water before and after passing through it. 25.0 cm3 samples were titrated with 0.010 M EDTA solution.\nM2+ + H2Y2- -> MY2- + 2H+\n\n(a)(i) Identify the two ions (M2+) that most commonly cause hardness.\n(ii) Explain the term total hardness.\n\n(b)(i) Name a suitable indicator for this titration.\n(ii) What colour change was observed at the end point?\n\n(c)(i) What pH does the buffer solution maintain in the conical flask?\n(ii) Why is it important to maintain this pH during the titration?\n\n(d) Describe the correct procedure for preparing a burette for this titration.\n\nOn average, 9.2 cm3 less of the 0.010 M EDTA solution was required for the deionised water than for the original hard water.\n\n(e) Calculate:\n(i) moles of EDTA in 9.2 cm3 of 0.010 M solution\n(ii) moles of M2+ ion that reacted with this quantity of EDTA\n(iii) moles of M2+ ion removed by the deioniser from one litre of the original supply\n(iv) the total hardness removed by the deioniser in g/L of CaCO3 and in p.p.m. CaCO3.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Calcium ions (Ca2+) and magnesium ions (Mg2+).\n(ii) Total hardness is the sum of permanent and temporary hardness, i.e. the total concentration of dissolved Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions.\n\n(b)(i) Solochrome Black (Eriochrome Black T).\n(ii) Wine-red (pink) to blue.\n\n(c)(i) pH 10 (accept 9-11).\n(ii) To ensure a sharp end-point colour change. The EDTA-metal complex (MY2-) is only stable at high pH for calcium and magnesium. At lower pH, other metal ions could interfere or the indicator would not work properly.\n\n(d) Clamp the burette vertically. Fill above the zero mark (use a funnel, then remove it). Run solution through the tap to fill the part below the tap (nozzle) and remove any air bubbles. Adjust the liquid level so that the bottom of the meniscus is on the zero mark. Read at eye level to avoid parallax error.\n\n(e)(i) Moles EDTA = (9.2 x 0.010) / 1000 = 9.2 x 10^-5 mol.\n(ii) From the equation, M2+ : EDTA = 1 : 1, so moles M2+ = 9.2 x 10^-5 mol.\n(iii) 25.0 cm3 portions were used, so per litre: 9.2 x 10^-5 x (1000/25.0) = 9.2 x 10^-5 x 40 = 3.68 x 10^-3 mol M2+ removed per litre.\n(iv) Mr of CaCO3 = 100.\nHardness in g/L = 3.68 x 10^-3 x 100 = 0.368 g/L CaCO3.\nIn p.p.m. = 0.368 x 1000 = 368 p.p.m. CaCO3.\n\nWhy: The EDTA titration measures total hardness (Ca2+ + Mg2+). The difference between the original water and deionised water gives the hardness removed by the deioniser. Multiplying by 40 converts from 25 cm3 to 1 litre."},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Ethene Preparation, Unsaturation Test & Recrystallisation",
     q:"(a) Draw a labelled diagram of apparatus for the preparation and collection over water of pure ethene gas. State one way of avoiding (i) a suck-back of cold water, (ii) a fire.\n\n(b) Some ethene was bubbled through a reagent. What colour change is observed with:\n(i) dilute acidified KMnO4?\n(ii) bromine water?\n(iii) Name the organic reaction type with bromine.\n(iv) Identify an organic product of the reaction between ethene and bromine solution.\n\n(c) Recrystallisation of benzoic acid containing water-soluble KCl and insoluble charcoal, using water as solvent. Two filtration stages shown.\n(i) Identify the substance(s) collected at locations X, Y and Z.\nWhat steps maximise the yield of pure benzoic acid:\n(ii) between the first and second filtrations?\n(iii) after the second filtration?\n(iv) How would you expect the melting point of the recrystallised sample to differ from that of an impure sample?",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Diagram: horizontal test tube with glass wool soaked in ethanol, alumina (Al2O3) catalyst near the centre, heated by a Bunsen burner. A delivery tube leads to collection of ethene over water in a trough.\n(i) Avoiding suck-back: Remove the delivery tube from the water before cooling, or loosen the stopper. Do not remove/reduce the heat while the delivery tube is still in water.\n(ii) Avoiding fire: Keep ethene gas and the Bunsen flame well separated. Ensure stoppers and tubing are tightly fitted to avoid leaks of flammable vapours. Remove the flame at the end before ethene escapes.\n\n(b)(i) Purple/pink decolourises to colourless.\n(ii) Brown/orange decolourises to colourless.\n(iii) Addition reaction.\n(iv) 1,2-dibromoethane (BrCH2CH2Br). Also acceptable: 2-bromoethanol.\n\n(c)(i) X: charcoal (carbon) -- removed in first (hot) filtration as it is insoluble.\nY: benzoic acid crystals -- collected on filter paper in second (cold) filtration.\nZ: potassium chloride (KCl) dissolved in the filtrate (and water) -- passes through in second filtration.\n(ii) Between filtrations: Allow the hot filtrate to cool slowly (or place in ice water). Evaporate some water by heating if needed to concentrate the solution, then allow crystals to form.\n(iii) After second filtration: Wash the crystals with ice-cold water and dry them. Rinse the flask to collect any remaining crystals.\n(iv) The recrystallised sample will have a higher and sharper (narrower range) melting point, closer to the literature value of 122 degrees C. Impure samples have a lower and broader melting point range."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Rates of Reaction — Decomposition of H2O2",
     q:"25.0 cm3 of a hydrogen peroxide solution decomposed using 0.5 g MnO2 catalyst. Volume of O2 monitored over time.\n\n(a) Write a balanced equation for the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide.\n\n(b) Give one risk associated with hydrogen peroxide. State how you could manage this risk.\n\n(c) Data: Time (min): 0, 1.0, 3.0, 5.0, 8.0, 10.0, 12.0, 14.0, 16.0\nVolume O2 (cm3): 0, 30, 66, 88, 104, 109, 111, 112, 112\nPlot a graph of volume vs. time. Use your graph to find:\n(i) the time taken for the reaction to be complete\n(ii) the average rate of reaction over the first 4.0 minutes (cm3 O2 per minute)\n(iii) the instantaneous rate at 4.0 minutes (cm3 O2 per minute)\n\n(d) Sketch and label curves for:\n(i) half the concentration, same catalyst mass\n(ii) same concentration, 0.6 g catalyst instead of 0.5 g\nJustify each curve.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a) 2H2O2 -> 2H2O + O2 (or H2O2 -> H2O + 1/2 O2).\n\n(b) Risk: Corrosive -- can burn/blister skin. Also an oxidiser (can cause fire).\nManagement: Wear PPE (gloves, eye protection), avoid contact with skin. Store in a cool, ventilated place away from incompatible substances. Use appropriate labels.\n\n(c) Plot volume (y-axis, cm3) vs time (x-axis, minutes). The curve rises steeply initially, then levels off.\n(i) Reaction complete at approximately 13-14 minutes (when volume stops increasing at 112 cm3).\n(ii) At t = 4.0 min, read from the graph: approximately 78 cm3.\nAverage rate = 78 / 4.0 = 19.5 cm3 O2 per minute (accept 19-20).\n(iii) Draw a tangent to the curve at t = 4.0 minutes. Read two points on the tangent, e.g. (0, 40) and (8.2, 120).\nSlope = (120 - 40) / (8.2 - 0) = 80 / 8.2 = 9.8 cm3 O2 per minute (accept 8.8-12.2).\n\n(d)(i) Half concentration: The curve starts at the origin, rises less steeply than the original. It levels off at approximately 56 cm3 (half the final volume). Justification: Rate is proportional to concentration, so the rate is slower. Half the moles of H2O2 gives half the total volume of O2.\n(ii) 0.6 g catalyst: The curve starts at the origin, rises initially as steeply or steeper than the original curve. It still levels off at 112 cm3 (same final volume). Justification: More catalyst provides greater surface area (more active sites), increasing the rate. But the total yield is the same because the same amount of H2O2 is present -- catalyst does not change the yield, only the rate."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions (Triads, Spectra, Ionisation, Avogadro)",
     q:"Answer eight of the following:\n(a) Li, Na, K is a Dobereiner triad. Explain Dobereiner's contribution to the periodic table.\n(b) Why is a photon emitted as an electron moves from one energy level to another in the Balmer series?\n(c) Why is the second ionisation energy of lithium significantly larger than its first?\n(d) What colour is observed in a flame test on a salt of (i) lithium, (ii) copper?\n(e) State Avogadro's law.\n(f) A diamond of 1098 carats contained exactly 18.3 moles of carbon. Calculate the mass of one carat in grams.\n(g) What reagents are used to confirm the presence of nitrate ions in aqueous solution?\n(h) Identify a substance commonly added during drinking water treatment (i) to cause flocculation, (ii) to fluoridate the water.\n(i) What elements are products of the electrolysis of aqueous KI using inert electrodes?\n(j) Which has the strongest C-C bonding: ethane, ethene, ethyne, benzene?\n(k) Calculate the average daily dose (in mg) when 0.146 moles of low-dose aspirin were taken over 365 days. Structure of aspirin shown.\n(l) A: Write balanced equations for the dissociation of carbonic acid in water. OR B: Distinguish molecular crystals from other crystal types.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Dobereiner observed that certain elements could be grouped in threes (triads) with similar chemical properties. The atomic mass of the middle element (Na, 23) is approximately the average of the other two: (7 + 39) / 2 = 23.\n\n(b) The electron in the higher energy level has more energy. When it drops to a lower energy level (n=2 for Balmer series), it loses energy. This energy is emitted as a photon of light. The energy of the photon equals the difference between the two energy levels: E = hf.\n\n(c) The first electron is removed from the outer shell (2s). The second electron must be removed from the inner, full 1s shell, which is much closer to the nucleus and not shielded. Removing an electron from a noble gas configuration (He-like) requires much more energy. Also, the second electron is being removed from a positive ion (Li+), which holds its electrons more tightly.\n\n(d)(i) Lithium: red (crimson). (ii) Copper: blue-green.\n\n(e) Avogadro's law: Equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules (moles).\n\n(f) Mass of carbon = 18.3 x 12 = 219.6 g.\nMass of one carat = 219.6 / 1098 = 0.2 g.\n\n(g) Add sodium hydroxide solution and aluminium foil (or Devarda's alloy). Warm gently. Test the gas produced with damp red litmus paper -- it turns blue (ammonia gas released if nitrate is present).\n\n(h)(i) Aluminium sulfate (alum) -- causes flocculation.\n(ii) Sodium fluoride (NaF) or hexafluorosilicic acid (H2SiF6).\n\n(i) Hydrogen gas (H2) at the cathode and iodine (I2) at the anode.\n\n(j) Ethyne (C2H2) has the strongest C-C bonding because it has a triple bond (bond energy approximately 839 kJ/mol), compared to a double bond in ethene or a single bond in ethane. Benzene has delocalised bonding intermediate between single and double.\n\n(k) Mr of aspirin (C9H8O4) = 180.\nTotal mass = 0.146 x 180 = 26.28 g = 26280 mg.\nAverage daily dose = 26280 / 365 = 72 mg per day.\n\n(l) A: H2CO3 <=> H+ + HCO3- (first dissociation)\nHCO3- <=> H+ + CO3(2-) (second dissociation)\nB: In molecular crystals, the particles at lattice points are discrete molecules. The intermolecular forces (van der Waals, dipole-dipole) holding them together are weak, giving low melting/boiling points. This distinguishes them from ionic, covalent network, and metallic crystals which have stronger bonding."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Atomic Structure — Rutherford, Isotopes, Radioactivity",
     q:"(a) Rutherford's gold foil experiment with alpha particles from radon-222. Most passed through undeflected, a small number deflected at large angles, very tiny number rebounded.\n(i) Based on Thomson's model of the atom, which observation was most unexpected?\n(ii) What conclusion did Rutherford reach about the structure of the atom?\n\n(b) Define (i) isotopes, (ii) relative atomic mass.\n\n(c) Write the s, p electron configuration for calcium in its ground state.\nIn a calcium atom in its ground state, how many:\n(i) main energy levels (shells) are occupied?\n(ii) orbitals are occupied?\nUse the electron configuration to predict how calcium behaves in a chemical reaction. Explain.\n\n(d)(i) Define radioactivity.\n(ii) Complete the nuclear equation for alpha decay of radon-222:\n222/86 Rn -> ___ + 4/2 He\n(iii) Suggest a reason why it is not possible to change lead into gold by chemical reactions.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) The most unexpected observation was that a very tiny number of alpha particles were reflected (rebounded) back along their original paths. Thomson's plum-pudding model had the positive charge spread out thinly throughout the atom, so nothing could repel alpha particles back so strongly.\n(ii) Rutherford concluded that the atom is mostly empty space (explaining why most alpha particles passed through). The positive charge and nearly all the mass are concentrated in a tiny, dense nucleus. Electrons orbit in the large space surrounding this nucleus.\n\n(b)(i) Isotopes are atoms of the same element (same atomic number / same number of protons) that have different mass numbers (different numbers of neutrons).\n(ii) Relative atomic mass is the average mass of the atoms of an element, taking into account the relative abundances of its naturally occurring isotopes, on a scale where carbon-12 is defined as exactly 12.\n\n(c) Electron configuration of Ca: 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2.\n(i) 4 main energy levels are occupied (shells 1, 2, 3, 4).\n(ii) 10 orbitals are occupied (1s, 2s, 2px, 2py, 2pz, 3s, 3px, 3py, 3pz, 4s).\nPrediction: Calcium will lose 2 electrons from the 4s subshell to form a Ca2+ ion, achieving a stable noble gas configuration (same as argon: 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6). It behaves as a metal and is a reducing agent in reactions.\n\n(d)(i) Radioactivity is the spontaneous breaking up (disintegration/decay) of unstable nuclei with the emission of radiation (alpha particles, beta particles, or gamma rays).\n(ii) 222/86 Rn -> 218/84 Po + 4/2 He (polonium-218 is the daughter nuclide).\n(iii) Chemical reactions involve only electrons (rearrangement of outer electron shells). They do not change the number of protons or neutrons in the nucleus. Converting lead (82 protons) to gold (79 protons) requires a change in atomic number, which is a nuclear process (transmutation), not achievable by chemical means."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Fuels, Octane Number & Thermochemistry (Hydrogen Fuel Cells)",
     q:"(a) Compare petrol and diesel in terms of (i) average molecular mass, (ii) boiling point.\n\n(b)(i) Explain the term octane number of a fuel.\nGive the IUPAC name and structure of the alkane fuel with:\n(ii) octane number of zero\n(iii) octane number of 100\n(iv) What structural feature accounts for the high octane number of 100?\n\nHydrogen fuel-cell buses in Dublin. Overall fuel-cell reaction: H2(g) + 1/2 O2(g) -> H2O(l).\nSteam reforming of methane: CH4(g) + H2O(g) -> CO(g) + 3H2(g), delta H = +206.2 kJ\n\n(c)(i) Why does elemental H2 not occur in nature on Earth as a fuel source?\n(ii) Give a major source of hydrogen gas other than steam reforming.\n(iii) Define heat of reaction.\n(iv) Explain why the same energy is released whether H2 is used in a fuel cell or burned.\n(v) Calculate the heat of formation of methane using the data: delta H (steam reforming) = +206.2 kJ, delta Hf (steam) = -241.8 kJ/mol, delta Hf (CO) = -110.5 kJ/mol.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Petrol has a lower average molecular mass than diesel (petrol: roughly C5-C10; diesel: roughly C12-C20).\n(ii) Petrol has a lower boiling point range than diesel.\n\n(b)(i) The octane number is a measure of a fuel's resistance to knocking (pre-ignition/auto-ignition). It is the percentage by volume of 2,2,4-trimethylpentane (isooctane) in a mixture of isooctane and heptane that has the same knocking characteristics as the fuel being tested.\n(ii) Octane number 0: heptane. IUPAC name: heptane. Structure: CH3CH2CH2CH2CH2CH2CH3 (straight-chain, 7 carbons).\n(iii) Octane number 100: 2,2,4-trimethylpentane (isooctane). Structure: (CH3)3C-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH3.\n(iv) Branching. Highly branched alkanes have higher octane numbers because they burn more smoothly and resist knocking.\n\n(c)(i) Hydrogen is highly reactive and combines readily with oxygen and other elements, so it does not accumulate as a free element on Earth. Any free H2 would escape the atmosphere due to its low molecular mass.\n(ii) Electrolysis of water.\n(iii) Heat of reaction is the heat change (energy released or absorbed) when a reaction takes place according to a balanced equation, with the number of moles shown in the equation reacting completely.\n(iv) The same amount of energy is released because the overall chemical change is the same in both cases (H2 + 1/2 O2 -> H2O). By Hess's Law, the enthalpy change depends only on the initial and final states, not on the pathway. In a fuel cell the energy appears as electrical energy; in combustion it appears as heat.\n(v) Steam reforming: CH4(g) + H2O(g) -> CO(g) + 3H2(g), delta H = +206.2 kJ.\nUsing Hess's Law: delta H = [delta Hf(CO) + 3 x delta Hf(H2)] - [delta Hf(CH4) + delta Hf(H2O(g))]\n206.2 = [(-110.5) + 3(0)] - [delta Hf(CH4) + (-241.8)]\n206.2 = -110.5 - delta Hf(CH4) + 241.8\n206.2 = 131.3 - delta Hf(CH4)\ndelta Hf(CH4) = 131.3 - 206.2 = -74.9 kJ/mol.\n\nWhy: The heat of formation of methane is -74.9 kJ/mol. This is a standard thermochemistry calculation using Hess's Law, rearranging the steam reforming equation."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Chemical Equilibrium — Methanol Synthesis",
     q:"CO(g) + 2H2(g) <=> CH3OH(g), delta H = -94.4 kJ/mol\n5.0 moles CO and 10.0 moles H2 in a 5.0 litre container, equilibrium at 250 degrees C with a catalyst.\n\n(a) Explain chemical equilibrium in terms of:\n(i) rates of reaction\n(ii) concentrations of substances involved.\n\n(b)(i) Write an expression for Kc.\n(ii) Calculate Kc given 25% conversion of CO to methanol at equilibrium.\n\n(c) Two different catalysts both reach the same equilibrium. What is an advantage of using a catalyst?\n\n(d)(i) State Le Chatelier's principle.\n(ii) How was the yield of methanol affected when more CO was added and equilibrium re-established?\n(iii) Explain the effect of increasing temperature on the yield of methanol.\n(iv) Explain the effect of decreasing the volume of the container on the yield of methanol.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) At chemical equilibrium, the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction. Both reactions continue to occur (dynamic equilibrium), but there is no net change.\n(ii) The concentrations of reactants and products remain constant (though not necessarily equal) at equilibrium.\n\n(b)(i) Kc = [CH3OH] / ([CO][H2]^2)\n(ii) 25% of 5.0 moles CO converted = 1.25 moles CH3OH formed.\nAt equilibrium:\nCO = 5.0 - 1.25 = 3.75 mol -> [CO] = 3.75/5.0 = 0.75 M\nH2 = 10.0 - 2(1.25) = 10.0 - 2.5 = 7.5 mol -> [H2] = 7.5/5.0 = 1.5 M\nCH3OH = 1.25 mol -> [CH3OH] = 1.25/5.0 = 0.25 M\nKc = 0.25 / (0.75 x 1.5^2) = 0.25 / (0.75 x 2.25) = 0.25 / 1.6875 = 0.148 (accept 0.15).\n\n(c) A catalyst speeds up both the forward and reverse reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached faster. This is the advantage: it reduces the time needed to reach equilibrium. The catalyst does not change the position of equilibrium or the value of Kc.\n\n(d)(i) Le Chatelier's principle: If a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change in concentration, pressure, or temperature, the equilibrium will shift in the direction that tends to counteract the change.\n(ii) Adding more CO increases the concentration of a reactant. By Le Chatelier's principle, the equilibrium shifts to the right (forward direction), producing more methanol. The yield of methanol increases.\n(iii) The forward reaction is exothermic (delta H = -94.4 kJ/mol). Increasing temperature favours the endothermic (reverse) reaction, shifting equilibrium to the left. The yield of methanol decreases.\n(iv) Decreasing the volume increases the pressure. There are 3 moles of gas on the left (1 CO + 2 H2) and 1 mole of gas on the right (CH3OH). The system shifts toward fewer moles of gas (right/forward direction). The yield of methanol increases."},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Chemistry — Alcohols, Acids, Esters & Mechanisms",
     q:"A reaction scheme involving ethanol, ethanoic acid, and related compounds.\n\n(a) Give the systematic (IUPAC) name and structural formula of ethanol.\n\n(b)(i) Give the systematic IUPAC name and molecular formula of an ester formed from ethanol and ethanoic acid.\n(ii) Draw the structural formula of this ester.\n(iii) What type of reaction forms an ester from an alcohol and a carboxylic acid?\n(iv) Name the catalyst used.\n\n(c)(i) What is the test for distinguishing between an aldehyde and a ketone?\n(ii) Describe the test using Fehling's solution.\n(iii) State the colour change.\n\n(d)(i) What type of reaction converts ethanol to ethene?\n(ii) Name the catalyst.\n(iii) Describe how the geometry around carbon changes.\n\n(e) Describe the mechanism for the reaction of ethane with chlorine in UV light (free radical substitution).\nExplain how the presence of trace amounts of butane in the products provides evidence for this mechanism.",
     a:"Answer:\n\n(a) IUPAC name: ethanol. Molecular formula: C2H5OH (CH3CH2OH). Structure: H-C-C-O-H with all hydrogens shown.\n\n(b)(i) Ethyl ethanoate (ethyl acetate). Molecular formula: C4H8O2 (CH3COOC2H5).\n(ii) Structure: CH3-C(=O)-O-CH2-CH3, showing the ester linkage (-COO-).\n(iii) Esterification (condensation reaction).\n(iv) Concentrated sulfuric acid (H2SO4) acts as a catalyst.\n\n(c)(i) Fehling's test (or Tollens' test or acidified K2Cr2O7). Aldehydes are easily oxidised; ketones are not.\n(ii) Fehling's test: Add Fehling's solution (mixture of Fehling's A and B) to the substance in a test tube. Heat gently in a water bath.\n(iii) Blue solution changes to a brick-red precipitate (Cu2O) if an aldehyde is present. No change with a ketone.\n\n(d)(i) Elimination reaction (dehydration).\n(ii) Aluminium oxide (Al2O3) or concentrated sulfuric acid (H2SO4) or concentrated phosphoric acid.\n(iii) Geometry changes from tetrahedral (109.5 degree bond angles, sp3) around each carbon in ethanol to trigonal planar (120 degree bond angles, sp2) around each carbon in ethene.\n\n(e) Mechanism of free radical substitution (ethane + Cl2, UV light):\nInitiation: Cl2 -> 2Cl* (UV light causes homolytic fission of the Cl-Cl bond, producing two chlorine free radicals).\nPropagation (two steps):\n(1) Cl* + C2H6 -> C2H5* + HCl (chlorine radical abstracts a hydrogen atom from ethane, forming an ethyl radical).\n(2) C2H5* + Cl2 -> C2H5Cl + Cl* (ethyl radical reacts with a Cl2 molecule, forming chloroethane and regenerating a Cl radical).\nTermination: Two radicals combine, e.g. Cl* + Cl* -> Cl2, or C2H5* + Cl* -> C2H5Cl, or C2H5* + C2H5* -> C4H10.\n\nEvidence from butane: The trace amount of butane (C4H10) is formed when two ethyl radicals combine: C2H5* + C2H5* -> C4H10. This can only occur if free radicals are present as intermediates, providing direct evidence for the free radical mechanism."},
  ],

  "2021": [
  {num:1, label:"Q1: Titration — Bleach (Hypochlorite/Iodine/Thiosulfate)", q:"To determine the concentration of hypochlorite ion (ClO-) in bleach.\n25.0 cm3 of bleach diluted to exactly 500 cm3.\n25.0 cm3 portions of diluted bleach acidified with H2SO4, excess KI added.\nClO- + 2I- + 2H+ -> Cl- + I2 + H2O\nIodine titrated with 0.09 M sodium thiosulfate.\nI2 + 2S2O3(2-) -> 2I- + S4O6(2-)\n\n(a) Describe how the 25.0 cm3 sample of the original bleach was diluted to exactly 500 cm3. (9)\n\n(b) Why must excess KI be added to the diluted bleach? (6)\n\n(c) State the colour change observed in the conical flask as a result of the first reaction. (6)\n\n(d)(i) Identify a suitable indicator for the titration stage.\n(ii) Using this indicator, what final colour change was observed at the end point? (9)\n\n(e) On average, 21.4 cm3 of 0.09 M Na2S2O3 were required. Calculate:\n(i) the average number of moles of sodium thiosulfate used\n(ii) the number of moles of iodine reduced in each titration\n(iii) the molarity of the original bleach in terms of ClO-\n(iv) the concentration of ClO- in the original bleach as a % (w/v). (20)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Use a funnel to transfer the 25.0 cm3 of original bleach from the beaker to a correctly prepared 500 cm3 volumetric flask. Rinse the beaker, funnel and glass rod into the volumetric flask with deionised water. Make up to the 500 cm3 mark by adding deionised water until the bottom of the meniscus is on the mark. Stopper and invert several times to mix.\n\n(b) Excess KI must be added to ensure all the hypochlorite (ClO-) reacts and is fully converted to iodine. This ensures all the bleach is detected / that iodine remains in solution as soluble triiodide (I3-).\n\n(c) The solution changes from colourless to brown (golden-brown / yellow-brown) as iodine is produced.\n\n(d)(i) Starch solution.\n(ii) From blue/black (starch-iodine complex) to colourless at the end point.\n\n(e)(i) Moles Na2S2O3 = (21.4 x 0.09) / 1000 = 0.001926 mol.\n(ii) From equation: Na2S2O3 : I2 = 2 : 1.\nMoles I2 = 0.001926 / 2 = 0.000963 mol.\n(iii) From the first equation: ClO- : I2 = 1 : 1.\nMoles ClO- in 25.0 cm3 of diluted bleach = 0.000963 mol.\nMolarity of diluted bleach = 0.000963 x (1000/25.0) = 0.03852 M.\nDilution factor = 500/25 = 20.\nMolarity of original bleach = 0.03852 x 20 = 0.7704 M.\n(iv) Mr of ClO- = 35.5 + 16 = 51.5.\nMass ClO- per litre = 0.7704 x 51.5 = 39.68 g/L.\n% (w/v) = 39.68 / 10 = 3.97 = 4% (w/v)."},
  {num:2, label:"Q2: Soap Preparation — Coconut Oil Hydrolysis", q:"Sodium cocoate soap manufactured by base hydrolysis of tri-ester molecules in coconut oil.\nTri-ester + 3NaOH -> 3CH3(CH2)10COONa + co-product\nCoconut oil, excess solid NaOH, water, ethanol, anti-bumping material refluxed ~20 min. Ethanol removed by distillation. Residue transferred to brine. Soap precipitated and washed.\n\n(a)(i) Why was the reaction mixture refluxed?\n(ii) Suggest a suitable heating method for refluxing and distilling.\n(iii) Justify the suitability of your suggested heating method.\n(iv) Why is it desirable to remove all the ethanol? (14)\n\n(b)(i) Name the co-product of the reaction.\n(ii) What is brine?\n(iii) Explain the function of the brine in the procedure. (12)\n\n(c)(i) Describe how the solid soap was separated from the solution in the beaker.\n(ii) Where is the excess NaOH at this stage? (9)\n\n(d) Explain why one end of a soap molecule is described as water loving (hydrophilic). (6)\n\n(e) When 0.03 moles of the tri-ester are hydrolysed, what is the theoretical yield, in grams, of CH3(CH2)10COONa? (9)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Refluxing speeds up the reaction (helps it reach completion in a shorter time) while preventing loss of volatile ethanol solvent.\n(ii) An oil bath (or water bath) heated with a Bunsen burner or hotplate, or a heating mantle.\n(iii) An oil/water bath provides gentle, controlled heating. It is safe (no direct flame on the flask) and allows even temperature distribution.\n(iv) It is desirable to remove all ethanol because ethanol dissolves soap. Removing it allows the soap to precipitate in the brine and improves the yield.\n\n(b)(i) The co-product is propane-1,2,3-triol (glycerol / glycerine).\n(ii) Brine is a saturated (concentrated) solution of salt (NaCl) in water.\n(iii) The brine causes the soap to precipitate (come out of solution). It also helps dissolve and wash away other components like excess NaOH and glycerol.\n\n(c)(i) Filter the mixture (or decant / pour off the brine/liquid). Wash the solid soap with a small volume of ice-cold water.\n(ii) The excess NaOH is dissolved in the brine (in the liquid / filtrate).\n\n(d) The COO-Na+ end (ionic end) of the soap molecule is hydrophilic. It is polar/ionic and can form ion-dipole interactions with water molecules, making it water-loving.\n\n(e) From the equation: 1 mol tri-ester produces 3 mol soap.\n0.03 mol tri-ester produces 0.03 x 3 = 0.09 mol soap.\nMr of CH3(CH2)10COONa = 12 + (11 x 14) + 12 + 32 + 23 = 222.\n[Detailed: 12 + (11 x 12) + (23 x 1) + 12 + 16 + 16 + 23 = 222.]\nMass = 0.09 x 222 = 19.98 g (accept 20 g)."},
  {num:3, label:"Q3: Flame Tests, Oxidising Properties of Bromine & Redox", q:"(a) Sodium sulfite and barium chloride are white crystalline salts.\nDescribe how a student could perform flame tests to distinguish between them. (15)\n\n(b) Investigating the oxidising properties of bromine using sodium sulfite, barium chloride and HCl.\n(i) Describe how to test a sample of sodium sulfite solution to verify it contains no sulfate ions.\n(ii) What was observed as bromine water was added dropwise to sodium sulfite solution?\nHow could the student verify that the sulfite ion was oxidised to sulfate ion? (18)\n\n(c) Magnesium ribbon added to copper(II) sulfate: blue colour disappeared completely.\nGranulated zinc added to same solution: blue colour faded but did not completely disappear.\nBrown precipitate formed in each case.\n(i) What do these observations tell about the reducing ability of Mg vs Cu?\n(ii) What about Zn vs Cu?\n(iii) Write a balanced equation for either reaction.\n(iv) Suggest why the blue colour completely disappeared with Mg but only faded with Zn. (17)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Procedure:\n1. Clean a platinum (or nichrome) wire by dipping it in concentrated HCl and holding it in the hot (blue) part of a Bunsen flame until no colour is seen.\n2. Dip the clean wire into the first salt sample.\n3. Hold it in the Bunsen flame and observe the flame colour.\n4. Repeat with a freshly cleaned wire for the second salt.\n\nResults:\n- Sodium sulfite: yellow (amber/orange) flame colour (due to sodium).\n- Barium chloride: yellow-green flame colour (due to barium).\n\n(b)(i) Add a few drops of barium chloride (BaCl2) solution to a small volume of the sodium sulfite solution. A white precipitate forms (barium sulfite, BaSO3). Then add a few drops of dilute hydrochloric acid -- if the precipitate dissolves completely, no sulfate ions are present (BaSO3 is soluble in acid, BaSO4 is not).\n\n(ii) Observation: The brown/red-brown colour of bromine water disappears (is decolourised) as it is added to the sodium sulfite solution.\nVerification: Add barium chloride solution to the resulting solution. A white precipitate forms. Add dilute hydrochloric acid -- if the precipitate does NOT dissolve (remains), it is barium sulfate (BaSO4), confirming that sulfite was oxidised to sulfate.\n\n(c)(i) Magnesium has a stronger reducing ability than copper. Mg is more easily oxidised than Cu (it displaces Cu from solution).\n(ii) Zinc also has a stronger reducing ability than copper. Zn is more easily oxidised than Cu.\n(iii) Mg + Cu2+ -> Mg2+ + Cu (or Mg + CuSO4 -> MgSO4 + Cu).\nAlternatively: Zn + Cu2+ -> Zn2+ + Cu.\n(iv) The blue colour completely disappeared with magnesium because the magnesium was in excess (or all the Cu2+ was used up). With zinc, the blue colour only faded because either: the zinc was the limiting reagent (fewer moles than needed), or the zinc reacted more slowly / was not sandpapered (had oxide layer preventing full reaction)."},
  {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions (Mixed Topics)", q:"Answer eight of the following:\n\n(a) How many (i) electrons, (ii) neutrons, does a S(2-) ion (Z=16, A=32) have?\n(b) Define an atomic orbital.\n(c) Write the s, p, d electron configuration for a nickel atom in the ground state.\n(d) Three containers, each with the same volume of a different gas at the same T and P. Which contains the largest number of atoms? Explain.\n(e) Define electronegativity.\n(f) Average bond energies: C-C = 348, C-triple-C = 839 kJ/mol. Why is the triple bond energy less than three times the single bond energy?\n(g) Two volumes of an oxide of nitrogen decomposed to form two volumes of N2 and one volume of O2 (same T, P). Deduce the formula of the oxide.\n(h) KBrOx contains 47.9% bromine by mass. Find the value of x.\n(i) Show by means of an equilibrium equation the self-ionisation of water.\n(j) Give a use that propanoic acid, benzoic acid and the salts of these acids have in common.\n(k) What is the average number of electrons shared between any two adjacent carbon atoms in (i) cyclohexane, (ii) benzene?\n(l) A: Give one advantage and one disadvantage of a batch process. OR B: What is the structural difference between LDPE and HDPE?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) S2- has 18 electrons (16 protons + 2 extra electrons gained).\n(ii) 16 neutrons (mass number 32 - atomic number 16).\n\n(b) An atomic orbital is a region (volume of space) around the nucleus of an atom where there is a high probability of finding an electron.\n\n(c) 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d8 (or [Ar] 4s2 3d8).\n\n(d) The container with a monatomic gas (e.g. He, Ne, Ar) contains the largest number of atoms. By Avogadro's law, all three containers contain the same number of molecules. However, diatomic (like O2, N2) or polyatomic molecules contain 2 or more atoms per molecule, so a monatomic gas has the same number of atoms as molecules, while others have fewer molecules but each contains more atoms. If all three gases are monatomic, they have the same number. If one gas is monatomic and others are polyatomic, the one with the most atoms per molecule has the most atoms overall.\n\n(e) Electronegativity is a measure of the relative attraction that an atom in a molecule has for a shared pair of electrons in a covalent bond.\n\n(f) A triple bond consists of one sigma bond and two pi bonds. The sigma bond is strong (head-on overlap), but pi bonds involve sideways overlap which is less effective. Also, the three bonding pairs are closer together, causing electron-electron repulsion. So the energy of the triple bond (839 kJ/mol) is less than 3 x 348 = 1044 kJ/mol.\n\n(g) By Avogadro's law, volumes are proportional to moles at same T and P.\n2 mol oxide -> 2 mol N2 + 1 mol O2.\nEach mole of oxide contains: (2 x 2)/2 = 2 nitrogen atoms and (1 x 2)/2 = 1 oxygen atom.\nFormula: N2O.\n\n(h) KBrOx: Mr = 39 + 80 + 16x.\n47.9% Br means: 80 / (39 + 80 + 16x) = 0.479.\n80 = 0.479(119 + 16x) = 57.001 + 7.664x.\n22.999 = 7.664x.\nx = 3.\nThe compound is KBrO3 (potassium bromate).\n\n(i) H2O + H2O <=> H3O+ + OH- (or 2H2O <=> H3O+ + OH-).\nAlternatively: H2O <=> H+ + OH-.\n\n(j) They are all used as food preservatives (antimicrobial / antifungal agents).\n\n(k)(i) Cyclohexane: 2 electrons shared (single C-C bond).\n(ii) Benzene: 3 electrons shared on average (the six delocalised pi electrons are shared equally among six C-C bonds, giving 1.5 bonds per pair = 3 electrons per bond).\n\n(l) A: Advantage: Batch process allows production of small quantities of different products / allows variation in the product / useful for expensive or specialist chemicals.\nDisadvantage: More labour-intensive / less efficient / higher cost per unit / equipment idle between batches.\nB: LDPE has a branched structure with many side-chains, giving lower density. HDPE has a more linear (unbranched or less branched) structure, allowing chains to pack closely together, giving higher density."},
  {num:5, label:"Q5: Periodic Table — Atomic Radius & Ionisation Energy", q:"Data: Cl (IE1=1251, IE2=2298), Ar (IE1=1521, IE2=2666), K (IE1=419, IE2=3052).\n\n(a)(i) How did Mendeleev arrange the elements in his 1869 periodic table?\n(ii) Suggest a reason why Mendeleev did not predict the existence of argon.\n(iii) How do we know that any new elements discovered in the future will be 'super-heavy'? (11)\n\n(b)(i) Why does an individual atom not have a definite boundary?\n(ii) How is atomic (covalent) radius defined?\n(iii) The bond length of the Cl-Cl bond is 0.199 nm. Predict the atomic (covalent) radius of chlorine.\n(iv) Account for the general decrease in atomic radii across the third period from Na to Cl.\n(v) Why is establishing an atomic (covalent) radius for argon problematic? (21)\n\n(c)(i) Define first ionisation energy.\n(ii) Why is the first ionisation energy of potassium lower than that of argon or chlorine?\n(iii) Why is the second ionisation energy of each element bigger than its first?\n(iv) Why is the second ionisation energy of potassium very significantly bigger than its first? (18)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Mendeleev arranged the elements in order of increasing atomic mass (relative atomic mass) and grouped elements with similar chemical properties together.\n(ii) Mendeleev did not predict argon because he grouped elements by their chemical properties, and argon is an inert (noble) gas with no known chemical reactions at that time. There was no gap in his table that would suggest a missing unreactive element. Also, the noble gas group (Group 0/18) did not exist in his table.\n(iii) All elements up to oganesson (Z=118) have been discovered and fill the periodic table completely through Period 7. Any new elements would need atomic numbers above 118, meaning they would have very large numbers of protons and neutrons, making them super-heavy.\n\n(b)(i) An individual atom does not have a definite boundary because the electron cloud extends outward from the nucleus with decreasing probability. There is no sharp cut-off point -- the probability of finding an electron simply gets smaller with distance.\n(ii) Atomic (covalent) radius is defined as half the distance between the nuclei of two identical atoms joined by a single covalent bond.\n(iii) Atomic radius of Cl = 0.199 / 2 = 0.0995 nm (approximately 0.100 nm).\n(iv) Across the third period from Na to Cl, the number of protons (nuclear charge) increases while electrons are added to the same energy level (third shell). The shielding effect remains approximately constant. The increased nuclear charge pulls the electrons closer to the nucleus, so the atomic radius decreases.\n(v) Argon does not form covalent bonds (it is a noble gas with a stable full outer shell). Since covalent radius is defined from a covalent bond, measuring it for argon is problematic -- there is no Ar-Ar covalent bond.\n\n(c)(i) First ionisation energy is the minimum energy required to remove the most loosely held electron from a neutral gaseous atom in its ground state.\n(ii) Potassium has a lower first ionisation energy because its outermost electron (4s1) is in a higher energy level (further from the nucleus) than the outer electrons of Ar (3p6) or Cl (3p5). It also has more inner electron shells providing greater shielding. The combination of greater distance and more shielding means the outer electron is held less tightly.\n(iii) The second ionisation energy is always higher than the first because, after removing the first electron, the remaining ion has a net positive charge. The extra positive charge holds the remaining electrons more tightly, so more energy is needed to remove the next electron.\n(iv) The second ionisation energy of potassium is enormously larger than the first because the second electron must be removed from a completely filled inner shell (3p6 -- the argon core). This inner shell electron is much closer to the nucleus, experiences much less shielding, and is very tightly held. Removing an electron from a stable noble gas configuration requires vastly more energy."},
  {num:6, label:"Q6: Biofuels, Hydrogen & Thermochemistry", q:"Bio-LPG is a co-product in making bio-diesel from renewable crops. Hydrogen gas is a reagent.\n\n(a)(i) Give two commercial sources of hydrogen gas.\n(ii) State two main advantages of using hydrogen gas itself as a fuel. (11)\n\n(b)(i) What are the two main hydrocarbon components of LPG?\n(ii) With the aid of a labelled diagram explain how crude oil is fractionally distilled.\n(iii) Show on your diagram where the refinery gas and gas oil fractions separate. (18)\n\n(c) What is the purpose of adding mercaptans to natural gas or LPG? (6)\n\n(d) Heat of combustion of ethanethiol:\nC2H5SH(l) + 4.5O2(g) -> 2CO2(g) + 3H2O(l) + SO2(g), delta H = -1867.5 kJ/mol.\nHeats of formation: CO2 = -393.5, H2O = -285.8, SO2 = -296.8 kJ/mol.\nCalculate the heat of formation of ethanethiol. (15)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Two commercial sources: (1) Steam reforming of natural gas (methane): CH4 + H2O -> CO + 3H2. (2) Electrolysis of water.\n(ii) Advantages: (1) When burned, the only product is water -- no CO2 or pollutants emitted (clean fuel). (2) High energy per gram (high specific energy).\n\n(b)(i) Propane (C3H8) and butane (C4H10).\n(ii) Crude oil is heated in a furnace and the vapours enter a fractionating column (tall tower). The column is hot at the bottom and cool at the top. Different fractions condense at different heights based on their boiling points: lighter fractions (lower boiling points, smaller molecules) rise to the top; heavier fractions (higher boiling points, larger molecules) condense lower down. Trays at different levels collect the condensed fractions.\n(iii) Refinery gas separates at the very top of the column (it has the lowest boiling point and exits as a gas). Gas oil separates lower down the column (it has a higher boiling point than kerosene/paraffin and naphtha/petrol).\n\n(c) Mercaptans are added as an odorant to give the gas a distinctive, unpleasant smell. Natural gas and LPG are odourless, so mercaptans allow gas leaks to be detected by smell for safety.\n\n(d) Using Hess's Law:\ndelta H(combustion) = sum of delta Hf(products) - sum of delta Hf(reactants).\n-1867.5 = [2(-393.5) + 3(-285.8) + (-296.8)] - [delta Hf(C2H5SH) + 0]\n-1867.5 = [-787.0 + (-857.4) + (-296.8)] - delta Hf(C2H5SH)\n-1867.5 = -1941.2 - delta Hf(C2H5SH)\ndelta Hf(C2H5SH) = -1941.2 + 1867.5 = -73.7 kJ/mol."},
  {num:7, label:"Q7: Acids, Bases & pH Calculations", q:"(a) Define an acid according to the theory of (i) Arrhenius, (ii) Bronsted and Lowry.\n(iii) State one limitation of Arrhenius' acid-base theory.\n(iv) What is a conjugate acid-base pair in Bronsted-Lowry theory? (17)\n\n(b) Distinguish between a strong acid and a weak acid. (6)\n\n(c) Sulfuric acid is a strong dibasic acid. Write a balanced equation to show that the conjugate base of H2SO4 acts as a Bronsted-Lowry acid in water. (6)\n\n(d)(i) Write a balanced equation for the dissociation of a weak monobasic acid HA in water.\nIf HA is 1.5% dissociated in a 0.10 M solution:\n(ii) Find the concentrations of H3O+ and A- in moles per litre.\n(iii) Calculate the pH of the 0.10 M HA solution.\n(iv) Calculate the value of the acid dissociation constant Ka for HA (take [HA] = 0.10 M). (21)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Arrhenius: An acid is a substance that dissociates in water to produce H+ ions (hydrogen ions).\n(ii) Bronsted-Lowry: An acid is a proton (H+) donor.\n(iii) Limitation: Arrhenius' theory only applies to aqueous solutions. It cannot explain acid-base behaviour in non-aqueous solvents (e.g. NH3 acting as a base in the gas phase).\n(iv) A conjugate acid-base pair consists of two species that differ by one proton (H+). The acid has the extra proton; the base has lost it. Example: HCl / Cl- or NH4+ / NH3.\n\n(b) A strong acid is fully (completely) dissociated into ions in aqueous solution.\nA weak acid is only partially dissociated into ions in aqueous solution (an equilibrium exists between the undissociated acid and its ions).\n\n(c) The conjugate base of H2SO4 is HSO4-.\nHSO4- + H2O -> H3O+ + SO42-.\n\n(d)(i) HA + H2O <=> H3O+ + A-.\n(ii) 1.5% of 0.10 M is dissociated:\n[H3O+] = [A-] = 0.015 x 0.10 = 0.0015 M = 1.5 x 10^-3 M.\n(iii) pH = -log[H3O+] = -log(1.5 x 10^-3) = 2.82.\n(iv) Ka = [H3O+][A-] / [HA] = (1.5 x 10^-3)(1.5 x 10^-3) / 0.10\n= 2.25 x 10^-6 / 0.10\n= 2.25 x 10^-5."},
  {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Reaction Scheme — Propan-2-ol (Isopropyl Alcohol)", q:"Compound Y (isopropyl alcohol) is produced from compound X with water. Y can also be produced by reduction of compound Z.\n\n(a)(i) What is the systematic IUPAC name for Y?\n(ii) Classify Y as a primary or a secondary alcohol. Justify. (8)\n\n(b)(i) Identify Z.\n(ii) Draw an expanded molecular structure for Y, indicating which bonds are formed when Z is reduced.\n(iii) How does the geometry around the carbon atoms in Z change during this reduction reaction? (15)\n\n(c)(i) What type of reaction is involved in the conversion of X to Y?\n(ii) Identify another product of the reaction of X with water. (6)\n\n(d)(i) Write a balanced equation for the reaction of Y with sodium.\n(ii) What information does this reaction give about the -OH functional group in an alcohol?\n(iii) Name another substance, containing an -OH group, that reacts with sodium. Compare the vigour of its reaction with Y. (12)\n\n(e)(i) Describe a chemical test to distinguish between Z and Y.\n(ii) Identify the organic product when Z reacts with the test reagent. (9)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Propan-2-ol.\n(ii) Y is a secondary alcohol. The -OH group is attached to a carbon that is bonded to two other carbon atoms (the middle carbon of the 3-carbon chain). In a primary alcohol, the -OH is on a carbon bonded to only one other carbon.\n\n(b)(i) Z is propanone (acetone, (CH3)2CO) -- a ketone. Reduction of a ketone gives a secondary alcohol.\n(ii) In propan-2-ol (CH3CH(OH)CH3), the bonds formed during reduction of propanone are: a C-H bond and an O-H bond on the central carbon (replacing the C=O double bond with C-OH and C-H single bonds).\n(iii) The central carbon in propanone (Z) has trigonal planar geometry (120 degree bond angles) due to the C=O double bond. After reduction to propan-2-ol (Y), the central carbon becomes tetrahedral (109.5 degree bond angles) with four single bonds.\n\n(c)(i) Addition reaction (specifically, hydration -- water adds across a double bond). X is propene (CH3CH=CH2).\n(ii) The other product could be propan-1-ol (the minor product from Markovnikov's rule considerations), but more correctly, X is propene and the main other species is just propan-2-ol itself. If considering acid-catalysed hydration, no other organic product forms -- only propan-2-ol from propene + water.\n\n(d)(i) 2CH3CH(OH)CH3 + 2Na -> 2CH3CH(ONa)CH3 + H2.\n(ii) This reaction shows that the -OH group in alcohols contains a slightly acidic hydrogen. The hydrogen of -OH can be replaced by sodium, releasing hydrogen gas. However, the reaction is less vigorous than with water, showing that the -OH in alcohols is less acidic than in water.\n(iii) Water also contains an -OH group and reacts with sodium: 2H2O + 2Na -> 2NaOH + H2. The reaction of sodium with water is much more vigorous (rapid, exothermic, sodium dances on the surface) compared to the gentle/slow reaction with propan-2-ol.\n\n(e)(i) Use Fehling's solution (or acidified KMnO4 or Tollens' reagent). Warm with the sample.\nPropanone (Z, a ketone) does NOT react -- no colour change.\nPropan-2-ol (Y, a secondary alcohol) also does NOT react with Fehling's.\nBetter test: Use Brady's reagent (2,4-DNPH). Both would give a precipitate for Z (ketone) but not Y. Or use iodoform test: both propanone and propan-2-ol give a positive iodoform test (yellow precipitate of CHI3 with I2/NaOH), so this does not distinguish them.\nBest distinguishing test: Add acidified potassium dichromate (K2Cr2O7). Propan-2-ol (secondary alcohol) is oxidised and the colour changes from orange to green. Propanone (ketone) does NOT react -- stays orange.\n(ii) When propan-2-ol is oxidised, the organic product is propanone (CH3COCH3). When propanone reacts with 2,4-DNPH, a yellow/orange precipitate (2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone) forms."},
  {num:9, label:"Q9: Electrochemistry & Corrosion", q:"(a)(i) Explain the terms electrolysis and electrochemical cell.\n(ii) In the electrolysis of dilute sulfuric acid using platinum electrodes, identify the products at each electrode.\n(iii) Write half-equations for the reactions at each electrode.\n(iv) Why is dilute sulfuric acid used rather than pure water? (21)\n\n(b)(i) What is rusting?\n(ii) Identify two conditions necessary for rusting.\n(iii) Give two methods of preventing rusting and explain how each works.\n(iv) Explain why zinc, rather than copper, is used in galvanising iron. (17)\n\n(c) In a Daniell cell: Zn(s) | Zn2+(aq) || Cu2+(aq) | Cu(s)\n(i) Which metal is the negative electrode?\n(ii) Write the half-equation for the reaction at each electrode.\n(iii) What is the function of the salt bridge? (12)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Electrolysis: The use of electrical energy to cause a non-spontaneous chemical reaction (decomposition of a compound by passing an electric current through it in molten or aqueous form).\nElectrochemical cell: A device that converts chemical energy into electrical energy through a spontaneous redox reaction (e.g. a battery or galvanic cell).\n(ii) At the cathode (negative electrode): hydrogen gas (H2). At the anode (positive electrode): oxygen gas (O2).\n(iii) Cathode: 4H+ + 4e- -> 2H2 (or 2H+ + 2e- -> H2).\nAnode: 2H2O -> O2 + 4H+ + 4e- (or 4OH- -> 2H2O + O2 + 4e-).\n(iv) Pure water is a very poor conductor of electricity because it contains very few ions (only from slight self-ionisation). Dilute sulfuric acid provides a high concentration of H+ and SO42- ions, greatly increasing the conductivity.\n\n(b)(i) Rusting is the corrosion of iron to form hydrated iron(III) oxide (Fe2O3.xH2O) -- a flaky, red-brown substance.\n(ii) Two conditions: (1) Water (moisture). (2) Oxygen (air). Both must be present.\n(iii) Method 1: Painting/coating -- provides a physical barrier preventing water and oxygen from reaching the iron surface.\nMethod 2: Galvanising (coating with zinc) -- zinc is more reactive (higher in the activity series) than iron, so it acts as a sacrificial anode. Even if the coating is scratched, zinc is preferentially oxidised instead of iron.\n(iv) Zinc is more reactive than iron (higher in the electrochemical series). It is oxidised preferentially, sacrificing itself to protect the iron. Copper is less reactive than iron, so if copper coating were scratched, the iron would corrode preferentially (copper would accelerate the rusting by creating a galvanic couple).\n\n(c)(i) Zinc is the negative electrode (anode).\n(ii) At the zinc anode (oxidation): Zn(s) -> Zn2+(aq) + 2e-.\nAt the copper cathode (reduction): Cu2+(aq) + 2e- -> Cu(s).\n(iii) The salt bridge completes the electrical circuit by allowing ions to flow between the two half-cells. It maintains electrical neutrality in each solution (e.g. anions migrate toward the anode solution where positive Zn2+ ions are accumulating, and cations migrate toward the cathode solution where Cu2+ ions are being removed)."},
  {num:10, label:"Q10: Organic Chemistry — Ethanol, Esters & Polymers (Answer 2 of 3)", q:"Answer any two of (a), (b) and (c). (2 x 25)\n\n(a) Ethanol:\n(i) Give two industrial uses of ethanol.\n(ii) Describe how ethanol is made industrially from ethene.\n(iii) Why is ethanol made from biomass considered 'carbon neutral'?\n(iv) Write a balanced equation for the complete combustion of ethanol.\n(v) Describe a chemical test to distinguish between ethanol and methanol.\n\n(b) Esters:\n(i) What is an ester?\n(ii) Name the ester formed from ethanol and propanoic acid. Write the equation.\n(iii) Name the catalyst used.\n(iv) Describe the procedure for preparing a small sample of this ester.\n(v) How would you detect the presence of the ester?\n\n(c) Polymers:\n(i) Distinguish between addition and condensation polymerisation.\n(ii) Give an example of each type.\n(iii) Draw two repeating units of poly(chloroethene) (PVC).\n(iv) Give one environmental concern about PVC.\n(v) Explain what is meant by a thermoplastic polymer.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Uses: (1) Solvent (in paints, perfumes, pharmaceuticals). (2) Fuel / biofuel (in petrol blends or as pure fuel). Also: manufacture of esters, antiseptic.\n(ii) Ethanol is made by hydration of ethene. Ethene and steam are passed over a phosphoric acid (H3PO4) catalyst at high temperature (~300 deg C) and high pressure (~60-70 atm): C2H4 + H2O -> C2H5OH.\n(iii) When biomass (e.g. sugar cane, corn) is grown, the plants absorb CO2 from the atmosphere via photosynthesis. When the ethanol made from them is burned, it releases the same amount of CO2 back. The net CO2 contribution is approximately zero, so it is considered carbon neutral.\n(iv) C2H5OH + 3O2 -> 2CO2 + 3H2O.\n(v) The iodoform test: Warm each with iodine (I2) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) solution. Ethanol produces a yellow precipitate of triiodomethane (iodoform, CHI3) and has a characteristic antiseptic smell. Methanol does not give a positive iodoform test (no precipitate).\n\n(b)(i) An ester is an organic compound formed by the reaction of a carboxylic acid with an alcohol, with the elimination of water (condensation).\n(ii) Ethyl propanoate. C2H5COOH + C2H5OH -> C2H5COOC2H5 + H2O.\n(iii) Concentrated sulfuric acid (H2SO4) acts as the catalyst.\n(iv) Place the alcohol and carboxylic acid in a test tube or round-bottomed flask. Add a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid. Warm gently in a hot water bath for several minutes. Pour the mixture into sodium carbonate solution (or cold water) in a beaker.\n(v) The ester can be detected by its fruity / sweet / pleasant smell.\n\n(c)(i) Addition polymerisation: Monomers with C=C double bonds join together by opening their double bonds. No small molecule is lost. The polymer has the same empirical formula as the monomer.\nCondensation polymerisation: Monomers with two functional groups join together with the elimination of a small molecule (usually water) at each link.\n(ii) Addition: poly(ethene) from ethene. Condensation: nylon (from a diamine and a dicarboxylic acid) or polyester (from a diol and a dicarboxylic acid).\n(iii) PVC: -[-CH2-CHCl-]-[-CH2-CHCl-]-\n(iv) When PVC is burned or incinerated, it can release toxic hydrogen chloride gas (HCl) and dioxins. Also, PVC is not easily biodegradable, contributing to plastic pollution.\n(v) A thermoplastic polymer softens and can be remoulded when heated, and hardens again on cooling. This process can be repeated. The polymer chains are held together by weak intermolecular forces (not cross-links), allowing them to slide over each other when heated."}
],

  "2020": [
  {num:1, label:"Q1: Titration — Washing Soda (Na2CO3.xH2O)", q:"Washing soda crystals (Na2CO3.xH2O) effloresce in dry air. 3.46 g dissolved in deionised water and made up to 250 cm3. 25.0 cm3 portions titrated with standardised HCl.\n2HCl + Na2CO3 -> 2NaCl + H2O + CO2\n\n(a) Identify a primary standard that could have been used to standardise the HCl solution. (5)\n\n(b) Describe how the 250 cm3 solution of washing soda was prepared starting with 3.46 g on a weighing boat. (12)\n\n(c)(i) Name a suitable indicator for this titration.\n(ii) Justify your choice of indicator.\n(iii) Using this indicator, what colour change was observed at the end point? (12)\n\n(d) On average 21.5 cm3 of 0.12 M HCl were required. Find by calculation:\n(i) the average number of moles of HCl used\n(ii) the number of moles of Na2CO3 neutralised in each titration\n(iii) the number of moles of Na2CO3 in 250 cm3\n(iv) the mass of Na2CO3 in 250 cm3\n(v) the mass of water of crystallisation and hence the number of moles of water in 3.46 g of crystals\n(vi) the ratio of moles water : moles Na2CO3 and hence the value of x. (21)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Anhydrous sodium carbonate (Na2CO3) is a suitable primary standard.\n\n(b) Transfer the washing soda from the weighing boat into a beaker and dissolve in deionised water. Rinse the weighing boat into the beaker. Use a glass rod and funnel to transfer the solution into a clean 250 cm3 volumetric flask. Rinse the beaker, glass rod and funnel into the flask with deionised water. Make up to the 250 cm3 mark by adding deionised water until the bottom of the meniscus is on the mark. Stopper and invert several times to mix.\n\n(c)(i) Methyl orange (or methyl red).\n(ii) This is a strong acid / weak base titration, so the end point occurs in acidic solution (below pH 7). Methyl orange changes colour in the range pH 3-5, which coincides with the end point.\n(iii) From orange (yellow) to red (pink).\n\n(d)(i) Moles HCl = (21.5 x 0.12) / 1000 = 0.00258 mol.\n(ii) From the equation, Na2CO3 : HCl = 1 : 2.\nMoles Na2CO3 = 0.00258 / 2 = 0.00129 mol in 25.0 cm3.\n(iii) Moles Na2CO3 in 250 cm3 = 0.00129 x 10 = 0.0129 mol.\n(iv) Mr of Na2CO3 = (2 x 23) + 12 + (3 x 16) = 106.\nMass = 0.0129 x 106 = 1.3674 g.\n(v) Mass of water = 3.46 - 1.3674 = 2.0926 g.\nMoles of water = 2.0926 / 18 = 0.1163 mol.\n(vi) Ratio = 0.1163 / 0.0129 = 9.01.\nx = 9 (to the nearest whole number).\nThe formula is Na2CO3.9H2O."},
  {num:2, label:"Q2: Organic Synthesis — Benzoic Acid from Phenylmethanol", q:"Benzoic acid synthesised by oxidation of phenylmethanol with KMnO4 in alkaline conditions.\n3C6H5CH2OH + 4KMnO4 -> 3C6H5COOH + 4MnO2 + H2O + 4KOH\nStages A-F shown: A and B are oxidation, HCl at C, Na2SO3 at D, crystals collected at F.\n\n(a)(i) Describe the appearance of phenylmethanol at room temperature.\n(ii) Name a suitable piece of apparatus to measure accurately 1.5 cm3 of phenylmethanol. (5)\n\n(b) How does the appearance of the reaction mixture change during heating with KMnO4 and Na2CO3? (6)\n\n(c) Describe what is observed during stages D and E. Explain these observations. (12)\n\n(d)(i) Name a suitable technique to purify the benzoic acid crystals collected at stage F.\n(ii) Describe how you measured the melting point of a sample of benzoic acid crystals. (12)\n\n(e) 3.16 g of KMnO4 used to oxidise 1.5 cm3 phenylmethanol (density 1.04 g/cm3).\n(i) Show by calculation whether there is sufficient KMnO4 to oxidise all the phenylmethanol.\n(ii) Find the theoretical yield in grams of benzoic acid. (15)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Colourless (or pale yellow), oily liquid.\n(ii) A graduated syringe (or graduated dropper / graduated micropipette / burette).\n\n(b) The mixture changes from purple (violet) to brown (muddy, dark-coloured) as MnO2 is formed.\n\n(c) Observations: The brown solid (MnO2) disappears when Na2SO3 is added at stage D. White crystals of benzoic acid form (precipitate) on cooling at stage E.\nExplanation: The MnO2 (Mn(IV)) is reduced to soluble Mn2+ (colourless). The benzoic acid crystallises (precipitates) out of solution on cooling.\n\n(d)(i) Recrystallisation.\n(ii) Place a small sample of the crystals in a melting point capillary tube. Insert the tube into a melting point apparatus (or aluminium block). Heat slowly while observing the crystals. Record the temperature range at which melting occurs using the thermometer.\n\n(e)(i) Mass of phenylmethanol = 1.5 x 1.04 = 1.56 g.\nMr of phenylmethanol (C6H5CH2OH) = 108.\nMoles phenylmethanol = 1.56 / 108 = 0.0144 mol.\nMr of KMnO4 = 158.\nMoles KMnO4 = 3.16 / 158 = 0.02 mol.\nRequired ratio: phenylmethanol : KMnO4 = 3 : 4.\nFor 0.0144 mol phenylmethanol, KMnO4 needed = (0.0144 x 4) / 3 = 0.0192 mol.\n0.02 mol KMnO4 available > 0.0192 mol required.\nThere is sufficient (excess) KMnO4 to oxidise all the phenylmethanol.\n\n(ii) Phenylmethanol is the limiting reagent.\n0.0144 mol phenylmethanol produces 0.0144 mol benzoic acid (1:1 ratio from equation).\nMr of benzoic acid (C6H5COOH) = 122.\nTheoretical yield = 0.0144 x 122 = 1.76 g (accept 1.7-1.8 g)."},
  {num:3, label:"Q3: Rates of Reaction — Na2S2O3 and HCl (Temperature Effect)", q:"Effect of temperature on the reaction between 0.05 M sodium thiosulfate and 3.0 M HCl.\n100 cm3 thiosulfate + 10 cm3 HCl. Reciprocal of time (1/t) taken as rate.\n\nData: Run 1: 0 deg C, t=500s, r=0.002. Run 2: 13 deg C, t=200s. Run 3: 22 deg C, t=109s. Run 4: 33 deg C, t=56s. Run 5: 40 deg C, t=34s. Run 6: 51 deg C, t=16s. Run 7: 59 deg C, t=9s.\n\n(a) Describe a method for adjusting and measuring the temperature of the reaction mixture for runs above room temperature. (9)\n\n(b)(i) What was observed in the reaction flasks during each run?\n(ii) Describe how this observation was used to obtain the reaction times. (9)\n\n(c) Copy the last column and fill in the missing rates correct to three decimal places. (6)\n\n(d)(i) Plot a graph of rate vs temperature.\n(ii) Use your graph to find x: 'The rate doubles for every x deg C rise in temperature.'\n(iii) Explain this result. (21)\n\n(e) Run 8 at 40 deg C in a different-shaped conical flask. Rate = 0.015 s-1.\n(i) Does data point (40, 0.015) lie on your curve?\n(ii) Was the depth of liquid greater or less than the original flasks? Justify. (5)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Heat the thiosulfate solution in a water bath (on a hotplate or with a Bunsen). Add the HCl and mix (stir/swirl). Use a thermometer in the flask to record the reaction mixture temperature immediately.\n\n(b)(i) A precipitate of sulfur appeared (pale yellow / cream / off-white cloudiness).\n(ii) A cross (X) was placed under the flask. The time was recorded for the cross to become obscured (invisible) when viewed through the solution from above.\n\n(c) Missing rates (1/t, to 3 decimal places):\nRun 2: 1/200 = 0.005\nRun 3: 1/109 = 0.009\nRun 4: 1/56 = 0.018\nRun 5: 1/34 = 0.029\nRun 6: 1/16 = 0.063\nRun 7: 1/9 = 0.111\n\n(d)(i) Plot temperature (x-axis) vs rate (y-axis). Label axes. Use appropriate scales. Plot all 7 points. Draw a smooth curve (not straight lines between points).\n(ii) x = 9-11 deg C. The rate approximately doubles for every 10 deg C rise in temperature.\n(iii) At higher temperatures, particles have more kinetic energy. More collisions exceed the activation energy, so more effective (successful) collisions occur per unit time.\n\n(e)(i) The point (40, 0.015) does NOT lie on the curve (it lies below the expected point at 40 deg C, which was 0.029).\n(ii) The depth of liquid was greater (deeper) in the Run 8 flask. This means the sulfur precipitate needs to build up more to obscure the cross through a greater depth, making the apparent reaction time longer and the measured rate lower."},
  {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions (Mixed Topics)", q:"Answer eight of the following:\n\n(a) Draw a labelled diagram of Thomson's plum-pudding model of the atom.\n(b) The diagram represents 10 electrons in the 4s and 3d sub-levels of a neutral atom. What is the element?\n(c) What isotope is produced when Am-241 (Z=95) releases an alpha particle?\n(d) Write chemical formulae for: (i) magnesium sulfite, (ii) copper(II) oxide.\n(e) Give two possible shapes of a covalent molecule with the general formula QX3.\n(f) 0.36 moles of CO2 released into a bicycle tube, volume 1.0 x 10^-3 m3 at 293 K. Calculate the pressure.\n(g) How can a catalyst lower the activation energy of a reaction?\n(h) Under what circumstances do ionic compounds conduct electricity?\n(i) Identify the main component of the limescale deposit in a kettle in a hard water area.\n(j) Vanillin structure given. (i) Circle the aldehyde group. (ii) Write the molecular formula.\n(k) A: Write a balanced equation for the photo-dissociation of ozone. OR B: Draw the structure of the monomer used to make poly(phenylethene).", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Small negative particles (electrons) embedded in a sphere of positive matter (charge). Diagram shows negative charges dotted throughout a positive sphere.\n\n(b) The element is nickel (Ni). Configuration: [Ar] 4s2 3d8 = 28 electrons.\n\n(c) Alpha particle = He-4 (Z=2). Am-241 (Z=95) loses an alpha particle:\nNew mass number = 241 - 4 = 237. New atomic number = 95 - 2 = 93.\nThe isotope produced is neptunium-237 (Np-237).\n\n(d)(i) MgSO3. (ii) CuO.\n\n(e) Trigonal planar and pyramidal (also accept T-shaped).\n\n(f) Using the ideal gas law: pV = nRT.\np = nRT / V = (0.36 x 8.3 x 293) / (1.0 x 10^-3)\np = 875.484 / 0.001 = 875484 Pa.\np = 8.8 x 10^5 Pa (to 2 significant figures).\n\n(g) A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway (mechanism) with a lower activation energy. It does this by forming temporary bonds with reactant molecules, allowing them to react via a lower-energy route.\n\n(h) Ionic compounds conduct electricity when they are: (1) dissolved in water (aqueous solution), or (2) molten (liquid state). In both cases, the ions are free to move and carry charge.\n\n(i) Calcium carbonate (CaCO3).\n\n(j)(i) The CHO (aldehyde) group should be circled.\n(ii) Molecular formula of vanillin: C8H8O3.\n\n(k) A: O3 -> O2 + O (photo-dissociation of ozone by UV light).\nB: Phenylethene (styrene): C6H5CH=CH2."},
  {num:5, label:"Q5: Atomic Structure — Energy Levels, Sub-levels & Electron Configurations", q:"Bohr proposed electrons occupy energy levels. Later sub-levels and orbitals were introduced.\n\n(a) What is an electron? (5)\n\n(b)(i) Why might the electron in a hydrogen atom not occupy the n=1 energy level?\n(ii) What colour light is associated with the electron moving from n=3 to n=2?\n(iii) Name the series of visible lines in the hydrogen emission spectrum. (9)\n\n(c)(i) How many sub-levels are associated with the n=3 energy level?\n(ii) What is an atomic orbital?\n(iii) How many orbitals are associated with the n=2 energy level?\n(iv) What is the maximum number of electrons that can occupy n=3 in a multi-electron atom? (15)\n\n(d) Write s, p electron configurations for Be, Ne, Mg and Kr.\nRefer to these to explain:\n(i) why Ne and Kr (Group 18) are chemically inert,\n(ii) why Be and Mg (Group 2) are reactive.\nWhy is Mg more reactive than Be? (21)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) An electron is a sub-atomic particle with a negative charge (charge = -1) and a very small mass (approximately 1/1836 of a proton).\n\n(b)(i) The electron may have absorbed energy (been excited) and moved to a higher energy level (e.g. n=2, n=3, etc.).\n(ii) Red light (the n=3 to n=2 transition produces red light in the Balmer series, wavelength about 656 nm).\n(iii) The Balmer series.\n\n(c)(i) Three sub-levels: 3s, 3p, 3d.\n(ii) An atomic orbital is a region (volume of space) around the nucleus where there is a high probability of finding an electron.\n(iii) Four orbitals: one 2s orbital and three 2p orbitals (2px, 2py, 2pz).\n(iv) Maximum of 18 electrons in n=3 (2 in 3s + 6 in 3p + 10 in 3d).\n\n(d) Electron configurations:\nBe: 1s2 2s2\nNe: 1s2 2s2 2p6\nMg: 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2\nKr: 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p6\n\n(i) Ne and Kr are chemically inert because they have completely filled outer energy levels (Ne: full 2s2 2p6; Kr: full 4s2 3d10 4p6). They have stable electron configurations with no tendency to gain, lose or share electrons.\n(ii) Be and Mg are reactive because they each have 2 electrons in their outer s sub-level (Be: 2s2; Mg: 3s2). These electrons can be easily lost to form M2+ ions, achieving a stable noble gas configuration.\nMg is more reactive than Be because the 3s electrons in Mg are further from the nucleus and experience more shielding from inner electron shells, so they are more easily removed (lower ionisation energy)."},
  {num:6, label:"Q6: Fuels — Hydrocarbons, Cracking & Heat of Combustion", q:"Petrol is a mixture of hydrocarbons from fractional distillation of crude oil.\nEquations:\nC14H30 -> C2H4 + 2X + C6H14\nCH3(CH2)5CH3 -> C6H5CH3 + 4H2\nCH3(CH2)6CH3 -> CH3CH(CH3)CH2C(CH3)2CH3\n\n(a) Explain the terms fractional distillation and octane number. (8)\n\n(b)(i) Name the type of oil refining reaction in each of the three equations.\n(ii) Deduce the formula of hydrocarbon X.\n(iii) Give the systematic IUPAC name for the product in the third equation. (15)\n\n(c)(i) Give an example of an oxygenate used in petrol.\n(ii) Why are oxygenates added to petrol? (6)\n\n(d)(i) Define heat of formation.\n(ii) Heats of formation: propane = -104.7, CO2 = -393.5, H2O = -285.8 kJ/mol.\nWrite a balanced equation for combustion of propane and calculate the heat of combustion. (21)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Fractional distillation: A method of separating a liquid mixture into its components (fractions) based on their different boiling points. The mixture is heated and the vapours condense at different heights in a fractionating column.\nOctane number: A measure of a fuel's ability to resist knocking (auto-ignition) in an engine. Heptane has an octane number of 0 and 2,2,4-trimethylpentane (isooctane) has an octane number of 100.\n\n(b)(i) Equation 1: Cracking (thermal or catalytic cracking).\nEquation 2: Reforming (catalytic reforming / dehydrogenation).\nEquation 3: Isomerisation.\n(ii) C14H30 -> C2H4 + 2X + C6H14.\nCarbon: 14 = 2 + 2n + 6, so 2n = 6, n = 3. Each X has 3 carbons.\nHydrogen: 30 = 4 + 2m + 14, so 2m = 12, m = 6. Each X has 6 hydrogens.\nX = C3H6 (propene).\n(iii) 2,2,4-trimethylpentane.\n\n(c)(i) Ethanol (or MTBE / methyl tert-butyl ether).\n(ii) Oxygenates increase the octane number of petrol and promote more complete combustion, reducing carbon monoxide emissions.\n\n(d)(i) Heat of formation is the heat change when one mole of a compound is formed from its elements in their standard states.\n(ii) C3H8(g) + 5O2(g) -> 3CO2(g) + 4H2O(l).\ndelta H(combustion) = [3(-393.5) + 4(-285.8)] - [(-104.7) + 0]\n= [-1180.5 + (-1143.2)] - [-104.7]\n= -2323.7 + 104.7\n= -2219.0 kJ/mol."},
  {num:7, label:"Q7: Acids, Bases & pH Calculations", q:"(a) According to Bronsted-Lowry theory, what is an acid?\nHow does this theory distinguish between a strong and a weak acid? (8)\n\n(b) What is (i) the conjugate acid, (ii) the conjugate base, of HSO4-? (6)\n\nSparkling mineral water (pH 5.6, source pH 7.2). Label data: Ca2+, Mg2+, HCO3-, NO3-, SO42-, Cl-.\n\n(c) Describe how you could test a sample for the sulfate ion. (6)\n\n(d) Calculate:\n(i) the H3O+ concentration of the sparkling water (pH 5.6),\n(ii) the OH- concentration of the source water (pH 7.2). (12)\n\nAn acid-base indicator HA is red (undissociated) and yellow (as A-). Ka = 4.0 x 10^-4.\n\n(e) Calculate the pH of an aqueous 1.5 x 10^-3 M solution of the indicator. (6)\n\n(f)(i) Write an equilibrium equation for the dissociation of the indicator in water.\n(ii) What is observed when a few drops of the indicator are added to a solution of a strong base? Explain. (12)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) A Bronsted-Lowry acid is a proton (H+) donor.\nA strong acid is fully dissociated (ionised) in aqueous solution -- it donates its protons completely.\nA weak acid is only partially dissociated in aqueous solution -- it donates only some of its protons (equilibrium lies to the left).\n\n(b)(i) Conjugate acid of HSO4- is H2SO4 (HSO4- gains a proton).\n(ii) Conjugate base of HSO4- is SO42- (HSO4- loses a proton).\n\n(c) Add dilute hydrochloric acid (to acidify) and then barium chloride solution (BaCl2) to the sample. A white precipitate (of barium sulfate, BaSO4) that is insoluble in acid confirms the presence of sulfate ions.\n\n(d)(i) pH = -log[H3O+], so [H3O+] = 10^(-pH) = 10^(-5.6) = 2.51 x 10^-6 M.\n(ii) pH of source water = 7.2.\npOH = 14 - 7.2 = 6.8.\n[OH-] = 10^(-6.8) = 1.58 x 10^-7 M.\nAlternatively: [H3O+] = 10^(-7.2) = 6.31 x 10^-8 M.\nKw = [H3O+][OH-] = 1.0 x 10^-14.\n[OH-] = 10^-14 / (6.31 x 10^-8) = 1.58 x 10^-7 M.\n\n(e) Ka = [H+][A-] / [HA] = x^2 / (1.5 x 10^-3 - x), approximating x << 1.5 x 10^-3:\nx^2 = 4.0 x 10^-4 x 1.5 x 10^-3 = 6.0 x 10^-7.\nx = [H+] = 7.75 x 10^-4 M.\npH = -log(7.75 x 10^-4) = 3.1.\n\n(f)(i) HA <=> H+ + A- (or HA + H2O <=> H3O+ + A-).\n(ii) A yellow colour is observed. In the strong base, the OH- ions react with H+ ions from the equilibrium, shifting it to the right. This causes nearly all HA (red) to dissociate into A- (yellow). Since almost all indicator exists as A-, the solution appears yellow."},
  {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Reaction Scheme — Ethene, Ethanol & PVC", q:"Reaction scheme with multiple conversions involving ethene, ethanol, ethanoic acid, chloroethene (vinyl chloride), compound X and PVC.\n\n(a) Identify in the scheme (i) a substitution reaction, (ii) an addition reaction, (iii) an elimination reaction. (9)\n\n(b)(i) What reagent and catalyst can be used in conversion 1 (ethene to ethanol)?\n(ii) Copy the structure of ethanol and identify clearly the bonds formed during conversion 1. (12)\n\n(c) Identify an organic substance in the scheme that is highly soluble in water. Justify your answer. (6)\n\n(d)(i) Identify compound X.\n(ii) How does the geometry around the carbon atoms change during conversion 7 (polymerisation of chloroethene)?\n(iii) Draw the structure of two repeating units of PVC. (12)\n\n(e) Describe a mechanism, that involves ions, for conversion 6 (addition of HCl to ethene). (11)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Substitution: e.g. the conversion of ethanol to chloroethane (an OH group is replaced by Cl).\n(ii) Addition: e.g. hydration of ethene to ethanol (H2O adds across the double bond), or addition of HCl to ethene.\n(iii) Elimination: e.g. dehydration of ethanol to ethene (H2O is removed).\n\n(b)(i) Reagent: steam (H2O). Catalyst: phosphoric acid (H3PO4) or concentrated sulfuric acid.\n(ii) In ethanol (CH3CH2OH), the bonds formed during the addition of water to ethene are: a C-H bond on one carbon and a C-OH bond on the other carbon (the pi bond of ethene breaks and these new sigma bonds form).\n\n(c) Ethanol (or ethanoic acid) is highly soluble in water. Justification: Ethanol has a polar -OH group that can form hydrogen bonds with water molecules, making it miscible in all proportions.\n\n(d)(i) Compound X is 1,2-dichloroethane (ClCH2CH2Cl) -- formed by addition of Cl2 to ethene.\n(ii) The geometry changes from trigonal planar (120 degree bond angles around C=C in chloroethene) to tetrahedral (109.5 degree bond angles around C-C in PVC).\n(iii) PVC repeating units: -[-CH2-CHCl-]-[-CH2-CHCl-]-\n\n(e) Electrophilic addition mechanism for HCl + ethene:\nStep 1: The HCl molecule approaches the electron-rich C=C double bond. The H-Cl bond polarises (H is delta+, Cl is delta-).\nStep 2: The electrophile (H+) attacks the pi bond of ethene. One carbon bonds to H, forming a carbocation (CH3CH2+) intermediate.\nStep 3: The nucleophile (Cl-) attacks the positively charged carbocation to form chloroethane (CH3CH2Cl)."},
  {num:9, label:"Q9: Chemical Equilibrium — Water-Gas Shift Reaction", q:"3.0 moles CO and 1.0 moles steam mixed in container of fixed volume V with catalyst at 800 K.\nCO(g) + H2O(g) <=> CO2(g) + H2(g), delta H = -41.2 kJ/mol.\n\n(a) Explain chemical equilibrium.\nWhy is a chemical equilibrium described as dynamic? (8)\n\n(b)(i) Write an expression for Kc.\n(ii) It was found that 0.75 moles of H2 were present at equilibrium. Calculate Kc. (12)\n\n(c) State Le Chatelier's principle. (6)\n\n(d)(i) Predict the effect on the yield of CO2 of increasing the temperature. Justify.\n(ii) Predict the effect on Kc of increasing the temperature. Justify.\n(iii) What is the effect of increasing the pressure on this equilibrium? Justify. (15)\n\n(e) What effect does a catalyst have on (i) the rate at which equilibrium is reached, (ii) the value of Kc? (9)", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Chemical equilibrium is a state in a reversible reaction where the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction. The concentrations of reactants and products remain constant (but not necessarily equal).\nIt is described as dynamic because both the forward and reverse reactions continue to occur -- molecules are constantly reacting in both directions, but the overall concentrations do not change.\n\n(b)(i) Kc = [CO2][H2] / ([CO][H2O])\n(ii) At equilibrium:\nH2 formed = 0.75 mol, CO2 formed = 0.75 mol (1:1 ratio from equation).\nCO remaining = 3.0 - 0.75 = 2.25 mol.\nH2O remaining = 1.0 - 0.75 = 0.25 mol.\nAll in volume V, so:\nKc = (0.75/V)(0.75/V) / ((2.25/V)(0.25/V)) = (0.75 x 0.75) / (2.25 x 0.25)\n= 0.5625 / 0.5625 = 1.0.\n\n(c) Le Chatelier's principle: If a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change in concentration, pressure, or temperature, the equilibrium will shift in the direction that tends to counteract (oppose) the change.\n\n(d)(i) The forward reaction is exothermic (delta H = -41.2 kJ/mol). Increasing the temperature favours the reverse (endothermic) direction. The yield of CO2 decreases.\n(ii) Since higher temperature shifts the equilibrium to the left (reverse direction), the value of Kc decreases at higher temperature.\n(iii) There are 2 moles of gas on each side of the equation (CO + H2O and CO2 + H2). Therefore, changing the pressure has no effect on the position of equilibrium -- the equilibrium does not shift.\n\n(e)(i) A catalyst increases the rate at which equilibrium is reached (equilibrium is established faster).\n(ii) A catalyst has no effect on the value of Kc. It speeds up both the forward and reverse reactions equally."},
  {num:10, label:"Q10: Bonding, Electrochemistry & Stoichiometry (Answer 2 of 3)", q:"Answer any two of (a), (b) and (c). (2 x 25)\n\n(a) Intermolecular forces and properties:\n(i) State the trend in boiling points of the noble gases from He to Xe. Explain.\n(ii) Explain why the boiling point of H2O is higher than expected based on its molecular mass.\n(iii) Explain what is meant by a polar molecule and give an example.\n(iv) Explain why CO2 is non-polar even though the C=O bonds are polar.\n\n(b) Electrochemistry:\n(i) Define oxidation in terms of electron transfer.\n(ii) In the electrolysis of molten NaCl, write half-equations for the reactions at each electrode.\n(iii) Identify the products at each electrode.\n(iv) Explain why solid NaCl does not conduct electricity but molten NaCl does.\n\n(c) Stoichiometry:\n(i) A sample of pure iron reacted with excess dilute H2SO4: Fe + H2SO4 -> FeSO4 + H2.\nCalculate the volume of H2 gas produced at s.t.p. from 1.12 g of iron.\n(ii) Explain the term limiting reagent.\n(iii) If 0.56 g Fe and 50 cm3 of 0.1 M H2SO4 are mixed, identify the limiting reagent and calculate the volume of H2 at s.t.p.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Boiling points increase from He to Xe. As atomic size increases, the electron cloud is larger and more polarisable, leading to stronger London (van der Waals) dispersion forces between atoms.\n(ii) H2O has an unexpectedly high boiling point because of hydrogen bonding between molecules. The O-H bonds are highly polar, and each water molecule can form up to 4 hydrogen bonds. These are much stronger than the van der Waals forces expected for a molecule of its mass.\n(iii) A polar molecule has an uneven distribution of charge -- it has a permanent dipole (one end is partially positive, the other partially negative). Example: HCl, H2O, NH3.\n(iv) CO2 is a linear molecule. Although each C=O bond is polar (O is more electronegative), the two bond dipoles are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction. They cancel out, so the overall molecule has no net dipole and is non-polar.\n\n(b)(i) Oxidation is the loss of electrons.\n(ii) At cathode (negative electrode): Na+ + e- -> Na (reduction).\nAt anode (positive electrode): 2Cl- -> Cl2 + 2e- (oxidation).\n(iii) Sodium metal at the cathode; chlorine gas at the anode.\n(iv) Solid NaCl: ions are held in a rigid crystal lattice and cannot move, so they cannot carry charge. Molten NaCl: the lattice breaks down, ions are free to move and carry charge, so it conducts.\n\n(c)(i) Mr of Fe = 56. Moles Fe = 1.12 / 56 = 0.02 mol.\nFrom equation, 1 mol Fe produces 1 mol H2.\nVolume H2 at s.t.p. = 0.02 x 22.4 = 0.448 litres (448 cm3).\n(ii) The limiting reagent is the reactant that is completely used up first in a reaction. It determines the maximum amount of product that can be formed.\n(iii) Moles Fe = 0.56 / 56 = 0.01 mol.\nMoles H2SO4 = (50 x 0.1) / 1000 = 0.005 mol.\nFrom equation, 1 mol Fe reacts with 1 mol H2SO4. Fe requires 0.01 mol H2SO4 but only 0.005 mol available.\nH2SO4 is the limiting reagent.\nMoles H2 = 0.005 mol.\nVolume at s.t.p. = 0.005 x 22.4 = 0.112 litres (112 cm3)."},
  {num:11, label:"Q11: Organic Chemistry, Redox & Water (Answer 2 of 3)", q:"Answer any two of (a), (b) and (c). (2 x 25)\n\n(a) Homologous series and functional groups:\n(i) Explain the term homologous series.\n(ii) Give the general formula for the carboxylic acids.\n(iii) Draw the structural formula of ethanoic acid and identify the functional group.\n(iv) Describe a chemical test to distinguish between ethanol and ethanoic acid.\n(v) Write a balanced equation for the reaction of ethanoic acid with sodium carbonate.\n\n(b) Redox reactions and oxidation numbers:\n(i) Assign oxidation numbers to each element in KMnO4.\n(ii) In the reaction: 2KMnO4 + 16HCl -> 2KCl + 2MnCl2 + 5Cl2 + 8H2O, identify the element oxidised and the element reduced.\n(iii) Define a reducing agent.\n(iv) Identify the reducing agent in the equation above.\n\n(c) Water treatment and hardness:\n(i) Explain how temporary hardness in water is caused.\n(ii) How can temporary hardness be removed? Write a balanced equation.\n(iii) Explain the ion-exchange method for softening water.\n(iv) State one advantage and one disadvantage of hard water.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) A homologous series is a group of organic compounds with the same general formula, similar chemical properties, and a gradual variation in physical properties. Each member differs from the next by a CH2 unit.\n(ii) CnH(2n+1)COOH or CnH(2n)O2.\n(iii) Ethanoic acid: CH3COOH. The functional group is the carboxyl group (-COOH).\n(iv) Add sodium carbonate (Na2CO3) or sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO3) to each. Ethanoic acid produces bubbles of gas (CO2), while ethanol does not react. Alternatively, use litmus paper: ethanoic acid turns blue litmus red, ethanol has no effect.\n(v) 2CH3COOH + Na2CO3 -> 2CH3COONa + H2O + CO2.\n\n(b)(i) In KMnO4: K = +1, Mn = +7, O = -2.\n(ii) Mn is reduced: from +7 in KMnO4 to +2 in MnCl2.\nCl is oxidised: from -1 in HCl to 0 in Cl2.\n(iii) A reducing agent is a substance that causes another substance to be reduced, while itself being oxidised. It donates electrons.\n(iv) HCl (specifically the Cl- ion) is the reducing agent.\n\n(c)(i) Temporary hardness is caused by dissolved calcium hydrogencarbonate (Ca(HCO3)2) or magnesium hydrogencarbonate (Mg(HCO3)2) in the water. These form when rainwater (containing dissolved CO2) passes through limestone rock: CaCO3 + CO2 + H2O -> Ca(HCO3)2.\n(ii) Temporary hardness is removed by boiling. On heating, the soluble hydrogencarbonate decomposes: Ca(HCO3)2 -> CaCO3 + H2O + CO2. The insoluble CaCO3 precipitates as limescale.\n(iii) Ion exchange: Hard water is passed through a column containing a resin loaded with sodium (Na+) or hydrogen (H+) ions. As the water passes through, the Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions are exchanged for Na+ (or H+) ions on the resin. The water coming out is softened.\n(iv) Advantage: Hard water provides calcium for healthy teeth and bones; it also gives a pleasant taste.\nDisadvantage: Hard water wastes soap (forms scum with soap instead of a lather); it causes limescale buildup in pipes, kettles and boilers."}
],

  "2019": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Dissolved Oxygen (Winkler Method)", q:"The dissolved oxygen concentration of a water system was determined. A stoppered bottle of water was shaken with a concentrated Mn2+ solution and then with alkaline KI. The dissolved O2 oxidised Mn2+ to Mnx+ ions (brown precipitate). Concentrated H2SO4 was added, and the Mnx+ ions oxidised I- to I2. The I2 was titrated with standard Na2S2O3 using starch indicator.\nI2 + 2S2O3(2-) -> 2I- + S4O6(2-)\nFour moles of S2O3(2-) are required for every one mole of dissolved O2.\n\n(a)(i) Identify the compound usually added to provide the Mn2+ ions.\n(ii) Explain why a large excess of KI was required.\n(iii) What change was observed when the concentrated sulfuric acid was added?\n\n(b)(i) Describe how the conical flask was prepared for a titration and then used during the titration.\n(ii) Give one way of ensuring that the level of liquid in the burette was at eye level.\n\n(c) What colour changes were observed at the titration stage:\n(i) up to the point when the indicator was added\n(ii) at the end point?\n\n(d) The Na2S2O3 solution contained 3.1 g Na2S2O3.5H2O per litre. On average, 6.0 cm3 were required for 200.0 cm3 portions.\nShow by calculation that the dissolved oxygen levels were a threat to fish survival by finding:\n(i) the average number of moles of sodium thiosulfate used\n(ii) the number of moles of O2 that released the I2\n(iii) the concentration of dissolved oxygen in mol/L\n(iv) the concentration of dissolved oxygen in p.p.m. (mg/L).", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) MnSO4.2H2O (manganese(II) sulfate) or MnCl2.4H2O (manganese(II) chloride).\n(ii) To ensure all dissolved O2 is detected: the excess KI ensures that all Mnx+ ions are reduced back, liberating the full amount of I2 corresponding to the O2 present. Also, excess KI keeps the iodine in solution (as KI3/I3-).\n(iii) The brown precipitate dissolved, and a brown/golden/yellow iodine solution formed.\n\n(b)(i) Rinse the conical flask with deionised water (not with the iodine solution). During the titration, swirl the flask to mix the contents. Wash down the walls/sides with deionised water. Place on a white tile for better colour observation.\n(ii) Adjust the height of the clamp stand to bring the burette liquid level to eye level, or bend/crouch down to eye level, or place the burette on a lower surface.\n\n(c)(i) The solution fades from brown/golden to pale yellow (straw-coloured) as thiosulfate is added. Starch indicator is added when the colour is pale yellow.\n(ii) At the end point: blue-black to colourless (starch-iodine complex decolourises).\n\n(d)(i) Mr of Na2S2O3.5H2O = 248.\nConcentration = 3.1 / 248 = 0.0125 M.\nMoles thiosulfate = (6.0 x 0.0125) / 1000 = 7.5 x 10^-5 mol.\n(ii) S2O3(2-) : O2 = 4 : 1.\nMoles O2 = 7.5 x 10^-5 / 4 = 1.875 x 10^-5 mol (in 200.0 cm3).\n(iii) Concentration = 1.875 x 10^-5 x (1000/200) = 9.375 x 10^-5 mol/L.\n(iv) Mass per litre = 9.375 x 10^-5 x 32 = 0.003 g/L = 3.0 mg/L = 3.0 p.p.m.\nSince 3.0 p.p.m. < 5 p.p.m. (the minimum for a varied fish population), the dissolved oxygen level is a threat to fish survival."},
    {num:2, label:"Q2: Organic Tests & Preparation of Ethene", q:"Ethanoic acid, ethanal and ethanol are three colourless organic liquids at 18 degrees C.\n\n(a) The student placed magnesium ribbon into ethanoic acid solution.\n(i) What was observed?\n(ii) Write formulae for both products.\n\n(b) Describe a chemical test to show that ethanal can be very easily oxidised.\n\n(c) Preparation of ethene from ethanol: C2H5OH -> C2H4 + H2O\n(i) Draw a diagram of the apparatus for preparation and collection of ethene.\n(ii) Identify the catalyst and describe its appearance.\n(iii) Explain the risk of hot glassware shattering due to rapid cooling. How can this risk be minimised?\n(iv) Calculate the volume of ethene gas produced in 26% yield from 2.9 cm3 of ethanol (density 0.8 g/cm3) at room temperature and pressure.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Bubbling (fizzing/effervescence) as hydrogen gas is produced. The magnesium ribbon dissolves.\n(ii) Products: H2 (hydrogen gas) and (CH3COO)2Mg (magnesium ethanoate / magnesium acetate).\n\n(b) Add Tollens' reagent (ammoniacal silver nitrate) to ethanal and heat gently in a water bath. A silver mirror (or silver precipitate) forms on the walls of the test tube, showing the aldehyde has been oxidised.\nAlternatively: Add Fehling's reagent (Cu2+ solution) and heat gently. A brick-red precipitate (Cu2O) forms.\nAlternatively: Add acidified dilute KMnO4 -- it decolourises from purple to colourless.\n\n(c)(i) Diagram: Horizontal (or slightly slanting) test tube containing glass wool soaked in ethanol. Aluminium oxide (Al2O3) catalyst placed near the centre of the tube. Heat source (Bunsen burner) positioned under the catalyst. Delivery tube leads from the test tube to collection of ethene gas over water.\n(ii) Catalyst: aluminium oxide (Al2O3 / alumina). Appearance: white powder/solid.\n(iii) Risk: a suck-back can occur. When heating stops, the gas inside the tube cools and contracts, creating a partial vacuum. Cold water can be sucked back into the hot test tube, causing thermal shock and shattering.\nMinimise: Remove the delivery tube from the water before reducing or removing the heat source. Or loosen the stopper before removing heat.\n(iv) Mass of ethanol = density x volume = 0.8 x 2.9 = 2.32 g.\nMoles ethanol = 2.32 / 46 = 0.0504 mol.\nAt 100% yield, volume of ethene = 0.0504 x 24,000 = 1209.6 cm3.\nAt 26% yield: 1209.6 x 0.26 = 314.5 cm3 (accept 300-315 cm3)."},
    {num:3, label:"Q3: Relative Molecular Mass of a Volatile Liquid", q:"To determine the relative molecular mass (Mr) of compound X, a volatile liquid, a small volume was vaporised. Atmospheric pressure was obtained using a smartphone barometric sensor.\n\n(a) Draw a large, labelled diagram of the apparatus showing:\n(i) how X was vaporised\n(ii) how the temperature of the vapour was obtained.\n\n(b) With reference to your diagram, explain:\n(i) why the pressure of the vapour at the end of the heating stage was equal to atmospheric pressure\n(ii) how the volume of the vapour was measured.\n\n(c) Describe how the mass of the vapour in the container at the end of the heating stage was found.\n\n(d) Calculate, correct to the nearest whole number, the relative molecular mass of X, given that 0.22 g of X occupied a volume of 76 cm3 at a pressure of 1.011 x 10^5 Pa and a temperature of 99 degrees C.\n\n(e) Bromine (Mr = 160) has a significantly greater relative molecular mass than water (Mr = 18). Account for the volatility of bromine (b.p. 58.8 C) compared to that of water (b.p. 100 C).", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Flask-in-beaker method:\nDiagram: A conical flask (approximately three-quarters immersed) in a beaker of water on a hotplate. The flask is sealed with aluminium foil secured by a rubber band, with a pinhole in the foil. A thermometer is placed in the water in the beaker.\nOR Gas syringe method:\nA gas syringe in an oven/steam jacket, with a hypodermic syringe used to inject the liquid through a rubber seal. Thermometer in the oven.\n\n(b)(i) Flask method: Some vapour escapes through the pinhole, so the pressure inside the flask equals atmospheric pressure.\nGas syringe method: The plunger of the gas syringe is free to move, so the gas inside is always at atmospheric pressure.\n(ii) Flask method: The volume of the flask is found by filling it completely with water and measuring the volume of water using a graduated cylinder.\nGas syringe method: Read the scale on the gas syringe -- subtract the initial reading from the final reading.\n\n(c) Flask method: Subtract the mass of the empty flask (with foil and rubber band) from the mass of the flask (with foil, rubber band and condensed vapour) after cooling.\nGas syringe method: Subtract the mass of the hypodermic syringe after injection from its mass before injection.\n\n(d) Using pV = nRT:\nT = 99 + 273 = 372 K.\nn = pV / RT = (1.011 x 10^5 x 76 x 10^-6) / (8.3 x 372)\n= 7.6836 / 3087.6\n= 2.49 x 10^-3 mol.\nMr = mass / moles = 0.22 / 2.49 x 10^-3 = 88 (accept 88 or 89).\n\nAlternatively using gas law to find volume at STP:\npV/T = p'V'/T' -> V' at STP = (1.011 x 10^5 x 76 x 273) / (1.013 x 10^5 x 372) = 55.7 cm3.\nn = 55.7 / 22400 = 2.49 x 10^-3 mol. Mr = 0.22 / 2.49 x 10^-3 = 88.\n\n(e) Bromine (Br2) is non-polar (pure covalent bond between two identical atoms). The only intermolecular forces are weak van der Waals (London dispersion) forces.\nWater (H2O) is polar and has strong hydrogen bonds between molecules (due to the high electronegativity of oxygen and its lone pairs).\nAlthough bromine has a much larger molecular mass, its intermolecular forces are much weaker than water's hydrogen bonds. Therefore bromine is more volatile (lower boiling point) despite its greater mass."},
    {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions (Mixed Topics)", q:"Answer eight of the following:\n(a) What are cathode rays?\n(b) Potassium-40 is radioactive. What is the daughter nucleus when K-40 emits an electron in beta decay?\n(c) State Avogadro's law.\n(d) What is the oxidation number of sulfur in (i) sulfur dioxide (SO2), (ii) the sulfate ion (SO4(2-))?\n(e) Give two differences between a sigma and a pi covalent bond.\n(f) In the electrolysis of aqueous KI with inert electrodes, one half-equation is: 2I-(aq) -> I2(aq) + 2e-. Write a balanced half-equation for the reaction at the negative electrode.\n(g) Define bond energy.\n(h) What is the principle of the separation of components in a mixture using any type of chromatography?\n(i) Ibuprofen structure shown. A single 5 cm3 dose contains 4.86 x 10^-4 moles of ibuprofen (Mr = 206). How many milligrams are in this dose?\n(j) Write a balanced equation for the reaction between limestone and carbonic acid that gives rise to temporary hardness.\n(k) A: Why is nitrogen gas unreactive? OR B: Draw two repeating units of the polymer poly(propene).", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Cathode rays are beams (streams) of electrons emitted from the cathode in a discharge tube under low pressure.\n\n(b) K-40 (19 protons) emits a beta particle (electron): a neutron becomes a proton.\nDaughter nucleus: calcium-40 (Ca-40), with 20 protons and 20 neutrons.\n\n(c) Equal volumes of all gases, at the same temperature and pressure, contain the same number of molecules (particles).\n\n(d)(i) In SO2: S has oxidation number +4.\n(ii) In SO4(2-): S has oxidation number +6.\n\n(e) (1) A sigma bond is formed by head-on (end-to-end) overlap of orbitals along the internuclear axis; a pi bond is formed by sideways (lateral) overlap of p orbitals above and below the internuclear axis.\n(2) A sigma bond is stronger than a pi bond. A sigma bond has a higher electron density between the nuclei. A sigma bond can rotate freely; a pi bond restricts rotation.\n\n(f) At the negative electrode (cathode): 2H2O(l) + 2e- -> H2(g) + 2OH-(aq).\n(Hydrogen gas is produced and the solution becomes alkaline around the cathode.)\n\n(g) Bond energy is the average amount of energy (in kJ/mol) required to break one mole of a particular type of covalent bond in gaseous molecules, measured under standard conditions.\n\n(h) The principle of chromatography is the differential distribution (partitioning) of the components of a mixture between a mobile phase and a stationary phase. Components with greater affinity for the stationary phase move more slowly, causing separation.\n\n(i) Mass = moles x Mr = 4.86 x 10^-4 x 206 = 0.10008 g = 100.08 mg (accept 100 mg).\n\n(j) CaCO3 + H2CO3 -> Ca(HCO3)2.\n(Limestone reacts with carbonic acid in rainwater to form soluble calcium hydrogencarbonate, which causes temporary hardness.)\n\n(k) A: Nitrogen gas (N2) is unreactive because of the very strong triple bond (N≡N) between the two nitrogen atoms. The bond energy of the N≡N bond is 945 kJ/mol, making it very difficult to break.\nB: Poly(propene): -[-CH2-CH(CH3)-]-[-CH2-CH(CH3)-]- showing two repeating units with the methyl (CH3) side group on alternating carbon atoms."},
    {num:5, label:"Q5: Periodic Table (History, Trends & Reactivity)", q:"2019 was designated the International Year of the Periodic Table.\n\n(a)(i) What is meant by the periodic system in the context of Mendeleev's 1869 periodic table?\n(ii) Comment on the positioning of tellurium (Te) and iodine (I) in the 1869 table.\n(iii) Why did the 1869 table not include any noble gases?\n\n(b) Modern periodic tables arrange elements in order of atomic number, not relative atomic mass.\n(i) Define the underlined terms (atomic number and relative atomic mass).\n(ii) Give an advantage of arranging elements in order of atomic number.\n\n(c) Explain:\n(i) why all elements of Group 18 are chemically inert\n(ii) how and why the reactivity of the halogens changes down Group 17.\n\n(d) Francium (Fr), atomic number 87, was discovered by Marguerite Perey in 1939.\n(i) How would you expect a small sample of francium to react in water? Justify.\n(ii) Predict the products of this reaction.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) The periodic system refers to the observation that when elements are arranged in order of increasing atomic weight (as Mendeleev did), elements with similar chemical properties recur at regular (periodic) intervals.\n(ii) Mendeleev placed tellurium (atomic weight 127.6) before iodine (atomic weight 126.9), even though this reversed the order of atomic weights. He did this to ensure that each element was placed in a group matching its chemical properties (Te with the Group 6 elements, I with the Group 7 elements). This showed that chemical properties were more important than strict atomic weight order.\n(iii) The noble gases had not yet been discovered in 1869. They are very unreactive and do not form compounds easily, so they were not identified until the 1890s (argon was discovered by Rayleigh and Ramsay in 1894).\n\n(b)(i) Atomic number: the number of protons in the nucleus of an atom of an element.\nRelative atomic mass: the weighted average of the masses of the naturally occurring isotopes of an element, on a scale where carbon-12 has a mass of exactly 12.\n(ii) Arranging by atomic number avoids the anomalies caused by atomic mass ordering (e.g. Te/I, Ar/K, Co/Ni), giving a consistent arrangement where all elements fall correctly into groups matching their properties.\n\n(c)(i) All Group 18 elements have full outer electron shells (complete octets, or full s2 for helium). This is a very stable configuration, so they have no tendency to gain, lose, or share electrons -- hence they are chemically inert.\n(ii) The reactivity of halogens decreases down Group 17. As you go down the group, the atomic radius increases and there are more electron shells causing greater shielding. The ability of the atom to attract an additional electron (to complete its outer shell) decreases because the outer shell is further from the nucleus. Therefore, fluorine is the most reactive halogen and iodine/astatine is the least reactive.\n\n(d)(i) Francium would react very vigorously (explosively) with water. It is in Group 1 (alkali metals), and reactivity increases dramatically down the group. Francium has the largest atomic radius and lowest ionisation energy of all alkali metals, so it loses its outer electron extremely easily.\n(ii) Products: francium hydroxide (FrOH) and hydrogen gas (H2).\n2Fr + 2H2O -> 2FrOH + H2."},
    {num:6, label:"Q6: Structural Isomers, Octane Numbers & Thermochemistry", q:"(a) Compounds A, B and C have the same molecular formula but different structural formulae.\n(i) What term is used to describe compounds like A, B and C?\n(ii) Why do A, B and C separate in the same fraction in the distillation of crude oil?\n(iii) Explain which of A, B and C is most likely to auto-ignite in a petrol engine.\n(iv) Give the systematic IUPAC names for A, B and C.\n\n(b)(i) Name the oil refining process in which one molecule of alkane W was converted into one molecule of octane and two propene molecules.\n(ii) Deduce the formula of W.\n\n(c) Tetraethyllead is a petrol additive, now used only in some aeroplane engines.\n(i) What is the advantage of adding tetraethyllead to petrol?\n(ii) Why was its use in car engines discontinued?\n(iii) Calculate the heat of reaction for the combustion of one mole of tetraethyllead:\n2(C2H5)4Pb(l) + 27O2(g) -> 16CO2(g) + 20H2O(l) + 2PbO(s)\nHeats of formation: (C2H5)4Pb = 52.7, CO2 = -393.5, H2O = -285.8, PbO = -219.0 kJ/mol.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Structural isomers (they have the same molecular formula but different structural formulae).\n(ii) They have similar boiling points because they have the same molecular formula (same molecular mass and similar intermolecular forces), so they condense at the same level in the fractionating column.\n(iii) The straight-chain isomer (A, pentane) is most likely to auto-ignite. Straight-chain alkanes have lower octane numbers than branched-chain isomers because they burn less smoothly and are more prone to pre-ignition (knocking). The most branched isomer (C, 2,2-dimethylpropane) is least likely to auto-ignite.\n(iv) A: pentane (CH3CH2CH2CH2CH3).\nB: 2-methylbutane (CH3CH(CH3)CH2CH3).\nC: 2,2-dimethylpropane ((CH3)4C).\n\n(b)(i) Cracking (thermal or catalytic cracking).\n(ii) W -> octane (C8H18) + 2 propene (2 x C3H6 = C6H12).\nFormula of W: C8H18 + C6H12 = C14H30.\n\n(c)(i) It increases the octane number of the petrol, reducing engine knock (pre-ignition).\n(ii) Lead is a toxic heavy metal. The combustion products (lead compounds like PbO) are released into the atmosphere, causing lead pollution and poisoning. Lead also damages catalytic converters in car exhaust systems.\n(iii) For 2 moles of (C2H5)4Pb:\ndelta H = [16(-393.5) + 20(-285.8) + 2(-219.0)] - [2(52.7) + 27(0)]\n= [-6296.0 + (-5716.0) + (-438.0)] - [105.4]\n= -12450.0 - 105.4\n= -12555.4 kJ for 2 moles.\nFor 1 mole: -12555.4 / 2 = -6277.7 kJ/mol (accept -6278 kJ/mol)."},
    {num:7, label:"Q7: Acids, Bases & Ionic Product of Water", q:"(a) Use equations to show that when dissolved in water:\n(i) HCl acts as a Bronsted-Lowry acid\n(ii) NH3 acts as a Bronsted-Lowry base.\nExplain why:\n(iii) HCl has a weak conjugate base\n(iv) NH3 has a strong conjugate acid.\n\n(b) Pure water self-ionises: 2H2O(l) <=> H3O+(aq) + OH-(aq).\nKw values at different temperatures given: 0 C (0.1 x 10^-14), 10 C (0.3 x 10^-14), 20 C (0.7 x 10^-14), 30 C (1.4 x 10^-14), 40 C (2.9 x 10^-14), 50 C (5.3 x 10^-14), 60 C (9.3 x 10^-14).\n(i) Write the Kw expression for water.\n(ii) Are the data consistent with self-ionisation being exothermic or endothermic? Justify.\n(iii) Plot a graph of Kw versus temperature.\n(iv) Use your graph to predict the value of Kw at body temperature, 37 C.\n(v) Use this value to calculate the H3O+ concentration of pure water at 37 C.\n(vi) The pH of pure water is close to 6.77 at one of the temperatures in the table. Find, by calculation, this temperature.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) HCl + H2O -> H3O+ + Cl-.\nHCl donates a proton (H+) to water, so it acts as a Bronsted-Lowry acid.\n(ii) NH3 + H2O -> NH4+ + OH-.\nNH3 accepts a proton from water, so it acts as a Bronsted-Lowry base.\n(iii) HCl is a strong acid -- it dissociates fully, meaning the reverse reaction barely occurs. Therefore its conjugate base (Cl-) has virtually no tendency to accept a proton, making it a very weak base.\n(iv) NH3 is a weak base -- it only partially accepts protons, so its conjugate acid (NH4+) has a strong tendency to donate a proton (re-establish the equilibrium). Therefore NH4+ is a relatively strong conjugate acid.\n\n(b)(i) Kw = [H3O+][OH-].\n(ii) Endothermic. As temperature increases, Kw increases (more dissociation). By Le Chatelier's principle, if increasing temperature shifts the equilibrium towards more products (more ions), then the forward reaction is endothermic.\n(iii) Plot Kw on y-axis (0 to 10 x 10^-14) against temperature on x-axis (0 to 60 degrees C). The curve rises steeply, showing an exponential-type increase.\n(iv) From the graph at 37 C: Kw is approximately 2.4 x 10^-14 (accept 2.0-2.5 x 10^-14).\n(v) In pure water, [H3O+] = [OH-], so [H3O+]^2 = Kw.\n[H3O+] = sqrt(2.4 x 10^-14) = 1.55 x 10^-7 M (accept 1.4-1.6 x 10^-7 M).\n(vi) pH = 6.77, so [H+] = 10^-6.77 = 1.698 x 10^-7 M.\nKw = [H+]^2 = (1.698 x 10^-7)^2 = 2.88 x 10^-14.\nFrom the table, Kw = 2.9 x 10^-14 corresponds to 40 degrees C."},
    {num:8, label:"Q8: Alcohols, Oxidation & Esters", q:"The table shows the boiling points of four primary alcohols and two secondary alcohols.\n\n(a)(i) What is a secondary alcohol?\n(ii) Plot the boiling points of these alcohols against the number of carbon atoms.\n(iii) State and explain the trend in boiling points of the four primary alcohols as their relative molecular masses increase.\n(iv) Predict the approximate boiling point of the next alcohol in the same series as propan-2-ol and butan-2-ol.\n\n(b) Consider the oxidation of alcohols in which no carbon-carbon bonds are broken.\n(i) Give the IUPAC names for the two possible organic products of such an oxidation of butan-1-ol.\n(ii) Draw the structure of the organic product when butan-2-ol is oxidised in this way.\n(iii) Identify clearly which bonds in butan-2-ol are broken in this oxidation.\n\n(c) The ester formed from methanol and propanoic acid is found in many fruits.\n(i) Draw the structure of this ester.\n(ii) How many carbon atoms in a molecule of this ester are tetrahedrally bonded?\n(iii) What are the products of the hydrolysis of this ester by NaOH?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) A secondary alcohol is one in which the carbon atom bearing the -OH group is directly bonded to two other carbon atoms.\n(ii) Plot: x-axis = number of carbon atoms, y-axis = boiling point (degrees C). Two series of points are plotted: primary alcohols form one line, secondary alcohols form another (slightly lower) parallel line.\n(iii) The boiling points of the primary alcohols increase as the relative molecular mass increases. Each additional CH2 group increases the molecular mass and the strength of van der Waals (London dispersion) forces between molecules. More energy is required to separate the larger molecules, so the boiling point rises.\n(iv) The next secondary alcohol after butan-2-ol would be pentan-2-ol (C5). From the graph, its boiling point would be approximately 118-120 degrees C (extrapolating the secondary alcohol trend line).\n\n(b)(i) Mild oxidation of butan-1-ol gives butanal (an aldehyde).\nFurther oxidation gives butanoic acid (a carboxylic acid).\n(ii) Butan-2-ol oxidises to butanone (CH3COCH2CH3). Draw: CH3-CO-CH2-CH3 showing the C=O double bond.\n(iii) The O-H bond and one C-H bond on the carbon bearing the -OH group are broken. These two hydrogen atoms are removed (along with their bonding electrons) as the C=O double bond forms.\n\n(c)(i) Methyl propanoate: CH3CH2-COO-CH3. Draw showing the ester linkage (-COO-).\n(ii) Two carbon atoms are tetrahedrally bonded: the CH3 of the methyl group and the CH2 of the propanoic acid chain. The carbonyl carbon (C=O) is trigonal planar.\n(iii) Hydrolysis by NaOH: sodium propanoate (CH3CH2COONa) and methanol (CH3OH). This is saponification (base hydrolysis) of the ester."},
    {num:9, label:"Q9: Rates of Reaction & Catalysis", q:"2A(g) -> B(g) + 3C(g), heterogeneously catalysed by X at temperature T.\nThe graph shows that 0.0024 mol/L of A decomposes to produce 0.0036 mol/L of C over 3000 seconds.\n\n(a) Define rate of reaction.\n\n(b) Use the graph to find the average rate of formation of C, in M/s, over the first 500 s. How does the instantaneous rate at 500 s compare with this average rate? Justify.\n\n(c)(i) Give the expression for the average rate of decomposition of A in terms of the average rate of formation of C.\n(ii) Calculate the average rate of decomposition of A over the entire 3000 s.\n\n(d)(i) Identify one feature of the graph that indicates that X is a heterogeneous catalyst.\n(ii) If the mass of catalyst X was halved, sketch on the graph the curve expected.\n(iii) How would the initial rate of reaction change if the temperature was increased?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Rate of reaction is the change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time.\n\n(b) From the graph, at t = 500 s, [C] is approximately 0.0018 M (read from graph).\nAverage rate of formation of C = 0.0018 / 500 = 3.6 x 10^-6 M/s.\nThe instantaneous rate at 500 s is less than this average rate. Justification: the graph of [C] vs time is a curve that becomes less steep over time (rate decreases as reactant A is used up). The average rate over 0-500 s includes the steeper early portion, so it is higher than the actual rate at t = 500 s.\n\n(c)(i) From the stoichiometry 2A -> 3C:\nRate of decomposition of A = (2/3) x rate of formation of C.\n(ii) Average rate of formation of C over 3000 s = 0.0036 / 3000 = 1.2 x 10^-6 M/s.\nAverage rate of decomposition of A = (2/3) x 1.2 x 10^-6 = 8.0 x 10^-7 M/s.\n(Or directly: 0.0024 / 3000 = 8.0 x 10^-7 M/s.)\n\n(d)(i) Features indicating heterogeneous catalysis: the graph may show an initial induction period or the rate depends on the surface area of the solid catalyst (X is in a different phase from the gaseous reactants).\n(ii) With half the mass of catalyst: the curve would have the same shape and reach the same final concentration of C (0.0036 M), but it would rise more slowly (less steep initially). Less catalyst means less surface area for adsorption, so the reaction is slower but the yield is unchanged.\n(iii) If temperature was increased, the initial rate of reaction would increase. Higher temperature means the molecules have greater kinetic energy, leading to more frequent and more energetic collisions, so more molecules exceed the activation energy."},
    {num:10, label:"Q10: Bonding, Structure & Electrochemistry", q:"Answer any two of parts (a), (b) and (c).\n\n(a) Intermolecular forces and solubility:\n(i) Define electronegativity.\n(ii) Explain, using electronegativity, why the O-H bond in water is polar.\n(iii) Explain what is meant by hydrogen bonding and give one effect it has on a physical property of water.\n(iv) Explain why ethanol is miscible with water but hexane is not.\n\n(b) Shapes of molecules:\n(i) State and explain the shape of BF3.\n(ii) State and explain the shape of NF3.\n(iii) Explain why BF3 is non-polar but NF3 is polar.\n\n(c) Electrochemistry:\n(i) Define electrolysis.\n(ii) In the electrolysis of molten NaCl, write half-equations for the reactions at each electrode.\n(iii) State the products and explain why different products are obtained when aqueous NaCl is electrolysed.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Electronegativity is a measure of the relative attraction of an atom in a molecule for a shared pair of electrons in a covalent bond.\n(ii) Oxygen has a much higher electronegativity (3.5) than hydrogen (2.1). In the O-H bond, the shared electrons are pulled closer to the oxygen atom, giving it a partial negative charge and the hydrogen a partial positive charge, making the bond polar.\n(iii) Hydrogen bonding is a strong type of intermolecular force that occurs when a hydrogen atom bonded to a highly electronegative atom (F, O, or N) is attracted to a lone pair on a nearby F, O, or N atom on another molecule.\nEffect: Water has an unusually high boiling point for its molecular mass (100 degrees C), because considerable energy is needed to break the hydrogen bonds between molecules.\n(iv) Ethanol is miscible with water because the -OH group in ethanol can form hydrogen bonds with water molecules. Hexane is non-polar and cannot form hydrogen bonds with water -- the strong water-water hydrogen bonds exclude the hexane molecules (like dissolves like).\n\n(b)(i) BF3 is trigonal planar (120 degree bond angles). Boron has 3 bonding pairs and no lone pairs on the central atom. The 3 bonding pairs repel equally, arranging themselves at 120 degrees.\n(ii) NF3 is pyramidal (trigonal pyramidal, approximately 107 degree bond angles). Nitrogen has 3 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair. The lone pair repels more strongly, compressing the bond angles below the tetrahedral 109.5 degrees.\n(iii) BF3 is non-polar because it is symmetrical (trigonal planar) -- the three equal B-F bond dipoles cancel out, giving zero net dipole. NF3 is polar because the pyramidal shape means the three N-F bond dipoles do not cancel. The lone pair on nitrogen also contributes to the overall dipole moment, giving a net dipole.\n\n(c)(i) Electrolysis is the decomposition of a compound (in the molten state or in aqueous solution) by the passage of an electric current through it.\n(ii) Molten NaCl:\nCathode (negative): Na+ + e- -> Na (sodium metal deposited).\nAnode (positive): 2Cl- -> Cl2 + 2e- (chlorine gas released).\n(iii) Aqueous NaCl:\nCathode: 2H2O + 2e- -> H2 + 2OH- (hydrogen gas, not sodium, because water is more easily reduced than Na+ ions).\nAnode: 2Cl- -> Cl2 + 2e- (chlorine gas -- Cl- is preferentially oxidised over water when the concentration is high enough).\nThe difference is that in aqueous solution, water molecules compete at the electrodes. At the cathode, H2O is reduced instead of Na+ because sodium has a very negative electrode potential."},
    {num:11, label:"Q11: Organic Synthesis, Redox & Stoichiometry", q:"Answer any two of parts (a), (b) and (c).\n\n(a) Organic synthesis:\n(i) Name the functional group present in ethanol, ethanal and ethanoic acid.\n(ii) Place ethanol, ethanal and ethanoic acid in order of increasing boiling point and explain.\n(iii) Write the balanced equation for the esterification of ethanol with ethanoic acid. Name the ester.\n(iv) State two conditions needed for esterification.\n\n(b) Redox and stoichiometry:\n(i) Define reduction in terms of oxidation numbers.\n(ii) In the reaction: 2KMnO4 + 5H2C2O4 + 3H2SO4 -> 2MnSO4 + K2SO4 + 10CO2 + 8H2O, identify the oxidising agent and the reducing agent.\n(iii) Calculate the mass of KMnO4 needed to react with 3.15 g of oxalic acid (H2C2O4).\n\n(c) Gas laws:\n(i) State Boyle's law.\n(ii) A gas occupies 500 cm3 at 25 degrees C and 1.0 x 10^5 Pa. Calculate its volume at 100 degrees C and 2.0 x 10^5 Pa.\n(iii) Explain why Boyle's law breaks down at high pressures.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Ethanol: hydroxyl group (-OH). Ethanal: aldehyde group (-CHO / carbonyl). Ethanoic acid: carboxyl group (-COOH).\n(ii) Order of increasing boiling point: ethanal (20 degrees C) < ethanol (78 degrees C) < ethanoic acid (118 degrees C).\nEthanal has dipole-dipole interactions but cannot form hydrogen bonds as a donor (no O-H). Ethanol can form hydrogen bonds via its O-H group. Ethanoic acid can dimerise through two hydrogen bonds between -COOH groups, giving the strongest intermolecular forces and the highest boiling point.\n(iii) CH3COOH + C2H5OH <=> CH3COOC2H5 + H2O.\nThe ester is ethyl ethanoate (ethyl acetate).\n(iv) Conditions: (1) An acid catalyst (concentrated sulfuric acid). (2) Heat (reflux).\n\n(b)(i) Reduction is a decrease in oxidation number.\n(ii) Oxidising agent: KMnO4 (Mn goes from +7 to +2 -- it is reduced, so it is the oxidising agent).\nReducing agent: H2C2O4 (C goes from +3 to +4 in CO2 -- it is oxidised, so it is the reducing agent).\n(iii) Mr of H2C2O4 = 90. Moles = 3.15 / 90 = 0.035 mol.\nFrom the equation: 2 mol KMnO4 reacts with 5 mol H2C2O4.\nMoles KMnO4 = (2/5) x 0.035 = 0.014 mol.\nMr of KMnO4 = 158. Mass = 0.014 x 158 = 2.212 g (accept 2.21 g).\n\n(c)(i) Boyle's law: At constant temperature, the volume of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its pressure. (pV = constant, or p1V1 = p2V2.)\n(ii) Using the combined gas law: p1V1/T1 = p2V2/T2.\nT1 = 25 + 273 = 298 K. T2 = 100 + 273 = 373 K.\n(1.0 x 10^5 x 500) / 298 = (2.0 x 10^5 x V2) / 373.\nV2 = (1.0 x 10^5 x 500 x 373) / (298 x 2.0 x 10^5) = 313 cm3 (accept 312-314 cm3).\n(iii) Boyle's law breaks down at high pressures because the gas molecules are forced close together. At high pressure, the volume of the gas molecules themselves becomes significant compared to the total volume (the ideal gas assumption of zero molecular volume fails). Also, intermolecular forces become significant when molecules are close together (the assumption of no intermolecular forces fails)."},
],

  "2018": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Water Hardness (edta Titration)", q:"The total hardness in a water supply was estimated by titrating 50.0 cm3 samples of the water with a standard solution of edta.\nM2+ + H2Y2- -> MY2- + 2H+\nThe average volume of 0.010 M edta solution required to react with 50.0 cm3 of the water was 9.3 cm3.\n\n(a) Identify a compound of calcium that is one of the main causes of permanent hardness in water.\n\n(b) Describe the procedure used to measure out 50.0 cm3 of the hard water from a beaker into a conical flask.\n\n(c)(i) Name the indicator added to the conical flask.\n(ii) What colour was observed in the presence of M2+?\n(iii) What colour was observed at the end point?\n\n(d)(i) Identify the solution added to the water samples before commencing the titrations.\n(ii) Why was this solution added?\n\n(e) Calculate:\n(i) the average number of moles of edta used in the titrations\n(ii) the number of moles of M2+ ion in 50.0 cm3 of hard water\n(iii) the total hardness of the water expressed in grams per litre of CaCO3\n(iv) the total hardness of the water expressed as p.p.m. (mg/L) of CaCO3.\n\n(f) Suggest a way to determine if this water supply contained temporary hardness.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Calcium sulfate (CaSO4) or calcium chloride (CaCl2). These are soluble calcium compounds that cause permanent hardness (not removed by boiling).\n\n(b) Use a 25 cm3 pipette twice.\nRinse the pipette first with deionised water, then with the hard water sample.\nFill the pipette using a pipette filler. Ensure the bottom of the meniscus is on the 25.0 cm3 mark, reading at eye level.\nAllow the pipette to drain by gravity into the conical flask. Touch the tip against the wall of the flask but do not blow out the last drop.\nRepeat to deliver a total of 50.0 cm3.\n\n(c)(i) Solochrome Black (Eriochrome Black T).\n(ii) Wine-red (pink/red) in the presence of M2+ ions.\n(iii) Blue at the end point.\n\n(d)(i) A pH 10 buffer solution (ammoniacal buffer).\n(ii) To maintain the pH at about 10, ensuring the indicator works properly and that all Ca2+/Mg2+ ions react with edta, giving a sharp, accurate end point.\n\n(e)(i) Moles edta = (9.3 x 0.010) / 1000 = 9.3 x 10^-5 mol.\n(ii) M2+ : edta = 1 : 1 (from the equation), so moles M2+ = 9.3 x 10^-5 mol in 50.0 cm3.\n(iii) Moles M2+ per litre = 9.3 x 10^-5 x 20 = 1.86 x 10^-3 mol/L.\nMr of CaCO3 = 100.\nTotal hardness = 1.86 x 10^-3 x 100 = 0.186 g/L (accept 0.19 g/L).\n(iv) 0.186 g/L = 0.186 x 1000 = 186 p.p.m. (accept 190 p.p.m.).\n\n(f) Boil a sample of the water, then filter. Repeat the titration with the boiled and filtered water.\nIf a lower titre is obtained, temporary hardness was present (boiling decomposes the hydrogen carbonates causing temporary hardness, removing some M2+ ions from solution)."},
    {num:2, label:"Q2: Soap Preparation, Liquid-Liquid Extraction & Recrystallisation", q:"(a) Diagram A shows an arrangement used to reflux the reaction mixture in an experiment to prepare a sample of soap.\n(i) Name the piece of glassware labelled X.\n(ii) What is the purpose of refluxing a reaction mixture?\n(iii) Identify the two reactants and the solvent initially present in the flask.\n\n(b) Diagram B shows a separating funnel in use in a liquid-liquid extraction of clove oil from the emulsion produced in a steam distillation of cloves.\n(i) What is an emulsion?\n(ii) Name a suitable organic solvent for use in the liquid-liquid extraction of clove oil.\n(iii) State one safety precaution that should be taken when using a separating funnel.\n(iv) How is the clove oil isolated following the liquid-liquid extraction?\n(v) Steam distillation and liquid-liquid extraction were used to isolate 0.15 cm3 of clove oil (density 1.05 g/cm3) from 5.0 g of cloves. What was the percentage yield, by mass, of clove oil?\n\n(c) A sample of benzoic acid, containing a small quantity of sodium chloride as the only impurity, was purified by recrystallisation.\n(i) Why was there no need, in this recrystallisation, to carry out a filtration of the hot solution?\n(ii) State one advantage of suction filtration over gravity filtration.\n(iii) Explain how you could verify that the recrystallised benzoic acid was purer than the original sample.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Condenser (Liebig condenser).\n(ii) To heat the reaction mixture to keep it boiling and speed up the reaction (help it go to completion), while condensing the solvent vapour so it returns to the flask -- preventing loss of volatile solvent.\n(iii) Reactants: a fat (e.g. glyceryl tristearate / lard / vegetable oil) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH).\nSolvent: ethanol.\n\n(b)(i) An emulsion is a fine dispersion of one liquid in another liquid in which it is immiscible (e.g. tiny oil droplets suspended in water).\n(ii) Cyclohexane, or dichloromethane, or diethyl ether, or ethyl ethanoate.\n(iii) Release pressure build-up regularly by opening the tap while inverted; or work in a fume cupboard; or keep the stopper firmly in place while shaking; or avoid pointing the nozzle at anyone.\n(iv) Evaporate (distil off) the organic solvent from the extract. The clove oil remains.\n(v) Mass of clove oil = density x volume = 1.05 x 0.15 = 0.1575 g.\nPercentage yield = (0.1575 / 5.0) x 100 = 3.15% (accept 3.15-3.2%).\n\n(c)(i) There were no insoluble impurities present. The only impurity (NaCl) is soluble in water, so hot filtration was unnecessary.\n(ii) Suction filtration is faster and helps to dry the crystals.\n(iii) Measure and compare the melting points of both samples. The recrystallised sample should have a higher melting point and a sharper (narrower) melting point range, closer to the expected value for pure benzoic acid (122 degrees C)."},
    {num:3, label:"Q3: Heat of Reaction (Neutralisation)", q:"A cardboard cup with plastic lid contained 150 cm3 of 1.0 M HCl at room temperature. 160 cm3 of 1.0 M NaOH was added quickly, the mixture was stirred, and the temperature rose by 6.4 K before falling gradually.\nHCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) -> NaCl(aq) + H2O(l)\n\n(a) Is this reaction exothermic or endothermic? Justify your answer.\n\n(b)(i) Why should the mixture be stirred before taking temperature readings?\n(ii) Suggest a reason why the temperature, having risen, then began to fall gradually.\n(iii) The thermometer was accurate to 0.1 K. What temperature rise would have been recorded if the thermometer was accurate to 1 K?\n\n(c)(i) Why is it advantageous to use moderately concentrated solutions instead of dilute solutions?\n(ii) Draw or describe the hazard warning pictogram for 1.0 M NaOH.\n\n(d) Calculate:\n(i) the number of moles of HCl neutralised\n(ii) the heat produced by the reaction mixture (total mass 310 g), taking specific heat capacity as 4.2 kJ/kg/K\n(iii) to one decimal place, the heat of reaction (delta H) for the neutralisation.\n\n(e) The result was about 3% below the expected value. State and explain a modification that would give a more accurate result.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Exothermic. The temperature of the mixture rose (heat was released to the surroundings).\n\n(b)(i) To distribute the heat evenly through the mixture and avoid measuring a localised hot or cold spot, giving a more accurate temperature reading.\n(ii) Heat was lost to the surroundings because the solution was hotter than the room and the cardboard cup is not a perfect insulator.\n(iii) 6 K (the reading would be rounded down to the nearest whole number).\n\n(c)(i) A bigger temperature rise is recorded, giving a smaller percentage error in the thermometer reading, making the result more accurate.\n(ii) Corrosive symbol: a pictogram showing liquid pouring from test tubes onto a hand or surface.\n\n(d)(i) Moles HCl = (150 x 1.0) / 1000 = 0.15 mol.\n(Note: NaOH is in excess -- 160 cm3 of 1.0 M = 0.16 mol, but only 0.15 mol of HCl reacts.)\n(ii) Mass = 310 g = 0.31 kg.\nHeat = mc(delta T) = 0.31 x 4.2 x 6.4 = 8.33 kJ (accept 8.3 to 8.333 kJ).\n(iii) delta H = -(heat / moles) = -(8.33 / 0.15) = -55.6 kJ/mol (accept -55.5 to -55.6 kJ/mol).\nThe negative sign indicates an exothermic reaction.\n\n(e) Use better insulation (e.g. polystyrene cup, wrap in cotton wool) to reduce heat loss to the surroundings, giving a more accurate temperature rise and a result closer to the accepted value.\n(Or: Start with acid and base 3 K below room temperature so that heat gained from surroundings and heat lost cancel out.)"},
    {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions (Mixed Topics)", q:"Answer eight of the following:\n(a) What was the purpose of Millikan's 'oil drop' experiments of 1908 to 1913?\n(b) State and give the reason for the trend in atomic radii across the second period of the periodic table.\n(c) Write the electron configuration (s, p, etc) for an iron atom.\n(d) Give two reasons why real gases deviate from ideal behaviour at high pressures and low temperatures.\n(e) Identify in the following reaction the Bronsted-Lowry acid and its conjugate base:\nPO4(3-) + HNO3 -> NO3- + HPO4(2-)\n(f) In the Olympics in Rio de Janeiro in 2016, NaOCl in the pools reacted with H2O2: NaOCl + H2O2 -> NaCl + H2O + O2. Use oxidation numbers to show whether chlorine was oxidised or reduced.\n(g) Write a balanced equation for the displacement reaction between chlorine gas and aqueous sodium bromide.\n(h) State and explain the effect of adding a little tetraethyllead to an equimolar mixture of methane and chlorine exposed to weak sunlight.\n(i) Epoxyethane is composed of 54.5% carbon, 9.1% hydrogen and 36.4% oxygen by mass. Find the empirical formula.\n(j) Explain the term biochemical oxygen demand.\n(k) A: Which gas makes the greatest contribution to the greenhouse effect -- CO2, water vapour or methane? Give a reason. OR B: Give an example of (i) a molecular crystal, (ii) a covalent macromolecular crystal.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) To measure (determine) the magnitude of the charge on an electron (the elementary electrical charge).\n\n(b) Atomic radii decrease across the second period. Reason: the nuclear charge increases (more protons) while electrons are added to the same shell, so the increased nuclear attraction pulls the electrons closer to the nucleus, reducing the atomic radius.\n\n(c) Iron (Fe, Z = 26): 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d6 4s2.\n\n(d) (1) At high pressure, gas molecules are close together and their own volume is significant compared to the total volume -- the ideal gas model assumes zero particle volume.\n(2) At low temperatures, molecules move slowly and intermolecular forces of attraction become significant -- the ideal gas model assumes no intermolecular forces.\n\n(e) Bronsted-Lowry acid: HNO3 (donates a proton to PO4(3-)).\nConjugate base: NO3- (what remains after HNO3 loses its proton).\n\n(f) In NaOCl, Cl has oxidation number +1.\nIn NaCl, Cl has oxidation number -1.\nChlorine goes from +1 to -1, which is a decrease. Therefore chlorine is reduced.\n\n(g) Cl2(aq) + 2NaBr(aq) -> 2NaCl(aq) + Br2(aq).\nChlorine displaces bromine because it is a more reactive halogen (higher electronegativity).\n\n(h) The tetraethyllead acts as a free-radical inhibitor. It produces free radicals that terminate chain reactions, so the rate of the free-radical substitution reaction between methane and chlorine decreases (the reaction slows down or stops).\n\n(i) C: 54.5/12 = 4.54; H: 9.1/1 = 9.1; O: 36.4/16 = 2.275.\nDivide by smallest (2.275): C = 2, H = 4, O = 1.\nEmpirical formula: C2H4O.\n\n(j) Biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) is the amount of dissolved oxygen consumed by biological organisms (microorganisms) when they decompose organic matter in a water sample over a specified period (usually 5 days at 20 degrees C). A high BOD indicates high levels of organic pollution.\n\n(k) A: Water vapour makes the greatest contribution to the greenhouse effect because it is the most abundant greenhouse gas in the atmosphere.\nB: (i) Molecular crystal: ice (H2O) or iodine (I2). (ii) Covalent macromolecular crystal: diamond or silicon dioxide (SiO2)."},
    {num:5, label:"Q5: Mass Spectrometry, Isotopes & Radioactivity", q:"(a) Define (i) mass number of an atom, (ii) relative atomic mass of an element.\n\n(b) A sample of magnesium metal was introduced into a mass spectrometer and vaporised. What were the next three fundamental processes that occurred in the mass spectrometer?\n\n(c) The mass spectrum shows that magnesium has three naturally occurring isotopes. Use the data given to calculate the relative atomic mass of magnesium correct to two decimal places.\n\n(d) The radioisotope carbon-14 emits beta particles and has a half-life of 5730 years.\n(i) Define radioactivity.\n(ii) What change takes place in the structure of the nucleus when beta decay occurs?\n(iii) Write a balanced equation for the beta decay of a carbon-14 nucleus.\n(iv) A piece of fossilised yew excavated from Boora Bog, Co. Offaly, was alive about 6000 years ago. When analysed, a fragment contained 1.5 x 10^12 carbon-14 atoms. Explain why the fragment must have contained 3.0 x 10^12 carbon-14 atoms 5730 years before the analysis. What mass of carbon-14 did the fragment contain 5730 years before the analysis?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Mass number is the total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.\n(ii) Relative atomic mass is the weighted average of the masses of the naturally occurring isotopes of an element, on a scale where the mass of a carbon-12 atom is exactly 12.\n\n(b) After vaporisation, the next three processes are:\n1. Ionisation (atoms are bombarded with high-energy electrons to form positive ions).\n2. Acceleration (the ions are accelerated by an electric field).\n3. Separation/Deflection (the ions are deflected by a magnetic field according to their mass-to-charge ratio, m/z).\nDetection then follows.\n\n(c) From the mass spectrum:\nMg-24: 78.99% (or given abundance), Mg-25: 10.00%, Mg-26: 11.01%.\nRelative atomic mass = (24 x 0.7899) + (25 x 0.1000) + (26 x 0.1101)\n= 18.9576 + 2.5000 + 2.8626 = 24.32 (accept 24.31-24.32).\n\n(d)(i) Radioactivity is the spontaneous disintegration (decay) of an unstable nucleus with the emission of one or more types of radiation (alpha, beta, or gamma).\n(ii) In beta decay, a neutron in the nucleus is converted into a proton and an electron (beta particle). The beta particle is ejected. The atomic number increases by 1, and the mass number stays the same.\n(iii) C-14 -> N-14 + e- (beta particle).\n(6,14)C -> (7,14)N + (−1,0)e.\n(iv) One half-life is 5730 years. The fragment was analysed 6000 years after the tree died, meaning approximately one half-life has elapsed.\nIn one half-life, half the carbon-14 decays. Working backwards, 5730 years before the analysis, there must have been double the amount: 2 x 1.5 x 10^12 = 3.0 x 10^12 atoms.\nMass = (3.0 x 10^12 / 6.0 x 10^23) x 14 = 7.0 x 10^-11 g."},
    {num:6, label:"Q6: Crude Oil Fractionation, LPG & Heats of Combustion", q:"Refinery gas, naphtha, kerosene and gas oil are products of the fractionation of crude oil.\n\n(a) Explain the term fractionation.\n\n(b)(i) Name the two major components of liquid petroleum gas (LPG), a fuel obtained from refinery gas.\n(ii) Why is methanethiol (CH3SH), a mercaptan, added to natural gas and to LPG?\n(iii) Calculate the heat of combustion of methanethiol:\nCH3SH(g) + 3O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2H2O(l) + SO2(g)\nHeats of formation: CH3SH = -22.8, CO2 = -393.5, H2O = -285.8, SO2 = -296.8 kJ/mol.\n\n(c) Octane is converted to various products by processes A, B and C:\nOctane -> (A) + 4H2; Octane -> (B) CH3CH2CHCH2 + CH3(CH2)2CH3; Octane -> (C)\n(i) Give the IUPAC names for the four hydrocarbon products shown above.\n(ii) Identify the processes B and C.\n(iii) Why are processes A, B and C carried out?\n\n(d) How would you expect the boiling points of petrol and diesel to compare?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Fractionation (fractional distillation) is the separation of a mixture into its components (fractions) based on differences in their boiling points. In crude oil refining, the oil is heated and the vapours rise up a fractionating column, condensing at different levels according to their boiling points.\n\n(b)(i) Propane and butane.\n(ii) Methanethiol is added as an odorant because natural gas and LPG are odourless. The distinctive smell of the mercaptan allows gas leaks to be detected.\n(iii) delta H(combustion) = [delta Hf(products)] - [delta Hf(reactants)]\n= [(-393.5) + 2(-285.8) + (-296.8)] - [(-22.8) + 3(0)]\n= [-393.5 - 571.6 - 296.8] - [-22.8]\n= -1261.9 + 22.8\n= -1239.1 kJ/mol.\n\n(c)(i) Process A produces a cycloalkane/arene + H2: the product with 4H2 removed from octane (C8H18) gives C8H10 -- this is ethylbenzene or a xylene isomer (a reforming product). The other products from process B: but-1-ene (CH3CH2CHCH2 = CH3CH2CH=CH2) and butane (CH3(CH2)2CH3).\nIUPAC names: but-1-ene, butane, and the arene product from A, plus the C8H10 product.\n(ii) Process B: Cracking (thermal or catalytic cracking -- breaking long-chain molecules into shorter, more useful ones).\nProcess C: Isomerisation (converting straight-chain alkanes to branched-chain isomers).\n(iii) To convert less useful fractions into more useful ones: cracking produces shorter-chain alkenes and alkanes for fuels and chemical feedstocks; isomerisation and reforming increase the octane number of fuels.\n\n(d) Diesel has a higher boiling point than petrol. Diesel contains larger molecules (14-19 carbon atoms) compared to petrol (5-10 carbon atoms). Larger molecules have stronger van der Waals forces and therefore higher boiling points."},
    {num:7, label:"Q7: Rates of Reaction (NO2 Decomposition)", q:"(a)(i) Define rate of a reaction.\n(ii) What is meant by the activation energy of a reaction?\n\n2NO2(g) -> 2NO(g) + O2(g), delta H = 114.4 kJ\nData table: Time (s) 0-300, Concentration NO2 (M) 0.0100-0.0038, Concentration O2 (M) 0-0.0031.\n\n(b) On the same graph paper and using the same axes, draw graphs to show how the concentrations of NO2 and O2 vary with time.\n\n(c) Find the instantaneous rate of decomposition (in M/s) of NO2 at 100 seconds.\n\n(d)(i) Suggest a reason why NO is not formed in every collision between a pair of NO2 molecules.\n(ii) State one way of increasing the frequency of collisions.\n(iii) Sketch a clearly labelled reaction profile diagram for this reaction.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Rate of reaction is the change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time.\n(ii) Activation energy (Ea) is the minimum energy that colliding particles must have in order for a reaction to occur.\n\n(b) Plot both curves on the same axes with time (s) on the x-axis and concentration (M) on the y-axis.\nNO2 curve: starts at 0.0100 M and decreases (steep at first, levelling off) to 0.0038 M at 300 s.\nO2 curve: starts at 0 M and increases (steep at first, levelling off) to 0.0031 M at 300 s.\nThe curves are mirror images in shape. Note: the stoichiometry means [O2] rises at half the rate that [NO2] falls.\n\n(c) Draw a tangent to the NO2 curve at t = 100 s.\nRead two points from the tangent line. For example, if the tangent passes through approximately (50, 0.0079) and (200, 0.0041):\nInstantaneous rate = -(0.0041 - 0.0079) / (200 - 50) = 0.0038 / 150 = 2.5 x 10^-5 M/s.\n(Accept values in the range 2-3 x 10^-5 M/s depending on tangent drawn.)\n\n(d)(i) Not all collisions have sufficient energy to overcome the activation energy barrier. Also, the molecules may not be oriented correctly for a successful reaction (collision geometry).\n(ii) Increase the temperature (molecules move faster, collide more often). Or increase the concentration/pressure of NO2.\n(iii) Reaction profile for an endothermic reaction:\nThe y-axis is energy, x-axis is reaction progress.\nReactants (2NO2) are at a lower energy level. Products (2NO + O2) are at a higher energy level.\nThere is an energy hump between reactants and products.\nLabel: Ea (activation energy) = the height from reactants to the top of the hump.\nLabel: delta H = +114.4 kJ (the difference between products and reactants energy levels, positive because endothermic)."},
    {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Chemistry (Alcohols, Aldehydes & Boiling Points)", q:"Consider compounds A (propan-1-ol, CH3CH2CH2OH), B (propan-2-ol-related primary alcohol), C (propanal, CH3CH2CHO) and D (ethanoic acid, CH3COOH), all with molecules of similar size and mass.\n\n(a)(i) Draw structures to show all the bonds in molecules of B and C.\n(ii) Which one of the four compounds contains only one tetrahedrally bonded carbon atom in its molecules?\n\n(b) Propan-2-ol and B are structural isomers. B is a primary alcohol.\n(i) Explain the underlined terms.\n(ii) C and another compound E are structural isomers. Draw a structure for E.\n(iii) Give the IUPAC name for E.\n\n(c) Explain clearly why:\n(i) the boiling point of propanal is much higher than that of butane\n(ii) the boiling point of ethanoic acid is higher than that of propan-1-ol.\n\n(d) What is the organic product of the reaction of C with acidified dilute KMnO4?\n\n(e) Write a balanced equation for the reaction between sodium and B.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) B (propan-1-ol): H-C-C-C-O-H with all H atoms shown. Three C atoms in a chain, OH on end carbon. Show all C-H, C-C, C-O and O-H bonds.\nC (propanal): H-C-C-C=O with H on the carbonyl carbon. Two CH groups and a CHO group. Show all bonds including the C=O double bond.\n(ii) Ethanoic acid (D) -- it has only one carbon bonded to four groups (the CH3 carbon is tetrahedral). The carbonyl carbon is trigonal planar.\n\n(b)(i) Structural isomers: compounds with the same molecular formula but different structural formulae (different arrangement of atoms).\nPrimary alcohol: an alcohol in which the carbon atom bearing the -OH group is bonded to at most one other carbon atom.\n(ii) Propanal (C3H6O) has an isomer: propanone (CH3COCH3) -- draw the structure of propanone showing the C=O between two CH3 groups.\n(iii) Propanone (dimethyl ketone / acetone).\n\n(c)(i) Propanal has a polar C=O (carbonyl) group which creates permanent dipole-dipole interactions between molecules. Butane is non-polar and has only weak van der Waals forces. The stronger intermolecular forces in propanal mean more energy is needed to separate the molecules, so it has a higher boiling point.\n(ii) Ethanoic acid molecules can form hydrogen bonds through the O-H group AND can dimerise through two hydrogen bonds between the COOH groups. Propan-1-ol also forms hydrogen bonds through O-H, but the dimeric hydrogen bonding in carboxylic acids is stronger, giving ethanoic acid a higher boiling point.\n\n(d) Propanoic acid (CH3CH2COOH). Acidified KMnO4 oxidises the aldehyde (propanal) to the corresponding carboxylic acid.\n\n(e) 2CH3CH2CH2OH + 2Na -> 2CH3CH2CH2ONa + H2.\n(Sodium reacts with the -OH group of the primary alcohol, replacing the hydrogen to produce sodium propan-1-olate (sodium propoxide) and hydrogen gas.)"},
    {num:9, label:"Q9: Chemical Equilibrium (Phosgene)", q:"Phosgene (COCl2) is formed from carbon monoxide and chlorine using a charcoal catalyst:\nCO(g) + Cl2(g) <=> COCl2(g), delta H = -109.6 kJ/mol\n(colourless) + (green) <=> (colourless)\n\n(a) What is meant by a chemical equilibrium?\n\n(b) Write the equilibrium constant (Kc) expression for the reaction.\n\n(c) Under certain conditions in a closed container this equilibrium mixture is green.\nState and explain the effect, if any:\n(i) on the colour of the equilibrium mixture if the pressure is increased by reducing the container size\n(ii) on the equilibrium yield of phosgene of using a higher temperature\n(iii) on the value of the equilibrium constant (Kc) of using the charcoal catalyst.\n\n(d) 0.20 mol CO and 0.20 mol Cl2 in a 10.0 litre container at temperature T give Kc = 1.67 x 10^3. Calculate the number of moles of each substance at equilibrium.\n\n(e)(i) Name a catalyst used in industry for this reaction.\n(ii) What type of catalysis is involved? Justify.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Chemical equilibrium is a dynamic state in which the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction. The concentrations of reactants and products remain constant (but not necessarily equal), and there is no net change in the system.\n\n(b) Kc = [COCl2] / ([CO][Cl2])\n\n(c)(i) Increasing pressure: the equilibrium shifts to the side with fewer moles of gas. Reactants have 2 moles of gas (CO + Cl2), products have 1 mole (COCl2). So the equilibrium shifts to the right (towards COCl2). This uses up Cl2 (the green gas), so the colour becomes less green (paler/fades).\n(ii) Higher temperature: the forward reaction is exothermic (delta H = -109.6 kJ/mol). Increasing temperature favours the endothermic (reverse) direction (Le Chatelier's principle). The equilibrium shifts to the left, so the yield of phosgene decreases.\n(iii) No effect on Kc. A catalyst speeds up both forward and reverse reactions equally. It allows equilibrium to be reached faster but does not change the position of equilibrium or the value of Kc.\n\n(d) Initial: [CO] = 0.20/10.0 = 0.02 M, [Cl2] = 0.02 M, [COCl2] = 0.\nLet x mol/L of CO react.\nAt equilibrium: [CO] = 0.02 - x, [Cl2] = 0.02 - x, [COCl2] = x.\n1.67 x 10^3 = x / ((0.02 - x)(0.02 - x)) = x / (0.02 - x)^2.\nSolving: let y = 0.02 - x, then x = 0.02 - y and 1670 = (0.02 - y) / y^2.\n1670y^2 + y - 0.02 = 0.\nUsing the quadratic formula or trial: y approx. 0.003, so x approx. 0.017.\nMoles in 10.0 L: COCl2 = 0.017 x 10 = 0.17 mol; CO = 0.003 x 10 = 0.03 mol; Cl2 = 0.03 mol.\n\n(e)(i) Charcoal (activated carbon).\n(ii) Heterogeneous catalysis, because the catalyst (solid charcoal) is in a different phase from the reactants (gases). The gas molecules adsorb onto the surface of the solid catalyst."},
    {num:10, label:"Q10: Electrochemistry, Bonding & Intermolecular Forces", q:"Answer any two of parts (a), (b) and (c).\n\n(a) Consider these electrode potentials: Fe2+/Fe = -0.44 V; Fe3+/Fe2+ = +0.77 V; Ag+/Ag = +0.80 V; Cu2+/Cu = +0.34 V.\n(i) Which metal, Fe or Ag, is the better reducing agent? Explain.\n(ii) Will Fe2+ ions be oxidised to Fe3+ ions by Ag+ ions? Justify using electrode potentials.\n(iii) Calculate the EMF of a cell using Cu and Ag electrodes.\n\n(b) Intermolecular forces:\n(i) Distinguish between intramolecular bonding and intermolecular forces.\n(ii) Explain why methane (CH4) has a much lower boiling point than water (H2O) despite similar molecular mass.\n(iii) Explain why iodine is slightly soluble in water.\n\n(c) Oxidation and reduction:\n(i) Define oxidation in terms of electron transfer.\n(ii) In the reaction: Mg + 2HCl -> MgCl2 + H2, use oxidation numbers to show this is a redox reaction.\n(iii) Identify the oxidising agent.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Iron (Fe) is the better reducing agent. It has the more negative electrode potential (-0.44 V vs. +0.80 V). A more negative electrode potential means the metal loses electrons more readily (is more easily oxidised).\n(ii) Yes. For the reaction Fe2+ -> Fe3+ + e- (reverse of Fe3+/Fe2+, E = -0.77 V as oxidation) and Ag+ + e- -> Ag (E = +0.80 V). Overall EMF = +0.80 - 0.77 = +0.03 V. Since EMF is positive, the reaction is spontaneous, so Ag+ ions can oxidise Fe2+ to Fe3+.\n(iii) EMF = E(cathode) - E(anode) = E(Ag+/Ag) - E(Cu2+/Cu) = +0.80 - 0.34 = +0.46 V.\n(Ag is the cathode because it has the higher electrode potential; Cu is the anode.)\n\n(b)(i) Intramolecular bonding: the strong bonds (ionic, covalent, or metallic) that hold atoms together within a molecule or compound.\nIntermolecular forces: the weaker forces of attraction that exist between separate molecules (e.g. van der Waals, dipole-dipole, hydrogen bonding).\n(ii) Water molecules form hydrogen bonds (O-H...O) between molecules due to the high electronegativity of oxygen and the presence of lone pairs. These are relatively strong intermolecular forces. Methane is non-polar and has only weak van der Waals (London dispersion) forces. Much less energy is needed to separate CH4 molecules, so methane has a much lower boiling point.\n(iii) Iodine (I2) is slightly soluble because the polar O-H bonds in water can induce temporary dipoles in the non-polar I2 molecules. Also, iodine can react with water to a small extent. However, iodine is mostly non-polar, so its solubility is limited.\n\n(c)(i) Oxidation is the loss of electrons.\n(ii) Mg: oxidation number changes from 0 (in Mg) to +2 (in MgCl2) -- oxidation (loss of electrons).\nH: oxidation number changes from +1 (in HCl) to 0 (in H2) -- reduction (gain of electrons).\nSince one species is oxidised and another is reduced, it is a redox reaction.\n(iii) HCl (or H+ ions) is the oxidising agent. It gains electrons (H+ is reduced to H2)."},
    {num:11, label:"Q11: Stoichiometry, Bonding & pH Calculations", q:"Answer any two of parts (a), (b) and (c).\n\n(a) Stoichiometry:\n(i) Calculate the mass of iron(III) oxide (Fe2O3) produced when 11.2 g of iron reacts completely with excess oxygen: 4Fe + 3O2 -> 2Fe2O3.\n(ii) Calculate the volume of oxygen gas (at STP) required for the complete combustion of 5.6 g of carbon monoxide: 2CO + O2 -> 2CO2.\n\n(b) Bonding and shapes:\n(i) Draw dot-and-cross diagrams for H2O and CO2.\n(ii) State the shape of each molecule and explain using VSEPR theory.\n(iii) Explain why CO2 is non-polar despite having polar bonds.\n\n(c) pH calculations:\n(i) Define pH.\n(ii) Calculate the pH of 0.05 M HCl.\n(iii) Calculate the pH of 0.01 M NaOH.\n(iv) Calculate the H+ concentration of a weak acid HA (Ka = 1.8 x 10^-5) at 0.10 M concentration. Find its pH.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Moles Fe = 11.2 / 56 = 0.2 mol.\nFrom equation: 4 mol Fe -> 2 mol Fe2O3, so 0.2 mol Fe -> 0.1 mol Fe2O3.\nMr of Fe2O3 = (2 x 56) + (3 x 16) = 160.\nMass = 0.1 x 160 = 16.0 g.\n(ii) Moles CO = 5.6 / 28 = 0.2 mol.\nFrom equation: 2 mol CO requires 1 mol O2, so 0.2 mol CO requires 0.1 mol O2.\nVolume at STP = 0.1 x 22.4 = 2.24 litres.\n\n(b)(i) H2O: oxygen in centre with two bonding pairs (to H atoms) and two lone pairs. Show dots and crosses.\nCO2: carbon in centre with two double bonds to oxygen atoms (each O has two lone pairs). Linear arrangement.\n(ii) H2O: V-shaped (bent/angular). The two bonding pairs and two lone pairs give a tetrahedral electron arrangement, but since lone pairs repel more strongly, the bond angle is compressed to about 104.5 degrees, giving a V-shape.\nCO2: Linear. Two bonding regions around the central carbon atom repel equally, maximising the angle to 180 degrees.\n(iii) CO2 is non-polar because although each C=O bond is polar (oxygen is more electronegative), the molecule is linear and symmetrical, so the two bond dipoles are equal and opposite -- they cancel out, giving a net dipole moment of zero.\n\n(c)(i) pH = -log10[H+]. It is a measure of the hydrogen ion concentration in a solution.\n(ii) HCl is a strong acid (fully dissociates): [H+] = 0.05 M.\npH = -log(0.05) = 1.3.\n(iii) NaOH is a strong base: [OH-] = 0.01 M.\n[H+] = Kw / [OH-] = 1.0 x 10^-14 / 0.01 = 1.0 x 10^-12 M.\npH = -log(1.0 x 10^-12) = 12.0.\n(iv) Ka = [H+][A-] / [HA] = x^2 / 0.10 (assuming x << 0.10).\nx^2 = 1.8 x 10^-5 x 0.10 = 1.8 x 10^-6.\nx = [H+] = 1.34 x 10^-3 M.\npH = -log(1.34 x 10^-3) = 2.87 (accept 2.9)."},
],

  "2017": [
  {num:1, label:"Q1: Titration — Iron(II) / KMnO4 (Primary Standard)", q:"(NH4)2SO4.FeSO4.6H2O (hydrated ammonium iron(II) sulfate) used as primary standard.\n8.82 g dissolved in dilute H2SO4 and made up to 250 cm3. Titrated in 25.0 cm3 portions against KMnO4.\nMean titre: 20.45 cm3 of KMnO4.\nReaction: MnO4- + 5Fe2+ + 8H+ -> Mn2+ + 5Fe3+ + 4H2O\n\n(a)(i) Explain the term primary standard.\n(ii) Give two essential properties of a primary standard.\n(b) Explain why dilute sulfuric acid was needed (i) when preparing the standard solution, (ii) during each titration.\n(c) Describe the procedure to ensure accuracy at the end point. What observation indicates the end point?\n(d) Calculate (i) moles of Fe2+ in 25.0 cm3, (ii) moles of KMnO4 required, (iii) molarity of KMnO4.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) A primary standard is a solution of accurately known concentration (molarity).\n(ii) Any two of: pure, stable in air, water-soluble, high molecular mass (so that weighing errors are minimised), not hygroscopic, not easily oxidised.\n\n(b)(i) Dilute sulfuric acid prevents air oxidation of Fe2+ to Fe3+ during preparation. Without it, Fe2+ would be oxidised by dissolved oxygen in solution, giving inaccurate results.\n(ii) During each titration, acid ensures that MnO4- is reduced fully to colourless Mn2+ (not to brown MnO2 precipitate, which would form without sufficient acid).\n\n(c) Procedure: Swirl the flask while adding KMnO4 from the burette. Rinse the inside walls of the flask with deionised water at intervals. Add KMnO4 dropwise as the end point approaches. Read the burette at eye level, keeping it vertical, reading the bottom of the meniscus. Carry out a rough titration first, then repeat until two or more titres agree to within 0.1 cm3.\nEnd point observation: A faint permanent pink colour remains in the conical flask (KMnO4 is self-indicating — no separate indicator needed).\n\n(d)(i) Mr of (NH4)2SO4.FeSO4.6H2O = 2(14+4) + 32+64 + 56+32+64 + 6(18) = 392.\nMolarity = (8.82 x 4) / 392 = 35.28 / 392 = 0.09 M.\nMoles Fe2+ in 25.0 cm3 = (25.0 x 0.09) / 1000 = 0.00225 mol.\n\n(ii) From the equation, Fe2+ : MnO4- = 5 : 1.\nMoles KMnO4 = 0.00225 / 5 = 0.00045 mol.\n\n(iii) Molarity of KMnO4 = (0.00045 x 1000) / 20.45 = 0.022 M."},
  {num:2, label:"Q2: Preparation of Ethene & Ethyne, Combustion Tests", q:"A student prepared ethene and ethyne and compared their properties.\n\n(a) Ethyne prepared from calcium carbide and water:\n(i) What was observed in the reaction flask?\n(ii) Why were the first few test-tubes of gas discarded?\n\n(b)(i) Draw a labelled diagram for preparation and collection of ethene.\n(ii) State and explain two safety precautions.\n\n(c) Combustion tests:\n(i) Compare observations for ethene and ethyne.\n(ii) Write a balanced equation for the complete combustion of ethyne.\n\n(d) Name a reagent used to test for unsaturation.\n\n(e) 3.2 g of CaC2 consumed. CaC2 + 2H2O -> Ca(OH)2 + C2H2. Calculate the maximum number of ethyne molecules produced.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Observations: Vigorous reaction — bubbling / effervescence. The mixture turns milky (white). The flask gets hot (exothermic). Vapour / steam may be seen.\n(ii) The first test-tubes were discarded because they contain air (nitrogen, oxygen, etc.) that was present in the apparatus. Discarding them ensures a purer sample of ethyne.\n\n(b)(i) Diagram: Horizontal test-tube (or round-bottomed flask) with glass wool soaked in ethanol at one end. Aluminium oxide (Al2O3) catalyst placed in the middle of the tube. Heat source (Bunsen burner) under the catalyst. Delivery tube leads from the apparatus to a trough of water for collection over water in an inverted test-tube.\n\n(ii) Precaution 1: Remove the delivery tube from the water before removing the heat source. Reason: To prevent suck-back of cold water into the hot apparatus, which could cause the glass to crack.\nPrecaution 2: Keep ethanol / ethene away from the flame. Reason: Ethanol vapour and ethene gas are flammable and could ignite.\n\n(c)(i) Ethyne burns with a smokier, sootier, brighter, more luminous (yellow) flame than ethene. Ethene burns with a cleaner, less smoky, slightly bluish flame. Both produce yellow/luminous flames in air, but ethyne is noticeably more sooty due to its higher carbon-to-hydrogen ratio.\n\n(ii) 2C2H2 + 5O2 -> 4CO2 + 2H2O.\n\n(d) Bromine water (Br2 solution) or acidified potassium manganate(VII) (KMnO4/H+).\n\n(e) Mr of CaC2 = 40 + 2(12) = 64.\nMoles CaC2 = 3.2 / 64 = 0.05 mol.\nFrom equation: 1 mol CaC2 produces 1 mol C2H2.\nMoles C2H2 = 0.05 mol.\nNumber of molecules = 0.05 x 6.0 x 10^23 = 3.0 x 10^22 molecules."},
  {num:3, label:"Q3: Rates — Na2S2O3 Concentration vs Rate", q:"Na2S2O3 + 2HCl -> 2NaCl + SO2 + S + H2O\nRate (r) = 1/t. Different Na2S2O3 concentrations tested using stock 30 g/L diluted with water.\n\nData: Run 1: 100 cm3 stock + 0 water, 30.0 g/L, t=50s. Run 2: 80+20, t=63s. Run 3: 60+40, t=83s. Run 4: 40+60, t=125s. Run 5: 20+80, t=250s. Run 6: 0+100, 0 g/L, r=0.\n\n(a) Define rate of reaction.\n(b) Describe a method to determine when the same mass of sulfur has formed.\n(c) Fill in the missing Na2S2O3 concentrations.\n(d)(i) Fill in the missing rates (3 decimal places).\n(ii) Plot concentration vs rate for all runs.\n(iii) What can you conclude?\n(e) Describe how to investigate the effect of temperature on this reaction.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Rate of reaction is the change in concentration of a reactant (or product) per unit time.\n\n(b) Place a cross (X) or printed mark on paper under the conical flask. Start the timer when reactants are mixed. Stop the timer when the sulfur precipitate makes the X no longer visible when viewed from above through the solution. Use the same flask (same depth of solution) for each run.\n\n(c) Missing concentrations:\nRun 2: 30 x (80/100) = 24.0 g/L.\nRun 3: 30 x (60/100) = 18.0 g/L.\nRun 4: 30 x (40/100) = 12.0 g/L.\nRun 5: 30 x (20/100) = 6.0 g/L.\n\n(d)(i) Rates (r = 1/t, to 3 decimal places):\nRun 1: 1/50 = 0.020 s^-1.\nRun 2: 1/63 = 0.016 s^-1.\nRun 3: 1/83 = 0.012 s^-1.\nRun 4: 1/125 = 0.008 s^-1.\nRun 5: 1/250 = 0.004 s^-1.\nRun 6: 0 s^-1.\n\n(ii) Plot concentration (g/L) on the x-axis and rate (s^-1) on the y-axis. All six points should lie approximately on a straight line passing through the origin.\n\n(iii) Conclusion: The rate of reaction is directly proportional to the concentration of sodium thiosulfate. (Doubling the concentration doubles the rate.)\n\n(e) To investigate temperature effect: Repeat one specific run (e.g. Run 1) at several different temperatures. Use a water bath (beaker of water heated on a hotplate) to bring both Na2S2O3 and HCl solutions to the desired temperature before mixing. Keep concentrations and volumes the same as the original run. Record the reaction time at each temperature. Plot a graph of rate (1/time) vs temperature. The rate increases with temperature."},
  {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions — Mixed Topics", q:"Answer eight of the following:\n(a) Why did Mendeleev place tellurium before iodine in his periodic table?\n(b) Identify the main energy levels in the electron transition for the first (red) line of the Balmer series.\n(c) Nuclear equation: Cs-137 -> X + e-. Identify X and atomic number Z.\n(d) Write the oxidation number for (i) oxygen in OF2, (ii) xenon in XeF4.\n(e) State Avogadro's law.\n(f) Calculate the mass of NaCl needed for 500 cm3 of 0.9% (w/v) saline.\n(g) Identify in ammonia (i) the type of intramolecular bonding, (ii) the type of intermolecular forces.\n(h) Eugenol structure given. (i) Write the molecular formula. (ii) Name a spectroscopic technique to confirm identity.\n(i) Write a balanced equation for Cu + AgNO3 -> copper(II) nitrate.\n(j) How does boiling remove temporary hardness caused by Ca(HCO3)2?\n(k) A: Explain using balanced equations how H2SO4 in rainwater forms from SO2. OR B: How is aluminium anodised?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Mendeleev placed tellurium before iodine because their chemical properties fitted better in that order — iodine belonged with the halogens (similar chemical properties to F, Cl, Br) and tellurium with the chalcogens. Listing strictly by atomic mass would have put them in the wrong groups.\n\n(b) The first (red) line of the Balmer series: electron transition from n = 3 to n = 2.\n\n(c) Cs-137 (Z=55) -> Ba-137 (Z=56) + e- (beta particle).\nX = Ba (barium). Z = 56.\n(Beta decay: a neutron converts to a proton, increasing atomic number by 1.)\n\n(d)(i) Oxygen in OF2: +2. (Fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen, so F = -1 each. For OF2: x + 2(-1) = 0, x = +2.)\n(ii) Xenon in XeF4: +4. (Each F = -1. x + 4(-1) = 0, x = +4.)\n\n(e) Avogadro's law: Equal volumes of all gases, at the same temperature and pressure, contain the same number of molecules.\n\n(f) 0.9% (w/v) means 0.9 g per 100 cm3 = 9 g per litre.\nFor 500 cm3: mass = 9 x 0.5 = 4.5 g of NaCl.\n\n(g)(i) Intramolecular bonding in ammonia: Polar covalent bonds (N-H bonds).\n(ii) Intermolecular forces: Hydrogen bonding (between N-H on one molecule and the lone pair on N of another molecule). Also van der Waals forces.\n\n(h)(i) Molecular formula of eugenol: C10H12O2.\n(ii) IR (infrared) spectroscopy. (Also acceptable: UV-visible spectroscopy, mass spectrometry, NMR, X-ray crystallography.)\n\n(i) Cu + 2AgNO3 -> Cu(NO3)2 + 2Ag.\n\n(j) Boiling decomposes calcium hydrogencarbonate:\nCa(HCO3)2(aq) -> CaCO3(s) + H2O(l) + CO2(g).\nThe soluble Ca(HCO3)2 is converted to insoluble CaCO3 (limescale), which precipitates out. The Ca2+ ions that caused hardness are removed from solution.\n\n(k) A: Step 1: SO2 + 1/2 O2 -> SO3 (sulfur dioxide oxidised in atmosphere).\nStep 2: SO3 + H2O -> H2SO4 (sulfur trioxide dissolves in rainwater to form sulfuric acid).\n\nB: The aluminium object is made the anode (positive electrode) in an electrolysis cell containing dilute sulfuric acid. Oxygen is produced at the anode and reacts with the aluminium surface, forming a thick protective layer of aluminium oxide (Al2O3)."},
  {num:5, label:"Q5: Atomic Orbitals, Atomic Radius & Ionisation Energy", q:"(a) Define an atomic orbital. Write the ground state s, p electron configuration for carbon. How many orbitals are occupied?\n\n(b) Why is it difficult to specify the absolute boundary of an atom? State the definition of (covalent) atomic radius.\n\n(c)(i) Describe and account for the trend in covalent radii down Group 1.\n(ii) Account for the general trend in covalent radii across the second period (bar chart given).\n\n(d) Define first ionisation energy. Why do first ionisation energy values generally increase across the second period?\n\n(e) Successive ionisation energies for carbon: {1086, 2353, 4620, 6223, 37831, 47277} kJ/mol.\nHow does this provide evidence for (i) the number of electrons, (ii) the number of electrons in each main energy level?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) An atomic orbital is a region of space around the nucleus where there is a high probability of finding an electron.\n\nCarbon electron configuration: 1s2 2s2 2p2.\nNumber of occupied orbitals: 4 (one 1s, one 2s, two of the three 2p orbitals — each 2p orbital holds one electron by Hund's rule).\n\n(b) It is difficult to specify the absolute boundary because the electron cloud has no sharp edge — the probability of finding an electron decreases gradually with distance from the nucleus but never reaches exactly zero.\n\nCovalent (atomic) radius: Half the distance between the nuclei of two identical atoms bonded together by a single covalent bond.\n\n(c)(i) Down Group 1 (Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs): Covalent radii increase. Each element has one more electron shell (main energy level) than the one above it. The outermost electron is further from the nucleus, and increased shielding from inner electrons reduces the effective nuclear charge felt by the outer electron.\n\n(ii) Across the second period (Li to F): Covalent radii generally decrease. The number of protons increases (nuclear charge increases) while electrons are added to the same energy level (same degree of shielding). The increased effective nuclear charge pulls the electron cloud closer to the nucleus, making the atom smaller.\n\n(d) First ionisation energy: The minimum energy required to remove the most loosely bound electron from a neutral gaseous atom in its ground state.\nM(g) -> M+(g) + e-\n\nGeneral increase across the second period: Nuclear charge increases (more protons) while electrons are added to the same shell (similar shielding). The greater effective nuclear charge means electrons are held more tightly, requiring more energy to remove.\n\n(e)(i) There are 6 successive ionisation energies, so there are 6 electrons in the carbon atom.\n\n(ii) The first 4 ionisation energies increase relatively gradually (1086, 2353, 4620, 6223). Then there is a very large jump to the 5th value (37831) and 6th value (47277). This huge jump between the 4th and 5th ionisation energies shows that the first 4 electrons are in the outer (second) energy level and the last 2 electrons are in the inner (first) energy level, much closer to the nucleus. So carbon has 2 electrons in the first energy level and 4 in the second: configuration 2, 4."},
  {num:6, label:"Q6: Hydrocarbons — Methane, Alkane Properties, Cracking & Hess's Law", q:"(a) Identify the gas produced by anaerobic bacterial decomposition of animal waste or vegetation. Give a major use. Why is its concentration in the atmosphere a concern?\n(b) Explain why high molecular mass alkanes (e.g. dodecane, bp 216 C) have much higher boiling points than low molecular mass alkanes (e.g. propane, bp -42 C).\n(c)(i) Write a balanced equation for dehydrocyclisation of heptane to methylbenzene and H2.\n(ii) What is the purpose of dehydrocyclisation in oil refining?\n(d) Catalytic cracking of C12H26 produces but-2-ene, 2-methylbutane, and alkene X.\n(i) Draw structural formulae for but-2-ene and 2-methylbutane.\n(ii) Deduce the molecular formula of X.\n(e) State Hess's law. Heats of formation: dodecane = -350.9, but-2-ene = -9.3, 2-methylbutane = -178.4, X = 20.0 kJ/mol. Calculate delta H for the cracking reaction.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Gas: Methane (CH4).\nMajor use: Fuel (heating, cooking, electricity generation — natural gas).\nConcern: Methane is a greenhouse gas. Increasing atmospheric methane concentration contributes to global warming / climate change. It is much more potent than CO2 as a greenhouse gas (traps more heat per molecule).\n\n(b) High molecular mass alkanes have more electrons and a larger surface area. This leads to stronger London (van der Waals) dispersion forces between the molecules. More energy is needed to overcome these stronger intermolecular forces, resulting in higher boiling points. Propane (C3H8) has weak intermolecular forces and is a gas at room temperature. Dodecane (C12H26) has much stronger forces and is a liquid.\n\n(c)(i) C7H16 -> C6H5CH3 + 4H2\n(Heptane -> methylbenzene (toluene) + 4 molecules of hydrogen gas.)\n\n(ii) Dehydrocyclisation converts straight-chain alkanes (low octane number) into aromatic hydrocarbons (high octane number). This improves the quality of petrol and reduces engine knocking.\n\n(d)(i) But-2-ene: CH3-CH=CH-CH3.\n2-methylbutane: CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH3.\n\n(ii) C12H26 -> C4H8 + C5H12 + X.\nCarbon: 12 = 4 + 5 + c, so c = 3.\nHydrogen: 26 = 8 + 12 + h, so h = 6.\nX = C3H6 (propene).\n\n(e) Hess's law: The total enthalpy change for a reaction is independent of the route taken, provided the initial and final conditions are the same. (The heat change depends only on the initial and final states.)\n\ndelta H = sum Hf(products) - sum Hf(reactants)\n= [(-9.3) + (-178.4) + (20.0)] - [(-350.9)]\n= [-167.7] - [-350.9]\n= -167.7 + 350.9\n= +183.2 kJ/mol.\n(The cracking reaction is endothermic.)"},
  {num:7, label:"Q7: Equilibrium — N2O4 / NO2 System", q:"N2O4(g) <=> 2NO2(g). Colourless <=> dark brown.\n\n(a) What is meant by chemical equilibrium? Why is it described as dynamic? State Le Chatelier's principle.\n(b) Write the Kc expression.\n(c) Kc = 0.2 at temperature T. 1 mol pure N2O4 sealed in a 10 L container. Calculate equilibrium concentrations of each gas.\n(d) The colour is paler at 0 C than at T (where T > 0 C). Deduce whether decomposition of N2O4 is exothermic or endothermic. Explain your reasoning.\n(e) Would Kc change if a different initial concentration of N2O4 were used? Explain.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Chemical equilibrium: A state in a reversible reaction where the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction, and the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant.\nDynamic: Both forward and reverse reactions are still occurring continuously — it is not a static situation. Molecules are constantly reacting in both directions, but at equal rates so there is no net change.\nLe Chatelier's principle: If a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change in concentration, pressure, or temperature, the equilibrium will shift in the direction that tends to counteract (oppose) that change.\n\n(b) Kc = [NO2]^2 / [N2O4]\n\n(c) Let x = moles of N2O4 that dissociate.\nAt equilibrium:\nN2O4 = (1 - x) mol, so [N2O4] = (1 - x) / 10 M.\nNO2 = 2x mol, so [NO2] = 2x / 10 = x/5 M.\n\nKc = (x/5)^2 / ((1-x)/10) = (x^2/25) / ((1-x)/10)\n= (x^2 x 10) / (25(1-x))\n= 10x^2 / (25(1-x))\n= 2x^2 / (5(1-x)) = 0.2.\n\nSo 2x^2 = 1.0(1-x) = 1.0 - x.\n2x^2 + x - 1 = 0.\nUsing the quadratic formula: x = (-1 + sqrt(1 + 8)) / 4 = (-1 + 3) / 4 = 0.5.\n\nAt equilibrium:\n[N2O4] = (1 - 0.5) / 10 = 0.05 M.\n[NO2] = 2(0.5) / 10 = 0.1 M.\n\nCheck: Kc = (0.1)^2 / 0.05 = 0.01 / 0.05 = 0.2. Correct.\n\n(d) At 0 C (lower temperature), the mixture is paler, meaning less dark brown NO2 is present. The equilibrium has shifted to the left (towards colourless N2O4).\nBy Le Chatelier's principle: decreasing temperature shifts equilibrium towards the exothermic direction. Since lowering temperature shifts equilibrium to the left, the reverse reaction (2NO2 -> N2O4) must be exothermic.\nTherefore, the forward reaction (decomposition of N2O4 to NO2) is endothermic.\n\n(e) No, the value of Kc would not change. Kc depends only on temperature. Changing the initial concentration of N2O4 at the same temperature T would change the equilibrium concentrations of both gases, but the ratio in the Kc expression would remain the same value (0.2)."},
  {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Scheme — Oxidation/Reduction of Alcohols, Aldehydes & Ketones", q:"Scheme: propan-1-ol -> (Na2Cr2O7/H+) -> propanal (A) -> (Na2Cr2O7/H+) -> propanoic acid.\npropan-2-ol -> (Na2Cr2O7/H+) -> propanone (B).\nphenylmethanol -> (KMnO4/OH-, then HCl) -> benzoic acid.\nNa2SO3 used as reducing agent.\n\n(a) What colour change occurs as phenylmethanol is converted to benzoic acid? Explain by reference to the transition metal reagent. Mark bonds broken in phenylmethanol and formed in benzoic acid.\n(b) Give IUPAC names for A and B. Draw their structures.\n(c) Identify the reactant and catalyst used to reduce B to propan-2-ol.\n(d) A is easily oxidised but B is very difficult to oxidise. Suggest a weak oxidising reagent to distinguish them.\n(e) Name an ester that is a structural isomer of propanoic acid. Identify the alcohol and acid used to make it.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Colour change: Purple (violet) to green (then brown on further standing).\nExplanation: KMnO4 (purple, containing Mn7+) is reduced. In alkaline conditions, Mn7+ is reduced to Mn(IV) or lower — the green colour is from manganate (MnO4^2-) and the brown is from MnO2. The transition metal ion (manganese) changes oxidation state, which causes the colour change.\n\nBonds broken in phenylmethanol: Two C-H bonds on the carbon attached to OH (the -CH2OH group).\nBonds formed in benzoic acid: A C=O double bond (carbonyl) and a C-O bond (in the -COOH group).\n\n(b) A = Propanal (CH3CH2CHO). Structure: CH3-CH2-C(=O)-H.\nB = Propanone (CH3COCH3). Structure: CH3-C(=O)-CH3.\n\n(c) Reactant: Hydrogen gas (H2).\nCatalyst: Nickel (Ni) — or platinum/palladium. (Catalytic hydrogenation.)\nAlternatively: Sodium borohydride (NaBH4) or lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH4) as a chemical reducing agent.\n\n(d) Fehling's solution (or Benedict's solution or Tollens' reagent). These are weak oxidising agents.\nWith A (propanal, an aldehyde): Fehling's blue solution changes to a brick-red precipitate (Cu2O). Tollens' gives a silver mirror.\nWith B (propanone, a ketone): No reaction. Ketones cannot be easily oxidised because they have no H on the carbonyl carbon.\n\n(e) Ester isomer of propanoic acid (C3H6O2): Methyl ethanoate (CH3COOCH3).\nAlcohol: Methanol (CH3OH).\nCarboxylic acid: Ethanoic acid (CH3COOH).\n(Another possible answer: Ethyl methanoate, HCOOC2H5, from ethanol + methanoic acid.)"},
  {num:9, label:"Q9: Acids, Bases, pH & Titration Curves", q:"(a) Define (i) a base, (ii) a conjugate acid-base pair (Bronsted-Lowry theory). What is the conjugate acid of H2O?\n\n(b) Define pH. The pH values of 0.10 M solutions of H2SO4, HCl, and methanoic acid are 0.70, 1.00, and 2.37 respectively.\nAccount for the pH difference between (i) HCl and H2SO4, (ii) HCl and methanoic acid.\nCalculate (iii) Ka for methanoic acid, (iv) pH of 0.05 M methanoic acid.\n\n(c) Draw a labelled pH curve for adding 50 cm3 of 0.10 M NaOH to 25 cm3 of 0.10 M methanoic acid. Name a suitable indicator. Explain your choice.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) A base is a proton (H+) acceptor (Bronsted-Lowry definition).\n(ii) A conjugate acid-base pair consists of two species that differ by one proton (H+). The conjugate acid has one more H+ than its conjugate base.\nConjugate acid of H2O: H3O+ (hydronium ion).\n\n(b) pH = -log10[H+].\n\n(i) H2SO4 (pH 0.70) vs HCl (pH 1.00): Both are strong acids (fully dissociated). But H2SO4 is dibasic — it produces 2 H+ ions per molecule, while HCl is monobasic (1 H+ per molecule). So 0.10 M H2SO4 gives [H+] = 0.20 M (pH = -log 0.20 = 0.70), while 0.10 M HCl gives [H+] = 0.10 M (pH = 1.00).\n\n(ii) HCl (pH 1.00) vs methanoic acid (pH 2.37): HCl is a strong acid (fully dissociated), giving [H+] = 0.10 M. Methanoic acid is a weak acid (only partially dissociated), so [H+] is much less than 0.10 M. Hence the pH of methanoic acid is higher (less acidic).\n\n(iii) pH 2.37, so [H+] = 10^-2.37 = 4.27 x 10^-3 M.\nKa = [H+][HCOO-] / [HCOOH].\nSince [H+] = [HCOO-] = 4.27 x 10^-3 M.\n[HCOOH] = 0.10 - 0.00427 = 0.0957 M (approx. 0.10 M).\nKa = (4.27 x 10^-3)^2 / 0.0957 = 1.82 x 10^-5 / 0.0957 = 1.9 x 10^-4.\n\n(iv) For 0.05 M methanoic acid:\nKa = x^2 / 0.05 (where x = [H+]).\n1.9 x 10^-4 = x^2 / 0.05.\nx^2 = 9.5 x 10^-6.\nx = 3.08 x 10^-3 M.\npH = -log(3.08 x 10^-3) = 2.51.\n\n(c) pH curve: Starts at about pH 2.4 (weak acid). Rises slowly at first (buffer region). At the equivalence point (25 cm3 NaOH added, since moles acid = moles base = 0.0025 mol), the pH jumps sharply from about pH 7 to about pH 11. The equivalence point pH is above 7 (around 8.5) because the salt formed (sodium methanoate) is basic. After 50 cm3 NaOH, pH levels off at about 13 (excess NaOH).\n\nSuitable indicator: Phenolphthalein (colour change pH 8.2-10.0, colourless to pink).\nExplanation: The equivalence point pH is approximately 8.5 (above 7 because a weak acid-strong base titration). Phenolphthalein changes colour in the range 8.2-10.0, which falls within the steep portion of the pH curve at the equivalence point. Methyl orange would be unsuitable as it changes at pH 3-4, missing the equivalence point."},
  {num:10, label:"Q10: Volatile Liquid Mr / Radioactivity / Electrochemistry", q:"Answer any two of (a), (b), (c). (2 x 25 marks)\n\n(a) Determine the relative molecular mass of a volatile liquid.\nDraw apparatus diagram. 0.28 g of liquid, volume 268 cm3 at 98 C (371 K) and 101 kPa.\n(i) Calculate the number of moles of vapour.\n(ii) Find the relative molecular mass.\n\n(b) What is an alpha particle?\nBased on Thomson's plum-pudding model:\n(i) What observation would be expected when alpha particles are fired at gold foil?\n(ii) What unexpected observation was actually made? Explain.\n(iii) What did Rutherford conclude about atomic structure?\n\n(c) Define electrolysis. Give a use for electroplating.\nDescribe, with the aid of a labelled diagram, how a copper object is electroplated with silver.\nState what is observed at the electrodes during electroplating.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Diagram: A small syringe or vial containing the volatile liquid, placed in an oven or boiling water bath. A gas syringe measures the volume of vapour at a known temperature. A thermometer records the temperature. The pressure is atmospheric (read from a barometer).\n\n(i) Using PV = nRT:\nP = 101 kPa = 101,000 Pa.\nV = 268 cm3 = 2.68 x 10^-4 m3.\nT = 98 + 273 = 371 K.\nR = 8.3 J K^-1 mol^-1.\nn = PV / RT = (101,000 x 2.68 x 10^-4) / (8.3 x 371)\n= 27.068 / 3079.3\n= 0.00879 mol (approx. 8.8 x 10^-3 mol).\n\n(ii) Mr = mass / moles = 0.28 / 0.00879 = 31.9.\nRelative molecular mass = 32 (to nearest whole number).\n(This is consistent with methanol, CH3OH, Mr = 32.)\n\n(b) An alpha particle is a helium-4 nucleus (2 protons + 2 neutrons), with a charge of +2. Symbol: He-4 (2+).\n\n(i) Based on Thomson's model (uniform spread of positive charge): Most alpha particles should pass straight through with only very slight deflections. No large deflections or rebounds would be expected.\n\n(ii) Unexpected observation: A small number of alpha particles were deflected at large angles, and a very few were reflected (bounced) back along their original paths.\nExplanation: This means there must be a small, very dense, positively charged region (the nucleus) inside the atom. Alpha particles deflected at large angles passed close to this dense positive nucleus and were repelled by electrostatic repulsion. Those that hit the nucleus head-on were reflected back.\n\n(iii) Rutherford concluded: The atom is mostly empty space (explaining why most alpha particles pass through). All the positive charge and nearly all the mass are concentrated in a very small, dense nucleus at the centre. Electrons orbit the nucleus at relatively large distances.\n\n(c) Electrolysis is the decomposition of a compound (in molten or aqueous state) by passing an electric current through it.\nUse for electroplating: To coat a cheaper metal object with a more attractive or corrosion-resistant metal layer (e.g. silver-plated jewellery, chrome-plated car parts).\n\nDiagram: The copper object (to be plated) is the cathode (negative electrode). A piece of pure silver is the anode (positive electrode). Both are immersed in an electrolyte of silver nitrate (AgNO3) solution. Connected to a DC power supply.\n\nObservations:\nAt the cathode: Silver metal is deposited — a shiny silver coating forms on the copper object. (Ag+ + e- -> Ag.)\nAt the anode: The silver anode gradually dissolves, getting smaller. (Ag -> Ag+ + e-.) Silver ions enter the solution, maintaining its concentration."},
],

  "2016": [
  {num:1, label:"Q1: Titration — Ethanoic Acid in Vinegar", q:"CH3COOH + NaOH -> CH3COONa + H2O\n25.0 cm3 of vinegar diluted to 250 cm3. Titrated with NaOH solution (1.20 g NaOH in 500 cm3).\nMean titre: 18.75 cm3 of diluted vinegar neutralised 25.0 cm3 of NaOH.\n\n(a) Describe the procedures for measuring 25.0 cm3 of vinegar and diluting it to exactly 250 cm3.\n(b) Name a suitable indicator. What colour change at the end point?\n(c) Calculate (i) moles of NaOH in 25.0 cm3, (ii) moles of ethanoic acid per cm3 of diluted vinegar.\n(d) Find concentration of ethanoic acid in the original vinegar (i) in mol/L, (ii) as % (w/v).\n(e) Starting from a primary standard of anhydrous sodium carbonate, what two titrations are required to standardise NaOH?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Measure: Use a 25 cm3 pipette (previously rinsed with deionised water, then rinsed with vinegar). Read at eye level with bottom of meniscus on the mark. Allow drainage time; do not blow out the last drop.\nDilute: Transfer to a 250 cm3 volumetric flask (previously rinsed with deionised water). Add deionised water until the bottom of the meniscus is on the 250 cm3 mark. Stopper and invert several times to mix.\n\n(b) Indicator: Phenolphthalein.\nColour change: Pink to colourless.\n\n(c)(i) Mr of NaOH = 40.\nMolarity of NaOH = 1.20 / (40 x 0.5) = 0.06 M.\nMoles NaOH in 25.0 cm3 = (25.0 x 0.06) / 1000 = 0.0015 mol.\n\n(ii) From the equation, CH3COOH : NaOH = 1 : 1.\nSo moles of ethanoic acid = 0.0015 mol in 18.75 cm3.\nMoles per cm3 = 0.0015 / 18.75 = 8.0 x 10^-5 mol/cm3.\n\n(d)(i) In diluted vinegar: 8.0 x 10^-5 x 1000 = 0.08 M.\nOriginal vinegar was diluted x10 (25 cm3 to 250 cm3).\nConcentration in original = 0.08 x 10 = 0.8 M.\n\n(ii) Mr of CH3COOH = 60.\nMass per litre = 0.8 x 60 = 48 g/L.\n% (w/v) = 48 / 10 = 4.8%.\n\n(e) Two titrations:\n1. Titrate anhydrous sodium carbonate (primary standard) with a strong acid (e.g. HCl) to standardise the acid.\n2. Titrate the standardised acid with the sodium hydroxide solution to determine its concentration."},
  {num:2, label:"Q2: Qualitative Analysis — Hydrocarbons, Liquids & Melting Point", q:"Two gaseous hydrocarbons A and B (one unsaturated). Liquids C, D, E are ethanal, ethanoic acid and eugenol (not necessarily in order). Solids F and G are benzoic acid samples.\n\n(a) Describe a chemical test for unsaturation of A or B.\n(b)(i) D evaporates fastest at 20 C. Deduce identity of D. Describe a test to confirm D is easily oxidised.\n(ii) Water added to C and E: C gives white emulsion, E gives colourless solution. Identify C and E. Cyclohexane added to C — what is observed?\n(iii) Magnesium ribbon added to E. Write a balanced equation.\n(c)(i) Describe how to measure the melting point of benzoic acid.\n(ii) F has lower, broader melting range than G. Which is purer?\n(iii) Name a technique to purify impure benzoic acid.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Add bromine water (or acidified KMnO4) to a sample of the gas. If the bromine water decolourises (orange/brown to colourless), the gas is unsaturated. If no colour change, it is saturated.\n\n(b)(i) D = Ethanal (CH3CHO). It has the lowest boiling point (21 C) of the three liquids, so it evaporates most quickly.\nTest for easy oxidation: Add Fehling's solution (blue) and warm gently. A brick-red precipitate forms, confirming ethanal is readily oxidised. (Alternatively, use Tollens' reagent — a silver mirror forms.)\n\n(ii) C = Eugenol. Eugenol is insoluble in water and forms a white emulsion when shaken.\nE = Ethanoic acid. It dissolves completely in water to give a colourless solution.\nWhen cyclohexane is added to C (eugenol + water emulsion) and shaken: two layers form. The upper organic layer (cyclohexane + eugenol) separates from the lower aqueous layer.\n\n(iii) Equation: 2CH3COOH + Mg -> (CH3COO)2Mg + H2\n(Ethanoic acid reacts with magnesium to produce magnesium ethanoate and hydrogen gas.)\n\n(c)(i) Place a small sample of benzoic acid in a melting-point capillary tube. Attach to a thermometer in a melting-point apparatus (or Thiele tube with oil bath). Heat slowly while observing the crystals. Record the temperature range at which the solid melts.\n\n(ii) G is the purer sample. A purer substance has a higher, sharper melting point.\n\n(iii) Recrystallisation."},
  {num:3, label:"Q3: Rates — H2O2 Decomposition with MnO2 Catalyst", q:"H2O2 decomposition using MnO2 catalyst. Two runs (same conditions except catalyst from different suppliers A and B).\nData: Time (min): 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6.5, 8, 9.5, 11, 12.\nRun A volumes (cm3): 0, 25, 41, 54, 72, 78, 81, 82, 82, 82.\nRun B volumes (cm3): 0, 16, 28, 39, 57, 68, 76, 80, 82, 82.\n\n(a) Draw a labelled diagram of apparatus.\n(b) Plot graphs for both runs on the same axes.\n(c) Calculate the instantaneous rate for Run A at 4.0 minutes.\n(d) Which run (i) reached completion first, (ii) had the slower initial rate? What type of catalysis? How might catalyst A differ from B?\n(e) What hazard do the GHS pictograms indicate for (i) H2O2, (ii) MnO2?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Diagram: Conical flask containing H2O2 solution and MnO2 catalyst. Connected by delivery tube to a gas syringe (or inverted graduated cylinder over water) to collect and measure oxygen gas.\n\n(b) Both curves start at origin. Run A rises more steeply initially, levels off at 82 cm3 by about 9.5 min. Run B rises less steeply, also levels off at 82 cm3 but by about 11 min. Both reach the same final volume (same amount of H2O2).\n\n(c) Draw a tangent to curve A at t = 4.0 min. Pick two points on the tangent, e.g. (1.0, 39) and (6.0, 82).\nInstantaneous rate = (82 - 39) / (6.0 - 1.0) = 43 / 5.0 = 8.6 cm3 O2 per minute.\n(Accept 8.2-9.5 cm3/min depending on tangent drawn.)\n\n(d)(i) Run A reached completion first (levelled off at ~9.5 min vs ~11 min for B).\n(ii) Run B had the slower initial rate (less steep curve at start).\nType of catalysis: Heterogeneous (surface) catalysis — the solid MnO2 catalyst is in a different phase from the liquid H2O2.\nCatalyst A likely had a greater surface area (more finely divided / smaller particle size) than catalyst B. More surface area gives more active sites, faster initial rate, and earlier completion.\n\n(e)(i) H2O2: Oxidiser (oxidising agent — causes other substances to burn more readily).\n(ii) MnO2: Harmful / irritant (health hazard on contact or inhalation)."},
  {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions — Mixed Topics", q:"Answer eight of the following:\n(a) M2+ has 25 electrons and 32 neutrons. Find (i) atomic number, (ii) mass number.\n(b) Describe Thomson's plum-pudding model of the atom.\n(c) Explain why the first ionisation energy of oxygen is lower than that of nitrogen.\n(d) Distinguish between sigma and pi covalent bonding.\n(e) Write (i) the conjugate acid, (ii) the conjugate base, of H2PO4-.\n(f) What was Gay-Lussac's observation about gaseous reactions?\n(g) How many moles of cinnamaldehyde (Mr = 132) in 1.65 g?\n(h) How would you confirm the presence of sulfite ion in solution?\n(i) What is meant by the activation energy of a reaction?\n(j) Explain the principle of infrared spectrometry.\n(k) A: Describe a chemical method for removing acidic gases from chimney waste. OR B: Name the analytical technique for Dorothy Hodgkin's 1964 Nobel Prize.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) M2+ has 25 electrons, so the neutral atom has 27 electrons = 27 protons.\n(i) Atomic number = 27 (cobalt).\n(ii) Mass number = 27 + 32 = 59.\n\n(b) Thomson's plum-pudding model: The atom is a sphere of uniformly distributed positive charge, with small negative electrons embedded (scattered) throughout it, like plums in a pudding.\n\n(c) Nitrogen has a half-filled 2p sublevel (three 2p orbitals each with one electron). This half-filled configuration is relatively stable. Oxygen has one 2p orbital with a paired electron, which experiences extra electron-electron repulsion, making it easier to remove. So oxygen's first ionisation energy is lower than nitrogen's.\n\n(d) Sigma bond: formed by head-on (end-to-end) overlap of atomic orbitals along the bond axis. Pi bond: formed by sideways overlap of p orbitals above and below the bond axis. In a multiple bond, the sigma bond is formed first (stronger); the pi bond is weaker and only forms after a sigma bond.\n\n(e)(i) Conjugate acid of H2PO4-: H3PO4.\n(ii) Conjugate base of H2PO4-: HPO4(2-).\n\n(f) Gay-Lussac observed that when gases react, the volumes of reactants and products (measured at the same temperature and pressure) are in simple whole-number ratios.\n\n(g) Mr of cinnamaldehyde = 132.\nMoles = 1.65 / 132 = 0.0125 mol.\n\n(h) Add barium chloride (BaCl2) solution. A white precipitate forms (BaSO3). Add dilute hydrochloric acid — the precipitate dissolves. (This distinguishes sulfite from sulfate, whose precipitate does not dissolve in dilute HCl.)\n\n(i) Activation energy is the minimum combined energy that colliding particles must have for a reaction to take place between them.\n\n(j) Different molecules (bonds, functional groups) absorb infrared radiation at different characteristic frequencies (wavelengths). The pattern of absorption provides a 'fingerprint' that can identify the compound.\n\n(k) A: Scrubbing — pass the chimney waste gases through a reaction with limestone (CaCO3) or calcium oxide (CaO). The acidic gases (e.g. SO2) react with the basic calcium compound.\nCaCO3 + SO2 -> CaSO3 + CO2.\n\nB: X-ray crystallography."},
  {num:5, label:"Q5: Atomic Structure — Hydrogen Spectrum, Flame Tests & Orbitals", q:"(a) Diagram shows visible lines in the hydrogen spectrum and energy levels.\n(i) Distinguish between the ground state and excited states of the electron in hydrogen.\n(ii) How can the electron become excited?\n(iii) Explain the origin of the visible line series. What is its name?\n(iv) Explain why there is no yellow line in the hydrogen emission spectrum.\n\n(b) Describe how to carry out a flame test to confirm the presence of lithium in a salt sample.\n\n(c) Define an atomic orbital. Distinguish between a 2p orbital and a 2p sublevel.\nWrite the s, p electron configuration for calcium.\nExplain why the electron arrangement in calcium is 2, 8, 8, 2 and not 2, 8, 10.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) Ground state: The electron is in the lowest energy level (n = 1), the most stable state.\nExcited states: The electron has absorbed energy and occupies a higher energy level (n = 2, 3, 4, etc.) — an unstable arrangement.\n\n(ii) The electron can be excited by absorbing energy — e.g. heat, light, electrical energy, or electromagnetic radiation.\n\n(iii) When excited electrons fall back from higher energy levels (n = 3, 4, 5, 6) to n = 2, they emit photons of specific energies (frequencies). Each transition produces a line of a specific colour. The series of visible lines is called the Balmer series.\n\n(iv) There is no yellow line because none of the energy transitions from higher levels to n = 2 produce a photon with the frequency (energy) corresponding to yellow light. The energy gaps between levels correspond only to red, blue-green, blue/violet and purple — not yellow.\n\n(b) Clean a nichrome (or platinum) wire loop by dipping it in concentrated HCl and holding it in a Bunsen flame until no colour is observed. Dip the wire into the salt sample (or a solution of it) and hold it in the edge of the Bunsen flame. Lithium gives a red (crimson) flame colour.\n\n(c) An atomic orbital is a region of space around the nucleus of an atom where there is a high probability of finding an electron.\n\nA 2p orbital is a single dumbbell-shaped region of space that can hold a maximum of 2 electrons. The 2p sublevel consists of three 2p orbitals (2px, 2py, 2pz), holding a total of up to 6 electrons.\n\nCalcium electron configuration: 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2.\n\nWhy 2, 8, 8, 2 and not 2, 8, 10: The 4s sublevel is lower in energy than the 3d sublevel. Electrons fill the lowest available energy sublevels first (Aufbau principle). So after filling 3s and 3p (giving 8 in the third shell), the next two electrons enter 4s rather than 3d. The third energy level holds only 8 electrons (in 3s and 3p) before the fourth level begins to fill."},
  {num:6, label:"Q6: Oil Refining — Fractional Distillation, Reforming & Cracking", q:"(a) Name the oil refining process shown (naphtha, kerosene, etc.). What physical property separates hydrocarbons into naphtha?\n(b) Give a major use for (i) kerosene, (ii) residue.\n(c) Name the process converting octane to 2,2,4-trimethylpentane. Name the process converting octane to ethylbenzene (C8H18 -> C8H10 + 4H2). Explain why these conversions are desirable. Calculate the heat of reaction, given Hf(octane) = -250.1, Hf(ethylbenzene) = -12.5 kJ/mol.\n(d) Catalytic cracking of C18H38 gives octane, propene, and a third hydrocarbon. Deduce its molecular formula.\n(e) Draw the molecular structures of (i) octane, (ii) 2,2,4-trimethylpentane, (iii) ethylbenzene.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Fractional distillation.\nThe physical property is boiling point (boiling point range). Hydrocarbons with similar boiling points condense at the same level and are collected together as naphtha.\n\n(b)(i) Kerosene: jet fuel (aircraft fuel / heating oil).\n(ii) Residue: bitumen for road surfacing, or fuel for power stations.\n\n(c) Octane to 2,2,4-trimethylpentane: Isomerisation (reforming).\nOctane to ethylbenzene: Dehydrocyclisation (catalytic reforming).\n\nThese conversions are desirable because branched-chain and aromatic hydrocarbons have higher octane numbers than straight-chain alkanes. Higher octane number means the fuel burns more smoothly with less knocking in engines.\n\nHeat of reaction (Hess's Law):\ndelta H = sum Hf(products) - sum Hf(reactants)\n= [(-12.5) + 4(0)] - [(-250.1)]\n= -12.5 + 250.1\n= +237.6 kJ/mol.\n(The reaction is endothermic.)\n\n(d) C18H38 -> C8H18 + C3H6 + ?\nCarbon: 18 = 8 + 3 + x => x = 7.\nHydrogen: 38 = 18 + 6 + y => y = 14.\nThird molecule: C7H14 (an alkene, e.g. heptene).\n\n(e)(i) Octane: CH3CH2CH2CH2CH2CH2CH2CH3 (straight chain, 8 carbons).\n(ii) 2,2,4-trimethylpentane: CH3-C(CH3)2-CH2-CH(CH3)-CH3.\n(iii) Ethylbenzene: Benzene ring with a -CH2CH3 group attached."},
  {num:7, label:"Q7: Equilibrium — Contact Process (SO2 + O2)", q:"2SO2(g) + O2(g) <=> 2SO3(g), delta H = -196 kJ. V2O5 catalyst.\n\n(a) State Le Chatelier's principle. Predict and explain optimal (i) temperature, (ii) pressure for maximum SO3 yield. Why aren't the predicted temperature conditions used industrially?\n(b) State and explain the effect of the catalyst on the equilibrium yield of SO3.\n(c) 96 g SO2 + 24 g O2 in a 50 L container. At equilibrium, 112 g SO3 present.\nWrite the Kc expression. Calculate Kc.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) Le Chatelier's principle: If a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change in concentration, pressure, or temperature, the equilibrium shifts in the direction that tends to counteract (oppose) that change.\n\n(i) Temperature: Low temperature favours SO3 yield. The forward reaction is exothermic (delta H = -196 kJ), so decreasing temperature shifts equilibrium to the right (towards products), increasing SO3 yield.\n\n(ii) Pressure: High pressure favours SO3 yield. There are 3 moles of gas on the left (2 SO2 + 1 O2) but only 2 moles on the right (2 SO3). Increasing pressure shifts equilibrium towards the side with fewer gas moles — i.e. to the right.\n\nIndustrially, very low temperatures are not used because the rate of reaction would be too slow to be economical, even with a catalyst. A compromise temperature of about 450 C is used.\n\n(b) The catalyst has no effect on the equilibrium yield of SO3. A catalyst speeds up both the forward and reverse reactions equally. It allows equilibrium to be reached faster but does not change the position of equilibrium or the value of Kc.\n\n(c) Kc = [SO3]^2 / ([SO2]^2 x [O2])\n\nMoles at start:\nSO2: 96 / 64 = 1.5 mol\nO2: 24 / 32 = 0.75 mol\nSO3: 0 mol\n\nAt equilibrium:\nSO3 = 112 / 80 = 1.4 mol\nFrom stoichiometry: 1.4 mol SO3 formed from 1.4 mol SO2 and 0.7 mol O2.\nSO2 remaining = 1.5 - 1.4 = 0.1 mol\nO2 remaining = 0.75 - 0.7 = 0.05 mol\n\nConcentrations (volume = 50 L):\n[SO3] = 1.4 / 50 = 0.028 M\n[SO2] = 0.1 / 50 = 0.002 M\n[O2] = 0.05 / 50 = 0.001 M\n\nKc = (0.028)^2 / ((0.002)^2 x 0.001)\n= 7.84 x 10^-4 / (4 x 10^-6 x 10^-3)\n= 7.84 x 10^-4 / 4 x 10^-9\n= 196,000.\n\nKc = 1.96 x 10^5 (accept ~2.0 x 10^5)."},
  {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Reaction Scheme — Ethanol, Ethene, Polymerisation & Esters", q:"Reaction scheme: ethane -> chloroethane (with Cl2/UV), ethanol (A) -> ethene (using substance X), ethene -> polymer B.\n\n(a) Name A and polymer B.\n(b) Identify substance X used to convert A to ethene. What organic reaction type? How does the geometry around carbon change?\n(c) What reaction type converts ethane to chloroethane? Describe the mechanism in detail. Explain the effect of tetraethyllead Pb(C2H5)4.\n(d) Ester C is formed from A + methanoic acid (H2SO4 catalyst). Name C and draw its structure. Circle the carbonyl group. What reaction type occurs between NaOH and C?", a:"Answer:\n\n(a) A = Ethanol (C2H5OH).\nPolymer B = Poly(ethene) / polyethylene.\n\n(b) Substance X: Concentrated sulfuric acid (H2SO4) or aluminium oxide (Al2O3) — acts as a dehydrating agent.\nReaction type: Elimination (dehydration).\nGeometry change: Carbon atoms change from tetrahedral (109.5 degree bond angles in ethanol, sp3) to trigonal planar (120 degree bond angles in ethene, sp2). A C=C double bond forms.\n\n(c) Reaction type: Free radical substitution.\n\nMechanism (three stages):\nInitiation: Cl2 -> 2Cl* (UV light causes homolytic fission of the Cl-Cl bond, producing two chlorine free radicals.)\n\nPropagation:\nStep 1: Cl* + CH3CH3 -> CH3CH2* + HCl (chlorine radical abstracts a hydrogen atom from ethane, forming an ethyl radical and HCl.)\nStep 2: CH3CH2* + Cl2 -> CH3CH2Cl + Cl* (ethyl radical reacts with a Cl2 molecule, forming chloroethane and regenerating a chlorine radical.)\n\nTermination: Two radicals combine, e.g.:\nCl* + Cl* -> Cl2\nCH3CH2* + Cl* -> CH3CH2Cl\nCH3CH2* + CH3CH2* -> C4H10 (butane)\n\nEffect of tetraethyllead: Pb(C2H5)4 decomposes to produce ethyl radicals. These extra radicals would interfere with the chain reaction, acting as an inhibitor — they terminate propagation chains by combining with chlorine radicals, slowing the reaction.\n\n(d) Ester C = Ethyl methanoate (HCOOC2H5).\nStructure: H-C(=O)-O-CH2-CH3. The carbonyl group is C=O in the HCOO part.\nThe reaction of NaOH with ester C is called hydrolysis (saponification). NaOH breaks the ester bond to form sodium methanoate and ethanol."},
  {num:9, label:"Q9: Water — BOD, Eutrophication, Lead & Hardness", q:"(a) BOD test on lake water:\n(i) Under what conditions was the second sample stored?\n(ii) After what time interval was it tested?\n(iii) Define eutrophication and state a harmful effect.\n(iv) Suggest a cause of eutrophication.\n\n(b) Why is lead in drinking water a concern? Name an instrumental method for analysing heavy metals. How can lead be removed?\n\n(c) How does temporary hardness arise in rainwater flowing over limestone?\n(i) Write a balanced equation for limescale formation when this water is boiled.\n(ii) Explain how this water is deionised using a cation-exchange resin (RH) and an anion-exchange resin (R*OH).", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) The second sample was stored in the dark, at 20 C (room temperature), in a sealed (stoppered) bottle.\n(ii) After 5 days.\n(iii) Eutrophication is the excessive enrichment of water by nutrients (especially nitrates and phosphates), leading to rapid growth of algae and aquatic plants. Harmful effect: When the algae die, bacteria decompose them using up dissolved oxygen, causing fish and other aquatic organisms to suffocate and die.\n(iv) Run-off of artificial fertilisers from farmland, or discharge of sewage (detergents containing phosphates) into the waterway.\n\n(b) Lead is a cumulative poison. It damages the nervous system and brain, and is especially harmful to children's development. Even low levels can cause learning difficulties.\nInstrumental method: Atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS).\nRemoval: Pass the water through an ion-exchange resin, or through a filter containing activated carbon. The resin replaces Pb2+ ions with harmless ions (e.g. Na+ or H+).\n\n(c) Rainwater absorbs CO2 from the air, forming a weak carbonic acid solution. This acidic water reacts with limestone (CaCO3) to form soluble calcium hydrogencarbonate:\nCaCO3(s) + CO2(aq) + H2O(l) -> Ca(HCO3)2(aq).\nThis dissolved Ca(HCO3)2 causes temporary hardness.\n\n(i) When boiled: Ca(HCO3)2(aq) -> CaCO3(s) + H2O(l) + CO2(g).\n(The insoluble CaCO3 precipitate is limescale.)\n\n(ii) Deionisation:\nCation-exchange resin (RH): Replaces Ca2+ ions with H+ ions.\nCa2+ + 2RH -> CaR2 + 2H+\n\nAnion-exchange resin (R*OH): Replaces HCO3- ions with OH- ions.\nHCO3- + R*OH -> R*HCO3 + OH-\n\nThe released H+ and OH- combine to form water: H+ + OH- -> H2O.\nThe result is pure deionised water."},
  {num:10, label:"Q10: Acids & pH / Redox & Balancing / Isotopes & Half-life", q:"Answer any two of (a), (b), (c). (2 x 25 marks)\n\n(a)(i) Distinguish between a strong and a weak acid.\n(ii) Calculate the molarity of H2SO4 with pH 2.0. Calculate the molarity of a weak monobasic acid (Ka = 1.8 x 10^-4) with pH 2.0.\n(iii) Define Kw. Kw at 59 C = 9.0 x 10^-14. Find [H+] in pure water at this temperature.\n\n(b) Define reduction in terms of (i) electron transfer, (ii) change in oxidation number.\nAssign oxidation numbers in: ClO- + S2O3(2-) + OH- -> Cl- + SO4(2-) + H+\nIdentify (iii) the reducing agent, (iv) the oxidising agent.\nBalance either equation:\nClO- + S2O3(2-) + OH- -> Cl- + SO4(2-) + H+\nor ClO3- + Br- + H+ -> Cl- + Br2 + H2O\n\n(c)(i) What are isotopes? What is a radioisotope? Define half-life.\n(ii) John Joly (Irish scientist) contributions to radioactivity/geology.", a:"Answer:\n\n(a)(i) A strong acid is fully dissociated (ionised) in aqueous solution. A weak acid is only partially dissociated in aqueous solution — an equilibrium exists between the undissociated acid and its ions.\n\n(ii) pH 2.0 means [H+] = 10^-2 = 0.01 M.\nFor H2SO4 (dibasic, strong): H2SO4 -> 2H+ + SO4(2-).\n[H+] = 2 x molarity, so molarity = 0.01 / 2 = 0.005 M.\n\nFor the weak monobasic acid (Ka = 1.8 x 10^-4):\nKa = [H+]^2 / [HA] (assuming [H+] << [HA]).\n1.8 x 10^-4 = (0.01)^2 / [HA]\n[HA] = 10^-4 / (1.8 x 10^-4) = 0.556 M.\nMolarity of weak acid = 0.556 + 0.01 = approx. 0.56 M.\n\n(iii) Kw is the ionic product of water: Kw = [H+][OH-].\nIn pure water, [H+] = [OH-].\nKw = [H+]^2 = 9.0 x 10^-14.\n[H+] = sqrt(9.0 x 10^-14) = 3.0 x 10^-7 M.\n(Note: this is higher than 10^-7 because the temperature is above 25 C.)\n\n(b)(i) Reduction is the gain of electrons.\n(ii) Reduction is a decrease in oxidation number.\n\nIn ClO- + S2O3(2-) + OH- -> Cl- + SO4(2-) + H+:\nCl in ClO- is +1; Cl in Cl- is -1 (reduced).\nS in S2O3(2-) is +2; S in SO4(2-) is +6 (oxidised).\n\n(iii) Reducing agent: S2O3(2-) (thiosulfate — it is oxidised, so it reduces the other species).\n(iv) Oxidising agent: ClO- (hypochlorite — it is reduced, so it oxidises the other species).\n\nBalanced equation (ClO3- + Br- + H+ -> Cl- + Br2 + H2O):\nBr goes from -1 to 0 (loss of 1 e- per Br, 2 e- per Br2).\nCl goes from +5 to -1 (gain of 6 e-).\nTo balance electrons: 6 Br- needed per ClO3-.\nClO3- + 6Br- + 6H+ -> Cl- + 3Br2 + 3H2O.\n\n(c)(i) Isotopes are atoms of the same element (same atomic number) with different mass numbers (different numbers of neutrons).\nA radioisotope is an isotope with an unstable nucleus that undergoes spontaneous radioactive decay, emitting radiation.\nHalf-life is the time taken for half of the atoms of a radioactive isotope to decay (or for the activity to fall to half its original value).\n\n(ii) John Joly (1857-1933) was an Irish physicist at Trinity College Dublin. He pioneered the use of radioactivity to estimate the age of geological samples (rocks and minerals). He developed methods of measuring the amount of helium (from alpha decay) trapped in rocks to determine their age. He also contributed to radiation therapy for cancer treatment."},
],

  "2015": [
  // ── Section A ──
  {num:1, label:"Q1 – Volumetric Analysis: Iodine-Thiosulfate Titration",
   q:"I2 + 2S2O3(2-) -> 2I- + S4O6(2-)\\nA student titrated sodium thiosulfate against 25.0 cm3 of standard iodine solution.\\n\\n(a) Explain how iodine (non-polar, low water solubility) is brought into aqueous solution. (6)\\n(b) Describe procedure for measuring 25.0 cm3 of iodine solution into a conical flask. (12)\\n(c) Name indicator, when to add it, colour change at end point. (9)\\n(d) 6.35 g I2 in 500 cm3. Calculate: (i) moles I2 in 25.0 cm3, (ii) moles Na2S2O3 needed, (iii) molarity of Na2S2O3 if 17.85 cm3 used, (iv) concentration in g/L of Na2S2O3.5H2O crystals. (18)\\n(e) Why would distilled water give a more accurate result than deionised water? (5)",
   a:"(a) Iodine is dissolved by reacting it with potassium iodide (KI) solution. The iodine reacts with iodide ions to form the soluble triiodide ion:\\nI2 + I- -> I3- (KI3)\\nThis brings the non-polar iodine into aqueous solution.\\n\\n(b) Procedure:\\n1. Pour some iodine solution into a clean, dry beaker.\\n2. Use a 25 cm3 pipette, previously rinsed with deionised water and then with the iodine solution.\\n3. Fill the pipette using a pipette filler until the bottom of the meniscus is on the 25.0 cm3 mark, reading at eye level.\\n4. Allow the pipette to drain into the conical flask under gravity. Touch the tip against the wall of the flask. Do not blow out the last drop.\\n\\n(c) Indicator: Starch solution.\\nWhen to add: When the colour in the conical flask is a pale yellow (straw colour) — i.e. near the end point, when nearly all the iodine has been used up.\\nColour change at end point: Blue-black to colourless.\\n\\n(d)(i) Moles of I2 in 500 cm3 = 6.35 / 254 = 0.025 mol (Mr of I2 = 2 x 127 = 254)\\nMoles in 25.0 cm3 = 0.025 x (25/500) = 0.00125 mol\\n\\n(ii) From equation: 1 mol I2 reacts with 2 mol S2O3(2-)\\nMoles Na2S2O3 = 0.00125 x 2 = 0.0025 mol\\n\\n(iii) 0.0025 mol in 17.85 cm3\\nMolarity = 0.0025 / 0.01785 = 0.140 M\\n\\n(iv) Mr of Na2S2O3.5H2O = 23x2 + 32x2 + 16x3 + 5(18) = 46 + 64 + 48 + 90 = 248\\nConcentration = 0.140 x 248 = 34.7 g/L ≈ 35 g/L\\n\\n(e) Distilled water is purer than deionised water. Deionised water has only had ions removed (by ion exchange) — it could still contain non-ionic dissolved substances that could be oxidised or reduced, or could contain traces of chlorine (an oxidising agent). Distilled water has had all dissolved substances removed by evaporation and condensation, making it more suitable for redox titrations."},

  {num:2, label:"Q2 – Mandatory Experiment: Oxidation of Phenylmethanol to Benzoic Acid",
   q:"Phenylmethanol (benzyl alcohol) oxidised to benzoic acid using KMnO4 in basic conditions.\\nMnO4- -> MnO2 then MnO2 -> Mn2+ (with reagents A and B added for separation).\\n\\n(a) Describe appearance of phenylmethanol at room temperature. (5)\\n(b) Colour change with KMnO4/Na2CO3. Identify A and B. Changes observed. Oxidation number changes of Mn. (24)\\n(c) Recrystallisation: one way to maximise yield. How does recrystallisation affect melting point range (was 112-118°C)? (9)\\n(d) 2.7 cm3 phenylmethanol (density 1.04 g/cm3) gave 1.83 g benzoic acid. Calculate % yield. (12)",
   a:"(a) Phenylmethanol at room temperature: Colourless (or pale yellow), oily liquid.\\n\\n(b) Colour change: Purple (violet) KMnO4 solution changes to dark brown (muddy/black) — due to formation of MnO2 (manganese dioxide).\\n\\nA = HCl (hydrochloric acid) — added to dissolve the MnO2 precipitate and acidify the solution.\\nB = Na2SO3 (sodium sulfite) — added to reduce any remaining MnO4- to Mn2+.\\n\\nChanges observed: The dark brown colour disappears (mixture decolourises/becomes clear) on addition of A and B. On cooling, white crystals of benzoic acid form (precipitate/appear).\\n\\nOxidation number changes of Mn:\\nMnO4- (Mn = +7) -> MnO2 (Mn = +4) -> Mn2+ (Mn = +2)\\nOverall: +7 to +4 to +2.\\n\\n(c) To maximise yield of recrystallisation: Use the minimum amount of boiling water (solvent) to dissolve the crystals, and cool fully (place in ice/cold water) before collecting crystals.\\n\\nEffect on melting point range: The melting point would be raised (closer to the true value of 122°C) and the range would be narrower/sharper. Purer crystals have a higher, sharper melting point.\\n\\n(d) Mass of phenylmethanol = 2.7 x 1.04 = 2.808 g\\nMr of phenylmethanol (C6H5CH2OH) = 108\\nMoles = 2.808 / 108 = 0.026 mol\\n\\n1 mol phenylmethanol -> 1 mol benzoic acid\\nMr of benzoic acid (C6H5COOH) = 122\\nTheoretical yield = 0.026 x 122 = 3.172 g\\n\\n% yield = (1.83 / 3.172) x 100 = 57.7% ≈ 58%"},

  {num:3, label:"Q3 – Water Analysis: Swimming Pool Quality Testing",
   q:"Water quality data for pools A-D given (suspended solids, dissolved solids, hardness, free chlorine, pH). Pools A and D unsuitable.\\n\\n(a) Describe how to measure concentration of suspended solids in sample A. (12)\\n(b) Calculate mass in grams of dissolved solids in 250 cm3 of sample B (1400 ppm). (6)\\n(c) What solution was titrated to measure total hardness? Why did boiling reduce hardness result from 220 to 175 ppm? (9)\\n(d) Identify reagent to detect free chlorine. Describe use of comparator or colorimeter. (15)\\n(e) Which test results made A and D unsuitable? Problems for swimmers in (i) A, (ii) D? (8)",
   a:"(a) Measuring suspended solids:\\n1. Measure a known volume of the water sample.\\n2. Filter through a filter paper of known mass.\\n3. Dry the filter paper (in oven) with the residue.\\n4. Find the new mass of the filter paper and residue by reweighing.\\n5. The difference in mass gives the mass of suspended solids. Calculate concentration in ppm.\\n\\n(b) 1400 ppm = 1400 mg per litre = 1.4 g per litre\\nMass in 250 cm3 = 1.4 x (250/1000) = 0.35 g\\n\\n(c) Solution titrated: EDTA (ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid) solution.\\n\\nWhy lower after boiling: Boiling removed temporary hardness. The soluble calcium hydrogencarbonate (Ca(HCO3)2) decomposed on boiling to form insoluble calcium carbonate (CaCO3), which was removed by filtration. Only permanent hardness remained, giving a lower reading (175 vs 220 ppm CaCO3).\\nCa(HCO3)2 -> CaCO3 (precipitate) + H2O + CO2\\n\\n(d) Reagent: DPD (diethylphenylenediamine).\\n\\nUsing a comparator: Add a DPD tablet/sachet to the water sample in the comparator compartment. Stir and allow the colour to develop. Compare the colour with the standard colour chart/disc on the comparator. Read the free chlorine concentration (in ppm) from the chart.\\n\\nOR Using a colorimeter: Prepare a number of standard solutions of known chlorine concentration with DPD reagent. Measure their absorbance readings. Plot a calibration graph of concentration vs absorbance. Then measure the absorbance of the sample (with DPD added) and read the concentration from the graph.\\n\\n(e) The free chlorine results made A and D unsuitable.\\n- Pool A: Free chlorine is too low (0.1 ppm) — insufficient to kill microorganisms. Risk of infection/disease (bacteria, fungi, athlete's foot).\\n- Pool D: Free chlorine is too high (7.0 ppm) — this could cause eye irritation, skin burning, unpleasant taste/smell, breathing problems, or formation of harmful chloramines."},

  // ── Section B ──
  {num:4, label:"Q4 – Short Questions (Section B)",
   q:"Answer eight of (a)-(k). (50)\\n(a) How many (i) electrons, (ii) neutrons has Al3+ (27/13)?\\n(b) Why are relative atomic masses rarely whole numbers?\\n(c) What change in the nucleus during beta decay?\\n(d) Account for the difference in shapes of BF3 and NH3.\\n(e) 19.05 g Cu + N -> 20.45 g copper nitride. Deduce empirical formula.\\n(f) State Avogadro's law.\\n(g) Aspirin structure: circle the carboxylic acid group, rectangle around carbonyl of ester group.\\n(h) Identify element: (i) same period as Mg but larger atoms, (ii) same group but smaller atoms.\\n(i) Purpose of tertiary sewage treatment?\\n(j) Calculate moles of N2 at P=1.85x10^5 Pa, T=293 K, V=6.50x10^-5 m3 (tennis ball). Answer to 1 sig fig.\\n(k) A: Two ways nitrogen is fixed in nature. OR B: Second and fifth stages of polystyrene recycling.",
   a:"(a)(i) Electrons in Al3+: Aluminium has 13 electrons, loses 3 to form Al3+, so 10 electrons.\\n(ii) Neutrons in Al-27: Mass number - atomic number = 27 - 13 = 14 neutrons.\\n\\n(b) Relative atomic masses are rarely whole numbers because they are the weighted average of the mass numbers of all naturally occurring isotopes of the element. Since most elements have a mixture of isotopes in non-whole-number proportions, the average is rarely a whole number.\\n\\n(c) During beta decay: A neutron in the nucleus changes into a proton, and an electron (beta particle) is emitted.\\nn -> p + e-\\nThe atomic number increases by one, mass number stays the same.\\n\\n(d) BF3: Boron has 3 bond pairs of electrons and no lone pairs — gives trigonal planar shape (120°).\\nNH3: Nitrogen has 3 bond pairs and 1 lone pair — the lone pair repels bond pairs more strongly, giving a pyramidal shape (107°).\\nThe presence of the lone pair in NH3 (4 electron pairs total) vs no lone pair in BF3 (3 electron pairs) accounts for the different shapes.\\n\\n(e) Mass of Cu = 19.05 g; Mass of N = 20.45 - 19.05 = 1.40 g\\nMoles: Cu = 19.05/63.5 = 0.300; N = 1.40/14 = 0.100\\nRatio: 0.300 : 0.100 = 3 : 1\\nEmpirical formula: Cu3N\\n\\n(f) Avogadro's law: Equal volumes of gases contain equal numbers of molecules (particles/moles) at the same temperature and pressure.\\n\\n(g) In the aspirin structure:\\n- Circle the -COOH group (carboxylic acid).\\n- Rectangle around the C=O (carbonyl) part of the -O-CO-CH3 ester group (not the whole ester, just the C=O).\\n\\n(h)(i) Same period as Mg (Period 3) but larger atoms: Sodium (Na) — it is to the left of Mg and has a larger atomic radius.\\n(ii) Same group as Mg (Group 2) but smaller atoms: Beryllium (Be) — it is above Mg in Group 2.\\n\\n(i) Purpose of tertiary sewage treatment: To reduce (remove) nitrates and phosphates from the water, thereby preventing eutrophication (algal bloom) in receiving waters downstream.\\n\\n(j) Using PV = nRT:\\nn = PV / RT\\nn = (1.85 x 10^5 x 6.50 x 10^-5) / (8.3 x 293)\\nn = 12.025 / 2431.9\\nn = 0.00494 mol\\nTo 1 significant figure: n ≈ 0.005 mol (5 x 10^-3)\\n\\n(k) A: Two ways nitrogen is fixed in nature:\\n1. Lightning (converts N2 + O2 to nitrogen oxides).\\n2. Nitrogen-fixing bacteria (e.g. Rhizobium in root nodules of legumes convert N2 to NH3/ammonium).\\n\\nB: The five stages of polystyrene recycling are: (1) sorting, (2) shredding, (3) washing, (4) drying, (5) re-extrusion (re-moulding).\\nSo the second stage is shredding and the fifth is re-extrusion."},

  {num:5, label:"Q5 – Atomic Theory: Cathode Rays, Rutherford, Bohr, Electron Configuration",
   q:"(a) One way to detect cathode rays. Name the scientist (~1897) who identified them and measured charge-to-mass ratio. Name the particle. (9)\\n(b) Name one 1903 Nobel Prize winner (radioactivity). (3)\\n(c) Rutherford's gold foil experiment: three observations. How he deduced nucleus is (i) positive, (ii) small and dense. (15)\\n(d) Explain how the line emission spectrum of hydrogen provides evidence for energy levels. (12)\\n(e) Element giving blue-green colour in fireworks. (3)\\n(f) Electron configuration of calcium (s, p). One difference between 2s and 3s electron. (8)",
   a:"(a) Detecting cathode rays: Fluorescence/glow on the glass wall, or a paddle wheel rotates showing they have momentum, or deflection by a magnetic/electric field, or shadow cast by an object placed in the path.\\n\\nScientist: J.J. Thomson (1897).\\nParticle: The electron.\\n\\n(b) One of the 1903 Nobel Prize winners: Henri Becquerel, or Marie Curie, or Pierre Curie.\\n\\n(c) Three observations from Rutherford's gold foil experiment:\\n1. Most alpha particles passed straight through the gold foil undeflected.\\n2. Some alpha particles were deflected at large angles.\\n3. A very few alpha particles were reflected (deflected straight back) along their original paths.\\n\\n(i) Nucleus is positive: Because the positively-charged alpha particles were repelled (deflected) — this shows the nucleus must also be positively charged.\\n\\n(ii) Nucleus is small and dense: Most alpha particles passed straight through, showing the atom is mostly empty space. The few that were reflected back experienced a very strong force, showing the positive charge and mass are concentrated in a very small, dense region (the nucleus).\\n\\n(d) In the ground state, the hydrogen electron occupies the lowest available energy level. When energy is supplied (heat, electricity, light), the electron can jump to a higher energy level (excited state). The excited state is unstable, so the electron falls back to a lower energy level. The energy difference is emitted as a photon of light of a specific frequency (E = hf). Different transitions produce photons of different frequencies, giving rise to distinct lines in the emission spectrum. Each line corresponds to a specific energy transition between two energy levels, providing evidence that energy levels are discrete (quantised).\\n\\n(e) Element giving blue-green colour: Copper (also barium or boron acceptable).\\n\\n(f) Electron configuration of calcium (Z=20):\\n1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 (or [Ar]4s2)\\n\\nOne difference between a 2s and 3s electron: The 2s electron has less energy (is closer to the nucleus/more strongly attracted) than the 3s electron. Alternatively: the 2s electron is less shielded from the nucleus, or the probability distribution (orbital size) of 2s is smaller than 3s."},

  {num:6, label:"Q6 – Fuels: Crude Oil Fractions, Octane, Heat of Formation, Hydrogen",
   q:"(a) What property determines which fraction a hydrocarbon appears in? (5)\\n(b)(i) Name the fraction used as jet fuel. (ii) Process to convert it to smaller molecules. (6)\\n(c) Two reference hydrocarbons for octane numbers. (6)\\n(d) Two advantages of adding oxygenates (e.g. ethanol) to petrol. (6)\\n(e) Calculate heat of formation of ethanol given: Hc(ethanol) = -1370.7, Hf(CO2) = -393.5, Hf(H2O) = -285.8 kJ/mol. (12)\\n(f)(i) Two properties making hydrogen a good fuel for Space Shuttle. (ii) One disadvantage. (iii) Two ways H2 is produced industrially. (15)",
   a:"(a) The property that determines which fraction: Relative molecular mass (or boiling/condensing point, or size of molecules, or number of carbon atoms, or extent of intermolecular/van der Waals forces).\\n\\n(b)(i) Fraction used as jet fuel: Kerosene (paraffin).\\n(ii) Process: Catalytic cracking.\\n\\n(c) The two reference hydrocarbons:\\n1. Heptane (n-heptane) — assigned octane number 0.\\n2. 2,2,4-trimethylpentane (isooctane) — assigned octane number 100.\\n\\n(d) Two advantages of adding oxygenates to petrol:\\n1. Increases the octane number (reduces knocking/pre-ignition).\\n2. Reduces pollution / less harmful emissions / more complete combustion / cleaner burning / eliminates need for lead compounds.\\n\\n(e) Combustion of ethanol: C2H5OH + 3O2 -> 2CO2 + 3H2O\\n\\nUsing Hess's law:\\nHc = Sum Hf(products) - Sum Hf(reactants)\\n-1370.7 = [2(-393.5) + 3(-285.8)] - [Hf(ethanol) + 0]\\n-1370.7 = [-787.0 + (-857.4)] - Hf(ethanol)\\n-1370.7 = -1644.4 - Hf(ethanol)\\nHf(ethanol) = -1644.4 + 1370.7\\nHf(ethanol) = -273.7 kJ/mol\\n\\n(f)(i) Two properties making H2 a good fuel:\\n1. Very light (low density/low molecular mass) — important for rocketry.\\n2. High energy content per kilogram (high calorific value).\\n(Also: clean/non-polluting — only product is water.)\\n\\n(ii) Disadvantage: Difficult to store/transport (leaks easily, must be liquefied at very low temperatures, explosive, low energy content per litre/unit volume).\\n\\n(iii) Two ways H2 is produced industrially:\\n1. Steam reforming of natural gas (methane): CH4 + H2O -> CO + 3H2\\n2. Electrolysis of water (or electrolysis of brine/NaCl solution).\\n(Also: by-product of catalytic cracking.)"},

  {num:7, label:"Q7 – Rates of Reaction: Catalysis, Activation Energy, Reaction Profile",
   q:"(a) What is a catalyst? (5)\\n(b) How does bonding type influence rate? How to reduce rate of a solution reaction? (9)\\n(c) Methanol oxidation using Pt wire: one observation, two major products. (9)\\n(d) Term for attachment of molecules to solid surface? Explain how this affects rate. How does a catalyst poison (sulfur) interfere? (9)\\n(e) Explain activation energy. Sketch reaction profile for exothermic reaction showing DeltaH and EA. (18)",
   a:"(a) A catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without being consumed (used up) in the reaction. It works by providing an alternative reaction pathway with lower activation energy.\\n\\n(b) Bonding type and rate: Ionic compounds in aqueous solution dissociate into ions, which react very quickly (almost instantaneously) because no bonds need to be broken — the ions are free to collide and react. Covalent compounds react more slowly because covalent bonds must be broken, which requires more energy.\\n\\nTo reduce the rate of a reaction in solution: Lower the temperature (or dilute/decrease concentration of reactants).\\n\\n(c) Observation: The platinum wire glows red (incandescence) — the oxidation of methanol on the platinum surface is exothermic.\\nTwo major products: Methanal (formaldehyde, HCHO) and carbon dioxide (CO2). (Water is also produced.)\\n\\n(d) Term: Adsorption (not absorption).\\n\\nHow adsorption affects rate: Methanol molecules adsorb onto the surface of the platinum catalyst. This weakens the bonds within the methanol molecules (lowers the activation energy), making them more reactive. The reactant molecules are held in a favourable orientation on the surface, increasing the likelihood of successful reaction.\\n\\nCatalyst poison: Sulfur molecules adsorb strongly onto the active sites of the platinum catalyst surface, blocking these sites so that methanol molecules cannot adsorb there. This prevents the catalyst from functioning, reducing or stopping the catalytic activity.\\n\\n(e) Activation energy: The minimum energy that colliding reactant molecules must possess in order to react (for a successful collision to occur). It is the energy barrier that must be overcome for a reaction to proceed.\\n\\nReaction profile diagram for exothermic reaction:\\n- x-axis: Reaction progress (course of reaction)\\n- y-axis: Energy (potential energy)\\n- Reactants are at a higher energy than products.\\n- A peak (hump) between reactants and products represents the transition state.\\n- EA = the energy difference from the reactant level up to the peak (activation energy).\\n- DeltaH = the energy difference from reactants down to products (negative for exothermic — products lower than reactants)."},

  {num:8, label:"Q8 – Organic Chemistry: Propene Reaction Scheme, Isomers, Intermolecular Forces",
   q:"Reaction scheme: C3H6 (A=propene) --X--> CH3CHOHCH3 (B=propan-2-ol) --Y--> CH3COCH3 (C=propanone).\\n\\n(a) IUPAC names for A, B, C. (8)\\n(b) Name the addition polymer from compound A. (3)\\n(c) Identify bonds broken and bonds formed in conversion Y. (9)\\n(d) An isomer of B can be made from an aldehyde. Name the isomer and aldehyde, draw their structures. How to convert aldehyde to isomer? (18)\\n(e) Boiling points: -48, 56, 82°C. Assign to A, B, C with justification (intermolecular forces). (12)",
   a:"(a) A (C3H6): Propene.\\nB (CH3CHOHCH3): Propan-2-ol.\\nC (CH3COCH3): Propanone (acetone).\\n\\n(b) Addition polymer from propene: Polypropene (polypropylene).\\n\\n(c) Conversion Y: Propan-2-ol is oxidised to propanone.\\nBonds broken: Two C-H bonds (one on the OH-bearing carbon and one O-H bond).\\nBonds formed: One C=O bond (the second bond of the carbonyl, converting C-OH to C=O) and H atoms are removed.\\nMore precisely: a C-H bond and an O-H bond are broken; a C=O pi bond is formed (and H2O is released with the oxidising agent).\\n\\n(d) Isomer of propan-2-ol: Propan-1-ol (CH3CH2CH2OH) — same molecular formula C3H8O but a primary alcohol.\\n\\nAldehyde: Propanal (CH3CH2CHO).\\nStructure of propanal: CH3-CH2-C(=O)H\\nStructure of propan-1-ol: CH3-CH2-CH2-OH\\n\\nConversion: The aldehyde (propanal) can be reduced to propan-1-ol by:\\n- Catalytic hydrogenation (H2 with Ni catalyst), or\\n- Using NaBH4 (sodium borohydride), or\\n- Using LiAlH4 (lithium aluminium hydride).\\n\\n(e) Assigning boiling points:\\n- A (propene, C3H6): bp = -48°C. Propene is a small non-polar hydrocarbon with only weak van der Waals forces. Lowest boiling point.\\n- C (propanone, CH3COCH3): bp = 56°C. Propanone has a polar C=O group, giving rise to permanent dipole-dipole interactions (stronger than van der Waals alone but no hydrogen bonding since there is no O-H or N-H).\\n- B (propan-2-ol, CH3CHOHCH3): bp = 82°C. Propan-2-ol has an -OH group, allowing hydrogen bonding between molecules. Hydrogen bonds are the strongest intermolecular force among these three, giving the highest boiling point."},

  {num:9, label:"Q9 – Acids, Bases & pH: Titration Curve, Ka Calculation",
   q:"(a) Define acid: (i) Arrhenius, (ii) Bronsted-Lowry. (6)\\n(b) Define pH. State one limitation. (9)\\nNaOH titrated into ethanoic acid. pH data given for the titration curve.\\n(c) Graph the pH curve. (15)\\n(d)(i) Calculate initial concentration of ethanoic acid (Ka = 1.8 x 10^-5). (ii) From graph, volume of NaOH for neutralisation. (12)\\n(e) Suitable indicator? Justify from pH curve. (8)",
   a:"(a)(i) Arrhenius acid: A substance that produces hydrogen ions (H+) when dissolved in water.\\n(ii) Bronsted-Lowry acid: A proton (H+) donor — a substance that donates a proton to another substance.\\n\\n(b) pH = -log10[H+] (or -log10[H3O+]).\\nOne limitation: The pH scale does not apply to non-aqueous solutions / only applies to aqueous solutions. (Or: pH has no meaning for very concentrated solutions above ~14 M.)\\n\\n(c) Plot pH (y-axis) vs volume of NaOH added (x-axis).\\nData points: (0, 3.3), (2, 4.1), (5, 4.5), (10, 4.8), (15, 5.1), (17.5, 5.6), (19, 6.2), (19.5, 6.6), (19.8, 7.0), (20.0, 8.9), (20.2, 10.7), (20.5, 11.1), (22, 11.6), (25.5, 12.1), (30, 12.3), (40, 12.4).\\nThe curve shows a gradual rise, then a very steep jump at ~20 cm3 (equivalence point at pH ≈ 8.9), then levels off.\\n\\n(d)(i) Initial pH = 3.3, so [H+] = 10^(-3.3) = 5.01 x 10^-4 M\\n\\nFor weak acid: Ka = [H+]^2 / c (assuming [H+] << c)\\nc = [H+]^2 / Ka = (5.01 x 10^-4)^2 / (1.8 x 10^-5)\\n= 2.51 x 10^-7 / 1.8 x 10^-5\\n= 0.0139 M ≈ 0.014 M\\n\\nOr more precisely: Ka = [H+]^2 / (c - [H+])\\n1.8 x 10^-5 = (5.01 x 10^-4)^2 / (c - 5.01 x 10^-4)\\nc ≈ 0.014 M\\n\\n(ii) From the graph: The neutralisation occurs at the equivalence point — the steepest part of the curve. This occurs at approximately 20.0 cm3 of NaOH.\\n\\n(e) Suitable indicator: Phenolphthalein.\\nJustification: This is a weak acid-strong base titration, so the equivalence point is above pH 7 (at approximately pH 8.9). Phenolphthalein changes colour in the range pH 8.2-10.0 (colourless to pink), which falls within the steep portion of the pH curve. This means a single drop of NaOH will trigger a clear colour change at the equivalence point.\\n(Methyl orange would not be suitable as it changes colour at pH 3.1-4.4, which is in the buffering region of the curve.)"},

  {num:10, label:"Q10 – Mixed: Benzene/Unsaturation, Electrolysis of Copper, Silicon Chemistry",
   q:"Answer any two of (a), (b), (c). (2 x 25)\\n\\n(a) Distinguish saturated and unsaturated hydrocarbons. Test for unsaturation. (13)\\nBenzene: how many pi-electrons? Does Kekule structure correctly describe (i) number, (ii) distribution of pi-electrons? Experimental evidence. (12)\\n\\n(b) Electrolysis to purify copper:\\n(i) Explain with labelled diagram. (15)\\n(ii) Balanced equations at electrodes. (6)\\n(iii) What happens to impurities? (4)\\n\\n(c) Silicon dioxide + Mg -> magnesium silicide. Magnesium silicide dissolves in HCl to produce silane (SiH4).\\nWrite equations. Calculate volume of silane at room T&P. Other questions on silicon chemistry.",
   a:"(a) Saturated hydrocarbon: Contains only single bonds (C-C and C-H) between carbon atoms. All bonds are sigma bonds.\\nUnsaturated hydrocarbon: Contains at least one double (C=C) or triple (C≡C) bond between carbon atoms.\\n\\nTest for unsaturation: Add bromine water (Br2(aq)) to the hydrocarbon.\\n- If unsaturated: The orange/brown bromine water is rapidly decolourised (addition reaction occurs across the double/triple bond).\\n- If saturated: No decolourisation — bromine water remains orange/brown.\\n\\nBenzene has 6 pi-electrons (one from each of the 6 carbon atoms).\\n\\n(i) Number: The Kekule structure correctly predicts 6 pi-electrons (3 double bonds x 2 electrons each = 6).\\n\\n(ii) Distribution: The Kekule structure does NOT correctly describe the distribution of pi-electrons. Kekule shows alternating single and double bonds (3 localised C=C), but in reality the 6 pi-electrons are delocalised (spread evenly) around the entire ring in a cloud above and below the plane.\\n\\nExperimental evidence: Benzene does not readily undergo addition reactions (like alkenes do). For example, it does not decolourise bromine water. Also, X-ray diffraction shows all C-C bond lengths in benzene are equal (1.40 A), intermediate between a single bond (1.54 A) and a double bond (1.34 A). If Kekule's structure were correct, there would be alternating short and long bonds.\\n\\n(b)(i) Electrolysis of copper (purification):\\n- Anode (positive electrode): impure copper.\\n- Cathode (negative electrode): pure copper (thin strip).\\n- Electrolyte: copper(II) sulfate solution (CuSO4) acidified with sulfuric acid.\\n- Impure copper dissolves at the anode, Cu2+ ions travel through the solution, and pure copper is deposited at the cathode.\\n\\n(ii) Electrode equations:\\nAnode (oxidation): Cu(s) -> Cu2+(aq) + 2e-\\nCathode (reduction): Cu2+(aq) + 2e- -> Cu(s)\\n\\n(iii) Impurities: More reactive metals (e.g. Zn, Fe) dissolve into solution as ions but are not deposited at the cathode. Less reactive metals (e.g. Ag, Au, Pt) do not dissolve — they fall to the bottom as 'anode sludge' (anode mud), which can be collected and is often valuable.\\n\\n(c) Silicon dioxide reacts with magnesium:\\n2Mg + SiO2 -> Mg2Si + ? (or: 4Mg + SiO2 -> 2MgO + Mg2Si)\\nThe dark blue magnesium silicide dissolves in HCl:\\nMg2Si + 4HCl -> 2MgCl2 + SiH4 (silane gas)\\nSilane (SiH4) is a gaseous product. At room temperature and pressure, 1 mole of gas occupies 24.0 litres. Volume calculations follow from the stoichiometry of the reactions."}
],

  "2014": [
  // ── Section A ──
  {num:1, label:"Q1 – Volumetric Analysis: Water of Crystallisation in Washing Soda",
   q:"A batch of washing soda crystals (hydrated sodium carbonate, Na2CO3.xH2O) had lost some water of crystallisation by efflorescence. A 2.50 g sample was dissolved in deionised water and made up to 250 cm3. 25.0 cm3 portions were titrated with 0.10 M HCl. Mean titre = 21.6 cm3.\\n\\n2HCl + Na2CO3 -> 2NaCl + H2O + CO2\\n\\n(a) Explain the underlined term (previously standardised). (5)\\n(b) Describe how the chemist dissolved the sample and made the solution up to exactly 250 cm3. (12)\\n(c) State one precaution near the end point and explain how it improves accuracy. (6)\\n(d) Name a suitable indicator. State the colour change at the end point. (9)\\n(e) Calculate the concentration of Na2CO3 in (i) mol/L, (ii) g/L. (9)\\n(f) Calculate the percentage water of crystallisation and the value of x. (9)",
   a:"(a) Previously standardised means the concentration (molarity) of the HCl was found by another titration (or colorimetry).\\n\\n(b) The crystals were washed (rinsed) into a beaker using deionised water and stirred to dissolve. The solution was poured through a funnel into a 250 cm3 volumetric flask, and the beaker was rinsed several times with deionised water, adding the rinsings to the flask. The last few drops of deionised water were added drop by drop (using a dropper) to bring the bottom of the meniscus level with the 250 cm3 mark, reading at eye level.\\n\\n(c) Precaution: Add the HCl drop by drop (slowly) near the end point.\\nExplanation: This ensures the end point is detected precisely and not overshot — one drop of acid would change the indicator colour near the end point.\\n\\n(d) Suitable indicator: methyl orange.\\nColour change: orange/yellow to red (pink).\\n(Methyl red also acceptable: yellow to red.)\\n\\n(e)(i) Using C1V1/n1 = C2V2/n2:\\n25.0 x M / 1 = 21.6 x 0.10 / 2\\nM = (21.6 x 0.10) / (2 x 25.0) = 2.16 / 50.0\\nBut the mole ratio is 2 HCl : 1 Na2CO3, so:\\n25.0 x M = 21.6 x 0.10 / 2\\nM = 0.0432 mol/L\\n\\n(ii) Concentration in g/L = 0.0432 x 106 = 4.58 g/L (Mr of Na2CO3 = 106)\\n\\n(f) Hydrated solution = 2.50 g / 250 cm3 = 10 g/L\\nAnhydrous Na2CO3 = 4.58 g/L\\nWater content = 10 - 4.58 = 5.42 g/L\\n\\n% water = (5.42 / 10) x 100 = 54.2%\\n\\nTo find x:\\n5.42 / 4.58 = 18x / 106\\nx = (5.42 x 106) / (4.58 x 18) = 574.52 / 82.44 = 6.97\\nx ≈ 7\\n\\nThe formula is Na2CO3.7H2O (the crystals lost some water from the original Na2CO3.10H2O)."},

  {num:2, label:"Q2 – Mandatory Experiment: Preparation of Soap",
   q:"A student prepared soap by refluxing 4.45 g of glyceryl tristearate with excess NaOH, anti-bumping material and ethanol. Four stages: (1) Reflux, (2) Distil, (3) Decant into brine, (4) Filter.\\n\\nglyceryl tristearate + 3NaOH -> 3 C17H35COONa (soap) + co-product\\n\\n(a) Purpose of refluxing? Name the type of reaction. (8)\\n(b) What substance was removed by distillation? (3)\\n(c) Explain the function of brine. (6)\\n(d) Why wash the soap? How? (6)\\n(e) Draw/name the co-product. Where was it at the end? (9)\\n(f) Calculate maximum yield of soap in grams. (12)\\n(g) How does sodium stearate dissolve both oils and salts in sweat? (6)",
   a:"(a) Purpose of refluxing: To allow time for the (slow) reaction to reach completion and to maximise yield — heating speeds up the reaction without losing volatile ethanol (the solvent).\\nType of reaction: Base-catalysed hydrolysis (saponification).\\n\\n(b) Ethanol was removed by distillation in Stage 2.\\n\\n(c) Brine (concentrated salt solution) was used to precipitate (separate/isolate) the soap, since soap is insoluble in brine (salting out).\\n\\n(d) Why: To remove excess sodium hydroxide, which is corrosive and could burn the skin when the soap is used.\\nHow: Wash the soap with brine or ice-cold water.\\n\\n(e) Co-product: Glycerol (propane-1,2,3-triol).\\nStructure: HOCH2-CHOH-CH2OH\\nLocation: In the brine/filtrate.\\n\\n(f) Mr of glyceryl tristearate = 890\\nMoles of fat = 4.45 / 890 = 0.005 mol\\nFrom equation: 1 mol fat gives 3 mol soap.\\nMoles of soap = 0.005 x 3 = 0.015 mol\\nMr of sodium stearate (C17H35COONa) = 306\\nMass of soap = 0.015 x 306 = 4.59 g\\n\\n(g) Sodium stearate has a long hydrocarbon tail (C17H35-) which is non-polar (hydrophobic) — this dissolves non-polar oils. The ionic head (-COO-Na+) is hydrophilic — this is attracted to (dissolves) the ionic salts in sweat. This dual structure allows soap to act as an emulsifier."},

  {num:3, label:"Q3 – Mandatory Experiment: Rate of Reaction vs Temperature (Na2S2O3 + HCl)",
   q:"Effect of temperature on rate of reaction between 0.05 M Na2S2O3 and excess 3 M HCl. Data given at temperatures 0-57°C with times and 1/time values.\\n\\n(a) Explain the term rate of reaction. (5)\\n(b)(i) Describe and explain the change observed. (ii) How was this used to get reaction times? (12)\\n(c) Plot graph of rate (1/time) vs temperature. (12)\\n(d) Describe and explain the relationship between rate and temperature. (9)\\n(e) Use graph to find reaction time at 35°C. (6)\\n(f) Effect on reaction times if 0.025 M Na2S2O3 used instead? Justify. (6)",
   a:"(a) Rate of reaction is the change in concentration of a reactant (or product) per unit time.\\n\\n(b)(i) A cloudiness (precipitate) is observed — this is due to the formation of sulfur (S) as a product.\\n(ii) The conical flask was placed on a cross (mark/print on paper). The time was noted (stopwatch started) when the cross underneath the flask became invisible (obscured by sulfur precipitate).\\n\\n(c) Graph: Plot temperature on x-axis, rate (1/time in s-1) on y-axis. Plot the six data points:\\n(0, 0.001), (12, 0.002), (23, 0.005), (30, 0.010), (39, 0.019), (47, 0.030), (57, 0.050).\\nDraw a smooth exponential curve through the points (not a straight line).\\n\\n(d) The rate increases with temperature, and the increase is exponential — for every steady rise in temperature (e.g. ~10°C), the rate roughly doubles/multiplies by a constant factor.\\nExplanation: At higher temperatures, more collisions have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy (more effective/successful collisions).\\n\\n(e) From the graph at 35°C, the rate ≈ 0.015 s-1 (approximately).\\nReaction time = 1 / 0.015 ≈ 67 s (accept 66-72 s).\\n\\n(f) The reaction times would double (reaction would be twice as slow).\\nJustification: The rate is directly proportional to the thiosulfate concentration. Halving the concentration halves the rate, so the time doubles."},

  // ── Section B ──
  {num:4, label:"Q4 – Short Questions (Section B)",
   q:"Answer eight of (a)-(k). (50)\\n(a) Flame test colours: (i) barium, (ii) lithium?\\n(b) Describe Thomson's plum pudding model.\\n(c) Balanced nuclear equation for beta decay of Fr-223.\\n(d) State Heisenberg's uncertainty principle.\\n(e) How many (i) sigma bonds, (ii) pi bonds in a nitrogen molecule?\\n(f) What is meant by one mole of a substance?\\n(g) Empirical formula: 40% S, 60% O by mass.\\n(h) Calculate daily mass of KI to supply 0.15 mg of I- ion (RDA).\\n(i) Two structural features of hydrocarbons with high octane numbers.\\n(j) Two processes in primary sewage treatment.\\n(k) A: Two uses for O2 from liquid air fractionation. OR B: Two advantages of anodising aluminium.",
   a:"(a)(i) Barium: green (yellow-green).\\n(ii) Lithium: crimson (red).\\n\\n(b) Thomson's plum pudding model: A mass of positively-charged material (like a pudding) with small negatively-charged electrons scattered (embedded) throughout it.\\n\\n(c) Fr(223,87) -> Ra(223,88) + e(0,-1)\\nFrancium-223 decays by beta emission to radium-223 plus a beta particle (electron).\\n\\n(d) Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle: The position (location) and momentum (velocity/speed) of an electron cannot both be measured (known) simultaneously with accuracy.\\n\\n(e)(i) 1 sigma bond.\\n(ii) 2 pi bonds.\\n(N≡N: the triple bond consists of 1 sigma + 2 pi bonds.)\\n\\n(f) One mole of a substance is the amount containing the same number of particles as there are atoms in 12 g (0.012 kg) of carbon-12 — i.e. 6.0 x 10^23 particles (Avogadro's number).\\n\\n(g) S: 40/32 = 1.25; O: 60/16 = 3.75\\nRatio: 1.25 : 3.75 = 1 : 3\\nEmpirical formula: SO3\\n\\n(h) Mr of KI = 39 + 127 = 166\\nFraction of I in KI = 127/166\\nMass of KI needed = 0.15 x (166/127) = 0.15 x 1.307 = 0.196 mg\\n\\n(i) Two features of high octane hydrocarbons:\\n1. Short chains\\n2. Branches (branched structure)\\n(Also: rings/cyclic/aromatic compounds.)\\n\\n(j) Two primary sewage treatment processes:\\n1. Screening\\n2. Settlement (sedimentation) / passing over grit channels (traps)\\n\\n(k) A: Two uses for O2: steel-making; medical/breathing aid (or welding, diving, rocket fuel).\\nB: Two advantages of anodising aluminium: corrosion resistance/protection; can be dyed (coloured)."},

  {num:5, label:"Q5 – Atomic Structure: Energy Levels, Electron Configuration, Ionisation Energy",
   q:"(a) Name the scientist (Google doodle). Distinguish between energy level and atomic orbital. Write electron configuration of silicon (s, p). How many (i) main energy levels, (ii) orbitals occupied? (26)\\n(b) Define first ionisation energy. Explain why silicon's 1st IE is (i) greater than aluminium's, (ii) less than carbon's. (15)\\n(c) Successive ionisation energies graph — explain how it provides evidence for energy levels. Other experimental evidence? (9)",
   a:"(a) Scientist: Niels Bohr.\\n\\nEnergy level: A fixed (discrete) energy value that an electron can have in an atom — an orbit/shell which electrons of a specific energy can occupy.\\n\\nAtomic orbital: A region of space around the nucleus where there is a high probability (≥95%) of finding an electron.\\n\\nElectron configuration of silicon (Z=14):\\n1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p2\\n(showing distribution: 1s2 2s2 2px2 2py1 2pz1 — Hund's rule for the 3p)\\n\\n(i) 3 main energy levels are occupied (n=1, 2, 3).\\n(ii) 8 orbitals are occupied (1s, 2s, 2px, 2py, 2pz, 3s, 3px, 3py).\\n\\n(b) First ionisation energy: The minimum energy required to completely remove the most loosely bound electron from a neutral gaseous atom in its ground state.\\n\\n(i) Silicon > Aluminium: Silicon has electron configuration ...3s2 3p2, aluminium has ...3s2 3p1. In aluminium the single 3p electron is easier to remove because the 3p sublevel is slightly higher in energy (less stable) than 3s. Silicon's outer 3p electrons experience slightly greater effective nuclear charge (Z=14 vs Z=13).\\n\\n(ii) Silicon < Carbon: Both have the configuration ns2 np2 in their outer shell. However, silicon's outer electrons are in the n=3 shell (further from nucleus, more shielded) compared to carbon's n=2 shell, so silicon's outer electrons are easier to remove.\\n\\n(c) The graph of successive ionisation energies of silicon shows large jumps at certain points — specifically after removing 4 electrons and again after removing 12 electrons. These jumps correspond to electrons being removed from a lower (closer) energy level, requiring significantly more energy. This pattern of jumps provides evidence for distinct energy levels in the atom.\\n\\nOther experimental evidence: Line emission spectra of elements (bright lines at specific wavelengths show electrons transitioning between fixed energy levels)."},

  {num:6, label:"Q6 – Organic Chemistry: Alkanes, Structural Isomers, Heat of Combustion",
   q:"Camping gas is a mixture of propane, butane, and a structural isomer of butane.\\n(a) Name the homologous series. Draw the structural formula of propane. (8)\\n(b) Explain why propane has a lower boiling point than butane. (6)\\n(c)(i) What are structural isomers? (ii) Draw the isomer of butane. (12)\\n(d) Define heat of combustion. Write balanced equation for combustion of butane. Calculate heat of combustion given Hf values: CO2 = -393.5, H2O = -285.8, butane = -125.7 kJ/mol. (24)",
   a:"(a) Homologous series: Alkanes.\\nStructural formula of propane:\\nCH3-CH2-CH3\\n(H3C-CH2-CH3 with all H atoms shown)\\n\\n(b) Propane (C3H8) has a lower boiling point than butane (C4H10) because propane molecules are smaller with fewer electrons, so the van der Waals (London dispersion) forces between propane molecules are weaker. Weaker intermolecular forces mean less energy is needed to separate the molecules, hence lower boiling point.\\n\\n(c)(i) Structural isomers are compounds that have the same molecular formula but different structural arrangements of atoms (different structural formulae).\\n\\n(ii) The structural isomer of butane (C4H10) is methylpropane (2-methylpropane):\\nCH3-CH(CH3)-CH3\\n\\n(d) Heat of combustion: The heat change (enthalpy change) when one mole of a substance is burned completely in excess oxygen, all reactants and products being in their standard states.\\n\\nBalanced equation:\\n2C4H10 + 13O2 -> 8CO2 + 10H2O\\n(or: C4H10 + 13/2 O2 -> 4CO2 + 5H2O)\\n\\nCalculation using Hess's law:\\nDeltaH(combustion) = Sum Hf(products) - Sum Hf(reactants)\\n= [4(-393.5) + 5(-285.8)] - [(-125.7) + 0]\\n= [-1574.0 + (-1429.0)] - [-125.7]\\n= -3003.0 - (-125.7)\\n= -3003.0 + 125.7\\n= -2877.3 kJ/mol"},

  {num:7, label:"Q7 – Acids & Bases: Strong vs Weak, pH, Nitrate Test",
   q:"Nitrous acid (HNO2) is a weak acid, readily oxidised to nitric acid (HNO3).\\n(a) Distinguish between strong acid and weak acid (Bronsted-Lowry). (8)\\n(b) HNO2 + H2O <=> H3O+ + NO2-. Identify the two bases. (6)\\n(c) Define pH. pH of 0.2 M HNO2 is 2.0 at 25°C. Calculate [H3O+]. Explain how this confirms HNO2 is weak. What concentration of HNO3 would have the same [H3O+]? Calculate [OH-]. (18)\\n(d) Describe a test for the nitrate anion in aqueous solution. (12)\\n(e) How do high nitrate levels reduce dissolved oxygen in lakes/rivers? (6)",
   a:"(a) Strong acid: An acid that fully dissociates (donates all its protons) in aqueous solution — every molecule donates a proton to water.\\nWeak acid: An acid that only partially dissociates (donates only some of its protons) in aqueous solution — an equilibrium exists between undissociated acid and its ions.\\n\\n(b) The two substances acting as bases:\\n1. H2O — it accepts a proton from HNO2 (forming H3O+).\\n2. NO2- — it can accept a proton from H3O+ (the conjugate base of HNO2 in the reverse reaction).\\n\\n(c) pH = -log10[H3O+]\\n\\n[H3O+] = 10^(-pH) = 10^(-2.0) = 0.01 mol/L\\n\\nThis confirms HNO2 is a weak acid because:\\nThe initial concentration is 0.2 M, but [H3O+] is only 0.01 M. If it were a strong acid (fully dissociated), [H3O+] would equal 0.2 M. Since only 0.01/0.2 = 5% has dissociated, HNO2 must be weak.\\n\\nConcentration of HNO3 with same [H3O+]:\\nHNO3 is a strong acid — it fully dissociates, so [H3O+] = [HNO3].\\nTherefore [HNO3] = 0.01 M.\\n\\n[OH-] in both solutions:\\nKw = [H3O+][OH-] = 1.0 x 10^-14\\n[OH-] = 1.0 x 10^-14 / 0.01 = 1.0 x 10^-12 mol/L\\nThe [OH-] is the same in both solutions since they have the same [H3O+].\\n\\n(d) Brown ring test for nitrate ion:\\nTo the sample solution, add freshly prepared iron(II) sulfate (FeSO4) solution. Mix well. Then carefully pour concentrated sulfuric acid (H2SO4) down the side of the test tube so it forms a layer at the bottom. A brown ring forms at the junction of the two layers, confirming the presence of nitrate ions. The brown ring is due to the formation of [Fe(NO)]2+ (iron(II) nitrosyl complex).\\n\\n(e) High nitrate levels act as fertiliser for algae and aquatic plants, causing excessive growth (algal bloom). When the algae die, bacteria decompose the dead organic matter. These bacteria use up dissolved oxygen in the water during aerobic decomposition. This reduces the dissolved oxygen content, a process called eutrophication, which can cause fish kills."},

  {num:8, label:"Q8 – Organic Chemistry: C2 Hydrocarbons, Preparation, Free Radical Substitution",
   q:"Hydrocarbons: A = C2H2 (ethyne), B = C2H4 (ethene), C = C2H6 (ethane).\\n(a) IUPAC name and structural formula of B. (5)\\n(b) Labelled diagram for preparation and collection of A (ethyne). (12)\\n(c) Chemical test to distinguish B and C. (9)\\n(d) C reacts with Cl2 in UV light: (i) name the mechanism, (ii) detailed description, (iii) explain how another hydrocarbon in the product mixture provides evidence for the mechanism. (24)",
   a:"(a) IUPAC name of B (C2H4): Ethene.\\nStructural formula: H2C=CH2 (showing the C=C double bond with H atoms).\\n\\n(b) Preparation of ethyne (C2H2):\\nLabelled diagram should show: a round-bottomed flask containing calcium carbide (CaC2), a dropping funnel/tap funnel above it to add water drop by drop, a delivery tube leading from the flask to a trough of water, and a gas jar/test tube collecting gas by displacement of water.\\nEquation: CaC2 + 2H2O -> Ca(OH)2 + C2H2\\n\\n(c) Test to distinguish ethene (B) from ethane (C):\\nAdd bromine water (Br2(aq)) to separate samples of each gas.\\n- Ethene (unsaturated): The orange/brown bromine water is decolourised (goes colourless) rapidly — addition reaction occurs.\\n- Ethane (saturated): No colour change with bromine water (in the absence of UV light).\\n\\n(d)(i) Free radical substitution.\\n\\n(ii) Mechanism of ethane + Cl2 in UV light:\\n\\nInitiation: UV light causes the Cl-Cl bond to break by homolytic fission, producing two chlorine free radicals.\\nCl2 -> 2Cl*\\n\\nPropagation (chain steps):\\nStep 1: Cl* + C2H6 -> C2H5* + HCl\\n(A chlorine radical abstracts a hydrogen atom from ethane, forming an ethyl radical and HCl.)\\nStep 2: C2H5* + Cl2 -> C2H5Cl + Cl*\\n(The ethyl radical reacts with a chlorine molecule, forming chloroethane and regenerating a chlorine radical.)\\n\\nTermination: Two radicals combine to form a stable molecule, e.g.:\\nCl* + Cl* -> Cl2\\nC2H5* + Cl* -> C2H5Cl\\nC2H5* + C2H5* -> C4H10 (butane)\\n\\n(iii) The presence of butane (C4H10) in the product mixture provides evidence for the free radical mechanism. Butane is formed when two ethyl radicals (C2H5*) combine in a termination step. The only way ethyl radicals can be present is if the mechanism involves free radical intermediates. This is strong evidence for the free radical chain mechanism."},

  {num:9, label:"Q9 – Chemical Equilibrium: Kc Calculation, Le Chatelier, Catalytic Converters",
   q:"2NO(g) <=> N2(g) + O2(g), DeltaH = -183 kJ. Kc = 20.25 at temperature T.\\n(a) Write the Kc expression. (5)\\n(b) Calculate moles of N2 at equilibrium when 2 moles of NO decompose at T. (12)\\n(c) State Le Chatelier's principle. Effect of increasing (i) temperature, (ii) pressure on Kc? Justify. (18)\\n(d) Catalytic converters: name two metals, type of catalysis, one way catalysts increase rate, name a catalyst poison. (15)",
   a:"(a) Kc = [N2][O2] / [NO]^2\\n\\n(b) Let x = moles of N2 formed at equilibrium.\\nFrom stoichiometry: x mol N2 and x mol O2 are formed, and 2x mol NO are consumed.\\n\\nAt equilibrium:\\n[NO] = 2 - 2x (in a container of volume V)\\n[N2] = x\\n[O2] = x\\n\\nKc = (x/V)(x/V) / ((2-2x)/V)^2 = x^2 / (2-2x)^2\\n\\nNote: The V terms cancel.\\n20.25 = x^2 / (2-2x)^2\\n\\nTake square root of both sides:\\n4.5 = x / (2-2x)\\n4.5(2-2x) = x\\n9 - 9x = x\\n9 = 10x\\nx = 0.9 mol\\n\\nThere are 0.9 moles of N2 at equilibrium.\\n\\n(c) Le Chatelier's Principle: If a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change in conditions (concentration, temperature, or pressure), the equilibrium shifts in the direction that tends to oppose (counteract) the change.\\n\\n(i) Effect of increasing temperature on Kc: The reaction is exothermic (DeltaH = -183 kJ). Increasing temperature favours the reverse (endothermic) direction. The equilibrium shifts left, producing more NO and less N2/O2. Therefore Kc decreases.\\n\\n(ii) Effect of increasing pressure on Kc: There are 2 moles of gas on the left (2NO) and 2 moles on the right (N2 + O2). Since the total moles of gas are equal on both sides, changing pressure has no effect on the equilibrium position. Kc is unchanged (Kc depends only on temperature).\\n\\n(d) Metals: Platinum (Pt) and rhodium (Rh) (also palladium acceptable).\\nType of catalysis: Heterogeneous catalysis (the catalyst is a solid, the reactants are gases).\\nOne way catalysts increase rate: They provide a surface for adsorption of reactant molecules, which weakens bonds in the reactants and lowers the activation energy.\\nCatalyst poison: Lead (from leaded petrol) / sulfur compounds."},

  {num:10, label:"Q10 – Mixed: Esters/Aldehydes, Mass Spectrometry, Oxidation/Reduction",
   q:"Answer any two of (a), (b), (c). (2 x 25)\\n\\n(a) Reaction scheme with compounds X (alcohol), Y (aldehyde), Z (acid), each with 2 carbons.\\n(i) Major use of compound Z. (4)\\n(ii) Draw the structure of aldehyde Y showing bonding. (6)\\n(iii) Draw the ester from X and Z, identify planar carbon. (9)\\n(iv) How could Y be reduced to X? (6)\\n\\n(b) Define (i) mass number, (ii) relative atomic mass. (9)\\nThree of five mass spectrometry processes are detection, acceleration, vaporisation. Name the other two. List all five in order. (9)\\nCalculate Ar of gallium: 60.1% Ga-69, 39.9% Ga-71. (7)\\n\\n(c) Define oxidation: (i) electron transfer, (ii) oxidation number. (6)\\nUse oxidation numbers to identify (iii) oxidising agent, (iv) reducing agent in:\\nCd + H+ + NO3- -> Cd2+ + NO + H2O (12)",
   a:"(a) The compounds: X = ethanol (C2H5OH), Y = ethanal (CH3CHO), Z = ethanoic acid (CH3COOH).\\n\\n(i) Major use of ethanoic acid (compound Z): As vinegar (food flavouring/preservative), or as a solvent, or in the manufacture of plastics/polymers.\\n\\n(ii) Structure of ethanal (Y) showing bonding:\\nH-C-C=O with H atoms: H3C-CHO\\nThe carbonyl C is double-bonded to O and single-bonded to H and the methyl C. Show: C-H sigma bonds, C-C sigma bond, C=O double bond (one sigma + one pi).\\n\\n(iii) Ester formed from ethanol (X) and ethanoic acid (Z):\\nCH3COOCH2CH3 (ethyl ethanoate / ethyl acetate)\\nStructure: CH3-C(=O)-O-CH2-CH3\\nThe carbon atom in the C=O of the ester group is in planar geometry (trigonal planar, sp2 hybridised).\\n\\n(iv) Aldehyde Y can be reduced to alcohol X by:\\n- Adding hydrogen gas (H2) in the presence of a nickel catalyst (catalytic hydrogenation), or\\n- Using sodium borohydride (NaBH4) as a reducing agent, or\\n- Using lithium aluminium hydride (LiAlH4).\\n\\n(b)(i) Mass number: The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus of an atom.\\n\\n(ii) Relative atomic mass: The average mass of the atoms of an element compared to 1/12 the mass of a carbon-12 atom, taking into account the relative abundances of the isotopes.\\n\\nThe two other processes: Ionisation and separation (deflection).\\n\\nOrder of all five:\\n1. Vaporisation\\n2. Ionisation\\n3. Acceleration\\n4. Separation (deflection)\\n5. Detection\\n\\nAr of gallium = (60.1 x 69 + 39.9 x 71) / 100\\n= (4146.9 + 2832.9) / 100\\n= 6979.8 / 100\\n= 69.8\\n\\n(c)(i) Oxidation (electron transfer): Loss of electrons.\\n(ii) Oxidation (oxidation number): An increase in oxidation number.\\n\\nIn the reaction: Cd + H+ + NO3- -> Cd2+ + NO + H2O\\n\\nOxidation numbers:\\n- Cd goes from 0 to +2 (oxidised — loses electrons)\\n- N in NO3- is +5, N in NO is +2 (reduced — gains electrons)\\n- H remains +1, O remains -2\\n\\n(iii) Oxidising agent: NO3- (nitrate ion) — it contains the nitrogen that is reduced from +5 to +2.\\n(iv) Reducing agent: Cd (cadmium) — it is oxidised from 0 to +2.\\n\\nBalanced equation: 3Cd + 8H+ + 2NO3- -> 3Cd2+ + 2NO + 4H2O"}
],

  "2013": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Dissolved Oxygen (Winkler Method)", q:"An experiment to measure dissolved oxygen in river water. Compound A and alkaline KI were added to a sample bottle (avoiding air bubbles). A brown precipitate was observed. Concentrated H2SO4 was then added.\n\n(a) Why was it important to avoid trapping air bubbles?\n(b) Identify compound A.\n(c) What was observed on addition of H2SO4 and mixing?\n(d) Describe how the burette was rinsed and filled for the titrations.\n(e) Name the indicator used.\n(f) Calculate the concentration of I2 in the sample bottle (titrated 200 cm3 with 0.02 M Na2S2O3, mean titre 9.4 cm3). Then calculate dissolved O2 in (i) mol/L, (ii) g/L, (iii) ppm.\nEquation: 2S2O3(2-) + I2 -> S4O6(2-) + 2I-\n(g) What if a white precipitate was observed instead of brown?\n(h) Why is immediate determination best practice?", a:"(a) Air bubbles must be avoided because they contain oxygen. Any extra oxygen dissolving into the sample would give a falsely high reading for dissolved oxygen content.\n\n(b) Compound A: Manganese(II) sulfate (MnSO4).\n\n(c) On addition of H2SO4 and mixing: The brown precipitate dissolved, and a brown (amber/yellow/iodine-coloured) solution formed. The acid dissolves the precipitate and liberates iodine (I2) into solution.\n\n(d) Rinsing and filling the burette:\n- Rinse the burette first with deionised water, then with a small amount of the sodium thiosulfate solution.\n- Fill the burette with sodium thiosulfate solution using a funnel.\n- Remove the funnel.\n- Open the tap to fill the jet (part below the tap) and remove any air bubbles.\n- Adjust the level so the bottom of the meniscus is on the zero mark, reading at eye level.\n\n(e) Indicator: Freshly-prepared starch solution (added near the end point when the solution is pale yellow; it turns blue-black and then goes colourless at the end point).\n\n(f) Calculation:\nMoles Na2S2O3 = (9.4 / 1000) x 0.02 = 1.88 x 10^-4 mol\nFrom equation: 2 mol S2O3(2-) reacts with 1 mol I2\nMoles I2 in 200 cm3 = 1.88 x 10^-4 / 2 = 9.4 x 10^-5 mol\nConcentration of I2 = 9.4 x 10^-5 / (200/1000) = 4.7 x 10^-4 mol/L\n\nGiven: 1 mol O2 produces 2 mol I2\n(i) Moles O2 per litre = 4.7 x 10^-4 / 2 = 2.35 x 10^-4 mol/L\n(ii) Mass O2 per litre = 2.35 x 10^-4 x 32 = 7.52 x 10^-3 g/L = 0.00752 g/L\n(iii) ppm = mg/L = 7.52 x 10^-3 x 1000 = 7.52 ppm\n\n(g) A white precipitate (instead of brown) would indicate that there was virtually no dissolved oxygen in the water sample. The white precipitate is manganese(II) hydroxide, which only turns brown when oxidised by dissolved oxygen.\n\n(h) Immediate determination is best practice because biological/biochemical processes (respiration by micro-organisms, photosynthesis) can change the dissolved oxygen content of the sample over time. Bacteria consume oxygen, so a delay would give a lower reading."},
    {num:2, label:"Q2: Chemical Tests & Chromatography", q:"Chemistry students performed practical tasks:\n(a) Describe a chemical test to distinguish between ethanoic acid and ethanol.\n(b) Describe how to show a sample of ethene gas is unsaturated.\n(c) Describe with a labelled diagram how to separate a mixture of indicators using chromatography.\n(d) Melting points of two benzoic acid samples: A = 117-120 degrees C, B = 120-121 degrees C. Which is purer? Justify. What solvent should be used for recrystallisation? Why?\n(e) A steam distillation apparatus was assembled incorrectly. Identify the flaw and how to rectify it.", a:"(a) Test to distinguish ethanoic acid from ethanol:\nAdd a small piece of sodium carbonate (Na2CO3) or sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO3) to each liquid.\n- Ethanoic acid: effervescence (bubbles of CO2 gas produced). Test the gas with limewater -- it turns milky.\n- Ethanol: no reaction, no effervescence.\nAlternatively: add universal indicator or pH paper. Ethanoic acid is acidic (pH below 7, indicator turns red/orange). Ethanol is neutral.\n\n(b) Test for unsaturation of ethene:\nAdd bromine water (brown/orange solution) to the test tube of ethene gas. Stopper and shake.\nObservation: The bromine water is decolourised (turns from brown/orange to colourless). This confirms that ethene is unsaturated (contains a C=C double bond), as bromine undergoes an addition reaction with the double bond.\nAlternatively: Add acidified potassium manganate(VII) (purple solution). It decolourises to colourless.\n\n(c) Chromatography (paper chromatography):\nDiagram: A beaker (tank) with a small amount of solvent (eluent, e.g. ethanol/water mixture) at the bottom. A piece of chromatography paper is hung from the top, dipping into the solvent.\nMethod: Apply a small spot of the indicator mixture about 2 cm from the bottom of the paper using a capillary tube or dropper. The spot must be above the solvent level. Allow the solvent to rise up the paper by capillary action. As it passes through the spot, different indicators are carried at different rates depending on their solubility in the mobile phase and their affinity for the stationary phase (paper). After sufficient time, remove the paper and mark the solvent front. The separated spots show the individual indicators.\n\n(d) Sample B is the purer sample.\nJustification: Sample B has a higher melting point (120-121 degrees C, closer to the expected 122 degrees C for pure benzoic acid) and a narrower melting point range (1 degree vs 3 degrees for sample A). Impurities lower the melting point and broaden the melting point range.\n\nSolvent for recrystallisation: Water.\nWhy: Benzoic acid is very soluble in hot water but only slightly soluble in cold water. This means it dissolves when heated, and crystals form on cooling -- ideal conditions for recrystallisation. Impurities either remain dissolved in the cold filtrate or are removed by hot filtration.\n\n(e) Flaw: The end of the safety tube (or steam inlet tube) was above the water surface in the flask rather than below it (or there was no steam trap / no gauze used).\nRectification: The end of the tube should be submerged below the water surface in the flask so that steam passes through the water/plant material properly. Alternatively, include a steam trap to prevent suck-back when heating stops."},
    {num:3, label:"Q3: Heat of Reaction (Neutralisation Experiment)", q:"75 cm3 of 1.0 M HCl at 13.0 degrees C was mixed with 75 cm3 of 1.0 M NaOH at 15.0 degrees C. Highest temperature = 20.9 degrees C.\n\nHCl + NaOH -> NaCl + H2O\n\n(a) Define heat of reaction.\n(b) Suggest a suitable material for the reaction vessel.\n(c) State (i) one advantage, (ii) one disadvantage, of using a burette instead of a graduated cylinder.\n(d) State two ways of ensuring accurate temperature measurement.\n(e) How many moles of HCl were neutralised? Calculate the heat produced and hence the heat of reaction. (Density = 1.0 g/cm3, specific heat capacity = 4.2 kJ/kg/K)\n(f) Identify the hazard with these solutions. Describe the warning symbol. What problem would arise using 0.1 M solutions instead?", a:"(a) Heat of reaction: The enthalpy change when the amounts of reactants shown in a balanced equation react completely under stated conditions.\n\n(b) Suitable material: Polystyrene (Styrofoam cup) -- a good insulator that minimises heat loss to the surroundings. The reaction vessel should have negligible heat capacity.\n\n(c)(i) Advantage of burette: More accurate measurement of volume (can be read to 0.05 cm3 accuracy).\n(ii) Disadvantage: A burette delivers liquid slowly, so it takes longer to add the NaOH. This allows more time for heat loss during addition, making the temperature rise less accurate.\n\n(d) Two ways to ensure accurate temperature measurement:\n1. Use a thermometer with 0.2 degree C (or better) graduations, or a temperature probe/sensor.\n2. Stir the mixture continuously and record the highest temperature reached. (Or: add the NaOH quickly to minimise heat loss; or use a graphical extrapolation method to find the true maximum temperature.)\n\n(e) Moles of HCl neutralised:\nMoles = concentration x volume = 1.0 x (75/1000) = 0.075 mol\n\nHeat produced:\nTotal volume of mixture = 75 + 75 = 150 cm3\nMass = 150 x 1.0 = 150 g = 0.15 kg\nAverage initial temperature = (13.0 + 15.0) / 2 = 14.0 degrees C\nTemperature rise = 20.9 - 14.0 = 6.9 K\nHeat = mass x specific heat capacity x temperature rise\nHeat = 0.15 x 4.2 x 6.9 = 4.347 kJ\n\nHeat of reaction:\ndelta H = -(heat produced / moles) = -(4.347 / 0.075) = -57.96 kJ/mol\nThe heat of reaction is approximately -58 kJ/mol (exothermic).\n\n(f) Hazard: Corrosive (1.0 M solutions of HCl and NaOH can cause burns to skin and damage to eyes).\nWarning symbol: A pictogram showing liquid drops falling onto a surface/hand causing damage (corrosion symbol -- test tubes pouring onto a surface and a hand, with corrosion shown).\nProblem with 0.1 M solutions: The temperature rise would be much smaller (about 0.69 K instead of 6.9 K). Such a small temperature change is very difficult to measure accurately, leading to a large percentage error in the result."},
    {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions (Mixed Topics)", q:"Answer eight of the following:\n(a) Write the electron configuration (s, p, etc.) of a zinc atom in its ground state.\n(b) Define relative atomic mass.\n(c) How many neutrons are there in 0.14 g of carbon-14?\n(d) Give the shape and bond angle for a molecule QX4 where Q is from Group 4.\n(e) 1.59 g of copper oxide gives 1.27 g of copper when reduced with hydrogen. Find the empirical formula.\n(f) Complete and balance: C2H5OH + Na -> ?\n(g) What reagents are needed to test for the nitrate ion?\n(h) State Charles' law.\n(i) What happens during secondary sewage treatment?\n(j) What is meant by heterogeneous catalysis?\n(k) A: Write a balanced equation for formation of calcium silicate from CaO in steelmaking. OR B: Write a balanced equation for the formation of ozone in the stratosphere.", a:"(a) Zinc (atomic number 30):\n1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 3d10 4s2\n(Or written as [Ar] 3d10 4s2)\n\n(b) Relative atomic mass: The weighted average of the masses of the isotopes of an element, measured relative to 1/12 the mass of a carbon-12 atom.\n\n(c) Carbon-14 has mass number 14 and atomic number 6, so each atom has 14 - 6 = 8 neutrons.\nMoles of C-14 = 0.14 / 14 = 0.01 mol\nNumber of atoms = 0.01 x 6.0 x 10^23 = 6.0 x 10^21 atoms\nNumber of neutrons = 6.0 x 10^21 x 8 = 4.8 x 10^22 neutrons\n\n(d) Shape: Tetrahedral. Bond angle: 109 degrees 28 minutes (approximately 109.5 degrees).\n(Q from Group 4 has 4 bonding pairs, no lone pairs -- adopts tetrahedral geometry.)\n\n(e) Mass of copper = 1.27 g, mass of oxygen = 1.59 - 1.27 = 0.32 g\nMoles Cu = 1.27 / 63.5 = 0.02 mol\nMoles O = 0.32 / 16 = 0.02 mol\nRatio Cu : O = 0.02 : 0.02 = 1 : 1\nEmpirical formula: CuO\n\n(f) 2C2H5OH + 2Na -> 2C2H5ONa + H2\n(Sodium reacts with ethanol to form sodium ethoxide and hydrogen gas.)\n\n(g) Reagents to test for nitrate ion: Freshly-prepared iron(II) sulfate (FeSO4) solution and concentrated sulfuric acid (H2SO4).\n(Brown ring test: Add FeSO4 solution to the sample. Carefully pour concentrated H2SO4 down the side of the test tube to form a layer underneath. A brown ring forms at the junction if nitrate is present.)\n\n(h) Charles' law: The volume of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute (kelvin) temperature, at constant pressure. V/T = constant (or V1/T1 = V2/T2).\n\n(i) Secondary sewage treatment: The liquid effluent from primary treatment is aerated (air or oxygen is bubbled through). Aerobic bacteria and micro-organisms digest (break down/oxidise) the dissolved organic matter, converting it to CO2, H2O and simple inorganic compounds. This is biological treatment (activated sludge process or trickling filter).\n\n(j) Heterogeneous catalysis: Catalysis in which the catalyst and the reactants are in different phases (states). For example, a solid catalyst with gaseous or liquid reactants. The reaction occurs at the surface of the catalyst. Example: iron catalyst (solid) in the Haber process for ammonia synthesis from N2(g) and H2(g).\n\n(k) A: CaO + SiO2 -> CaSiO3\n(Calcium oxide reacts with silicon dioxide to form calcium silicate, a component of slag in steelmaking.)\nB: O* + O2 -> O3 (where O* is an oxygen atom/free radical formed by UV photolysis of O2)\n(Or: O2 -> 2O (UV), then O + O2 -> O3)"},
    {num:5, label:"Q5: Boyle, Bohr & Atomic Orbitals", q:"(a) Boyle contributed to the use of the term 'element'. What was his understanding of this term?\n(b) Use Bohr's atomic theory (1913) to account for the emission spectrum of hydrogen. Explain why different flame colours are observed for different metals. What colour is observed for lithium chloride? Describe the testing procedure.\n(c) Heisenberg's uncertainty principle (1927) led to modification of Bohr's theory. Explain the underlined term. Give one other factor that contributed. What is an atomic orbital?", a:"(a) Boyle's understanding: An element is a substance that cannot be broken down into anything simpler by chemical means. It is a simple, fundamental substance that cannot be further divided or decomposed.\n\n(b) Bohr's theory and the hydrogen emission spectrum:\n- In a hydrogen atom, the electron occupies fixed energy levels (discrete, quantised energies).\n- In the ground state, the electron is in the lowest available energy level.\n- When energy is supplied (heat, light, electricity), the electron can absorb a specific amount of energy and jump to a higher energy level (excited state).\n- The excited state is unstable/temporary. The electron falls back to a lower energy level.\n- When it falls back, it emits the excess energy as a photon of light with a specific frequency: E = hf, where the energy of the photon equals the difference between the two energy levels (E2 - E1 = hf).\n- Each possible transition produces light of a definite frequency/wavelength, giving a line in the spectrum.\n- The many possible transitions produce the series of lines observed in the emission spectrum.\n\nDifferent flame colours for different metals:\nDifferent metal atoms have different sets of energy levels (different electron configurations). Each metal has its own unique set of electron transitions, so each emits photons of different frequencies/wavelengths. This produces different characteristic colours.\n\nLithium chloride flame colour: Red (crimson).\n\nTesting procedure: Dip a clean platinum (or nichrome) wire into concentrated hydrochloric acid, then into the lithium chloride sample (or a solution of it). Hold the wire in the edge/top of a Bunsen burner flame. Observe the colour produced.\n\n(c) Heisenberg's uncertainty principle: It is not possible to simultaneously know both the exact position and the exact momentum (velocity/speed) of an electron (or any subatomic particle) in an atom at the same time. The more precisely one is known, the less precisely the other can be determined.\n\nOther factor contributing to modification: The wave nature (wave-particle duality) of the electron, discovered by de Broglie. Also: higher-resolution spectra showed splitting of spectral lines (sub-levels), which Bohr's model could not explain.\n\nAtomic orbital: A region (volume of space) around the nucleus of an atom where there is a high probability (greater than 90%) of finding an electron."},
    {num:6, label:"Q6: Octane Numbers, Isomerisation & Thermochemistry", q:"(a) Define the octane number of a fuel.\n(b) Compound A (straight-chain, from crude oil) is converted to compound B by isomerisation.\n(i) Give IUPAC names for A and B.\n(ii) Explain isomerisation. Draw another isomer of A and B.\n(iii) Predict which has higher octane number. Justify.\n(c) Calculate the heat change for: CaC2(s) + 2H2O(l) -> C2H2(g) + Ca(OH)2(s)\nHeats of formation: CaC2 = -59.8, H2O = -285.8, C2H2 = +227.4, Ca(OH)2 = -985.2 kJ/mol.\n(d) Describe the structure of benzene in terms of (i) C-H bonding, (ii) C-C bonding.", a:"(a) Octane number: A measure of a fuel's ability to resist auto-ignition (knocking). It is based on a scale where heptane (straight-chain, knocks easily) is rated 0 and 2,2,4-trimethylpentane (isooctane, resists knocking) is rated 100. A fuel's octane number is its percentage equivalent performance compared to a mixture of these two reference fuels.\n\n(b)(i) Compound A (straight-chain C5H12): Pentane.\nCompound B (branched C5H12): 2-methylbutane (or methylbutane).\n(ii) Isomerisation: A chemical process that converts a compound into one of its structural isomers (same molecular formula but different structural arrangement of atoms). The straight chain is rearranged to a branched chain.\nAnother isomer of C5H12: 2,2-dimethylpropane (neopentane). Structure: a central carbon bonded to four methyl groups.\n(iii) Compound B (2-methylbutane) has the higher octane number. Branched-chain alkanes have higher octane numbers than their straight-chain isomers because the branched structure resists auto-ignition (pre-ignition/knocking) better than straight chains. Straight chains compress and ignite more readily.\n\n(c) Using Hess's Law:\ndelta H(reaction) = [delta Hf(products)] - [delta Hf(reactants)]\n= [delta Hf(C2H2) + delta Hf(Ca(OH)2)] - [delta Hf(CaC2) + 2 x delta Hf(H2O)]\n= [(+227.4) + (-985.2)] - [(-59.8) + 2(-285.8)]\n= [-757.8] - [-59.8 + (-571.6)]\n= [-757.8] - [-631.4]\n= -757.8 + 631.4\n= -126.4 kJ\nThe heat change for the reaction is -126.4 kJ (exothermic).\n\n(d)(i) C-H bonding in benzene: Each carbon atom is bonded to one hydrogen atom by a single sigma covalent bond. The C-H bonds are formed by overlap of a carbon sp2 hybrid orbital with a hydrogen 1s orbital.\n(ii) C-C bonding: Each carbon atom is sp2 hybridised. Adjacent carbon atoms are joined by sigma bonds (head-on overlap of sp2 hybrid orbitals). Each carbon also has one unhybridised p orbital perpendicular to the plane of the ring. These six p orbitals overlap sideways to form a delocalised pi electron system above and below the plane of the ring. The six pi electrons are shared equally over all six carbon atoms (delocalised), giving all C-C bonds equal length (intermediate between single and double bonds). This is why benzene does not behave like a typical alkene."},
    {num:7, label:"Q7: Rates of Reaction & Concentration Effects", q:"(a) Define the rate of a chemical reaction.\n(b) Explain why there is an almost instantaneous reaction between aqueous NaCl and AgNO3.\n(c) When H2 and N2 are mixed 3:1 at room temperature, NH3 formation is very slow. Suggest two ways to increase the rate and explain each.\n(d) Describe how to measure reaction time when 10 cm3 of 1.0 M HCl and 50 cm3 of 0.20 M Na2S2O3 react:\nNa2S2O3 + 2HCl -> 2NaCl + SO2 + S + H2O\nUsing additional Na2S2O3 solutions (0.04, 0.08, 0.12, 0.16 M), describe how to show rate is directly proportional to concentration.\n(e) Draw a reaction profile for an exothermic reaction showing (i) activation energy, (ii) heat of reaction.", a:"(a) Rate of reaction: The change in concentration (or amount) of a reactant or product per unit time. It can be measured in mol/L/s, g/s, cm3/s, etc.\n\n(b) The reaction between NaCl(aq) and AgNO3(aq) is almost instantaneous because both are fully dissociated ionic compounds in solution. The ions (Ag+ and Cl-) are already free in solution and simply need to collide to form the insoluble precipitate AgCl. Since the ions are already separated and mobile, no bonds need to be broken -- only the lattice of AgCl needs to form. The reaction involves no activation energy barrier.\n\n(c) Two ways to increase the rate of N2 + 3H2 -> 2NH3:\n1. Increase temperature: Raising the temperature increases the kinetic energy of the gas molecules. They move faster, collide more frequently, and a greater proportion of collisions have sufficient energy to overcome the high activation energy (the N-N triple bond is very strong). More effective collisions occur per unit time.\n2. Add a catalyst (e.g. iron): A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy. More collisions now have sufficient energy to react, greatly increasing the rate. (Industrially, the Haber process uses finely divided iron catalyst at about 450 degrees C.)\n\n(d) Measuring reaction time:\nMix 50 cm3 of Na2S2O3 solution with 10 cm3 of 1.0 M HCl in a conical flask placed on a piece of white paper with an X (cross) drawn on it. Start a stopwatch immediately on mixing. Observe the mixture from above. When the X is no longer visible (obscured by the sulfur precipitate), stop the stopwatch. Record the time.\n\nTo show rate is proportional to concentration:\nRepeat the experiment with each of the five Na2S2O3 concentrations (0.04, 0.08, 0.12, 0.16, 0.20 M), always using the same volume of Na2S2O3 (50 cm3) and the same volume of HCl (10 cm3 of 1.0 M) at the same temperature. Record the time for each. Calculate rate = 1/time for each.\nPlot a graph of rate (1/time) on the y-axis vs concentration of Na2S2O3 on the x-axis.\nIf rate is directly proportional to concentration, the graph will be a straight line passing through the origin.\n\n(e) Reaction profile for an exothermic reaction:\nX-axis: Progress of reaction (reaction coordinate).\nY-axis: Energy (potential energy / enthalpy).\nThe reactants start at a higher energy level. The curve rises to a peak (the transition state / activated complex). The difference between the peak and the reactant energy level is the activation energy (EA). The curve then falls to the product energy level, which is lower than the reactants. The difference between the reactant energy level and the product energy level is the heat of reaction (delta H), which is negative (exothermic -- energy is released)."},
    {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Reaction Scheme (Ethene, PVC & Mechanisms)", q:"Reaction scheme: C2H6 (ethane) and C2H4 (ethene) connected by conversion X. Ethane converts to CH3CH2Cl (conversion Y) and to CH2ClCH2Cl (conversion Z). C2H4 converts to A by reaction W, and A leads to PVC by polymerisation.\n\n(a) Explain 'homologous series'. What type of reaction is conversion X? How does the geometry around carbon atoms change?\n(b) Identify the reagent for (i) conversion Y, (ii) conversion Z, (iii) conversion W.\n(c) Describe the mechanism of reaction W (addition of HCl to ethene).\n(d) Draw the structure of A and give its name.\n(e) Draw two repeating units of PVC.", a:"(a) Homologous series: A series of organic compounds with the same general formula, each successive member differing by CH2. Members have similar chemical properties and show a gradual trend (gradation) in physical properties. Examples: alkanes (CnH2n+2), alkenes (CnH2n).\n\nConversion X (ethane to ethene): Elimination reaction (specifically, dehydrogenation or cracking -- removal of H2).\n\nGeometry change: In ethane, each carbon is sp3 hybridised with tetrahedral geometry (bond angles approximately 109.5 degrees). In ethene, each carbon is sp2 hybridised with trigonal planar geometry (bond angles approximately 120 degrees). The molecule changes from a 3D tetrahedral arrangement to a flat planar arrangement around each carbon.\n\n(b)(i) Conversion Y (ethane to chloroethane, CH3CH2Cl): Chlorine gas (Cl2) in the presence of ultraviolet light (UV). This is a free radical substitution reaction.\n(ii) Conversion Z (ethene to 1,2-dichloroethane, CH2ClCH2Cl): Chlorine gas (Cl2). This is an addition reaction -- Cl2 adds across the C=C double bond.\n(iii) Conversion W (ethene to compound A): HCl (hydrogen chloride gas). This is an electrophilic addition reaction -- HCl adds across the C=C double bond.\n\n(c) Mechanism of reaction W (electrophilic addition of HCl to ethene):\nStep 1: The HCl molecule approaches the electron-rich C=C double bond. The pi electrons of the double bond attract the H-delta+ end of the polar H-Cl bond.\nStep 2: The pi electrons attack the H+, forming a new C-H bond. The H-Cl bond breaks heterolytically: H goes to the carbon, and Cl- is released. A carbocation intermediate (CH3CH2+) forms.\nStep 3: The Cl- ion (nucleophile) rapidly attacks the positively charged carbocation, forming a C-Cl bond.\nProduct: Chloroethane (CH3CH2Cl).\n\n(d) Compound A: Chloroethene (vinyl chloride), CH2=CHCl.\nStructure: H2C=CHCl (one H on the first carbon is replaced by Cl compared to ethene).\nIUPAC name: Chloroethene.\n\nNote: Looking at the scheme more carefully, W is the addition of Cl2 to ethene giving 1,2-dichloroethane (CH2ClCH2Cl), and then -HCl gives compound A = chloroethene (CH2=CHCl). So A is chloroethene (vinyl chloride), the monomer for PVC.\n\n(e) Two repeating units of PVC (polyvinyl chloride / poly(chloroethene)):\n-[-CH2-CHCl-]-[-CH2-CHCl-]-\nEach repeating unit has a CH2 group bonded to a CHCl group, with the chain continuing in both directions."},
    {num:9, label:"Q9: Chemical Equilibrium (Fe3+ / CNS-)", q:"(a) What is meant by chemical equilibrium? Why is it described as dynamic? State Le Chatelier's principle.\n(b) Fe3+(aq) + CNS-(aq) <=> Fe(CNS)2+(aq). Yellow + colourless = red.\nExplain (i) the effect on Fe3+ concentration of adding KCNS, (ii) why pressure has no effect.\n(c) Write the Kc expression. Calculate Kc given: 1.0 x 10^-3 moles each of FeCl3 and KCNS in 1 litre; at equilibrium, 1.1 x 10^-4 moles Fe(CNS)2+ present.\n(d) The red colour faded when placed in an ice-water bath. Is Kc bigger or smaller at the lower temperature? Is the forward reaction exothermic or endothermic? Justify.", a:"(a) Chemical equilibrium: A state in which the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction. The concentrations of reactants and products remain constant (but are not necessarily equal).\n\nDynamic: The equilibrium is described as dynamic because both the forward and reverse reactions are still occurring -- they have not stopped. Reactants are continuously being converted to products and products back to reactants, but at equal rates so that there is no net change in concentrations.\n\nLe Chatelier's principle: If a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change in concentration, temperature, or pressure, the equilibrium will shift in the direction that tends to oppose (counteract/minimise) that change.\n\n(b)(i) Adding KCNS increases the concentration of CNS- ions. By Le Chatelier's principle, the equilibrium shifts to the right (forward direction) to use up some of the added CNS-. This means more Fe(CNS)2+ is formed and some Fe3+ is consumed. Therefore, the Fe3+ concentration decreases.\n\n(ii) Changing pressure has no effect on this equilibrium because all species are in aqueous solution. Pressure changes only affect equilibria involving gases (where there is a difference in the number of moles of gas on each side). Since this is an equilibrium in solution, pressure changes do not shift it.\n\n(c) Kc = [Fe(CNS)2+] / ([Fe3+][CNS-])\n\nAt equilibrium:\n[Fe(CNS)2+] = 1.1 x 10^-4 mol/L\n[Fe3+] = 1.0 x 10^-3 - 1.1 x 10^-4 = 8.9 x 10^-4 mol/L\n[CNS-] = 1.0 x 10^-3 - 1.1 x 10^-4 = 8.9 x 10^-4 mol/L\n\nKc = (1.1 x 10^-4) / ((8.9 x 10^-4) x (8.9 x 10^-4))\nKc = (1.1 x 10^-4) / (7.92 x 10^-7)\nKc = 138.9 (approximately 139)\n\n(d) The red colour faded when cooled (placed in ice-water), meaning less Fe(CNS)2+ (red) was present at equilibrium at the lower temperature. The equilibrium shifted to the left.\n\nKc is smaller at the lower temperature (less product formed relative to reactants).\n\nThe forward reaction is endothermic. Reasoning: Lowering the temperature shifts the equilibrium in the exothermic direction (to release heat and oppose the temperature decrease). Since the equilibrium shifted to the left (reverse direction), the reverse reaction is exothermic, so the forward reaction must be endothermic. (Alternatively: Kc decreases with decreasing temperature, which is consistent with an endothermic forward reaction.)"},
    {num:10, label:"Q10: Intermolecular Forces, Redox & Electrochemistry", q:"Answer any two of (a), (b) and (c).\n\n(a) Distinguish between intramolecular bonding and intermolecular forces. Explain each of the following:\n(i) The boiling point of H2 (20 K) is much lower than O2 (90.2 K).\n(ii) Iodine has very low solubility in water.\n(iii) A stream of water from a burette is deflected by a charged rod.\n\n(b) The thermite reaction: 8Al + 3Fe3O4 -> 4Al2O3 + 9Fe (highly exothermic).\n(i) Define oxidation in terms of oxidation number. Show this is a redox reaction. Identify the reducing agent.\n(ii) What is the mole ratio of Al to Fe? What mass of iron can be produced from 2.7 g of Al?\n\n(c)(i) Define (i) electrolysis, (ii) electrode.\n(ii) Give two uses of electrolysis.\n(iii) In electrolysis of molten NaCl, write equations for reactions at each electrode. Identify which is oxidation and which is reduction.\n(iv) What mass of sodium is produced by passing a current for a given time?", a:"(a) Intramolecular bonding: The strong forces (bonds) that hold atoms together within a molecule. These include ionic bonds, covalent bonds, and metallic bonds. They involve the sharing or transfer of electrons.\n\nIntermolecular forces: The relatively weak forces of attraction between separate molecules. These include van der Waals forces (London dispersion forces), dipole-dipole interactions, and hydrogen bonds. They do not involve electron sharing or transfer.\n\n(i) H2 has a much lower boiling point (20 K) than O2 (90.2 K): Both are non-polar molecules held together only by van der Waals (London dispersion) forces. The strength of van der Waals forces increases with the number of electrons (molecular size). O2 has 16 electrons while H2 has only 2, so O2 has much stronger intermolecular forces and requires more energy (higher temperature) to boil.\n\n(ii) Iodine has very low solubility in water: Iodine (I2) is a non-polar molecule. Water is a polar solvent. 'Like dissolves like' -- non-polar iodine does not interact well with polar water molecules. The energy required to break the hydrogen bonds between water molecules is not compensated by the weak interactions between I2 and H2O molecules.\n\n(iii) Water is deflected by a charged rod: Water molecules are polar (they have a permanent dipole moment because of the bent shape and the electronegativity difference between O and H). When a charged rod is brought near the stream, the polar water molecules align themselves -- the opposite charge end of the dipole is attracted towards the rod. This causes a net deflection of the water stream towards the rod.\n\n(b)(i) Oxidation: An increase in oxidation number.\nAluminium: Oxidation number changes from 0 (in Al) to +3 (in Al2O3) -- oxidised (increase).\nIron: Oxidation number changes from +8/3 (approximately +2.67 in Fe3O4, or considering Fe3O4 as FeO.Fe2O3, Fe is +2 and +3) to 0 (in Fe) -- reduced (decrease).\nSince one element is oxidised and another is reduced, this is a redox reaction.\nReducing agent: Aluminium (Al) -- it is oxidised (donates electrons), causing the reduction of iron.\n\n(ii) From the equation: 8 mol Al produces 9 mol Fe.\nMoles of Al = 2.7 / 27 = 0.1 mol\nMoles of Fe = (9/8) x 0.1 = 0.1125 mol\nMass of Fe = 0.1125 x 56 = 6.3 g\n\n(c)(i) Electrolysis: The decomposition (breaking down) of an ionic compound (in the molten state or in aqueous solution) by passing an electric current through it.\nElectrode: A conductor (usually metal or graphite) through which electric current enters or leaves the electrolyte.\n\n(ii) Uses of electrolysis: Electroplating (coating objects with a thin layer of metal); extraction/purification of metals (e.g. aluminium from bauxite, purification of copper); production of chlorine and sodium hydroxide from brine.\n\n(iii) Electrolysis of molten NaCl:\nAt the cathode (negative electrode): Na+ + e- -> Na (reduction -- gain of electrons)\nAt the anode (positive electrode): 2Cl- -> Cl2 + 2e- (oxidation -- loss of electrons)\n\n(iv) This depends on the specific current and time given. Using Faraday's laws:\nMoles of electrons = (current x time) / (96500)\nFrom the cathode equation, 1 mol of electrons produces 1 mol of Na (mass 23 g).\nMass of Na = moles of electrons x 23 g."},
    {num:11, label:"Q11: Acids/Bases, Bonding Theory & Stoichiometry", q:"Answer any two of (a), (b) and (c).\n\n(a)(i) Define a weak acid. Deduce what 'monoprotic' means.\n(ii) Ka expression for a weak monoprotic acid HA. What is the relationship between Ka and acid strength?\n(iii) Calculate the pH of a 0.10 M solution of a weak monoprotic acid with Ka = 4.0 x 10^-5.\n(iv) Explain the term buffer solution and how it works.\n\n(b)(i) Define (i) ionic bond, (ii) covalent bond.\n(ii) Give an example of each.\n(iii) Explain why NaCl has a high melting point. Explain why NaCl conducts when molten but not solid.\n\n(c)(i) Define heat of neutralisation.\n(ii) Calculate the heat of neutralisation when 50 cm3 of 2.0 M HCl neutralises 50 cm3 of 2.0 M NaOH, given a temperature rise of 13.7 K. (Density = 1.0 g/cm3, SHC = 4.2 J/g/K)\n(iii) Why is the value approximately the same for all strong acid-strong base neutralisations?", a:"(a)(i) A weak acid is an acid that is only partially dissociated (ionised) in aqueous solution. An equilibrium exists between the undissociated acid and its ions.\nMonoprotic: An acid that can donate only one proton (H+) per molecule. For example, HCl, CH3COOH.\n\n(ii) Ka = [H+][A-] / [HA]\nKa is the acid dissociation constant. The larger the value of Ka, the stronger the acid (more dissociated). A large Ka means a greater proportion of the acid molecules have donated their protons.\n\n(iii) Ka = [H+]^2 / [HA] (since [H+] = [A-] for a monoprotic acid)\n4.0 x 10^-5 = x^2 / 0.10 (assuming x << 0.10)\nx^2 = 4.0 x 10^-6\nx = [H+] = 2.0 x 10^-3 M\npH = -log(2.0 x 10^-3) = 2.70\n\n(iv) Buffer solution: A solution that resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added to it. A common buffer consists of a weak acid and its conjugate base (its salt), e.g. ethanoic acid and sodium ethanoate.\nHow it works:\n- When a small amount of acid (H+) is added, the conjugate base (A-) reacts with it: A- + H+ -> HA, removing the added H+ and preventing a pH drop.\n- When a small amount of base (OH-) is added, the weak acid (HA) reacts with it: HA + OH- -> A- + H2O, removing the added OH- and preventing a pH rise.\n\n(b)(i) Ionic bond: The electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions, formed by the transfer of one or more electrons from a metal atom to a non-metal atom.\nCovalent bond: A bond formed by the sharing of one or more pairs of electrons between two atoms (usually non-metals).\n\n(ii) Ionic bond example: NaCl (sodium chloride). Covalent bond example: H2O (water) or HCl.\n\n(iii) High melting point of NaCl: NaCl has a giant ionic lattice structure with strong electrostatic forces of attraction between the Na+ and Cl- ions in all directions. A large amount of energy is needed to overcome these strong forces, so the melting point is high.\nConduction: In the solid state, the ions are held in fixed positions in the lattice and cannot move, so NaCl does not conduct electricity when solid. When molten, the ions are free to move and carry charge, so molten NaCl conducts electricity.\n\n(c)(i) Heat of neutralisation: The enthalpy change when one mole of water is formed by the reaction of an acid with a base under standard conditions.\n\n(ii) Moles of HCl = 2.0 x (50/1000) = 0.10 mol\nTotal volume = 100 cm3, mass = 100 g\nHeat = mass x SHC x delta T = 100 x 4.2 x 13.7 = 5754 J = 5.754 kJ\nHeat of neutralisation = -(5.754 / 0.10) = -57.54 kJ/mol (approximately -57.5 kJ/mol)\n\n(iii) The heat of neutralisation is approximately the same (-57.5 kJ/mol) for all strong acid-strong base reactions because strong acids and strong bases are fully dissociated. The net reaction in every case is simply: H+(aq) + OH-(aq) -> H2O(l). Since the same reaction occurs regardless of which strong acid or strong base is used, the enthalpy change is the same."}
  ],

  "2012": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Volumetric Analysis (Na2CO3 / HCl Titration)", q:"A student determined the concentration of a hydrochloric acid solution by titration with 25.0 cm3 portions of a 0.05 M primary standard solution of anhydrous sodium carbonate. The hydrochloric acid was added from a burette. The mean titre was 20.8 cm3.\n\n2HCl + Na2CO3 -> 2NaCl + H2O + CO2\n\n(a) Explain the term primary standard.\n(b) Describe how to prepare 500 cm3 of 0.05 M standard solution from a known mass of pure anhydrous sodium carbonate on a clock glass. Calculate the exact mass of Na2CO3 required.\n(c)(i) Describe how the liquid level in the burette was adjusted to the zero mark.\n(ii) Why was a pipette filler used?\n(d) Name a suitable indicator for this titration. State the colour change at the end point.\n(e) Calculate, correct to two decimal places, the concentration of the HCl solution in (i) moles per litre, (ii) grams per litre.", a:"(a) A primary standard is a substance of known high purity that can be accurately weighed and dissolved to make a solution of precisely known concentration.\n\n(b) Calculate mass of Na2CO3:\nMr of Na2CO3 = (2 x 23) + 12 + (3 x 16) = 106\nMoles needed = 0.05 x (500/1000) = 0.025 mol\nMass = 0.025 x 106 = 2.65 g\n\nPreparation: Weigh exactly 2.65 g of anhydrous Na2CO3 on a clock glass. Transfer carefully to a beaker. Rinse the clock glass into the beaker with deionised water. Dissolve fully by stirring with a glass rod. Transfer the solution to a 500 cm3 volumetric flask using a funnel. Rinse the beaker, glass rod and funnel into the flask with deionised water. Add deionised water carefully until the bottom of the meniscus is on the 500 cm3 mark. Stopper and invert several times to mix.\n\n(c)(i) Fill the burette above the zero mark. Open the tap to allow liquid to fill the jet (part below the tap). Adjust the level by running liquid out until the bottom of the meniscus is on the zero mark, reading at eye level.\n(ii) A pipette filler was used for safety -- to avoid sucking hazardous solutions into the mouth.\n\n(d) Indicator: Methyl orange.\nColour change: Yellow to orange/red (or methyl orange changes from yellow in base to red in acid).\n\n(e)(i) Moles Na2CO3 in 25.0 cm3 = (25.0/1000) x 0.05 = 0.00125 mol\nFrom equation: 2 mol HCl reacts with 1 mol Na2CO3\nMoles HCl = 2 x 0.00125 = 0.0025 mol\nConcentration of HCl = 0.0025 / (20.8/1000) = 0.0025 / 0.0208 = 0.12 M\n\n(ii) Mr of HCl = 1 + 35.5 = 36.5\nConcentration in g/L = 0.12 x 36.5 = 4.38 g/L"},
    {num:2, label:"Q2: Preparation of Ethene from Ethanol", q:"Ethene gas can be prepared from ethanol in a school laboratory.\n\n(a) Draw a labelled diagram showing the apparatus and reagents used in the preparation and collection of ethene.\n(b) Regarding 'suck-back':\n(i) At what stage is suck-back most likely to occur?\n(ii) Give one possible consequence.\n(iii) How could it be avoided?\n(c) Describe how you could test the gas produced for unsaturation.\n(d) Write a balanced equation for the preparation of ethene from ethanol.\n(e) When ethanol is converted to ethene, a 60% yield can be expected. What is the maximum number of 75 cm3 test tubes of ethene that could be collected at room temperature and pressure when 2.4 cm3 of ethanol (density 0.8 g/cm3) react?", a:"(a) Diagram: Round-bottomed flask (or test tube) containing ethanol and aluminium oxide (Al2O3) catalyst (or concentrated sulfuric acid). Heat source beneath. Glass delivery tube leads from the flask to a trough of water where gas is collected by displacement of water in inverted test tubes.\n\n(b)(i) Suck-back is most likely to occur when heating is stopped and the apparatus cools. The gas inside contracts, creating a partial vacuum that can suck water back into the hot flask.\n(ii) Consequence: The cold water entering the hot flask could cause the glass to crack or shatter.\n(iii) Prevention: Remove the delivery tube from the water before stopping heating (or disconnect the tubing).\n\n(c) Test for unsaturation: Add bromine water (brown/orange solution) to a test tube of the gas and shake. If unsaturated, the bromine water decolourises (turns colourless). This confirms a C=C double bond is present.\n\n(d) C2H5OH -> C2H4 + H2O\n(Ethanol is dehydrated using Al2O3 or excess concentrated H2SO4 as catalyst at approximately 170 degrees C.)\n\n(e) Mass of ethanol = volume x density = 2.4 x 0.8 = 1.92 g\nMr of ethanol (C2H5OH) = (2 x 12) + (6 x 1) + 16 = 46\nMoles of ethanol = 1.92 / 46 = 0.04174 mol\nFrom the equation, 1 mol ethanol produces 1 mol ethene.\nMoles of ethene (theoretical) = 0.04174 mol\nAt 60% yield: moles of ethene = 0.6 x 0.04174 = 0.02504 mol\nVolume of ethene at RTP = 0.02504 x 24000 cm3 = 601.0 cm3\nNumber of 75 cm3 test tubes = 601.0 / 75 = 8.01\nMaximum number of test tubes = 8"},
    {num:3, label:"Q3: Relative Molecular Mass of a Volatile Liquid", q:"The relative molecular mass of a volatile liquid can be found by vaporising a known mass at known temperature and pressure.\n\n(a) Give an example of a liquid suitable for use in this experiment.\n(b) Describe how (i) the mass, (ii) the volume, of the vapour is determined.\n(c) Explain why the pressure of the vapour is the same as atmospheric pressure.\n(d) The vapour of 0.63 g of a pure liquid occupies 330 cm3 at 100 degrees C and 101 kPa. Calculate the number of moles and hence the relative molecular mass.\n(e) Why is this method unsuitable for non-volatile liquids? What modern instrumental technique could be used instead?", a:"(a) A suitable liquid: propanone (acetone), ethanol, or cyclohexane -- any liquid with a relatively low boiling point.\n\n(b)(i) Mass: The mass of the liquid injected into the apparatus is found by weighing the syringe (or container) before and after injection. The difference gives the mass of liquid.\n(ii) Volume: Using Apparatus A (foil over flask): Heat the flask in boiling water until all liquid has vaporised and excess vapour has escaped through the pinhole. Cool and weigh to find mass of vapour. Volume = volume of the flask (measured by filling with water and measuring). Using Apparatus B (gas syringe): The plunger of the gas syringe is pushed out by the expanding vapour. Read the volume directly from the gas syringe scale.\n\n(c) The pressure of the vapour equals atmospheric pressure because the apparatus is open to the atmosphere (through the pinhole in Apparatus A, or the gas syringe can move freely in Apparatus B). The vapour can expand or escape until its pressure equals the surrounding atmospheric pressure.\n\n(d) Using the ideal gas equation: PV = nRT\nP = 101 kPa = 101000 Pa\nV = 330 cm3 = 330 x 10^-6 m3 = 3.30 x 10^-4 m3\nT = 100 + 273 = 373 K\nR = 8.3 J K^-1 mol^-1\n\nn = PV / RT = (101000 x 3.30 x 10^-4) / (8.3 x 373)\nn = 33.33 / 3095.9 = 0.01077 mol\n\nRelative molecular mass = mass / moles = 0.63 / 0.01077 = 58.5\nMr is approximately 58 or 59 (consistent with propanone, C3H6O, Mr = 58).\n\n(e) Non-volatile liquids have very high boiling points, so they cannot be easily vaporised at accessible temperatures without decomposing. The method requires complete vaporisation.\nModern technique: Mass spectrometry can accurately determine the relative molecular mass of volatile, non-volatile, liquid, solid and gaseous substances."},
    {num:4, label:"Q4: Short Questions (Mixed Topics)", q:"Answer eight of the following:\n(a) State the number of (i) sub-levels (subshells), (ii) orbitals, occupied by electrons in an argon atom in its ground state.\n(b) Write a balanced nuclear reaction for beta decay of iodine-131.\n(c) Define relative atomic mass.\n(d) Distinguish between sigma and pi covalent bonding.\n(e) Using oxidation numbers, balance: Cu + NO3- + H+ -> Cu2+ + NO2 + H2O\n(f) State Avogadro's law.\n(g) Why does raising temperature generally increase rates of chemical reactions?\n(h) Name (i) the gas, (ii) the ion, formed at the negative electrode when aqueous sodium sulfate is electrolysed.\n(i) Define bond energy.\n(j) What happens during secondary treatment of sewage?\n(k) A: Structural difference between LDPE and HDPE? OR B: What happens in scrubbing of waste acidic gases?", a:"(a)(i) Sub-levels occupied in Ar (1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6): 5 sub-levels (1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p).\n(ii) Orbitals occupied: 9 orbitals (1 + 1 + 3 + 1 + 3 = 9).\n\n(b) In beta decay, a neutron converts to a proton and an electron (beta particle) is emitted.\nI-131 (53 protons) -> Xe-131 (54 protons) + e- (beta particle)\n131/53 I -> 131/54 Xe + 0/-1 e\n\n(c) Relative atomic mass is the weighted average of the masses of the isotopes of an element, measured relative to 1/12 the mass of a carbon-12 atom.\n\n(d) Sigma bond: Formed by head-on (end-on) overlap of atomic orbitals along the bond axis. Electron density is concentrated between the nuclei along the internuclear axis.\nPi bond: Formed by sideways (lateral) overlap of p orbitals above and below the bond axis. Electron density is above and below the internuclear axis. Pi bonds are weaker than sigma bonds.\n\n(e) Assign oxidation numbers:\nCu: 0 -> +2 (oxidised, loses 2e-)\nN in NO3-: +5 -> N in NO2: +4 (reduced, gains 1e-)\nTo balance electrons: 1 Cu loses 2e-, each N gains 1e-, so need 2 NO3-.\nBalanced equation: Cu + 2NO3- + 4H+ -> Cu2+ + 2NO2 + 2H2O\n\n(f) Avogadro's law: Equal volumes of all gases, at the same temperature and pressure, contain the same number of molecules (particles).\n\n(g) Raising temperature increases the kinetic energy of particles. This means particles move faster, collide more frequently, and a greater proportion of collisions have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy. Therefore, the rate of effective collisions increases.\n\n(h)(i) Gas formed at the negative electrode (cathode): Hydrogen (H2).\n(ii) Ion formed at the cathode: Hydroxide ion (OH-).\n(At the cathode: 2H2O + 2e- -> H2 + 2OH-)\n\n(i) Bond energy is the energy required to break one mole of a particular covalent bond in the gaseous state, measured in kJ/mol.\n\n(j) Secondary treatment of sewage: The effluent from primary treatment is aerated (oxygen/air is bubbled through). Aerobic bacteria and micro-organisms break down (oxidise/digest) the dissolved organic matter in the sewage into simpler, harmless substances (CO2 and H2O). This is biological treatment.\n\n(k) A: LDPE (low density polyethene) has a branched chain structure, while HDPE (high density polyethene) has a linear (unbranched) chain structure. The branching in LDPE prevents chains from packing closely together, resulting in lower density.\nB: Scrubbing involves passing waste acidic gases (e.g. SO2, HCl) through a spray or solution of a base (e.g. calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)2). The base neutralises the acidic gases before they are released into the atmosphere. For example: Ca(OH)2 + SO2 -> CaSO3 + H2O."},
    {num:5, label:"Q5: Atomic Structure, Bonding & Periodicity", q:"(a) Write the electron configuration (s, p) of an oxygen atom showing the arrangement of electrons in atomic orbitals.\n(b) Define atomic radius (covalent radius). State and explain the trend in atomic radii across the second period.\n(c) Give one reason why electronegativity values increase across the second period.\n(d) Consider H2O, NH3, PH3, HCl:\n(i) How does bonding in PH3 differ from the other three? What is the reason?\n(ii) Identify the hydride(s) in which hydrogen bonding occurs. Give one property affected.\n(iii) State the shape of PH3 and explain using VSEPR theory.\n(e) BCl3 is a colourless gas. Would you expect (i) the B-Cl bonds, (ii) the BCl3 molecules, to be polar or non-polar? Justify.", a:"(a) Oxygen (atomic number 8): 1s2 2s2 2p4\nOrbital diagram: 1s: [up,down] 2s: [up,down] 2px: [up,down] 2py: [up] 2pz: [up]\n(Two of the 2p orbitals have single electrons, one has a pair -- following Hund's rule.)\n\n(b) Atomic radius (covalent radius): Half the distance between the nuclei of two identical atoms joined by a single covalent bond.\nTrend: Atomic radii decrease across the second period (Li to Ne).\nExplanation: As you go across the period, the nuclear charge (number of protons) increases by one each time, but electrons are added to the same energy level (same shell). The increased nuclear charge pulls the electron cloud closer to the nucleus, and shielding remains roughly constant, so the effective nuclear charge increases and the atoms get smaller.\n\n(c) Electronegativity increases across the second period because the nuclear charge increases while the shielding remains approximately constant. The bonding electrons are more strongly attracted to the nucleus.\n\n(d)(i) In PH3, the bonding is purely covalent (non-polar covalent). In H2O, NH3 and HCl, the bonds are polar covalent. The reason is that the electronegativity difference between P and H is very small (both have similar electronegativity values around 2.1-2.2), so the P-H bond is essentially non-polar. In contrast, O, N, and Cl are significantly more electronegative than H.\n\n(ii) Hydrogen bonding occurs in H2O and NH3. These molecules have a highly electronegative atom (O or N) bonded directly to hydrogen, and both O and N have lone pairs -- the conditions required for hydrogen bonding.\nProperty affected: Unusually high boiling point (higher than expected based on molecular mass alone). H2O has a boiling point of 100 degrees C, much higher than H2S (which has no H-bonding).\n\n(iii) Shape of PH3: Pyramidal (trigonal pyramidal).\nVSEPR explanation: P has 5 valence electrons. In PH3, there are 3 bonding pairs (one shared with each H) and 1 lone pair on P. Total = 4 electron pairs, which adopt a tetrahedral arrangement. However, the lone pair is not seen in the molecular shape, so the shape described by the atoms is pyramidal. The bond angle is slightly less than 109.5 degrees because the lone pair repels more strongly than the bonding pairs, compressing them.\n\n(e)(i) The B-Cl bonds are polar. Boron and chlorine have different electronegativities (Cl is more electronegative), so each B-Cl bond has a dipole with partial negative charge on Cl.\n(ii) The BCl3 molecule overall is non-polar. BCl3 has a trigonal planar shape (3 bonding pairs, no lone pairs on B). The three bond dipoles are symmetrically arranged at 120 degrees, so they cancel each other out, giving a net zero dipole moment."},
    {num:6, label:"Q6: Oil Refining, Fuels & Thermochemistry", q:"Several processes convert less useful hydrocarbons into more useful ones:\n(i) C6H14(l) -> 4H2(g) + aromatic(l) [dehydrocyclisation]\n(ii) C14H30(l) -> C7H16(l) + C3H6(g) + X(g) [cracking]\n(iii) CH3(CH2)6CH3(l) -> branched isomer(l) [isomerisation]\n\n(a) For each conversion (i) to (iii), name the process.\n(b) Name hydrocarbon X. Name the product of process (iii) and state its octane number.\n(c) Explain why MTBE is added to motor fuel. Name the substance previously used for the same purpose and state why it was discontinued.\n(d) Define heat of combustion. Name the apparatus used to measure heats of combustion.\n(e) Calculate the heat of formation of C8H18 produced in process (iii), given its heat of combustion is -5502 kJ/mol and the heats of formation of CO2 and H2O are -394 and -286 kJ/mol respectively.", a:"(a)(i) Dehydrocyclisation (reforming) -- converts a straight-chain alkane into a cyclic aromatic hydrocarbon with release of hydrogen.\n(ii) Catalytic cracking -- breaks a large hydrocarbon molecule into smaller, more useful molecules.\n(iii) Isomerisation -- converts a straight-chain alkane into its branched-chain isomer.\n\n(b) Hydrocarbon X: C4H8 (butene, an alkene).\nCheck: C14H30 -> C7H16 + C3H6 + C4H8 (carbon: 14=7+3+4; hydrogen: 30=16+6+8). Correct.\nProduct of process (iii): 2,2,4-trimethylpentane (isooctane).\nOctane number: 100 (by definition, 2,2,4-trimethylpentane is the reference standard for octane number 100).\n\n(c) MTBE (methyl tert-butyl ether) is added to motor fuel to increase its octane number (reduce engine knocking/pre-ignition) and promote more complete combustion.\nPrevious substance: Tetraethyllead (Pb(C2H5)4) was previously added for the same purpose.\nDiscontinued because: Lead compounds are toxic. Lead in exhaust fumes pollutes the environment, causes health problems (particularly neurological damage), and poisons catalytic converters in car exhaust systems.\n\n(d) Heat of combustion: The enthalpy change when one mole of a substance is completely burned in excess oxygen under standard conditions.\nApparatus: Bomb calorimeter (for accurate measurement of heats of combustion of fuels and foodstuffs).\n\n(e) Combustion of C8H18:\nC8H18(l) + 12.5 O2(g) -> 8CO2(g) + 9H2O(l)\n\nUsing Hess's Law:\ndelta H(combustion) = [8 x delta Hf(CO2) + 9 x delta Hf(H2O)] - [delta Hf(C8H18)]\n-5502 = [8(-394) + 9(-286)] - delta Hf(C8H18)\n-5502 = [-3152 + (-2574)] - delta Hf(C8H18)\n-5502 = -5726 - delta Hf(C8H18)\ndelta Hf(C8H18) = -5726 - (-5502) = -5726 + 5502 = -224 kJ/mol\n\nThe heat of formation of C8H18 (2,2,4-trimethylpentane) is -224 kJ/mol."},
    {num:7, label:"Q7: Water Treatment & Hard Water", q:"(a) Explain how hard water is caused and how it wastes soap. How can hard water be softened by ion exchange to produce deionised water?\n(b) In water treatment, what is the purpose of adding: (i) a flocculating agent, (ii) chlorine, (iii) a fluorine-containing compound, (iv) calcium hydroxide, (v) sulfuric acid? State the problem when each of any two of these is added in excessive quantity.\n(c) Why is pollution by heavy metal ions (Hg2+ or Pb2+) a concern? Name an instrumental technique to detect heavy metals. Explain how Hg2+ or Pb2+ ions can be removed from a water supply.\n(d) Describe a test for the presence of chloride ion (Cl-) in water.", a:"(a) Cause: Hard water is caused by the presence of dissolved calcium ions (Ca2+) and/or magnesium ions (Mg2+). These dissolve when rainwater (containing dissolved CO2, forming weak carbonic acid) flows over limestone (CaCO3) or dolomite: CaCO3 + CO2 + H2O -> Ca(HCO3)2.\n\nWaste of soap: Hard water wastes soap because Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions react with the sodium stearate (soap) to form an insoluble scum (calcium/magnesium stearate). No lather forms until all the Ca2+/Mg2+ has been removed.\n\nIon exchange for deionised water: Pass the hard water through a cation-exchange column (resin RH) which replaces Ca2+ and Mg2+ with H+ ions: Ca2+ + 2RH -> CaR2 + 2H+. Then pass through an anion-exchange column (resin R'OH) which replaces anions (Cl-, HCO3-, SO42-) with OH- ions. The H+ and OH- combine to form water. The result is deionised water.\n\n(b)(i) Flocculating agent (e.g. aluminium sulfate/alum): Causes small suspended particles to clump together (flocculate) into larger particles that settle out or can be filtered.\n(ii) Chlorine: Kills bacteria and other harmful micro-organisms (disinfection/sterilisation).\n(iii) Fluorine-containing compound (e.g. H2SiF6 or NaF): Prevents tooth decay (strengthens tooth enamel).\n(iv) Calcium hydroxide (lime): Adjusts (raises) the pH of acidic water to an acceptable level.\n(v) Sulfuric acid: Adjusts (lowers) the pH of alkaline water to an acceptable level.\n\nProblems from excess:\n- Excess chlorine: Unpleasant taste and smell; can form harmful chlorinated compounds; irritation to skin and eyes.\n- Excess fluoride: Fluorosis (mottling/discolouration of teeth; at very high levels, bone damage).\n\n(c) Concern: Heavy metal ions (Hg2+, Pb2+) are toxic and accumulate in the body (bioaccumulation). They are not broken down and can cause serious damage to the nervous system, kidneys, and other organs even at very low concentrations.\nInstrumental technique: Atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS) can detect and measure the concentration of heavy metal ions in water.\nRemoval: Heavy metal ions can be removed by ion exchange (passing water through an ion-exchange resin that exchanges the heavy metal ions for harmless ions like Na+ or H+). Alternatively, precipitation or adsorption onto activated carbon can be used.\n\n(d) Test for chloride ion: Add a few drops of dilute nitric acid (to acidify the solution and prevent false positives from carbonate or sulfite ions). Then add a few drops of silver nitrate (AgNO3) solution. If chloride ions are present, a white precipitate of silver chloride (AgCl) forms: Ag+ + Cl- -> AgCl (white precipitate)."},
    {num:8, label:"Q8: Organic Reaction Scheme (Alcohols, Aldehydes, Esters)", q:"Study the reaction scheme involving:\nAlcohol A, Propan-1-ol, Propanal, Propanoic acid, Propanone, Ester B, Methanoic acid.\nConversions labelled R involve oxidation.\n\n(a) Give the IUPAC name for (i) Alcohol A, (ii) Ester B.\n(b) Alcohol A and propan-1-ol are structural isomers. Explain this term. What is the structural difference between a primary and secondary alcohol? Identify another pair of structural isomers from the scheme.\n(c) Identify a compound in the scheme whose carbon atoms are all in tetrahedral geometry.\n(d) Name the reagent and catalyst used to bring about conversions R.\n(e) Propanal is oxidised by Fehling's reagent. Describe how this reaction is carried out. Why does propanone not react with Fehling's reagent?\n(f) Which compound in the scheme would you expect to have a fruity odour?", a:"(a)(i) Alcohol A: Propan-2-ol (it is oxidised to propanone, so it must be the secondary alcohol isomer of propan-1-ol).\n(ii) Ester B: The ester formed from propan-1-ol and methanoic acid is methyl methanoate -- however, since propan-1-ol is oxidised to propanoic acid, and looking at the scheme, Ester B is formed between propanoic acid and propan-1-ol, which gives propyl propanoate. Alternatively, if the ester involves methanoic acid and propan-1-ol: propyl methanoate. The most likely answer from the scheme: Ester B = propyl methanoate (propan-1-yl methanoate) -- the ester of methanoic acid and propan-1-ol.\n\n(b) Structural isomers: Compounds that have the same molecular formula but different structural formulae (different arrangement of atoms). Alcohol A (propan-2-ol) and propan-1-ol both have the formula C3H7OH but differ in the position of the -OH group.\n\nPrimary alcohol: The carbon bearing the -OH group is bonded to at most one other carbon atom (e.g. propan-1-ol: CH3CH2CH2OH).\nSecondary alcohol: The carbon bearing the -OH group is bonded to two other carbon atoms (e.g. propan-2-ol: CH3CH(OH)CH3).\n\nAnother pair of structural isomers: Propanal (CH3CH2CHO) and propanone (CH3COCH3) -- both have the formula C3H6O but different structures.\n\n(c) Alcohol A (propan-2-ol) or propan-1-ol -- all carbon atoms are sp3 hybridised (tetrahedral geometry with approximately 109.5 degree bond angles). In contrast, propanal, propanone and acids have carbonyl carbons in trigonal planar geometry.\n\n(d) Reagent: Acidified potassium dichromate (K2Cr2O7 / H2SO4) or acidified sodium dichromate (Na2Cr2O7 / H+).\nCatalyst: Sulfuric acid (H2SO4) acts as the acid catalyst.\n(The dichromate solution changes from orange to green as Cr(VI) is reduced to Cr(III).)\n\n(e) Fehling's test on propanal: Add Fehling's solution A (copper(II) sulfate, blue) and Fehling's solution B (sodium hydroxide and sodium potassium tartrate) to a test tube containing propanal. Heat gently in a water bath. A brick-red precipitate of copper(I) oxide (Cu2O) forms, and the blue colour disappears. This shows that propanal (an aldehyde) has been oxidised to propanoic acid.\n\nPropanone does not react with Fehling's reagent because propanone is a ketone, not an aldehyde. Ketones do not have a hydrogen atom on the carbonyl carbon and cannot be easily oxidised under mild conditions. They are resistant to oxidation by mild oxidising agents like Fehling's solution.\n\n(f) Ester B would be expected to have a fruity odour. Esters are characterised by their pleasant, fruity smells."},
    {num:9, label:"Q9: Rates of Reaction (Marble Chips & HCl)", q:"(a) Define rate of reaction.\n\nData: Loss of mass of 50 cm3 of 2 M HCl + excess marble chips vs time:\nLoss of mass (g): 0.00, 0.10, 0.18, 0.29, 0.35, 0.39, 0.41, 0.41\nTime (s): 0, 20, 40, 80, 120, 160, 220, 240\n\nPlot a graph of CO2 produced vs time. Find the instantaneous rate at 60 seconds. Mark and justify the curve expected with 1 M HCl.\n\n(b) When H2O2 is added to warm potassium sodium tartrate, a slow reaction occurs. Co2+ ions catalyse the reaction.\nWhat type of catalysis is involved? What colour changes are observed? Explain their significance.", a:"(a) Rate of reaction: The change in concentration (or amount) of a reactant or product per unit time.\n\nGraph: Plot time (s) on x-axis (0-240) and loss of mass / CO2 produced (g) on y-axis (0-0.45). The curve rises steeply at first and then levels off at 0.41 g (reaction complete).\n\nInstantaneous rate at 60 s: Draw a tangent to the curve at t = 60 s. Calculate the slope of the tangent.\nFrom the data, at t = 40 s loss = 0.18 g and at t = 80 s loss = 0.29 g.\nApproximate rate at 60 s = change in mass / change in time from the tangent.\nUsing points on the tangent (e.g. reading from graph): approximately (0.29 - 0.10)/(80 - 20) = 0.19/60 = 0.0032 g/s (accept 0.003-0.004 g/s depending on tangent drawn).\n\n1 M HCl curve: The curve starts at the origin (same starting point). It rises less steeply (lower initial rate) because the lower concentration means fewer acid particles per unit volume, so fewer effective collisions per second. It levels off at approximately half the final mass loss (about 0.21 g) because 1 M acid has half as many moles of HCl as 2 M acid, so it produces half as much CO2 before the acid is used up. The marble chips are in excess in both cases.\n\n(b) Type of catalysis: Homogeneous catalysis (the Co2+ ions are in the same phase -- aqueous solution -- as the reactants).\n\nColour changes: The solution initially is pink/red (colour of Co2+ ions). When acting as catalyst, the colour changes to green (Co3+ or a cobalt complex with tartrate is formed as an intermediate). The green colour then fades back to pink as the Co2+ ions are regenerated.\n\nSignificance: The colour changes show that the catalyst is involved in the reaction mechanism. The catalyst forms an intermediate compound (different oxidation state of cobalt, hence different colour), which then reacts further to regenerate the original catalyst. The catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy. The fact that the original colour returns proves the catalyst is not consumed overall."},
    {num:10, label:"Q10: Alkene Reactions, Acids/Bases & Redox Calculations", q:"Answer any two of (a), (b) and (c).\n\n(a)(i) Account for the greater reactivity of alkenes compared to alkanes.\n(ii) Describe the mechanism of the addition of Br2 to ethene. State one piece of evidence to support it.\n\n(b) Define an acid (Bronsted-Lowry). What is a conjugate pair?\nAn indicator HIn (green) dissociates: HIn <=> H+ + In- (purple).\nState and explain the colour in 0.5 M NaOH.\nCalculate the pH of (i) 0.5 M NaOH, (ii) a 0.1 M solution of the indicator (Ka = 2.0 x 10^-5).\n\n(c) Reaction: 3Ag2S + 2Al -> 6Ag + Al2S3. Mass of bracelet decreased by 0.0096 g.\n(i) What was oxidised?\n(ii) How many moles of S were removed?\n(iii) What mass of Al was used?\n(iv) What would the mass loss have been if all Ag2S were removed by polishing?", a:"(a)(i) Alkenes are more reactive than alkanes because they contain a C=C double bond. The double bond consists of a sigma bond and a pi bond. The pi bond has electron density above and below the plane of the molecule, making it exposed and accessible to electrophilic attack. The pi electrons are loosely held and easily donated to electrophiles. Alkanes only have strong sigma bonds that are harder to break.\n\n(ii) Mechanism of addition of Br2 to ethene (electrophilic addition):\nStep 1: As the Br2 molecule approaches the electron-rich C=C double bond, the Br-Br bond becomes polarised (induced dipole). The nearer Br atom becomes slightly positive (electrophile) and is attracted to the pi electrons of the double bond.\nStep 2: The pi electrons attack the Br-delta+, forming a C-Br bond. The Br-Br bond breaks heterolytically, releasing Br- ion. A carbocation intermediate (or bromonium ion intermediate) forms.\nStep 3: The Br- ion (nucleophile) attacks the carbocation, forming the second C-Br bond.\nProduct: 1,2-dibromoethane (CH2BrCH2Br).\n\nEvidence: When the reaction is carried out in the presence of NaCl (chloride ions), the product mixture contains 1-bromo-2-chloroethane (BrCH2CH2Cl) as well as 1,2-dibromoethane. This shows that a carbocation intermediate must form, which can be attacked by either Br- or Cl- -- supporting an ionic (electrophilic) mechanism rather than a radical one.\n\n(b) Bronsted-Lowry acid: A proton (H+) donor.\nConjugate pair: An acid and base that differ by one proton (H+). When an acid donates a proton, it forms its conjugate base. When a base accepts a proton, it forms its conjugate acid.\n\nColour in 0.5 M NaOH: Purple. The NaOH is a strong base; the OH- ions react with H+ from the indicator equilibrium (HIn <=> H+ + In-), removing H+ and shifting the equilibrium to the right. This produces more In- (purple), so the solution appears purple.\n\n(i) pH of 0.5 M NaOH:\n[OH-] = 0.5 M\npOH = -log(0.5) = 0.30\npH = 14 - 0.30 = 13.70\n\n(ii) pH of 0.1 M indicator solution (Ka = 2.0 x 10^-5):\nKa = [H+][In-] / [HIn] = x^2 / (0.1 - x), where x is small compared to 0.1.\nx^2 = 2.0 x 10^-5 x 0.1 = 2.0 x 10^-6\nx = [H+] = 1.41 x 10^-3 M\npH = -log(1.41 x 10^-3) = 2.85\n\n(c)(i) Aluminium (Al) was oxidised. Al goes from oxidation state 0 to +3 in Al2S3 (it loses electrons).\n\n(ii) The mass of the bracelet decreased by 0.0096 g. This mass loss represents the aluminium that was incorporated minus the sulfur removed, but more precisely:\nIn the reaction, Al replaces Ag in Ag2S. The mass decrease of the bracelet = mass of Ag2S removed - mass of Ag returned = mass of S removed + mass of Ag removed - mass of Ag returned. Since Ag is returned, the mass loss = mass of S removed - mass of Al added.\n\nActually, the mass decrease comes from S atoms leaving the bracelet (as Al2S3) being greater than Al atoms added. Let's reconsider:\nThe net mass change of the bracelet: Ag2S is on the bracelet. The reaction converts Ag2S to Ag (stays on bracelet) + Al2S3 (leaves). So the bracelet loses sulfur but gains nothing -- the Al dissolves into solution as Al2S3.\nMass lost by bracelet = mass of sulfur that was in the Ag2S coating.\nMoles of S removed = 0.0096 / 32 = 0.0003 mol = 3.0 x 10^-4 mol\n\n(iii) From the equation: 3Ag2S + 2Al -> 6Ag + Al2S3\n3 mol S corresponds to 2 mol Al (since each Ag2S has 1 S, and 3 mol Ag2S has 3 mol S).\nMoles of Al = (2/3) x 0.0003 = 0.0002 mol\nMass of Al = 0.0002 x 27 = 0.0054 g\n\n(iv) If all Ag2S were removed by polishing (physically removing the tarnish layer entirely):\nMoles of Ag2S = 0.0003 mol (same as moles of S)\nMass of Ag2S = 0.0003 x (2 x 108 + 32) = 0.0003 x 248 = 0.0744 g\nThe loss in mass would be 0.0744 g (all the silver sulfide is removed, including the silver atoms)."},
    {num:11, label:"Q11: Rutherford's Experiment, Electrochemistry & Stoichiometry", q:"Answer any two of (a), (b) and (c).\n\n(a) In 1909 Rutherford bombarded gold foil with alpha particles.\n(i) What are alpha particles?\n(ii) Describe Thomson's 'plum pudding' model.\n(iii) Explain why some alpha particles were deflected at large angles.\n(iv) Why were some reflected back? Why only a very small number?\n(v) Why were Rutherford's observations not consistent with Thomson's model?\n\n(b)(i) Define electronegativity.\n(ii) Why do electronegativity values increase across a period?\n(iii) List H2O, NH3, CH4 in order of increasing bond angle. Explain.\n(iv) H2O has a much higher boiling point than H2S. Explain.\n\n(c)(i) Define heat of formation.\n(ii) C2H4(g) + 3O2(g) -> 2CO2(g) + 2H2O(l). Calculate delta H of combustion of ethene, given heats of formation: C2H4 = +52, CO2 = -394, H2O = -286 kJ/mol.\n(iii) Calculate the volume of CO2 produced at STP when 7.0 g of ethene burns completely.", a:"(a)(i) Alpha particles are helium nuclei, consisting of 2 protons and 2 neutrons (He-4 nuclei, charge +2).\n\n(ii) Thomson's plum pudding model: The atom is a sphere of uniformly distributed positive charge (the 'pudding') with negatively charged electrons embedded within it (the 'plums'), like raisins in a pudding. The total positive and negative charges are equal, making the atom neutral.\n\n(iii) Some alpha particles were deflected at large angles because they passed close to a concentrated region of positive charge (the nucleus). The electrostatic repulsion between the positive alpha particle and the positive nucleus caused the deflection. The closer the alpha particle passed to the nucleus, the greater the deflection.\n\n(iv) A very small number were reflected straight back because they were on a direct collision course with the nucleus. The nucleus is extremely small, dense and positively charged, causing the alpha particles to rebound. Only a very small number experienced this because the nucleus occupies a tiny fraction of the total volume of the atom -- most of the atom is empty space.\n\n(v) Thomson's model had the positive charge spread uniformly throughout the atom. This diffuse distribution of charge would not be able to cause large-angle deflections or reflections of fast-moving, massive alpha particles. Only a concentrated mass of positive charge (a nucleus) could do this.\n\n(b)(i) Electronegativity: A measure of the relative attraction that an atom in a molecule has for a shared pair of electrons in a covalent bond.\n\n(ii) Across a period, the nuclear charge increases (more protons) while the number of inner shielding shells stays the same. The increased effective nuclear charge means the atom attracts bonding electrons more strongly, increasing electronegativity.\n\n(iii) Order of increasing bond angle: H2O (104.5 degrees) < NH3 (107 degrees) < CH4 (109.5 degrees).\nExplanation: All three molecules have 4 pairs of electrons around the central atom (tetrahedral electron arrangement). CH4 has 4 bonding pairs with no lone pairs -- symmetrical tetrahedral shape (109.5 degrees). NH3 has 3 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair; the lone pair repels more strongly, compressing the bond angles to 107 degrees. H2O has 2 bonding pairs and 2 lone pairs; two lone pairs compress the bond angle further to 104.5 degrees.\n\n(iv) H2O has a much higher boiling point than H2S because water molecules are held together by hydrogen bonds (strong intermolecular forces). Hydrogen bonding occurs in H2O because oxygen is highly electronegative and small, with lone pairs that can interact with the H of neighbouring molecules. In H2S, sulfur is less electronegative and larger, so hydrogen bonding does not occur. H2S molecules are held together only by weaker van der Waals forces, requiring less energy to separate.\n\n(c)(i) Heat of formation: The enthalpy change when one mole of a compound is formed from its elements in their standard states under standard conditions.\n\n(ii) delta H(combustion) = sum of delta Hf(products) - sum of delta Hf(reactants)\n= [2(-394) + 2(-286)] - [+52 + 0]\n= [-788 + (-572)] - [52]\n= -1360 - 52 = -1412 kJ/mol\nThe heat of combustion of ethene is -1412 kJ/mol.\n\n(iii) Mr of C2H4 = (2 x 12) + (4 x 1) = 28\nMoles of C2H4 = 7.0 / 28 = 0.25 mol\nFrom equation: 1 mol C2H4 produces 2 mol CO2\nMoles of CO2 = 2 x 0.25 = 0.50 mol\nVolume at STP = 0.50 x 22.4 = 11.2 litres of CO2"}
  ]
};
